[$\mathcal{F}$ is a $w^{\ast}$-algebra thus one can define the spectrum of
elements, spectral expansions and spectral measures. Positive elements have
a positive spectrum.]

Using the $\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}$ functional we can define a complex Hilbert
space $\mathcal{A}_{HS}$ with inner product given by 
\begin{equation}
\left\langle \left. A\right\vert B\right\rangle _{HS}=\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}%
\left\{ A^{\dagger}B\right\}
\end{equation}
and thus norm 
\begin{equation}
\left\Vert A\right\Vert _{\mathcal{HS}}=\left( \mathit{\limfunc{tr}}\left\{
A^{\dagger}A\right\} \right) ^{\frac{1}{2}}.
\end{equation}
(Other positive linear functionals belonging to $\mathcal{A}^{d}$ produce
Hilbert spaces through the GNS construction). The limit of the $\mathit{%
\limfunc{tr}}$ functional of a sequence of operators depends on the topology
used and may be infinite e.g. 
\begin{equation}
\lim\limits_{j\rightarrow\infty}\left\{ \mathit{\limfunc{tr}}\left\{
A_{j}^{\dagger}A_{j}\right\} =\infty\right\} ,
\end{equation}
as not all operators belong to $\mathcal{A}_{HS}.$We can use this Hilbert
space to define an operator norm, (generated by the left regular
representation), $\left\Vert A\right\Vert _{\mathcal{A}},$ on the $\ast $%
-algebra $\mathcal{A}_{finite}$ by 
\begin{align}
\left\Vert A\right\Vert _{\mathcal{A}} & =\sup\limits_{X\in\mathcal{A}%
_{HS}}\left\{ \frac{\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}\left\{ \left( AX\right)
^{\dagger}AX\right\} }{\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}\left\{ X^{\dagger }X\right\} }%
\right\} =\sup\limits_{\left\Vert X\right\Vert _{\mathcal{HS}}=1}\left\{ 
\limfunc{tr}\left\{ \left( AX\right) ^{\dagger }AX\right\} \right\}
=\sup\limits_{\left\Vert X\right\Vert _{\mathcal{HS}}=1}\left\{ \limfunc{tr}%
\left\{ X^{\dagger}A^{\dagger}AX\right\} \right\} =\sup\limits_{\left\Vert
X\right\Vert _{\mathcal{HS}}=1}\left\{ \limfunc{tr}\left\{
A^{\dagger}AXX^{\dagger}\right\} \right\} \\
& =\sup\limits_{D\in\mathcal{S}_{\mathcal{A}}}\left\{ \limfunc{tr}\left\{
A^{\dagger}AD\right\} \right\} ,
\end{align}
where $\mathcal{S}_{\mathcal{A}}$ is the convex set of normal states of $%
\mathcal{A}.$

The closure of $\mathcal{A}_{finite}$ wrt the $\left\Vert {}\right\Vert _{%
\mathcal{A}}$ norm produces a $c^{\ast}$-algebra $\mathcal{A}^{c},$ that has
a normalized trace $\limfunc{tr}$ (though observe that not all elements of $%
\mathcal{A}^{c}$ have finite trace). One should note that the existance of
the normalized trace $\limfunc{tr}$ is not essential for the
definition/charactrization of $\mathcal{A}^{c},$ it is however essential for
the definition of $\mathcal{A}_{HS}.$ Note that the $c^{\ast}$-algebra of
bounded operators $\mathcal{B}\left( \mathcal{A}_{HS}\right) $ acting in $%
\mathcal{A}_{HS}$ \emph{contains} the space of operators produced by the
action of left multiplication.

The closure of $\mathcal{A}_{finite}$ in the strong operator topology
induced by $\mathcal{A}_{HS}$ and the left regular representation i.e. 
\begin{equation}
\lim\limits_{j\rightarrow\infty}\left\{ \mathit{\limfunc{tr}}\left\{
X^{\dagger}\left( A-A_{j}\right) ^{\dagger}\left( A-A_{j}\right) X\right\}
\right\} \forall X=0\equiv s\text{-}\lim\limits_{j}A_{j}=A
\end{equation}
is a $w^{\ast}$-algebra, $\mathcal{A}^{w},$ called the enveloping $w^{\ast}$%
-algebra of the $c^{\ast}$-algebra $\mathcal{A}^{c}.$ The existance of
HS-normalized elements (i.e. amplitudes and their associated density
operators) in the infinite case is very significant as the $\mathit{\limfunc{%
tr}}$-HS\ limit leads to a type $II_{1}$ Von Neumann Algebra $\mathcal{A}%
^{\prime\prime}$, where \emph{normal} pure states do not exist.

The norm, $\left\Vert {}\right\Vert _{\mathcal{A}},$ can be \emph{extended}
to general linear operators $T\in\mathcal{L}\left( \mathcal{A}\right) $
acting in $\mathcal{A}$ 
\begin{equation}
T:\mathcal{A\longrightarrow A}
\end{equation}
(not just multiplication operators) by%
\begin{equation}
\left\Vert T\right\Vert _{\mathcal{A}}=\sup\limits_{\left\Vert X\right\Vert
_{\mathcal{HS}}=1}\left\{ \limfunc{tr}\left\{ T\left( X\right)
^{\dagger}T\left( X\right) \right\} \right\} ,
\end{equation}
and even further by 
\begin{equation}
\left\Vert T\right\Vert _{\mathcal{L}\left( \mathcal{A}\right)
}=\sup\limits_{\left\Vert X\right\Vert _{\mathcal{A}}=1}\left\{ \limfunc{tr}%
\left\{ T\left( X\right) ^{\dagger}T\left( X\right) \right\} \right\} .
\end{equation}
This allows one to define the space of bounded operators, $\mathcal{B}\left( 
\mathcal{A}\right) ,$ acting in $\mathcal{A}$ by%
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{B}\left( \mathcal{A}\right) =\left\{ A\left\vert \left\Vert
T\right\Vert _{\mathcal{L}\left( \mathcal{A}\right) }<\infty\right. \right\}
.
\end{equation}

A question one needs to be answered is whether $\mathcal{A}^{w}$ is closed
in the topology induced by the norm $\left\Vert {}\right\Vert _{\mathcal{A}}$%
? An answer can be deduced by the following:

Every Banach space is a closed subspace of its double dual (which by
definition is a closed Banach space) i.e. 
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{A}^{\ast\ast}\supseteq\mathcal{A},
\end{equation}
the Banach space is reflexive if the two spaces are equal and if the space
is not reflexive then $\mathcal{A}$ is a closed subspace of $\mathcal{A}%
^{\ast\ast}.$ From below 
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{A}^{w}=\left( \mathcal{A}^{c}\right) ^{\ast\ast}\supseteq \mathcal{A%
}^{c}
\end{equation}
and the Banach space norms in $\mathcal{A}^{w}$ and $\mathcal{A}^{c}$ are
the same.

\subsubsection{Charaterization of $w^{\ast}$-algebras}

A $w^{\ast}$-algebra is characterized by the strong operator closure
property \emph{or equivalently} by

\begin{enumerate}
\item \emph{the algebraic property} 
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{A}^{w}=\left( \mathcal{A}^{w}\right) ^{\prime\prime}.
\end{equation}
One must define in which set the commutant operation $^{\prime}$ takes
place. It seems that $\mathcal{B}\left( \mathcal{A}_{HS}\right) $ would be a
good candidate.

It is obvious that in general 
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{A}^{\prime\prime}\supseteq\mathcal{A}
\end{equation}
so the $w^{\ast}$-algebra case is a special case where equality holds,

\item a $c^{\ast}$-algebra that has a predual $\mathcal{A}_{\ast},$which is
a Banach space.
\end{enumerate}

A link between $w^{\ast}$ and $c^{\ast}$ algebras is that if $\mathcal{A}$
is a non-degenerate $c^{\ast}$-algebra then its double dual $\mathcal{A}%
^{\ast\ast}$ is a $w^{\ast}$-algebra. In general 
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{A}^{\ast\ast}\supseteq\mathcal{A}.
\end{equation}

Thus 
\begin{equation}
\left( \mathcal{A}^{\ast\ast}\right) ^{\prime\prime}=\mathcal{A}^{\ast\ast}
\end{equation}
where $\mathcal{A}$ is a $c^{\ast}$-algebra, showing that $\mathcal{A}%
^{\ast\ast}$ is a $w^{\ast}$-algebra.

$\lceil$ It is always true that 
\begin{align}
\mathcal{A}^{\prime\prime\prime} & =\mathcal{A}^{\prime} \\
\mathcal{A}^{\prime\prime\prime\prime} & =\mathcal{A}^{\prime\prime}
\end{align}
so $\mathcal{A}^{\prime\prime},$ the double commutant of $\mathcal{A},$ is
always a $w^{\ast}$-algebra. However in general 
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{A}^{\ast\ast\ast}\neq\mathcal{A}^{\ast}.
\end{equation}
$\rfloor$

The \emph{Sherman--Takeda theorem} states that if $\mathcal{A}$ is a $%
c^{\ast }$-algebra then its double dual is a $w^{\ast}$-algebra, and any
homomorphism of $\mathcal{A}$ to a $w^{\ast}$-algebra factors through its
double dual. The theorem was announced by \emph{Sherman} (1950) and proved
by \emph{Takeda} (1954). The double dual of $\mathcal{A}$ is called the
universal enveloping $w^{\ast}$-algebra of $\mathcal{A}$. By the
Sherman--Takeda theorem, the double dual of a $c^{\ast}$-algebra $\mathcal{A}
$, $\mathcal{A}^{\ast\ast}$, can be identified with $\mathcal{A}%
^{\prime\prime}$ as Banach spaces i.e. *******%
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{A}^{\prime\prime}\overset{Banach}{=}\mathcal{A}^{\ast\ast}.
\end{equation}
$\lceil$As an example 
\begin{align}
\mathcal{B}\left( \mathcal{H}\right) ^{\prime} & =\left\{ I\right\} \\
\left\{ I\right\} ^{\prime} & =\mathcal{B}\left( \mathcal{H}\right)
^{\prime\prime}=\mathcal{B}\left( \mathcal{H}\right) ,
\end{align}
where the cummutators are taken in $\mathcal{B}\left( \mathcal{H}\right) .$%
Thus%
\begin{align}
\mathcal{B}\left( \mathcal{H}\right) & =\mathcal{B}\left( \mathcal{H}\right)
^{\ast\ast}\text{ (thefore reflexive)} \\
\mathcal{B}\left( \mathcal{H}\right) ^{\ast} & =\mathcal{B}_{1}\left( 
\mathcal{H}\right) ^{\ast\ast}\supseteq\mathcal{B}_{1}\left( \mathcal{H}%
\right) .
\end{align}
Dixmier proves 
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{B}\left( \mathcal{H}\right) =\mathcal{B}_{0}\left( \mathcal{H}%
\right) ^{\ast\ast},
\end{equation}
where 
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{B}_{0}\left( \mathcal{H}\right) =\overline{\mathcal{B}%
_{finite}\left( \mathcal{H}\right) }=\overline{\mathcal{B}_{1}\left( 
\mathcal{H}\right) }=\overline{\mathcal{B}_{2}\left( \mathcal{H}\right) }
\end{equation}
and closures are taken in operator norm, $\left\Vert {}\right\Vert _{%
\mathcal{B}\left( \mathcal{H}\right) },$ topology. Is $\mathcal{B}_{0}\left( 
\mathcal{H}\right) $ a $c^{\ast}$-algebra.$\rfloor$

The double commutant $\left( \mathcal{A}^{c}\right) ^{\prime\prime}$ is a $%
w^{\ast}$-algebra $\mathcal{A}^{w},$ which in general does not have a
normalized trace and is the closure of $\mathcal{A}_{finite}$ in the strong,
weak, ultra strong and ultraweak topologies in $\mathcal{A}_{finite}$
induced by $\mathcal{A}_{HS}$. It is also closed in the norm topology that
is the same in $\mathcal{A}^{w}$ and $\mathcal{A}^{c}.$

$\lceil$Another example, this time commutative, is given by 
\begin{equation}
L_{\infty}\left( \mathbb{R},\lambda\right) =L_{1}\left( \mathbb{R}%
,\lambda\right) ^{\ast}
\end{equation}%
\begin{equation}
L_{\infty}\left( \mathbb{R},\lambda\right) ^{\ast}=L_{1}\left( \mathbb{R}%
,\lambda\right) ^{\ast\ast}=M\left( \mathbb{R},\lambda\right) \supset
L_{1}\left( \mathbb{R},\lambda\right) ,
\end{equation}
where $M\left( \mathbb{R},\lambda\right) $ is the space of measures on $%
\mathbb{R}.$ Note that $L_{1}\left( \mathbb{R},\lambda\right) $ is not an
algebra.$\rfloor$

\section{Type $I_{\infty}$ Von Neumann Algebra $\mathcal{B}\left( \mathcal{H}%
_{\mathcal{F}}\right) $}

Consider the standerd infinite dimensional fermionic Fock space 
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{H}_{\mathcal{F}}=\limfunc{linspan}\left\{ \left. \left\vert
\Psi_{j}\right\rangle \right\vert ;1\leq j\leq2^{r}\right\} =\wedge\left( 
\mathcal{H}^{1}\right) .
\end{equation}
this space is also an exterior algebra, when equipped with the standerd
antisymmetric tensor product $\wedge$ 
\begin{equation}
\left\vert \Psi_{j}\right\rangle \wedge\left\vert \Psi_{k}\right\rangle
=\left\vert \Psi_{j}\wedge\Psi_{k}\right\rangle \in\mathcal{H}_{\mathcal{F}},
\end{equation}
note that in general 
\begin{equation}
\left\vert \Psi_{j}\right\rangle \wedge\left\vert \Psi_{k}\right\rangle
\neq\alpha\left\vert \Psi_{k}\right\rangle \wedge\left\vert \Psi
_{j}\right\rangle ;\alpha\in\mathbb{C}
\end{equation}
as $\Psi_{j}$ and $\Psi_{k}$ are not in general homogeneous. The Banach
space of bounded operators defined in $\mathcal{H}_{\mathcal{F}}$ 
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{B}\left( \mathcal{H}_{\mathcal{F}}\right) =\limfunc{linspan}\left\{
\left. \left( \left\vert \Psi_{j}\right\rangle \left\langle
\Psi_{k}\right\vert \right) \right\vert ;1\leq j,k\leq2^{r}\right\} .
\end{equation}
is a $w^{\ast}$-algebra, which can be equiped with a product $\square,$ (box
product), defined by 
\begin{equation}
\left\vert \Psi_{j}\right\rangle \left\langle \Psi_{k}\right\vert
\square\left\vert \Psi_{l}\right\rangle \left\langle \Psi_{m}\right\vert
=\left\vert \Psi_{j}\right\rangle \left\langle \Psi_{k}\right\vert
\cdot\left\vert \Psi_{l}\right\rangle \left\langle \Psi_{m}\right\vert
=\left\vert \Psi_{j}\wedge\Psi_{l}\right\rangle \left\langle
\Psi_{k}\wedge\Psi_{m}\right\vert .
\end{equation}
$\left( \text{Grueb defines this as "}\cdot",\text{ but we have shown that }%
\square\text{ can be extended to }\cdot\text{and hence }\square\equiv
\cdot\right) $

$\lceil$%
\begin{align}
\Psi_{j}\wedge\Psi_{l} & =\left( \sum\limits_{0\leq p\leq r}\sum
\limits_{1\leq j_{1}<\cdots<j_{p}\leq r}\Psi_{jj_{1}\cdots
j_{p}}\psi_{j_{1}}\wedge\cdots\wedge\psi_{j_{p}}\right) \wedge\left(
\sum\limits_{0\leq q\leq r}\sum\limits_{1\leq l_{1}<\cdots<l_{q}\leq
r}\Psi_{ll_{1}\cdots l_{q}}\psi_{l_{1}}\wedge\cdots\wedge\psi_{l_{q}}\right)
\\
& =\sum\limits_{\substack{ 0\leq p\leq r \\ 0\leq q\leq r}}\sum \limits
_{\substack{ 1\leq j_{1}<\cdots<j_{p}\leq r \\ 1\leq l_{1}<\cdots <l_{q}\leq
r}}\Psi_{jj_{1}\cdots j_{p}}\Psi_{ll_{1}\cdots
l_{q}}\psi_{j_{1}}\wedge\cdots\wedge\psi_{j_{p}}\wedge\psi_{l_{1}}\wedge%
\cdots\wedge\psi _{l_{q}}
\end{align}
$\rfloor$

The convex set of States on the algebra $\mathcal{B}\left( \mathcal{H}_{%
\mathcal{F}}\right) $ are defined by 
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{S}\left( \mathcal{B}\left( \mathcal{H}_{\mathcal{F}}\right) \right)
=\left\{ \left. f\left( I_{\mathcal{B}\left( \mathcal{H}_{\mathcal{F}%
}\right) }\right) =1\right\vert f:\mathcal{B}\left( \mathcal{H}_{\mathcal{F}%
}\right) \rightarrow\mathbb{C},f\left( \alpha A+\beta B\right) =\alpha
f\left( A\right) +\beta f\left( B\right) \right\} ,f\geq0,
\end{equation}
and normal states 
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{S}\left( \mathcal{B}\left( \mathcal{H}_{\mathcal{F}}\right) \right)
\supseteq\mathcal{S}_{normal}\left( \mathcal{B}\left( \mathcal{H}_{\mathcal{F%
}}\right) \right)
\end{equation}
by%
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{S}_{normal}\left( \mathcal{B}\left( \mathcal{H}_{\mathcal{F}%
}\right) \right) =\left\{ \left. f\left( A\right) =\func{Tr}\left\{
AD_{f}\right\} \right\vert D_{f}\in\mathcal{B}_{1}\left( \mathcal{H}_{%
\mathcal{F}}\right) ,D_{f}\geq0,\func{Tr}\left\{ D_{f}\right\} =1\right\} .
\end{equation}
The norm in $\mathcal{B}\left( \mathcal{H}_{\mathcal{F}}\right) $ is given
by 
\begin{align}
\left\Vert A\right\Vert _{\mathcal{B}\left( \mathcal{H}_{\mathcal{F}}\right)
}^{2} & =\sup\limits_{D_{f}\in\mathcal{S}\left( \mathcal{B}\left( \mathcal{H}%
_{\mathcal{F}}\right) \right) }\left\{ \func{Tr}\left\{
A^{\dagger}AD_{f}\right\} \right\} =\sup\limits_{D_{f}\in\limfunc{ext}%
\left\{ \mathcal{S}\left( \mathcal{B}\left( \mathcal{H}_{\mathcal{F}}\right)
\right) \right\} }\left\{ \func{Tr}\left\{ A^{\dagger }AD_{f}\right\}
\right\} =\sup\limits_{D_{f}=D_{f}^{2}}\left\{ \func{Tr}\left\{
A^{\dagger}AD_{f}\right\} \right\} \\
& =\sup\limits_{D_{f}=D_{f}^{2}}\left\{ \func{Tr}\left\{
U\alpha^{\dagger}\alpha U^{\dagger}D_{f}\right\} \right\} =\sup
\limits_{D_{f}=D_{f}^{2}}\left\{ \func{Tr}\left\{ \alpha^{\dagger }\alpha
D_{f}\right\} \right\} =\sup\limits_{D_{f}=D_{f}^{2}}\left\{ \func{Tr}%
\left\{ \left\vert \alpha\right\vert ^{2}D_{f}\right\} \right\} =\rho\left(
\left\vert \alpha\right\vert ^{2}\right) ,
\end{align}
where 
\begin{equation}
\rho\left( A\right) =\sup\left\{ \left. \left\vert \lambda\right\vert
\right\vert \lambda\in\sigma\left( A\right) \right\} .
\end{equation}
If $A$ is self adjoint $\sigma\left( A\right) \subseteq\mathbb{R}$ giving 
\begin{gather}
\left\Vert A\right\Vert _{\mathcal{B}\left( \mathcal{H}_{\mathcal{F}}\right)
}^{2}=\left\Vert A^{2}\right\Vert _{\mathcal{B}\left( \mathcal{H}_{\mathcal{F%
}}\right) }=\rho\left( \alpha\right) ^{2} \\
\Downarrow \\
\left\Vert A\right\Vert _{\mathcal{B}\left( \mathcal{H}_{\mathcal{F}}\right)
}=\left\vert \rho\left( \alpha\right) \right\vert .
\end{gather}

A normalized trace cannot be defined on all of $\mathcal{B}\left( \mathcal{H}%
_{\mathcal{F}}\right) ,$as the limit of normalized trace finite dimensional
operator spaces leads to a Type $II_{1}$ $w^{\ast}$-algebra that contain no
atomic projectors and 
\begin{equation}
\func{Tr}\left\{ I_{\mathcal{B}\left( \mathcal{H}_{\mathcal{F}}\right)
}\right\} =\infty.
\end{equation}

$\lceil$ As $\mathcal{B}\left( \mathcal{H}_{\mathcal{F}}\right) $ is a $%
w^{\ast}$-algebra one has 
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{B}\left( \mathcal{H}_{\mathcal{F}}\right) =\mathcal{B}\left( 
\mathcal{H}_{\mathcal{F}}\right) ^{\prime\prime}
\end{equation}

\begin{equation}
\mathcal{B}\left( \mathcal{H}_{\mathcal{F}}\right) =\mathcal{B}_{1}\left( 
\mathcal{H}_{\mathcal{F}}\right) ^{\ast}
\end{equation}%
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{B}\left( \mathcal{H}_{\mathcal{F}}\right) ^{\ast}=\mathcal{B}%
_{1}\left( \mathcal{H}_{\mathcal{F}}\right) ^{\ast\ast}\supseteq \mathcal{B}%
_{1}\left( \mathcal{H}_{\mathcal{F}}\right) .
\end{equation}%
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{B}\left( \mathcal{H}_{\mathcal{F}}\right) ^{\ast}=\mathcal{B}%
_{1}\left( \mathcal{H}_{\mathcal{F}}\right) ^{\ast\ast}
\end{equation}
$\rfloor$

\section{GNS\ Construction}

A state, $f\in\mathcal{S}\left( \mathcal{A}\right) ,$ on a $c^{\ast}$%
-algebra is defined by a linear functional that has the properties 
\begin{align}
f & :\mathcal{A\longrightarrow}\mathbb{C} \\
f\left( A\right) & =\overline{f\left( A^{\dagger}\right) } \\
f\left( \alpha A+\beta B\right) & =\alpha f\left( A\right) +\beta f\left(
B\right) \\
f\left( I_{\mathcal{A}}\right) & =1 \\
A & \geq0\Rightarrow f\left( A\right) \geq0.
\end{align}
($A\geq0\Longleftrightarrow A=Q^{\dagger}Q$ for some $Q,$ these $Q^{\prime}s$
are obviously non unique as $QU,$ where $UU^{\dagger}=I_{\mathcal{A}}$ also
give the same $Q^{\dagger}Q$)$.$

$\lceil$%
\begin{equation}
QUQU
\end{equation}
$\rfloor$

States are \emph{normal} if there exists a density operator $D_{f}\in%
\mathcal{A},D_{f}\geq0$ and $\limfunc{tr}\left\{ D_{f}\right\} =1$, such
that 
\begin{equation}
f\left( A\right) =\limfunc{tr}\left\{ AD_{f}\right\} .
\end{equation}
$\lceil$This needs to be examined carefully, for instance in a type $II_{1}$
VnA, the infinite dimensional CAR\ algebra, which is an infinite Clifford
algebra, normalized density operators and amplitudes exist, but they cannot
be atomic.$\rfloor$

States are \emph{faithful} if 
\begin{equation}
\text{for }A\geq0\text{ then }f\left( A\right) =0\,\Longleftrightarrow A=0,
\end{equation}
in particular the $\limfunc{tr}$ functional (normalized trace) is a faithful
state i.e. 
\begin{equation}
A\geq0\text{ and }\limfunc{tr}\left\{ A\right\} =0\Longleftrightarrow A=0.
\end{equation}
$\lceil$Non faithful states are more common e.g.%
\begin{equation}
f_{\left\vert \Psi\right\rangle \left\langle \Psi\right\vert }\left(
c_{1}\left\vert \Phi_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle \Phi_{1}\right\vert
+c_{2}\left\vert \Phi_{2}\right\rangle \left\langle \Phi_{2}\right\vert
\right) =\dim\left\{ \mathcal{A}\right\} \limfunc{tr}\left\{ \left(
c_{1}\left\vert \Phi_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle \Phi_{1}\right\vert
+c_{2}\left\vert \Phi_{2}\right\rangle \left\langle \Phi_{2}\right\vert
\right) ^{\dagger}\left\vert \Psi\right\rangle \left\langle \Psi\right\vert
\right\} =0;c_{1},c_{2}\in\mathbb{R}^{+},c_{1}+c_{2}=1,
\end{equation}
when 
\begin{equation}
\left\langle \left. \Psi\right\vert \Phi_{1}\right\rangle =\left\langle
\left. \Psi\right\vert \Phi_{2}\right\rangle =0.
\end{equation}
$\rfloor$

States are \emph{extreme }$\equiv\emph{pure}$ if they cannot be expressed as
convex sums of other states.

States define Hilbert spaces, $\Phi_{f}\left( \mathcal{A}\right) ,$ by the
GNS construction%
\begin{align}
\Phi_{f} & :\mathcal{A\longrightarrow H}_{f} \\
\ker\left\{ \Phi_{f}\right\} & =\left\{ \left. \xi\right\vert f\left(
\xi^{\dagger}\xi\right) =0\right\} \\
\Phi_{f}\left( A+\xi\right) & =\Phi_{f}\left( A\right) \\
\Phi_{f}\left( \alpha A+\beta B\right) & =\alpha\Phi_{f}\left( A\right)
+\beta\Phi_{f}\left( B\right) \\
\left\Vert \Phi_{f}\left( A\right) \right\Vert _{\mathcal{H}_{f}} & =\left(
f\left( A^{\dagger}A\right) \right) ^{\frac{1}{2}}\overset{\text{normal state%
}}{=}\left( \limfunc{tr}\left\{ A^{\dagger }AD_{f}\right\} \right) ^{\frac{1%
}{2}} \\
\left\langle \left. \Phi_{f}\left( A\right) \right\vert \Phi_{f}\left(
B\right) \right\rangle _{\mathcal{H}_{f}} & =f\left( A^{\dagger}B\right) 
\overset{\text{normal state}}{=}\limfunc{tr}\left\{
A^{\dagger}BD_{f}\right\} .
\end{align}
Hence 
\begin{equation}
\xi\in\ker\left\{ \Phi_{f}\right\} \Longleftrightarrow\left\Vert \Phi
_{f}\left( \xi\right) \right\Vert _{\mathcal{H}_{f}}^{2}=f\left(
\xi^{\dagger}\xi\right) \overset{\text{normal state}}{=}\limfunc{tr}\left\{
\xi^{\dagger}\xi D_{f}\right\} =0\equiv\Phi_{f}\left( \xi\right) =0.
\end{equation}
One can observe 
\begin{equation}
\xi\in\ker\left\{ \Phi_{f}\right\} \Rightarrow\left\langle \left. \Phi
_{f}\left( \xi\right) \right\vert \Phi_{f}\left( I_{\mathcal{F}}\right)
\right\rangle _{\mathcal{H}_{f}}=f\left( \xi^{\dagger}\right) =\limfunc{tr}%
\left\{ \xi^{\dagger}D_{f}\right\} =0
\end{equation}
and 
\begin{equation}
\xi\in\ker\left\{ \Phi_{f}\right\} \Rightarrow\left\langle \left. \Phi
_{f}\left( I_{\mathcal{F}}\right) \right\vert \Phi_{f}\left( \xi\right)
\right\rangle _{\mathcal{H}_{f}}=f\left( \xi\right) =\limfunc{tr}\left\{ \xi
D_{f}\right\} =0,
\end{equation}
\emph{but be especially cognizant that } 
\begin{equation}
\xi\in\ker\left\{ \Phi_{f}\right\} \nRightarrow\xi^{\dagger}\in\ker\left\{
\Phi_{f}\right\} \text{ i.e. }\nRightarrow f\left( \xi\xi^{\dagger}\right)
=0.
\end{equation}
$\lceil$An example of this is 
\begin{equation}
f\left( a^{\dagger}a\right) =0=f\left( I_{\mathcal{F}}-aa^{\dagger}\right)
\Rightarrow f\left( aa^{\dagger}\right) =1.
\end{equation}
$\rfloor$

If a state $f$ is faithful then 
\begin{equation}
\ker\left\{ \Phi_{f}\right\} =0.
\end{equation}

One should note that

\begin{enumerate}
\item 
\begin{equation}
f\left( \xi^{\dagger}\right) =f\left( \xi\right) =0\nRightarrow\xi\in
\ker\left\{ \Phi_{f}\right\} ,\text{ nor }\xi^{\dagger}\in\ker\left\{
\Phi_{f}\right\} ,
\end{equation}

\item 
\begin{equation*}
0=f\left( \xi^{\dagger}\xi\right) =\limfunc{tr}\left\{ \xi^{\dagger }\xi
D_{f}\right\} =\limfunc{tr}\left\{ \xi D_{f}\xi^{\dagger }\right\} =\limfunc{%
tr}\left\{ D_{f}\xi^{\dagger}\xi\right\} \Leftrightarrow\xi\in\ker\left\{
\Phi_{f}\right\} \Leftrightarrow\xi D_{f}\xi^{\dagger}=0, 
\end{equation*}

\item 
\begin{equation}
\limfunc{tr}\left\{ \xi^{\dagger}\xi D_{f}\right\} =\limfunc{tr}\left\{
\xi^{\dagger}\xi Q_{f}Q_{f}^{\dagger}\right\} =\limfunc{tr}\left\{
Q_{f}^{\dagger}\xi^{\dagger}\xi Q_{f}\right\} =\limfunc{tr}\left\{ \left(
\xi Q_{f}\right) ^{\dagger}\xi Q_{f}\right\} ,
\end{equation}
thus 
\begin{equation}
\limfunc{tr}\left\{ \xi^{\dagger}\xi D_{f}\right\} =0\Leftrightarrow \xi
Q_{f}=0,
\end{equation}
as $\limfunc{tr}$ is faithful.
\end{enumerate}

$\lceil$When considering vacuum states $Q_{f}\in\ker\left\{ Q_{f}\right\} .$
One can observe that $\Phi_{f}\left( Q_{f}\right) =0$ Iff $Q_{f}$ and $%
Q_{f}^{\dagger}$ are nilpotent as 
\begin{equation}
\left\langle \left. \Phi_{f}\left( Q_{f}\right) \right\vert \Phi_{f}\left(
Q_{f}\right) \right\rangle =\limfunc{tr}\left\{
Q_{f}^{\dagger}Q_{f}Q_{f}Q_{f}^{\dagger}\right\} =\limfunc{tr}\left\{
Q_{f}Q_{f}Q_{f}^{\dagger}Q_{f}^{\dagger}\right\} =0
\end{equation}
giving%
\begin{equation}
\Phi_{f}\left( Q_{f}\right) =0\Leftrightarrow Q_{f}^{2}\Leftrightarrow
Q_{f}^{\dagger2}=0.
\end{equation}
If $\Phi_{f}\left( Q_{f}^{\dagger}\right) =0$ then the state $%
Q_{f}Q_{f}^{\dagger}$ is also zero as 
\begin{gather}
\left\langle \left. \Phi_{f}\left( Q_{f}^{\dagger}\right) \right\vert
\Phi_{f}\left( Q_{f}^{\dagger}\right) \right\rangle =\limfunc{tr}\left\{
Q_{f}Q_{f}^{\dagger}Q_{f}Q_{f}^{\dagger}\right\} =\limfunc{tr}\left\{
Q_{f}^{\dagger}Q_{f}Q_{f}^{\dagger}Q_{f}\right\} =0 \\
\Updownarrow \\
\Phi_{f}\left( Q_{f}^{\dagger}\right) =0\Leftrightarrow
Q_{f}^{\dagger}Q_{f}=Q_{f}Q_{f}^{\dagger}=0,
\end{gather}
so the situation is trivial if both $Q_{f}$ and $Q_{f}^{\dagger}$ are
nilpotent and belong to $\ker\left\{ \Phi_{f}\right\} .$

$\rfloor$

Representations of $\mathcal{A}$ can then be constructed (GNS construction)
by 
\begin{align}
\Pi_{f} & :\mathcal{A\hookrightarrow B}\left( \mathcal{H}_{f}\right) \\
\Pi_{f}\left( A\right) \Phi_{f}\left( X\right) & =\Phi_{f}\left( AX\right) \\
\left\Vert \Pi_{f}\left( A\right) \right\Vert _{\mathcal{B}\left( \mathcal{H}%
_{f}\right) }^{2} & =\sup\limits_{\Phi_{f}\left( X\right) \in\mathcal{H}%
_{f}}\left\{ \frac{\left\langle \left. \Phi_{f}\left( X\right) \right\vert
\Pi_{f}\left( A\right) ^{\dagger}\Pi_{f}\left( A\right) \Phi_{f}\left(
X\right) \right\rangle _{\mathcal{H}_{f}}}{\left\langle \left.
\Phi_{f}\left( X\right) \right\vert \Phi_{f}\left( X\right) \right\rangle _{%
\mathcal{H}_{f}}}\right\} \\
& =\sup\limits_{\Phi_{f}\left( X\right) \in\mathcal{H}_{f}}\left\{ \frac{%
\limfunc{tr}\left\{ X^{\dagger}A^{\dagger}AXD_{f}\right\} }{\limfunc{tr}%
\left\{ X^{\dagger}XD_{f}\right\} }\right\} \\
& =\sup\limits_{\Phi_{f}\left( X\right) \in\mathcal{H}_{f}}\left\{
\left\vert \func{Tr}\left\{ \Pi_{f}\left( A\right) ^{\dagger}\Pi_{f}\left(
A\right) \frac{\left\vert \Phi_{f}\left( X\right) \right\rangle \left\langle
\Phi_{f}\left( X\right) \right\vert }{\left\langle \left. \Phi_{f}\left(
X\right) \right\vert \Phi_{f}\left( X\right) \right\rangle _{\mathcal{H}_{f}}%
}\right\} \right\vert \right\} \\
& =\sup\limits_{\Phi_{f}\left( X\right) \in\mathcal{H}_{f}}\left\{
\left\vert \frac{\func{Tr}\left\{ \Pi_{f}\left( A\right) ^{\dagger
}\Pi_{f}\left( A\right) \left\vert \Phi_{f}\left( X\right) \right\rangle
\left\langle \Phi_{f}\left( X\right) \right\vert \right\} }{\func{Tr}\left\{
\left\vert \Phi_{f}\left( X\right) \right\rangle \left\langle \Phi_{f}\left(
X\right) \right\vert \right\} }\right\vert \right\} ,
\end{align}
where $\Pi_{f}\left( \mathcal{A}\right) $ \emph{is in general a subalgebra}
of $\mathcal{B}\left( \mathcal{H}_{f}\right) .$ As $\Pi_{f}\left( \mathcal{A}%
\right) $ is a subalgebra not all elements of $\Pi_{f}\left( \mathcal{A}%
\right) ^{\prime\prime}$ are contained in $\mathcal{B}\left( \mathcal{H}%
_{f}\right) $ e.g. if $\Pi_{f}\left( \mathcal{A}\right) ^{\prime\prime}$ is
a type $III$ Von Neumann algebra than it contains no finite dimensional
projctors, thus no atomic projections and \emph{no pure states}. However the
action of $\Pi_{f}\left( \mathcal{A}\right) ^{\prime\prime}$ is defined on
all vectors in $\mathcal{H}_{f}$ $\,$e.g. 
\begin{equation}
\left\langle \left. v\right\vert \Pi_{f}\left( A\right) v\right\rangle _{%
\mathcal{H}_{f}}\in\mathbb{R};\Pi_{f}\left( A\right) =\Pi_{f}\left( A\right)
^{\dagger}
\end{equation}
is always a finite expectation value of the self adjoint operator $\Pi
_{f}\left( A\right) $ even though 
\begin{equation}
\left\vert v\right\rangle \left\langle v\right\vert \notin\Pi_{f}\left( 
\mathcal{A}\right) .
\end{equation}
\emph{In particular} it is possible that the projector onto the generating
state 
\begin{equation}
\left\vert \Phi_{f}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}}\right) \right\rangle \left\langle
\Phi_{f}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}}\right) \right\vert \notin\Pi_{f}\left( 
\mathcal{A}\right) .
\end{equation}
A state, $f,$ is \emph{normal} if $\exists D_{f}\in\Pi_{f}\left( \mathcal{A}%
\right) ^{\prime\prime}$ such that 
\begin{equation}
f\left( A\right) =\func{Tr}\left\{ \Pi_{f}\left( A\right) D_{f}\right\}
;\,D_{f}\in\Pi_{f}\left( \mathcal{A}\right) ^{\prime\prime },\func{Tr}%
\left\{ D_{f}\right\} =1,
\end{equation}
so \emph{it is possible} that \emph{no normal} states exist for a given $f$
and $\Pi_{f}\left( \mathcal{A}\right) ^{\prime\prime}.$

$\lceil$It is always true that 
\begin{equation}
f\left( A\right) =\left\langle \left. \Phi_{f}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}}\right)
\right\vert \Pi_{f}\left( A\right) \Phi_{f}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}}\right)
\right\rangle ,
\end{equation}
however it is possible that 
\begin{equation}
\left\vert \Phi_{f}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}}\right) \right\rangle \left\langle
\Phi_{f}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}}\right) \right\vert \notin\Pi_{f}\left( 
\mathcal{A}\right) ^{\prime\prime}.
\end{equation}
In particular if $\Pi_{f}\left( \mathcal{A}\right) ^{\prime\prime}$ is type $%
II$ or $III$ then no atomic projections exist belonging to $\Pi_{f}\left( 
\mathcal{A}\right) ^{\prime\prime}$, so $\left\vert \Phi_{f}\left( I_{%
\mathcal{A}}\right) \right\rangle \left\langle \Phi_{f}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}%
}\right) \right\vert \notin\Pi_{f}\left( \mathcal{A}\right)
^{\prime\prime}.\rfloor$

The vector $\Phi_{f}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}}\right) $ is a \emph{cyclic}
vector for $\Pi_{f}\left( \mathcal{A}\right) $ i.e. 
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{H}_{f}=\overline{\left\{ \left. \Pi_{f}\left( \mathcal{A}\right)
\Phi_{f}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}}\right) \right\vert A\in\mathcal{A}\right\} }.
\end{equation}
If 
\begin{equation}
\ker\left\{ \Phi_{f}\right\} =\left\{ 0\right\}
\end{equation}
then $f$ is also \emph{seperating, }which also implies\emph{\ }that $f$ is
faithful\emph{. }

A vector $v$ is \emph{cyclic} for an subalgebra $\mathcal{A}_{sub}$ if 
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{H}=\overline{\left\{ \left. Av\right\vert A\in\mathcal{A}%
_{sub}\right\} }.
\end{equation}
A vector $v$ is \emph{seperating} for a subalgebra $\mathcal{A}_{sub}$ if
the only solution to 
\begin{equation}
Av=0;\text{ }\forall A\in\mathcal{A}_{sub}
\end{equation}
is 
\begin{equation}
A=0.
\end{equation}
In terms of a GNS representation this is equivalent to $\Pi_{f}$ being
faithful i.e. 
\begin{equation}
\Pi_{f}\left( A\right) \Phi_{f}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}}\right) =0\forall
A\Longleftrightarrow\Pi_{f}\left( A\right) =0.
\end{equation}

\bigskip One can observe that $\ker\left\{ \Phi_{f}\right\} $ is a left
ideal in $\mathcal{A},$ $\left( \text{\emph{which is not in general self
adjoint}}\right) ,$ as 
\begin{equation}
\Pi_{f}\left( A\right) \Phi_{f}\left( \xi\right) =\Phi_{f}\left( A\xi\right)
=0\text{ if }\xi\in\ker\left\{ \Phi_{f}\right\}
\end{equation}
i.e. 
\begin{equation}
A\xi\in\ker\left\{ \Phi_{f}\right\} \text{ if }\xi\in\ker\left\{ \Phi
_{f}\right\} .
\end{equation}
In particular this shows that $\ker\left\{ \Phi_{f}\right\} $ is closed
under multiplication and as it is trivially closed under addition (as $%
\Phi_{f}$ is linear) \underline{\emph{it is a non self adjoint subalgebra.}}

If the state $f$ is faithful i.e. 
\begin{equation}
\ker\left\{ \Phi_{f}\right\} =\left\{ 0\right\} ,
\end{equation}
then the condition that the vector $\Phi_{f}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}}\right) $
is seperating for $\Pi_{f}\left( \mathcal{A}\right) $ 
\begin{equation}
\Pi_{f}\left( A\right) \Phi_{f}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}}\right) =\Phi
_{f}\left( A\right) =0\,\forall A\in\mathcal{A}
\end{equation}
implies that 
\begin{equation}
A=0.
\end{equation}
This shows that if 
\begin{equation}
\Pi_{f}\left( A-B\right) \Phi_{f}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}}\right) =\Phi
_{f}\left( A-B\right) =0
\end{equation}
and if $f$ is faithful then 
\begin{equation}
A-B=0\equiv A=B
\end{equation}
and thus $\Pi_{f}$ is faithful i.e. 
\begin{equation}
\ker\left\{ \Pi_{f}\right\} =\left\{ 0\right\} .
\end{equation}
\emph{The converse is not true} i.e. $\Pi_{f}$ being faithful$\,\nRightarrow
f$ is faithful. \emph{\ }$f$\emph{\ is a pure state Iff }$\Pi_{f}$\emph{\ is
irreducible}, pure states are in general non faithful but irreducible
representations are usually faithful. \emph{A positive linear functional is
called pure if the GNS representation is irreducible.}

The representation $\Pi_{f}$ is faithful if 
\begin{equation}
\ker\left\{ \Pi_{f}\right\} =\left\{ 0\right\}
\end{equation}
i.e. $\Pi_{f}$ is injective. The kernel of the representation, $\ker\left\{
\Pi_{f}\right\} ,$ is a self adjoint c$^{\ast}$-subalgebra. If $A\in
\ker\left\{ \Pi_{f}\right\} $ one has the following:

\begin{enumerate}
\item 
\begin{gather}
\limfunc{tr}\left\{ B^{\dagger}A^{\dagger}ABQ_{f}Q_{f}^{\dagger }\right\}
=0\forall B\in\mathcal{A} \\
\Updownarrow \\
ABQ_{f}=0\forall B\in\mathcal{A},
\end{gather}
Hence

\item 
\begin{align}
AQ_{f} & =0 \\
A\left( I-B\right) Q_{f} & =0\Leftrightarrow AQ_{f}=ABQ_{f}\,\forall B\in%
\mathcal{A} \\
& \Rightarrow0=ABQ_{f}\,\forall B\in\mathcal{A},
\end{align}
and

\item 
\begin{equation}
AQ_{f}^{\dagger}Q_{f}=A^{\dagger}Q_{f}^{\dagger}Q_{f}=Q_{f}^{%
\dagger}Q_{f}A=Q_{f}^{\dagger}Q_{f}A^{\dagger}=0.
\end{equation}
\end{enumerate}

$\lceil$Any operator, $B$ $\in$ $\mathcal{A}$ can be expanded in normal form
as 
\begin{equation}
B=\beta_{0}I_{\mathcal{A}}+\sum_{1\leq j_{1}<\cdots<j_{n}\leq r}\beta
_{j_{1}\cdots j_{n}}b_{j_{1}}^{\dagger}\cdots
b_{j_{n}}^{\dagger}+\sum_{1\leq k_{1}<\cdots<k_{m}\leq r}\beta_{k_{1}\cdots
k_{m}}b_{k_{1}}\cdots b_{k_{m}}+\sum_{1\leq m,n\leq r}\sum_{\substack{ 1\leq
j_{1}<\cdots<j_{n}\leq r \\ 1\leq k_{1}<\cdots<k_{m}\leq r}}%
\beta_{j_{1}\cdots j_{n}k_{1}\cdots k_{m}}b_{j_{1}}^{\dagger}\cdots
b_{j_{n}}^{\dagger}b_{k_{1}}\cdots b_{k_{m}}
\end{equation}
giving%
\begin{align}
Bb_{l_{1}}^{\dagger}\cdots b_{l_{p}}^{\dagger}Q_{b} & =\left( \beta _{0}I_{%
\mathcal{A}}+\sum_{1\leq j_{1}<\cdots<j_{n}\leq r}\beta_{j_{1}\cdots
j_{n}}b_{j_{1}}^{\dagger}\cdots b_{j_{n}}^{\dagger}+\sum_{1\leq
k_{1}<\cdots<k_{m}\leq r}\beta_{k_{1}\cdots k_{m}}b_{k_{1}}\cdots
b_{k_{m}}\right. \\
& \left. +\sum_{1\leq m,n\leq r}\sum_{\substack{ 1\leq
j_{1}<\cdots<j_{n}\leq r \\ 1\leq k_{1}<\cdots<k_{m}\leq r}}%
\beta_{j_{1}\cdots j_{n}k_{1}\cdots k_{m}}b_{j_{1}}^{\dagger}\cdots
b_{j_{n}}^{\dagger}b_{k_{1}}\cdots b_{k_{m}}\right)
b_{l_{1}}^{\dagger}\cdots b_{l_{p}}^{\dagger}b_{1}\cdots b_{r}
\end{align}
and antinormal form as%
\begin{equation}
B=\beta_{a0}I_{\mathcal{A}}+\sum_{1\leq j_{1}<\cdots<j_{n}\leq r}\beta
_{aj_{1}\cdots j_{n}}b_{j_{1}}^{\dagger}\cdots
b_{j_{n}}^{\dagger}+\sum_{1\leq k_{1}<\cdots<k_{m}\leq r}\beta_{ak_{1}\cdots
k_{m}}b_{k_{1}}\cdots b_{k_{m}}+\sum_{1\leq m,n\leq r}\sum_{\substack{ 1\leq
j_{1}<\cdots<j_{n}\leq r \\ 1\leq k_{1}<\cdots<k_{m}\leq r}}%
\beta_{ak_{1}\cdots k_{m}j_{1}\cdots j_{n}}b_{k_{1}}\cdots
b_{k_{m}}b_{j_{1}}^{\dagger}\cdots b_{j_{n}}^{\dagger}
\end{equation}
giving%
\begin{align}
Bb_{l_{1}}^{\dagger}\cdots b_{l_{p}}^{\dagger}Q_{b} & =\left( \beta _{a0}I_{%
\mathcal{A}}+\sum_{1\leq j_{1}<\cdots<j_{n}\leq r}\beta_{aj_{1}\cdots
j_{n}}b_{j_{1}}^{\dagger}\cdots b_{j_{n}}^{\dagger}+\sum_{1\leq
k_{1}<\cdots<k_{m}\leq r}\widetilde{\beta}_{ak_{1}\cdots
k_{m}}b_{k_{1}}\cdots b_{k_{m}}\right. \\
& \left. +\sum_{1\leq m,n\leq r}\sum_{\substack{ 1\leq
j_{1}<\cdots<j_{n}\leq r \\ 1\leq k_{1}<\cdots<k_{m}\leq r}}%
\beta_{ak_{1}\cdots k_{m}j_{1}\cdots j_{n}}b_{k_{1}}\cdots
b_{k_{m}}b_{j_{1}}^{\dagger}\cdots b_{j_{n}}^{\dagger }\right) \left(
b_{l_{1}}^{\dagger}\cdots b_{l_{p}}^{\dagger}b_{1}\cdots b_{r}\right) ,
\end{align}
where%
\begin{equation}
Q_{b}=b_{1}\cdots b_{r}
\end{equation}
and 
\begin{equation}
b_{l_{1}}^{\dagger}\cdots b_{l_{p}}^{\dagger}b_{1}\cdots
b_{r}=b_{l_{1}}^{\dagger}b_{l_{1}}\cdots b_{l_{p}}^{\dagger}b_{l_{p}}\tprod
\limits_{1\leq q\neq l_{1},\cdots,l_{p}\leq r}b_{q}.
\end{equation}
$\rfloor$

$\lceil$Let $r=1$ then%
\begin{equation}
D_{f_{b}}=Q_{b}Q_{b}^{\dagger}=b_{1}b_{1}^{\dagger}=h_{1}
\end{equation}
If $\limfunc{tr}\left\{ \bullet Q_{b}Q_{b}^{\dagger}\right\} $ were faithful
then 
\begin{equation}
\limfunc{tr}\left\{ A^{\dagger}AQ_{b}Q_{b}^{\dagger}\right\}
=0\Leftrightarrow A=0.
\end{equation}
Let 
\begin{align}
Q_{b} & =\sqrt{2}b \\
Q_{b}Q_{b}^{\dagger} & =2bb^{\dagger} \\
\limfunc{tr}\left\{ 2bb^{\dagger}\right\} & =1=\limfunc{tr}\left\{
2b^{\dagger}b\right\} \\
\limfunc{tr}\left\{ bb^{\dagger}+b^{\dagger}b\right\} & =1=\limfunc{tr}%
\left\{ I_{\mathcal{A}}\right\} \\
\left( bb^{\dagger}\right) \left( b^{\dagger}b\right) & =0.
\end{align}
In this case 
\begin{equation}
\limfunc{tr}\left\{ \left( b^{\dagger}b\right) \left( 2bb^{\dagger }\right)
\right\} =0
\end{equation}
but 
\begin{equation}
b^{\dagger}b\neq0
\end{equation}
so $\limfunc{tr}\left\{ \bullet\left( 2bb^{\dagger}\right) \right\} $ is not
faithful.

One has the following 
\begin{align}
\ker\left\{ \Phi_{f_{b}}\right\} & =\left\{ \left. f_{b}\left(
q^{\dagger}q\right) =0\right\vert q\in\mathcal{A}\right\} \\
\Phi_{f_{b}}\left( q\right) & =0 \\
f_{b}\left( q^{\dagger}q\right) & =\limfunc{tr}\left\{ q^{\dagger
}qD_{f_{b}}\right\} =\limfunc{tr}\left\{
q^{\dagger}qQ_{b}Q_{b}^{\dagger}\right\} =0\Leftrightarrow
Q_{b}^{\dagger}q^{\dagger}=qQ_{b}=0 \\
\ker\left\{ \Phi_{f_{b}}\right\} & =\ker\left\{ R_{Q_{b}}\right\}
=\ker\left\{ L_{Q_{b}^{\dagger}}\right\} ^{\dagger}=\ker\left\{
R_{Q_{b}Q_{b}^{\dagger}}\right\} =\ker\left\{
L_{Q_{b}Q_{b}^{\dagger}}\right\} ^{\dagger},
\end{align}%
\begin{equation}
\ker\left\{ \Phi_{f_{b}}\right\} =\left\{ b_{1},b_{1}^{\dagger}b_{1}\right\}
=\left\{ b_{1},n_{1}\right\} ,
\end{equation}%
\begin{equation}
\ker\left\{ \Phi_{f_{b}}\right\} ^{c}=\left\{ I_{\mathcal{A}%
},b_{1}^{\dagger}\right\} ,
\end{equation}%
\begin{align}
\ker\left\{ R_{Q_{b}}\right\} & =\ker\left\{ R_{b_{1}}\right\} =\left\{
\left. qb_{1}=0\right\vert q\in\mathcal{A}\right\} =\left\{
b_{1},n_{1}\right\} \\
\ker\left\{ L_{Q_{b}^{\dagger}}\right\} & =\ker\left\{ L_{b_{1}^{\dagger
}}\right\} =\left\{ \left. b_{1}^{\dagger}q=0\right\vert q\in \mathcal{A}%
\right\} =\left\{ b_{1},n_{1}\right\} ^{\dagger}=\left\{
b_{1}^{\dagger},n_{1}\right\} \\
\ker\left\{ L_{Q_{b}Q_{b}^{\dagger}}\right\} & =\ker\left\{
L_{b_{1}^{\dagger}b_{1}}\right\} =\left\{ \left. qb_{1}b_{1}^{\dagger
}=0\right\vert q\in\mathcal{A}\right\} =\left\{ b_{1},n_{1}\right\} \\
\ker\left\{ R_{Q_{b}Q_{b}^{\dagger}}\right\} & =\ker\left\{
R_{b_{1}^{\dagger}b_{1}}\right\} =\left\{ \left. b_{1}b_{1}^{\dagger
}q=0\right\vert q\in\mathcal{A}\right\} =\left\{
b_{1}^{\dagger},n_{1}\right\} .
\end{align}
The kernel condition gives 
\begin{align}
\limfunc{tr}\left\{ b_{1}^{\dagger}b_{1}D_{f_{b}}\right\} & =0 \\
\limfunc{tr}\left\{ n_{1}D_{f_{b}}\right\} & =0
\end{align}
and%
\begin{equation}
\limfunc{tr}\left\{ b_{1}^{\dagger}D_{f_{b}}\right\} =\limfunc{tr}\left\{
b_{1}D_{f_{b}}\right\} =0,
\end{equation}
If $\exists A$ such that 
\begin{gather}
A:\ker\left\{ \Phi_{f_{b}}\right\} ^{c}\rightarrow\ker\left\{
\Phi_{f_{b}}\right\} ;A\in\mathcal{A} \\
\text{then }AB\in\ker\left\{ \Phi_{f_{b}}\right\} ,\forall B\in\ker\left\{
\Phi_{f_{b}}\right\} ^{c} \\
AB\in\ker\left\{ \Phi_{f_{b}}\right\} ,\forall B\in\ker\left\{
\Phi_{f_{b}}\right\} \text{ is always true as }\ker\left\{
\Phi_{f_{b}}\right\} \text{ is a left ideal} \\
\Pi_{f_{b}}\left( A\right) \Phi_{f_{b}}\left( B\right) =\Phi_{f_{b}}\left(
AB\right) =0\forall B
\end{gather}
then 
\begin{equation}
\Pi_{f_{b}}\left( A\right) =0.
\end{equation}
If $Aq\in\ker\left\{ \Phi_{f_{b}}\right\} $ $\forall q\in\mathcal{A}$ then $%
\Pi_{f_{b}}\left( A\right) =0$ and 
\begin{gather}
f_{b}\left( q^{\dagger}A^{\dagger}Aq\right) =\limfunc{tr}\left\{ \left(
Aq\right) ^{\dagger}AqD_{f_{b}}\right\} =\limfunc{tr}\left\{ \left(
Aq\right) ^{\dagger}AqQ_{b}Q_{b}^{\dagger}\right\} =\limfunc{tr}\left\{
\left( AqQ_{b}\right) ^{\dagger}\left( AqQ_{b}\right) \right\} =0 \\
\Updownarrow \\
AqQ_{b}=0\forall q\in\mathcal{A}
\end{gather}
i.e 
\begin{equation}
Aq\in\ker\left\{ \Phi_{f_{b}}\right\} =\ker\left\{ L_{Q_{b}}\right\}
=\ker\left\{ L_{Q_{b}Q_{b}^{\dagger}}\right\} =\ker\left\{
R_{Q_{b}^{\dagger}}\right\} =\ker\left\{ R_{Q_{b}Q_{b}^{\dagger}}\right\} ,
\end{equation}
which is confirmed by%
\begin{equation}
\limfunc{tr}\left\{ \left( AqQ_{b}\right) ^{\dagger}\left( AqQ_{b}\right)
\right\} =\limfunc{tr}\left\{ Q_{b}^{\dagger
}q^{\dagger}A^{\dagger}AqQ_{b}\right\} =\limfunc{tr}\left\{
q^{\dagger}A^{\dagger}AqQ_{b}Q_{b}^{\dagger}\right\} =\limfunc{tr}\left\{
Q_{b}Q_{b}^{\dagger}q^{\dagger}A^{\dagger}Aq\right\} .
\end{equation}
The 
\begin{equation}
\limfunc{Range}\left\{ A\right\} =\left\{ \left. AB\right\vert B\in\mathcal{A%
}\right\} .
\end{equation}
So if $\limfunc{Range}\left\{ A\right\} $ is contained in the null space of $%
R_{Q_{b}Q_{b}^{\dagger}}=\ker\left\{ R_{Q_{b}^{\dagger}}\right\}
=\ker\left\{ \Phi_{f_{b}}\right\} $ then $\Pi_{f_{b}}\left( A\right) =0.$ 
\begin{align}
\limfunc{tr}\left\{ A^{\dagger}AQ_{b}Q_{b}^{\dagger}\right\} &
=0\Leftrightarrow AQ_{b}=0 \\
\left\langle \left. \Phi_{f_{b}}\left( A\right) \right\vert
\Phi_{f_{b}}\left( A\right) \right\rangle _{f_{b}} & =\left\langle \left.
\Pi _{f_{b}}\left( A\right) \Phi_{f_{b}}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}}\right)
\right\vert \Pi_{f_{b}}\left( A\right) \Phi_{f_{b}}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}%
}\right) \right\rangle _{f_{b}}=\left\langle \left. \Phi_{f_{b}}\left( I_{%
\mathcal{A}}\right) \right\vert \Pi_{f_{b}}\left( A\right)
^{\dagger}\Pi_{f_{b}}\left( A\right) \Phi_{f_{b}}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}%
}\right) \right\rangle _{f_{b}}=\limfunc{tr}\left\{
A^{\dagger}AQ_{b}Q_{b}^{\dagger}\right\} .
\end{align}
So if $\limfunc{Range}\left\{ A\right\} $ is contained in the null space of $%
R_{Q_{b}Q_{b}^{\dagger}}=\ker\left\{ R_{Q_{b}^{\dagger}}\right\}
=\ker\left\{ \Phi_{f_{b}}\right\} $ then $\Pi_{f_{b}}\left( A\right) =0.$ 
\begin{align}
\limfunc{tr}\left\{ A^{\dagger}AQ_{b}Q_{b}^{\dagger}\right\} &
=0\Leftrightarrow AQ_{b}=0 \\
\left\langle \left. \Phi_{f_{b}}\left( A\right) \right\vert
\Phi_{f_{b}}\left( A\right) \right\rangle _{f_{b}} & =\left\langle \left.
\Pi _{f_{b}}\left( A\right) \Phi_{f_{b}}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}}\right)
\right\vert \Pi_{f_{b}}\left( A\right) \Phi_{f_{b}}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}%
}\right) \right\rangle _{f_{b}}=\left\langle \left. \Phi_{f_{b}}\left( I_{%
\mathcal{A}}\right) \right\vert \Pi_{f_{b}}\left( A\right)
^{\dagger}\Pi_{f_{b}}\left( A\right) \Phi_{f_{b}}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}%
}\right) \right\rangle _{f_{b}}=\limfunc{tr}\left\{
A^{\dagger}AQ_{b}Q_{b}^{\dagger}\right\} .
\end{align}
In the $r=1$ case%
\begin{equation}
D_{f_{b}}=Q_{b}Q_{b}^{\dagger}=b_{1}b_{1}^{\dagger}=h_{1},
\end{equation}%
\begin{equation}
\ker\left\{ \Phi_{f_{b}}\right\} =\left\{ b_{1},b_{1}^{\dagger}b_{1}\right\}
=\left\{ b_{1},n_{1}\right\}
\end{equation}
and 
\begin{equation}
\ker\left\{ \Phi_{f_{b}}\right\} ^{c}=\left\{ I_{\mathcal{A}%
},b_{1}^{\dagger}\right\} .
\end{equation}%
\begin{gather}
\text{If }L_{A}:\left\{ I_{\mathcal{A}},b_{1}^{\dagger}\right\}
\rightarrow\left\{ b_{1},b_{1}^{\dagger}b_{1}\right\} \\
\text{then }AB\in\ker\left\{ \Phi_{f_{b}}\right\} ,\forall B\in\ker\left\{
\Phi_{f_{b}}\right\} ^{c}
\end{gather}
then 
\begin{equation}
\Pi_{f_{b}}\left( A\right) =0.
\end{equation}
Let 
\begin{equation}
A=\beta_{0}I_{\mathcal{A}}+\beta_{1}b_{1}^{\dagger}+\beta_{11}b_{1}^{\dagger
}b_{1}
\end{equation}
and then 
\begin{align}
\left( \beta_{0}I_{\mathcal{A}}+\beta_{1}b_{1}^{\dagger}+\beta_{11}b_{1}^{%
\dagger}b_{1}\right) I_{\mathcal{A}} & =\beta_{0}I_{\mathcal{A}%
}+\beta_{1}b_{1}^{\dagger}+\beta_{11}b_{1}^{\dagger}b_{1} \\
\left( \beta_{0}I_{\mathcal{A}}+\beta_{1}b_{1}^{\dagger}+\beta_{11}b_{1}^{%
\dagger}b_{1}\right) b_{1}^{\dagger} & =\beta_{0}b_{1}^{\dagger
}+\beta_{11}b_{1}^{\dagger}=\left( \beta_{0}+\beta_{11}\right)
b_{1}^{\dagger}
\end{align}
and%
\begin{equation}
L_{A}:\left\{ I_{\mathcal{A}},b_{1}^{\dagger}\right\} \nrightarrow\left\{
b_{1},b_{1}^{\dagger}b_{1}\right\} ,
\end{equation}
so $\Pi_{f_{b}}$ is injective $\equiv$ faithful. This is confirmed by 
\begin{align}
\mathcal{H}_{f_{b}} & =\left\{ \Phi_{f_{b}}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}}\right)
,\Phi_{f_{b}}\left( b_{1}^{\dagger}\right) \right\} \\
\Pi_{f_{b}}\left( b_{1}\right) \Phi_{f_{b}}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}}\right) &
=\Phi_{f_{b}}\left( b_{1}\right) =0 \\
\Pi_{f_{b}}\left( b_{1}\right) \Phi_{f_{b}}\left( b_{1}^{\dagger}\right) &
=\Phi_{f_{b}}\left( b_{1}b_{1}^{\dagger}\right) \neq0 \\
\Pi_{f_{b}}\left( b_{1}^{\dagger}b_{1}\right) \Phi_{f_{b}}\left( I_{\mathcal{%
A}}\right) & =\Phi_{f_{b}}\left( b_{1}^{\dagger}b_{1}\right) =0 \\
\Pi_{f_{b}}\left( b_{1}^{\dagger}b_{1}\right) \Phi_{f_{b}}\left(
b_{1}^{\dagger}\right) & =\Phi_{f_{b}}\left(
b_{1}^{\dagger}b_{1}b_{1}^{\dagger}\right) =\Phi_{f_{b}}\left(
b_{1}^{\dagger}\right) \neq0 \\
\Pi_{f_{b}}\left( b_{1}^{\dagger}\right) \Phi_{f_{b}}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}%
}\right) & =\Phi_{f_{b}}\left( b_{1}^{\dagger}\right) \neq0 \\
\Pi_{f_{b}}\left( b_{1}^{\dagger}\right) \Phi_{f_{b}}\left( b_{1}^{\dagger
}\right) & =\Phi_{f_{b}}\left( b_{1}^{\dagger}b_{1}^{\dagger}\right) =0 \\
\Pi_{f_{b}}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}}\right) \Phi_{f_{b}}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}%
}\right) & =\Phi_{f_{b}}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}}\right) \neq0 \\
\Pi_{f_{b}}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}}\right) \Phi_{f_{b}}\left( b_{1}^{\dagger
}\right) & =\Phi_{f_{b}}\left( b_{1}^{\dagger}\right) \neq0.
\end{align}
$\lceil$%
\begin{align}
\limfunc{tr}\left\{
b_{1}b_{1}^{\dagger}b_{1}b_{1}^{\dagger}b_{1}b_{1}^{\dagger}\right\} & =%
\limfunc{tr}\left\{ b_{1}b_{1}^{\dagger }\right\} \\
\limfunc{tr}\left\{
b_{1}b_{1}^{\dagger}b_{1}b_{1}^{\dagger}b_{1}b_{1}^{\dagger}b_{1}b_{1}^{%
\dagger}\right\} & =\limfunc{tr}\left\{ b_{1}b_{1}^{\dagger}\right\}
\end{align}
$\rfloor$

$\rfloor$

\bigskip$\lceil$\ Let $r=2$ then the state is given by 
\begin{equation}
D_{f_{b}}=Q_{b}Q_{b}^{\dagger}=b_{1}b_{2}b_{2}^{\dagger}b_{1}^{%
\dagger}=h_{1}h_{2},
\end{equation}
the kernal of $\Phi_{f_{b}}$ by 
\begin{align}
\ker\left\{ \Phi_{f_{b}}\right\} & =\left\{ \left. f_{b}\left(
q^{\dagger}q\right) =0\right\vert q\in\mathcal{A}\right\} \\
\Phi_{f_{b}}\left( q\right) & =0 \\
f_{b}\left( q^{\dagger}q\right) & =\limfunc{tr}\left\{ q^{\dagger
}qD_{f_{b}}\right\} =\limfunc{tr}\left\{
q^{\dagger}qQ_{b}Q_{b}^{\dagger}\right\} =0\Leftrightarrow
Q_{b}^{\dagger}q^{\dagger}=qQ_{b}=0 \\
\ker\left\{ \Phi_{f_{b}}\right\} & =\ker\left\{ R_{Q_{b}}\right\}
=\ker\left\{ L_{Q_{b}^{\dagger}}\right\} ^{\dagger}=\ker\left\{
R_{Q_{b}Q_{b}^{\dagger}}\right\} =\ker\left\{
L_{Q_{b}Q_{b}^{\dagger}}\right\} ^{\dagger},
\end{align}
and 
\begin{align}
\ker\left\{ \Phi_{f_{b}}\right\} & =\left\{
b_{1},b_{2},b_{1}b_{2},b_{1}^{\dagger}b_{1},b_{2}^{\dagger}b_{2},b_{2}^{%
\dagger}b_{1},b_{1}^{\dagger}b_{2},b_{2}^{\dagger}b_{1}^{%
\dagger}b_{2},b_{2}^{\dagger}b_{1}^{\dagger}b_{1},b_{1}^{%
\dagger}b_{1}b_{2},b_{2}^{\dagger}b_{1}b_{2},b_{1}^{\dagger}b_{2}^{%
\dagger}b_{2}b_{1}\right\} \\
& =\left\{
b_{1},b_{2},b_{1}b_{2},n_{1},n_{2},b_{2}^{\dagger}b_{1},b_{1}^{%
\dagger}b_{2},n_{1}b_{2},n_{2}b_{1},n_{1}b_{2}^{\dagger},n_{2}b_{1}^{%
\dagger},n_{1}n_{2}\right\}
\end{align}
and its compliment by 
\begin{equation}
\ker\left\{ \Phi_{f_{b}}\right\} ^{c}=\left\{ I_{\mathcal{A}%
},b_{1}^{\dagger},b_{2}^{\dagger},b_{1}^{\dagger}b_{2}^{\dagger}\right\} .
\end{equation}
$A$ acts by left multiplication i.e. $L_{A}$ 
\begin{gather}
L_{A}:\left\{ I_{\mathcal{A}},b_{1}^{\dagger},b_{2}^{\dagger},b_{1}^{\dagger
}b_{2}^{\dagger}\right\} \rightarrow\left\{
b_{1},b_{2},b_{1}b_{2},b_{1}^{\dagger}b_{1},b_{2}^{\dagger}b_{2},b_{2}^{%
\dagger}b_{1},b_{1}^{\dagger}b_{2},b_{2}^{\dagger}b_{1}^{%
\dagger}b_{2},b_{2}^{\dagger}b_{1}^{\dagger}b_{1},b_{1}^{%
\dagger}b_{1}b_{2},b_{2}^{\dagger}b_{1}b_{2},b_{1}^{\dagger}b_{2}^{%
\dagger}b_{2}b_{1}\right\} \\
\text{then }AB\in\ker\left\{ \Phi_{f_{b}}\right\} ,\forall B\in\ker\left\{
\Phi_{f_{b}}\right\} ^{c}
\end{gather}
then 
\begin{equation}
\Pi_{f_{b}}\left( A\right) =0.
\end{equation}
Various kernals are given by 
\begin{align}
\ker\left\{ R_{Q_{b}}\right\} & =\ker\left\{ R_{b_{1}b_{2}}\right\} \\
& =\left\{ \left. qb_{1}b_{2}=0\right\vert q\in\mathcal{A}\right\} =\left\{
b_{1},b_{2},b_{1}b_{2},n_{1},n_{2},b_{2}^{\dagger}b_{1},b_{1}^{%
\dagger}b_{2},n_{1}b_{2},n_{2}b_{1},n_{1}b_{2}^{\dagger},n_{2}b_{1}^{%
\dagger},n_{1}n_{2}\right\} \\
\ker\left\{ L_{Q_{b}^{\dagger}}\right\} & =\ker\left\{ L_{b_{1}^{\dagger
}b_{2}^{\dagger}}\right\} \\
& =\left\{ \left. b_{1}^{\dagger}b_{2}^{\dagger}q=0\right\vert q\in\mathcal{A%
}\right\} =\left\{
b_{1},b_{2},b_{1}b_{2},n_{1},n_{2},b_{2}^{\dagger}b_{1},b_{1}^{%
\dagger}b_{2},n_{1}b_{2},n_{2}b_{1},n_{1}b_{2}^{\dagger},n_{2}b_{1}^{%
\dagger},n_{1}n_{2}\right\} ^{\dagger} \\
\ker\left\{ L_{Q_{b}Q_{b}^{\dagger}}\right\} & =\ker\left\{
L_{b_{1}b_{2}b_{2}^{\dagger}b_{1}^{\dagger}}\right\} \\
& =\left\{ \left. qb_{1}b_{2}b_{2}^{\dagger}b_{1}^{\dagger}=0\right\vert q\in%
\mathcal{A}\right\} =\left\{
b_{1},b_{2},b_{1}b_{2},n_{1},n_{2},b_{2}^{\dagger}b_{1},b_{1}^{%
\dagger}b_{2},n_{1}b_{2},n_{2}b_{1},n_{1}b_{2}^{\dagger},n_{2}b_{1}^{%
\dagger},n_{1}n_{2}\right\} \\
\ker\left\{ R_{Q_{b}Q_{b}^{\dagger}}\right\} & =\ker\left\{
R_{b_{1}b_{2}b_{2}^{\dagger}b_{1}^{\dagger}}\right\} \\
& =\left\{ \left. b_{1}b_{2}b_{2}^{\dagger}b_{1}^{\dagger}q=0\right\vert q\in%
\mathcal{A}\right\} =\left\{
b_{1},b_{2},b_{1}b_{2},n_{1},n_{2},b_{2}^{\dagger}b_{1},b_{1}^{%
\dagger}b_{2},n_{1}b_{2},n_{2}b_{1},n_{1}b_{2}^{\dagger},n_{2}b_{1}^{%
\dagger},n_{1}n_{2}\right\} ^{\dagger}.
\end{align}

The kernel condition gives 
\begin{align}
\limfunc{tr}\left\{ b_{1}^{\dagger}b_{1}D_{f_{b}}\right\} & =\limfunc{tr}%
\left\{ b_{2}^{\dagger}b_{2}D_{f_{b}}\right\} =\limfunc{tr}\left\{
b_{1}^{\dagger}b_{2}^{\dagger}b_{1}b_{2}D_{f_{b}}\right\} =0 \\
\limfunc{tr}\left\{ n_{1}D_{f_{b}}\right\} & =\limfunc{tr}\left\{
n_{2}D_{f_{b}}\right\} =\limfunc{tr}\left\{ n_{1}n_{2}D_{f_{b}}\right\} =%
\limfunc{tr}\left\{ n_{1}b_{2}^{\dagger }D_{f_{b}}\right\} =\limfunc{tr}%
\left\{ n_{2}b_{1}^{\dagger}D_{f_{b}}\right\} =0
\end{align}
and%
\begin{equation}
\limfunc{tr}\left\{ b_{1}^{\dagger}D_{f_{b}}\right\} =\limfunc{tr}\left\{
b_{1}D_{f_{b}}\right\} =0,
\end{equation}
while Guage invariance gives 
\begin{equation}
\limfunc{tr}\left\{ b_{2}b_{1}D_{f_{b}}\right\} =\limfunc{tr}\left\{
b_{1}^{\dagger}b_{2}^{\dagger}D_{f_{b}}\right\} =\limfunc{tr}\left\{
b_{2}^{\dagger}b_{1}b_{2}D_{f_{b}}\right\} =\limfunc{tr}\left\{
b_{1}^{\dagger}b_{1}b_{2}D_{f_{b}}\right\} =0.
\end{equation}

$\lceil$If $Aq\in\ker\left\{ \Phi_{f_{b}}\right\} $ $\forall q\in \mathcal{A}
$ then $\Pi_{f_{b}}\left( A\right) =0$ and 
\begin{gather}
f_{b}\left( q^{\dagger}A^{\dagger}Aq\right) =\limfunc{tr}\left\{ \left(
Aq\right) ^{\dagger}AqD_{f_{b}}\right\} =\limfunc{tr}\left\{ \left(
Aq\right) ^{\dagger}AqQ_{b}Q_{b}^{\dagger}\right\} =\limfunc{tr}\left\{
\left( AqQ_{b}\right) ^{\dagger}\left( AqQ_{b}\right) \right\} =0 \\
\Updownarrow \\
AqQ_{b}=0\forall q\in\mathcal{A}
\end{gather}
i.e 
\begin{equation}
Aq\in\ker\left\{ \Phi_{f_{b}}\right\} =\ker\left\{ L_{Q_{b}}\right\}
=\ker\left\{ L_{Q_{b}Q_{b}^{\dagger}}\right\} =\ker\left\{
R_{Q_{b}^{\dagger}}\right\} =\ker\left\{ R_{Q_{b}Q_{b}^{\dagger}}\right\} ,
\end{equation}
which is confirmed by%
\begin{equation}
\limfunc{tr}\left\{ \left( AqQ_{b}\right) ^{\dagger}\left( AqQ_{b}\right)
\right\} =\limfunc{tr}\left\{ Q_{b}^{\dagger
}q^{\dagger}A^{\dagger}AqQ_{b}\right\} =\limfunc{tr}\left\{
q^{\dagger}A^{\dagger}AqQ_{b}Q_{b}^{\dagger}\right\} =\limfunc{tr}\left\{
Q_{b}Q_{b}^{\dagger}q^{\dagger}A^{\dagger}Aq\right\} .
\end{equation}%
\begin{equation}
\limfunc{Range}\left\{ A\right\} =\left\{ \left. AB\right\vert B\in\mathcal{A%
}\right\} .
\end{equation}
$\rfloor$

If $\exists A$ such that 
\begin{gather}
A:\ker\left\{ \Phi_{f_{b}}\right\} ^{c}\rightarrow\ker\left\{
\Phi_{f_{b}}\right\} ;A\in\mathcal{A}\text{ ,\qquad i.e.}\,\text{\thinspace}%
AB\in \ker\left\{ \Phi_{f_{b}}\right\} ,\forall B\in\ker\left\{
\Phi_{f_{b}}\right\} ^{c} \\
(AB\in\ker\left\{ \Phi_{f_{b}}\right\} ,\forall B\in\ker\left\{ \Phi
_{f_{b}}\right\} \text{ is always true as }\ker\left\{ \Phi_{f_{b}}\right\} 
\text{ is a left ideal)} \\
\Pi_{f_{b}}\left( A\right) \Phi_{f_{b}}\left( B\right) =\Phi_{f_{b}}\left(
AB\right) =0\forall B
\end{gather}
then 
\begin{equation}
\Pi_{f_{b}}\left( A\right) =0.
\end{equation}

Thus we only need to consider operators belonging to $\ker\left\{ \Phi
_{f_{b}}\right\} ^{c}$ as $\ker\left\{ \Phi_{f_{b}}\right\} $ is a left
ideal and ask if 
\begin{equation}
A\left( \beta_{0}I_{\mathcal{A}}+\beta_{1}b_{1}^{\dagger}+\beta_{2}b_{2}^{%
\dagger}+\beta_{12}b_{1}^{\dagger}b_{2}^{\dagger}\right) \in \ker\left\{
\Phi_{f_{b}}\right\}
\end{equation}

if $A\neq0.\rfloor$

$\Pi_{f}$ is faithful if 
\begin{equation}
\Pi_{f_{b}}\left( A\right) \Phi_{f_{b}}\left( b_{j_{1}}^{\dagger}\cdots
b_{j_{n}}^{\dagger}\right) =0\Rightarrow A=0;1\leq j_{1}<\cdots<j_{n}\leq
r;0\leq n\leq r,
\end{equation}
i.e. 
\begin{equation}
\Phi_{f_{b}}\left( Ab_{j_{1}}^{\dagger}\cdots b_{j_{n}}^{\dagger}\right)
=0\Rightarrow A=0;1\leq j_{1}<\cdots<j_{n}\leq r;0\leq n\leq r.
\end{equation}

\subsubsection{Concrete Example of $\mathcal{B}\left( \mathcal{H}_{\mathcal{F%
}}\right) $}

Consider $\mathcal{B}\left( \mathcal{H}_{\mathcal{F}}\right) $ (a type $%
I_{\infty}$ VnA) and a pure normal state $\left\vert \Psi_{1}\right\rangle
\left\langle \Psi_{1}\right\vert $ wrt $\func{Tr},$ which corresponds to the
pure normal state $\left( \dim\left\{ \mathcal{H}_{\mathcal{F}}\right\}
\right) \left\vert \Psi_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle \Psi _{1}\right\vert $
wrt $\limfunc{tr}$ 
\begin{align}
f\left( A\right) & =\left\langle A\right\rangle _{\left\vert \Psi
_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle \Psi_{1}\right\vert }=\func{Tr}\left\{
A\left\vert \Psi_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle \Psi_{1}\right\vert \right\}
\\
f\left( I_{\mathcal{H}_{\mathcal{F}}}\right) & =\left\langle I_{\mathcal{H}_{%
\mathcal{F}}}\right\rangle _{\left\vert \Psi_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle
\Psi_{1}\right\vert }=\func{Tr}\left\{ I_{\mathcal{H}_{\mathcal{F}%
}}\left\vert \Psi_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle \Psi _{1}\right\vert
\right\} =1 \\
\func{Tr}\left\{ I_{\mathcal{H}_{\mathcal{F}}}\right\} & =\dim\left\{ 
\mathcal{H}_{\mathcal{F}}\right\} \\
f\left( A\right) & =\left\langle A\right\rangle _{\left( \dim\left\{ 
\mathcal{H}_{\mathcal{F}}\right\} \left\vert \Psi_{1}\right\rangle
\left\langle \Psi_{1}\right\vert \right) }=\limfunc{tr}\left\{ A\left(
\dim\left\{ \mathcal{H}_{\mathcal{F}}\right\} \left\vert \Psi
_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle \Psi_{1}\right\vert \right) \right\} =\left(
\dim\left\{ \mathcal{H}_{\mathcal{F}}\right\} \right) ^{-1}\func{Tr}\left\{
A\dim\left\{ \mathcal{H}_{\mathcal{F}}\right\} \left\vert
\Psi_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle \Psi_{1}\right\vert \right\} =\func{Tr}%
\left\{ A\left\vert \Psi_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle \Psi_{1}\right\vert
\right\} \\
f\left( I_{\mathcal{H}_{\mathcal{F}}}\right) & =\left\langle I_{\mathcal{H}_{%
\mathcal{F}}}\right\rangle _{\left( \dim\left\{ \mathcal{H}_{\mathcal{F}%
}\right\} \left\vert \Psi_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle \Psi_{1}\right\vert
\right) }=\limfunc{tr}\left\{ I_{\mathcal{H}_{\mathcal{F}}}\left(
\dim\left\{ \mathcal{H}_{\mathcal{F}}\right\} \left\vert
\Psi_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle \Psi_{1}\right\vert \right) \right\}
=\left( \dim\left\{ \mathcal{H}_{\mathcal{F}}\right\} \right) ^{-1}\func{Tr}%
\left\{ I_{\mathcal{H}_{\mathcal{F}}}\dim\left\{ \mathcal{H}_{\mathcal{F}%
}\right\} \left\vert \Psi_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle \Psi_{1}\right\vert
\right\} \\
& =\func{Tr}\left\{ I_{\mathcal{H}_{\mathcal{F}}}\left\vert \Psi
_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle \Psi_{1}\right\vert \right\} =1 \\
\limfunc{tr}\left\{ I_{\mathcal{H}_{\mathcal{F}}}\right\} & =\left(
\dim\left\{ \mathcal{H}_{\mathcal{F}}\right\} \right) ^{-1}\func{Tr}\left\{
I_{\mathcal{H}_{\mathcal{F}}}\right\} =1.
\end{align}

The $\ker\left\{ \Phi_{\left\vert \Psi_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle \Psi
_{1}\right\vert }\right\} $ 
\begin{equation}
\left\{ A\in\mathcal{A}\left\vert \func{Tr}\left\{ A^{\dagger }A\left\vert
\Psi_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle \Psi_{1}\right\vert \right\} =0\right.
\right\}
\end{equation}
is given by linear combinations of the operators $\left\{ \left. \left\vert
\Psi_{j}\right\rangle \left\langle \Psi_{k}\right\vert \right\vert 2\leq
j\leq2^{r},1\leq k\leq2^{r}\right\} ,$ as 
\begin{align}
\func{Tr}\left\{ \left\vert \Psi_{j}\right\rangle \left\langle
\Psi_{j}\right\vert \left\vert \Psi_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle \Psi
_{1}\right\vert \right\} & =0=\func{Tr}\left\{ \left\vert \Psi
_{j}\right\rangle \left\langle \Psi_{k}\right\vert \left\vert \Psi
_{k}\right\rangle \left\langle \Psi_{j}\right\vert \left\vert \Psi
_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle \Psi_{1}\right\vert \right\} \\
& =\func{Tr}\left\{ \left( \left\vert \Psi_{k}\right\rangle \left\langle
\Psi_{j}\right\vert \right) ^{\dagger}\left( \left\vert
\Psi_{k}\right\rangle \left\langle \Psi_{j}\right\vert \right) \left\vert
\Psi_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle \Psi_{1}\right\vert \right\} ;2\leq
j\leq2^{r},1\leq k\leq2^{r}
\end{align}
so that%
\begin{equation}
\ker\left\{ \Phi_{\left\vert \Psi_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle \Psi
_{1}\right\vert }\right\} =\limfunc{linspan}\left\{ \left. \left\vert
\Psi_{j}\right\rangle \left\langle \Psi_{k}\right\vert \right\vert ;2\leq
j,k\leq2^{r}\right\} ,\left\{ \left. \left\vert \Psi_{1}\right\rangle
\left\langle \Psi_{j}\right\vert \right\vert ;2\leq j\leq2^{r}\right\}
\end{equation}
giving 
\begin{equation}
\dim\left\{ \ker\left\{ \Phi_{\left\vert \Psi_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle
\Psi_{1}\right\vert }\right\} \right\} =2^{2r}-2^{r}.
\end{equation}
The isotropy group of $\left\vert \Psi_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle \Psi
_{1}\right\vert $ is $U\left( 2^{r-1}\right) $ showing that the set of all
atomic projectors is the coherent state manifold 
\begin{equation}
\frac{U\left( 2^{r}\right) }{U\left( 2^{r-1}\right) },
\end{equation}
as 
\begin{equation}
U\left( 2^{r}\right) =\frac{U\left( 2^{r}\right) }{U\left( 2^{r-1}\right) }%
U\left( 2^{r-1}\right) .
\end{equation}

The compliment of $\ker\left\{ \Phi_{\left\vert \Psi_{1}\right\rangle
\left\langle \Psi_{1}\right\vert }\right\} $ in $\mathcal{B}\left( \mathcal{H%
}_{\mathcal{F}}\right) $ is 
\begin{equation}
\ker\left\{ \Phi_{\left\vert \Psi_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle \Psi
_{1}\right\vert }\right\} ^{c}=\limfunc{linspan}\left\{ \left\vert
\Psi_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle \Psi_{1}\right\vert ,\left\{ \left\vert
\Psi_{j}\right\rangle \left\langle \Psi_{1}\right\vert ;2\leq j\leq
2^{r}\right\} \right\} \cong\mathcal{H}_{\mathcal{F}}
\end{equation}

One should note that $\left\vert \Psi_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle \Psi
_{1}\right\vert $ can be factored into the following positive products of
nilpotents 
\begin{equation}
\left\vert \Psi_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle \Psi_{1}\right\vert
=\left\vert \Psi_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle \Psi_{j}\right\vert
\left\vert \Psi _{j}\right\rangle \left\langle \Psi_{1}\right\vert =\left(
\left\vert \Psi_{j}\right\rangle \left\langle \Psi_{1}\right\vert \right)
^{\dagger }\left( \left\vert \Psi_{j}\right\rangle \left\langle
\Psi_{1}\right\vert \right) ;1\leq j\leq2^{r},
\end{equation}
where 
\begin{equation}
\left( \left\vert \Psi_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle \Psi_{j}\right\vert
\right) ^{2}=0;2\leq j\leq2^{r}.
\end{equation}
The reverse products 
\begin{equation}
\left( \left\vert \Psi_{j}\right\rangle \left\langle \Psi_{1}\right\vert
\right) \left( \left\vert \Psi_{j}\right\rangle \left\langle \Psi
_{1}\right\vert \right) ^{\dagger}=\left\vert \Psi_{j}\right\rangle
\left\langle \Psi_{1}\right\vert \left\vert \Psi_{1}\right\rangle
\left\langle \Psi_{j}\right\vert =\left\vert \Psi_{j}\right\rangle
\left\langle \Psi _{j}\right\vert
\end{equation}
so $\left\{ \left. \left\vert \Psi_{j}\right\rangle \left\langle \Psi
_{1}\right\vert \right\vert 2\leq j\leq2^{r}\right\} $ are partial
isometries, with initial subspace $\left\{ \left\vert \Psi_{1}\right\rangle
\right\} $ and final subspaces $\left\{ \left. \left\vert \Psi
_{j}\right\rangle \right\vert 2\leq j\leq2^{r}\right\} .$

In general $\left\vert \Psi_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle
\Psi_{1}\right\vert $ can be factored into the following positive products
as 
\begin{equation}
\left( \left\vert \Psi_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle \Psi\right\vert \right)
\left( \left\vert \Psi_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle \Psi\right\vert \right)
^{\dagger}=\left\vert \Psi_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle \Psi\right\vert
\left\vert \Psi\right\rangle \left\langle \Psi_{1}\right\vert ,
\end{equation}
which are not in general nilpotent as 
\begin{equation}
\left\vert \Psi_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle \Psi\right\vert \left\vert
\Psi_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle \Psi\right\vert \neq0
\end{equation}
unless $\Psi_{1}\bot\Psi.$ The reverse products in these cases are 
\begin{equation}
\left\vert \Psi\right\rangle \left\langle \Psi_{1}\right\vert \left\vert
\Psi_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle \Psi\right\vert =\left\vert \Psi
\right\rangle \left\langle \Psi\right\vert ,
\end{equation}
so $\left\{ \left. \left\vert \Psi\right\rangle \left\langle \Psi
_{1}\right\vert \right\vert \Psi\neq\Psi_{1}\right\} $ are still partial
isometries.

One can prove that the representation $\Pi_{\left\vert \Psi_{1}\right\rangle
\left\langle \Psi_{1}\right\vert }$ is faithful i.e.

If 
\begin{equation}
\Pi_{\left\vert \Psi_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle \Psi_{1}\right\vert
}\left( A\right) =0\Leftrightarrow A=0
\end{equation}
by observing 
\begin{equation}
\Pi_{\left\vert \Psi_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle \Psi_{1}\right\vert
}\left( A\right) \Phi_{\left\vert \Psi_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle
\Psi_{1}\right\vert }\left( \left\vert \Psi_{j}\right\rangle \left\langle
\Psi_{1}\right\vert \right) =0;1\leq j\leq2^{r},
\end{equation}
which gives 
\begin{equation}
\Pi_{\left\vert \Psi_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle \Psi_{1}\right\vert
}\left( \sum\limits_{1\leq p,q\leq2^{r}}A_{pq}\left\vert
\Psi_{p}\right\rangle \left\langle \Psi_{q}\right\vert \right)
\Phi_{\left\vert \Psi_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle \Psi_{1}\right\vert
}\left( \left\vert \Psi_{j}\right\rangle \left\langle \Psi_{1}\right\vert
\right) =0;1\leq j\leq2^{r},
\end{equation}
then%
\begin{equation}
\Phi_{\left\vert \Psi_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle \Psi_{1}\right\vert
}\left( \sum\limits_{1\leq p,q\leq2^{r}}A_{pq}\left\vert
\Psi_{p}\right\rangle \left\langle \Psi_{q}\right\vert \left\vert
\Psi_{j}\right\rangle \left\langle \Psi_{1}\right\vert \right) =0;1\leq
j\leq2^{r},
\end{equation}
and finally%
\begin{equation}
\Phi_{\left\vert \Psi_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle \Psi_{1}\right\vert
}\left( \sum\limits_{1\leq p\leq2^{r}}A_{pj}\left\vert \Psi_{p}\right\rangle
\left\langle \Psi_{1}\right\vert \right) =0;1\leq j\leq2^{r}.
\end{equation}
$\lceil$%
\begin{align}
\Phi_{\left\vert \Psi_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle \Psi_{1}\right\vert
}\left( \sum\limits_{1\leq p\leq3}A_{p1}\left\vert \Psi_{p}\right\rangle
\left\langle \Psi_{1}\right\vert \right) & =\sum\limits_{1\leq p\leq
3}A_{p1}\Phi_{\left\vert \Psi_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle \Psi
_{1}\right\vert }\left( \left\vert \Psi_{p}\right\rangle \left\langle
\Psi_{1}\right\vert \right) =0\Leftrightarrow A_{p1}=0,1\leq p\leq3 \\
\Phi_{\left\vert \Psi_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle \Psi_{1}\right\vert
}\left( \sum\limits_{1\leq p\leq3}A_{p2}\left\vert \Psi_{p}\right\rangle
\left\langle \Psi_{1}\right\vert \right) & =\sum\limits_{1\leq p\leq
3}A_{p2}\Phi_{\left\vert \Psi_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle \Psi
_{1}\right\vert }\left( \left\vert \Psi_{p}\right\rangle \left\langle
\Psi_{1}\right\vert \right) =0\Leftrightarrow A_{p2}=0,1\leq p\leq3 \\
\Phi_{\left\vert \Psi_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle \Psi_{1}\right\vert
}\left( \sum\limits_{1\leq p\leq3}A_{p3}\left\vert \Psi_{p}\right\rangle
\left\langle \Psi_{1}\right\vert \right) & =\sum\limits_{1\leq p\leq
3}A_{p3}\Phi_{\left\vert \Psi_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle \Psi
_{1}\right\vert }\left( \left\vert \Psi_{p}\right\rangle \left\langle
\Psi_{1}\right\vert \right) =0\Leftrightarrow A_{p3}=0,1\leq p\leq3.
\end{align}
$\rfloor$

Thus 
\begin{equation}
\Phi_{\left\vert \Psi_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle \Psi_{1}\right\vert
}\left( \sum\limits_{1\leq p\leq2^{r}}A_{pj}\left\vert \Psi_{p}\right\rangle
\left\langle \Psi_{1}\right\vert \right) =0\Leftrightarrow\sum\limits_{1\leq
p\leq2^{r}}A_{pj}\left\vert \Psi_{p}\right\rangle \left\langle \Psi
_{1}\right\vert \in\ker\left\{ \Phi_{\left\vert \Psi_{1}\right\rangle
\left\langle \Psi_{1}\right\vert }\right\} ;1\leq j\leq2^{r},
\end{equation}
i.e. 
\begin{equation}
\sum\limits_{1\leq p\leq2^{r}}A_{pj}\left\vert \Psi_{p}\right\rangle
\left\langle \Psi_{1}\right\vert \in\limfunc{linspan}\left\{ \left.
\left\vert \Psi_{j}\right\rangle \left\langle \Psi_{k}\right\vert
\right\vert ;2\leq j,k\leq2^{r}\right\} ,\left\{ \left. \left\vert
\Psi_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle \Psi_{j}\right\vert \right\vert ;2\leq
j\leq 2^{r}\right\} ,\text{ }
\end{equation}
i.e.%
\begin{equation}
\sum\limits_{1\leq p\leq2^{r}}A_{pj}\left\vert \Psi_{p}\right\rangle
\left\langle \Psi_{1}\right\vert =\sum\limits_{2\leq
p,q\leq2^{r}}A_{pq}\left\vert \Psi_{p}\right\rangle \left\langle
\Psi_{q}\right\vert +\sum\limits_{2\leq q\leq2^{r}}A_{1q}\left\vert
\Psi_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle \Psi_{q}\right\vert ;1\leq j\leq2^{r},
\end{equation}
which shows that 
\begin{equation}
A=0.
\end{equation}
$\lceil$%
\begin{equation*}
A\left\vert \Psi_{p}\right\rangle \left\langle \Psi_{q}\right\vert
\left\vert \Psi_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle \Psi_{j}\right\vert
=0\forall\left\vert \Psi_{p}\right\rangle \left\langle \Psi_{q}\right\vert
,q\neq1\in \mathcal{B}\left( \mathcal{H}_{\mathcal{F}}\right) 
\end{equation*}
$\rfloor$

The GNS\ Hilbert space generated by $\left\vert \Psi_{1}\right\rangle
\left\langle \Psi_{1}\right\vert $ from the Type $I_{\infty}$ VnA $\mathcal{B%
}\left( \mathcal{H}_{\mathcal{F}}\right) ,$ where $\mathcal{H}_{\mathcal{F}}=%
\limfunc{linspan}\left\{ \left. \left\vert \Psi _{j}\right\rangle
\right\vert ;1\leq j\leq2^{r}\right\} $ is given by 
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{H}_{\left\vert \Psi_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle
\Psi_{1}\right\vert }=\limfunc{linspan}\left\{ \left. \Phi_{\left\vert
\Psi_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle \Psi_{1}\right\vert }\left( \left\vert
\Psi _{j}\right\rangle \left\langle \Psi_{1}\right\vert \right) \right\vert
;1\leq j\leq2^{r}\right\} \cong\mathcal{H}_{\mathcal{F}}.
\end{equation}
One can observe that as 
\begin{equation}
\left\{ \left. \left\vert \Psi_{j}\right\rangle \left\langle \Psi
_{j}\right\vert \right\vert 2\leq j\leq2^{r}\right\} \in\ker\left\{
\Phi_{\left\vert \Psi_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle \Psi_{1}\right\vert
}\right\}
\end{equation}
one has that 
\begin{equation}
\Phi_{\left\vert \Psi_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle \Psi_{1}\right\vert
}\left( \left\vert \Psi_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle \Psi_{1}\right\vert
\right) =\Phi_{\left\vert \Psi_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle \Psi
_{1}\right\vert }\left( I_{\mathcal{F}}\right) .
\end{equation}

One also has the creation and annihilation relationships 
\begin{gather}
\Pi_{\left\vert \Psi_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle \Psi_{1}\right\vert
}\left( \left\vert \Psi_{j}\right\rangle \left\langle \Psi_{1}\right\vert
\right) \Phi_{\left\vert \Psi_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle \Psi
_{1}\right\vert }\left( I_{\mathcal{F}}\right) =\Phi_{\left\vert \Psi
_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle \Psi_{1}\right\vert }\left( \left\vert
\Psi_{j}\right\rangle \left\langle \Psi_{1}\right\vert \right) \\
\Pi_{\left\vert \Psi_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle \Psi_{1}\right\vert
}\left( \left\vert \Psi_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle \Psi_{j}\right\vert
\right) \Phi_{\left\vert \Psi_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle \Psi
_{1}\right\vert }\left( I_{\mathcal{F}}\right) =0 \\
2\leq j\leq2^{r},
\end{gather}
showing that $\Phi_{\left\vert \Psi_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle \Psi
_{1}\right\vert }\left( I_{\mathcal{F}}\right) =$ $\Phi_{\left\vert \Psi
_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle \Psi_{1}\right\vert }\left( \left\vert
\Psi_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle \Psi_{1}\right\vert \right) $ is a vacuum
type vector.

The algebra $\mathcal{B}\left( \mathcal{H}_{\mathcal{F}}\right) $ has the
operator product $\square$ inherited from the exterior algebra $\mathcal{H}_{%
\mathcal{F}}$, where 
\begin{equation}
\left\vert \Psi_{a}\right\rangle \wedge\left\vert \Psi_{b}\right\rangle \in%
\mathcal{H}_{\mathcal{F}};\left\vert \Psi_{a}\right\rangle ,\left\vert
\Psi_{b}\right\rangle \in\mathcal{H}_{\mathcal{F}}
\end{equation}
and $\wedge$ is the exterior product in $\mathcal{H}_{\mathcal{F}}$.

The box product has the following properties wrt $\Pi_{\left\vert \Psi
_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle \Psi_{1}\right\vert }$ and $\Phi_{\left\vert
\Psi_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle \Psi_{1}\right\vert }$:

\begin{enumerate}
\item 
\begin{equation}
\Pi_{\left\vert \Psi_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle \Psi_{1}\right\vert
}\left( A\square B\right) =\Pi_{\left\vert \Psi_{1}\right\rangle
\left\langle \Psi_{1}\right\vert }\left( A\right) \square\Pi_{\left\vert
\Psi_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle \Psi_{1}\right\vert }\left( B\right)
\end{equation}

\item 
\begin{equation}
I_{\mathcal{F}}\square B=B
\end{equation}

\item 
\begin{equation}
\Pi_{\left\vert \Psi_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle \Psi_{1}\right\vert
}\left( A\square B\right) \Phi_{\left\vert \Psi_{1}\right\rangle
\left\langle \Psi_{1}\right\vert }\left( I_{\mathcal{F}}\right)
=\Phi_{\left\vert \Psi_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle \Psi_{1}\right\vert
}\left( A\square B\right) =\Phi_{\left\vert \Psi_{1}\right\rangle
\left\langle \Psi_{1}\right\vert }\left( A\right) \wedge\Phi_{\left\vert
\Psi_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle \Psi_{1}\right\vert }\left( B\right)
\end{equation}

\item 
\begin{equation}
\Pi_{\left\vert \Psi_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle \Psi_{1}\right\vert
}\left( I_{\mathcal{F}}\square B\right) =\Pi_{\left\vert
\Psi_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle \Psi_{1}\right\vert }\left( I_{\mathcal{F}%
}\right) \square\Pi_{\left\vert \Psi_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle \Psi
_{1}\right\vert }\left( B\right) =\Pi_{\left\vert \Psi_{1}\right\rangle
\left\langle \Psi_{1}\right\vert }\left( B\right)
\end{equation}
Thus 
\begin{equation}
\Phi_{\left\vert \Psi_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle \Psi_{1}\right\vert
}\left( I_{\mathcal{F}}\right) \wedge\Phi_{\left\vert \Psi_{1}\right\rangle
\left\langle \Psi_{1}\right\vert }\left( B\right) =\Phi_{\left\vert \Psi
_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle \Psi_{1}\right\vert }\left( B\right)
\end{equation}
showing that $\Phi_{\left\vert \Psi_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle \Psi
_{1}\right\vert }\left( I_{\mathcal{F}}\right) $ is a \emph{grade zero vector%
}.
\end{enumerate}

$\lceil$%
\begin{equation}
\Pi_{\left\vert \Psi_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle \Psi_{1}\right\vert
}\left( I_{\mathcal{F}}\square B\right) \Phi_{\left\vert
\Psi_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle \Psi_{1}\right\vert }\left( I_{\mathcal{F}%
}\right) =\Phi_{\left\vert \Psi_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle \Psi
_{1}\right\vert }\left( I_{\mathcal{F}}\square B\right) =\Phi_{\left\vert
\Psi_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle \Psi_{1}\right\vert }\left( I_{\mathcal{F}%
}\right) \wedge\Phi_{\left\vert \Psi_{1}\right\rangle \left\langle \Psi
_{1}\right\vert }\left( B\right) =\Phi_{\left\vert \Psi_{1}\right\rangle
\left\langle \Psi_{1}\right\vert }\left( B\right)
\end{equation}
$\rfloor$

All finite dimensional $w^{\ast}$-algebras are type $I_{m}$ and are
isomorphic to $\mathcal{B}\left( \mathcal{H}\right) ,$where $\dim\left\{ 
\mathcal{H}\right\} =m<\infty,$ it is the limiting process that produces
different types of $w^{\ast}$-algebras. The limiting process \emph{involves
states and dimension}. The limit of CAR algebras with normalized trace is a $%
w^{\ast}$-algebra of type $II_{1}.$

\subsubsection{Vacuum States}

$\lceil$%
\begin{align}
& K\left( 
\begin{array}{cc}
a^{\dagger} & a%
\end{array}
\right) =\left( 
\begin{array}{cc}
a^{\dagger} & a%
\end{array}
\right) \mathbb{K}=\left( 
\begin{array}{cc}
a^{\dagger} & a%
\end{array}
\right) \left( 
\begin{array}{cc}
\frac{1}{\sqrt{2}} & \frac{i}{\sqrt{2}} \\ 
\frac{1}{\sqrt{2}} & \frac{-i}{\sqrt{2}}%
\end{array}
\right) =\left( 
\begin{array}{cc}
x_{1} & x_{2}%
\end{array}
\right) \\
& K^{\dagger}\left( 
\begin{array}{cc}
x_{1} & x_{2}%
\end{array}
\right) =\left( 
\begin{array}{cc}
x_{1} & x_{2}%
\end{array}
\right) \mathbb{K}^{\dagger}=\left( 
\begin{array}{cc}
x_{1} & x_{2}%
\end{array}
\right) \left( 
\begin{array}{cc}
\frac{1}{\sqrt{2}} & \frac{1}{\sqrt{2}} \\ 
\frac{-i}{\sqrt{2}} & \frac{i}{\sqrt{2}}%
\end{array}
\right) =\left( 
\begin{array}{cc}
a^{\dagger} & a%
\end{array}
\right) \\
& a^{\dagger}a=\frac{1}{2}\left( x_{1}-ix_{2}\right) \left(
x_{1}+ix_{2}\right) =\frac{1}{2}\left(
x_{1}^{2}+x_{2}^{2}-ix_{2}x_{1}+ix_{1}x_{2}\right) \\
& aa^{\dagger}=\frac{1}{2}\left( x_{1}+ix_{2}\right) \left(
x_{1}-ix_{2}\right) =\frac{1}{2}\left(
x_{1}^{2}+x_{2}^{2}+ix_{2}x_{1}-ix_{1}x_{2}\right) \\
& a^{\dagger}a+aa^{\dagger}=I_{\mathcal{F}} \\
& =\frac{1}{2}\left( x_{1}^{2}+x_{2}^{2}-ix_{2}x_{2}+ix_{1}x_{2}\right) +%
\frac{1}{2}\left( x_{1}^{2}+x_{2}^{2}+ix_{2}x_{2}-ix_{1}x_{2}\right)
=x_{1}^{2}+x_{2}^{2}
\end{align}%
\begin{equation}
\Longleftrightarrow x_{1}^{2}=x_{2}^{2}=\frac{1}{2}
\end{equation}
$\left( \text{as it is always true }x_{1}^{2}=x_{2}^{2}\right) .$ Thus 
\begin{align}
a^{\dagger}a & =\frac{1}{2}I_{\mathcal{F}}+ix_{1}x_{2} \\
aa^{\dagger} & =\frac{1}{2}I_{\mathcal{F}}-ix_{1}x_{2}
\end{align}
$\rfloor$

Normal vacuum states are states, $D_{f_{b}},$that have the \emph{necessary}
properties

\begin{enumerate}
\item 
\begin{gather}
\limfunc{tr}\left\{ D_{f_{b}}\right\} =1 \\
D_{f_{b}}\geq0\Leftrightarrow D_{f_{b}}=Q_{f_{b}}Q_{f_{b}}^{\dagger} \\
\widehat{\mathcal{N}}_{b}\left( D_{f_{b}}\right) =\left[ \emph{\mathcal{N}}%
_{b},D_{f_{b}}\right] =0 \\
\limfunc{tr}\left\{ \mathcal{N}_{b}D_{f_{b}}\right\} =0,
\end{gather}
where 
\begin{align}
\emph{\mathcal{N}}_{b} & =\sum_{1\leq j\leq r}b_{j}^{\dagger}b_{j} \\
\left[ \mathcal{N}_{b},b_{j}\right] & =-b_{j}\Longleftrightarrow\left[ 
\mathcal{N}_{b},b_{j}^{\dagger}\right] =b_{j}^{\dagger}.
\end{align}
$\lceil$%
\begin{align}
\left( b_{1}^{\dagger}b_{1}+b_{2}^{\dagger}b_{2}+b_{3}^{\dagger}b_{3}\right)
b_{3} & =b_{1}^{\dagger}b_{1}b_{3}+b_{2}^{\dagger}b_{2}b_{3} \\
-b_{3}\left(
b_{1}^{\dagger}b_{1}+b_{2}^{\dagger}b_{2}+b_{3}^{\dagger}b_{3}\right) &
=-b_{1}^{\dagger}b_{1}b_{3}-b_{2}^{\dagger}b_{2}b_{3}-\left(
1-b_{3}^{\dagger}b_{3}\right)
b_{3}=-b_{1}^{\dagger}b_{1}b_{3}-b_{2}^{\dagger}b_{2}b_{3}-b_{3} \\
\widehat{\mathcal{N}}_{b}\left( b_{3}\right) & =\left[ \mathcal{N}_{b},b_{3}%
\right] =-b_{3} \\
\left[ b_{3}^{\dagger},\mathcal{N}_{b}\right] & =-b_{3}^{\dagger
}\Longleftrightarrow\left[ \mathcal{N}_{b},b_{3}^{\dagger}\right]
=b_{3}^{\dagger}
\end{align}
$\rfloor$

$\lceil$%
\begin{equation}
\widehat{\mathcal{N}}_{b}\left( Q_{f_{b}}Q_{f_{b}}^{\dagger}\right) =%
\widehat{\mathcal{N}}_{b}\left( Q_{f_{b}}\right) Q_{f_{b}}^{\dagger
}+Q_{f_{b}}\widehat{\mathcal{N}}_{b}\left( Q_{f_{b}}^{\dagger}\right)
\end{equation}
as%
\begin{align}
\left[ A,BC\right] & =ABC-BCA \\
\left[ A,B\right] C+B\left[ A,C\right] & =ABC-BAC+BAC-BCA=ABC-BCA \\
\left[ A,BC\right] & =\left[ A,B\right] C+B\left[ A,C\right] .
\end{align}
$\rfloor$

\item 
\begin{equation}
\left[ \emph{\mathcal{N}}_{b},D_{f_{b}}\right] =0\Rightarrow
D_{f_{b}}=\eta_{0}I_{\mathcal{F}}+\tsum \limits_{1\leq n\leq r}\tsum \limits
_{\substack{ 1\leq j_{1}<\cdots<j_{n}\leq r \\ 1\leq k_{1}<\cdots<k_{n}\leq r
}}\eta_{j_{1}\cdots j_{n}k_{1}\cdots k_{n}}b_{j_{1}}^{\dagger}\cdots
b_{j_{n}}^{\dagger}b_{k_{n}}\cdots b_{k_{1}}.
\end{equation}
Note that in general%
\begin{equation}
\limfunc{tr}\left\{ \tsum \limits_{1\leq n\leq r}\tsum \limits_{\substack{ %
1\leq j_{1}<\cdots<j_{n}\leq r \\ 1\leq k_{1}<\cdots<k_{n}\leq r}}%
\eta_{j_{1}\cdots j_{n}k_{1}\cdots k_{n}}b_{j_{1}}^{\dagger}\cdots
b_{j_{n}}^{\dagger}b_{k_{n}}\cdots b_{k_{1}}\right\} \neq0.
\end{equation}

\item $U\left( r\right) $ has unitary (spinor) representations $\mathcal{R}%
_{b}\left( U\left( r\right) \right) $ in $\mathcal{A}$ given by 
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{R}_{b}\left( U\left( r\right) \right) \left( A\right) =\exp\left\{
i\tsum \limits_{1\leq j,k\leq r}\beta_{jk}b_{j}^{\dagger}b_{k}\right\}
\left( A\right) \exp\left\{ -i\tsum \limits_{1\leq j,k\leq
r}\beta_{jk}b_{j}^{\dagger}b_{k}\right\} ;\beta_{jk}=\overline{\beta}%
_{kj};\forall A\in\mathcal{A}
\end{equation}
These represenations are unitarily equivalent as 
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{R}_{\left[ b^{\prime}\right] }\left( U\left( r\right) \right) =%
\mathcal{R}_{\mathcal{A}}\left( O\left( 2r,\mathbb{R}\right) \right) \left( 
\mathcal{R}_{\left[ b\right] }\left( U\left( r\right) \right) \right) ,
\end{equation}
where $\mathcal{R}_{\mathcal{A}}\left( O\left( 2r,\mathbb{R}\right) \right) $
is a unitary representation of $O\left( 2r,\mathbb{R}\right) $ in $\mathcal{A%
}.$ Clearly as $U\left( r\right) \subset O\left( 2r,\mathbb{R}\right) $ 
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{R}_{\left[ b\right] }\left( U\left( r\right) \right) =\mathcal{R}_{%
\mathcal{A}}\left( U\left( r\right) \right) \left( \mathcal{R}_{\left[ b%
\right] }\left( U\left( r\right) \right) \right) ,
\end{equation}
where $\mathcal{R}_{\left[ b\right] }$ is an equivalence class of $U\left(
r\right) $ equivalent representations within $\mathcal{R}_{\mathcal{A}%
}\left( O\left( 2r,\mathbb{R}\right) \right) $.

$\lceil$ One can factor $O\left( 2r,\mathbb{R}\right) $ in terms of a coset
decomposition as 
\begin{equation}
O\left( 2r,\mathbb{R}\right) =\frac{O\left( 2r,\mathbb{R}\right) }{U\left(
r\right) }U\left( r\right) ,
\end{equation}
which is represented in $\mathcal{A}$ as 
\begin{equation}
\left( \widehat{\exp\left\{ i\tsum \limits_{1\leq j<k\leq r}\left(
\alpha_{jk}b_{j}^{\dagger}b_{k}^{\dagger}+\overline{\alpha}%
_{jk}b_{k}^{\dagger}b_{j}^{\dagger}\right) \right\} }\right) \left( \widehat{%
\exp\left\{ i\tsum \limits_{1\leq j,k\leq
r}\beta_{jk}b_{j}^{\dagger}b_{k}\right\} }\right)
\end{equation}
the term 
\begin{equation}
\left( \widehat{\exp\left\{ i\tsum \limits_{1\leq j,k\leq
r}\beta_{jk}b_{j}^{\dagger}b_{k}\right\} }\right)
\end{equation}
can be be replaced by any 
\begin{equation}
\left( \widehat{\exp\left\{ i\tsum \limits_{1\leq j,k\leq
r}\gamma_{jk}c_{j}^{\dagger}c_{k}\right\} }\right) \in\mathcal{R}_{\left[ b%
\right] }\left( U\left( r\right) \right) .
\end{equation}
Different equivalence classes $\left[ b\right] \neq\left[ c\right] $ form
unitarily \emph{equivalent} representations of $U\left( r\right) $ in $%
\mathcal{A},$ through transformations belonging to $\mathcal{R}_{\left[ b%
\right] }\left( \frac{O\left( 2r,\mathbb{R}\right) }{U\left( r\right) }%
\right) $ or $\mathcal{R}_{\left[ c\right] }\left( \frac{O\left( 2r,\mathbb{R%
}\right) }{U\left( r\right) }\right) .$

\item Different $\mathcal{R}_{\left[ b\right] }\left( U\left( r\right)
\right) $ are associated with different vacuum states leading to different
vacuum vectors in the GNS Hilbert space induced by these vacuum vectors,
that can be identified as lowest weight vectors associated with the
representation.

All vacuum states lie on the orbit produced by the action of the coset $%
\mathcal{R}_{\left[ b\right] }\left( \frac{O\left( 2r,\mathbb{R}\right) }{%
U\left( r\right) }\right) $ on the vacuum state $D_{f_{b}}.$ This orbit
forms a Homogeneous manifold (Symmetric space, K\"{a}hler manifold?) and are
coherent states.

\item $U\left( r\right) $-invariance%
\begin{gather}
\widehat{\exp\left\{ i\tsum \limits_{1\leq j,k\leq
r}\beta_{jk}b_{j}^{\dagger}b_{k}\right\} }\left( D_{f_{b}}\right)
=\exp\left\{ i\tsum \limits_{1\leq j,k\leq
r}\beta_{jk}b_{j}^{\dagger}b_{k}\right\} \left( D_{f_{b}}\right) \exp\left\{
-i\tsum \limits_{1\leq j,k\leq r}\beta_{jk}b_{j}^{\dagger}b_{k}\right\}
=D_{f_{b}};\beta_{jk}=\overline{\beta }_{kj} \\
\left[ b_{j}^{\dagger}b_{k},D_{f_{b}}\right] =0.
\end{gather}
These conditions for can be strengthened by observing that $\mathbf{\beta}$
is hermitian and thus it can always be diagonalized by a unitary $r\times r$
matrix $\mathbf{V}$ to give%
\begin{equation}
\beta_{jk}=\tsum \limits_{1\leq l\leq r}\overline{V}_{lj}\gamma_{ll}V_{lk}.
\end{equation}
This shows that 
\begin{align}
\tsum \limits_{1\leq j,k\leq r}\beta_{jk}b_{j}^{\dagger}b_{k} & =\tsum
\limits_{1\leq j,k\leq r}\tsum \limits_{1\leq l\leq r}\overline{V}%
_{lj}\gamma_{ll}V_{lk}b_{j}^{\dagger}b_{k}=\tsum \limits_{1\leq l\leq
r}\gamma_{ll}\tsum \limits_{1\leq j,k\leq r}\overline{V}_{lj}b_{j}^{%
\dagger}b_{k}V_{lk}=\tsum \limits_{1\leq l\leq
r}\gamma_{ll}c_{l}^{\dagger}c_{l} \\
c_{l}^{\dagger} & =\tsum \limits_{1\leq j\leq r}\overline{V}%
_{lj}b_{j}^{\dagger};\quad c_{l}=\tsum \limits_{1\leq k\leq r}V_{lk}b_{k},
\end{align}
and hence%
\begin{gather}
\exp\left\{ i\tsum \limits_{1\leq j,k\leq
r}\beta_{jk}b_{j}^{\dagger}b_{k}\right\} \left( D_{f_{b}}\right) \exp\left\{
-i\tsum \limits_{1\leq j,k\leq r}\beta_{jk}b_{j}^{\dagger}b_{k}\right\}
=D_{f_{b}} \\
\Updownarrow \\
\exp\left\{ i\tsum \limits_{1\leq l\leq
r}\gamma_{ll}c_{l}^{\dagger}c_{l}\right\} \left( D_{f_{b}}\right)
\exp\left\{ -i\tsum \limits_{1\leq l\leq
r}\gamma_{ll}c_{l}^{\dagger}c_{l}\right\} =D_{f_{b}}.
\end{gather}

Hence if 
\begin{equation}
\left[ b_{j}^{\dagger}b_{j},D_{f_{b}}\right] =0;1\leq j\leq r
\end{equation}
for a basis set $\left\{ \left. b_{j}^{\dagger}\right\vert 1\leq j\leq
r\right\} $ then $D_{f_{b}}$ is $U\left( r\right) $-invariant. This further
shows that 
\begin{equation}
\left[ \tprod \limits_{1\leq j\leq r}n_{bj},D_{f_{b}}\right] =\left[
n_{bj_{1}}\cdots n_{bj_{m}},D_{f_{b}}\right] =0;1\leq j_{1},\cdots,j_{m}\leq
r.
\end{equation}
$\lceil$As%
\begin{align}
\left[ D_{f_{b}},n_{bj_{1}}\cdots n_{bj_{m}}\right] & =\left[
D_{f_{b}},n_{bj_{1}}\right] n_{bj_{2}}\cdots n_{bj_{m}}+n_{bj_{1}}\left[
D_{f_{b}},n_{bj_{2}}\cdots n_{bj_{m}}\right] \\
& =\left[ D_{f_{b}},n_{bj_{1}}\right] n_{bj_{2}}\cdots
n_{bj_{m}}+n_{bj_{1}}\left( \left[ D_{f_{b}},n_{bj_{2}}\right]
n_{bj_{3}}\cdots n_{bj_{m}}+n_{bj_{2}}\left[ D_{f_{b}},n_{bj_{3}}\cdots
n_{bj_{m}}\right] \right) \\
& =\left[ D_{f_{b}},n_{bj_{1}}\right] n_{bj_{2}}\cdots n_{bj_{m}}+n_{bj_{1}}%
\left[ D_{f_{b}},n_{bj_{2}}\right] n_{bj_{3}}\cdots
n_{bj_{m}}+\cdots+n_{bj_{1}}\cdots n_{bj_{m-1}}\left[ D_{f_{b}},n_{bj_{n}}%
\right] .
\end{align}
$\rfloor$

As $\left\{ \left. n_{j}\right\vert 1\leq j\leq r\right\} $ is set of
commuting orthogonal (however, not orthogonal to each other) projections
i.e. 
\begin{equation}
\left[ n_{j},n_{k}\right] =0,n_{j}=n_{j}^{\dagger};1\leq j,k\leq r
\end{equation}
$D_{f_{b}}$ \emph{can be expanded in terms of these projectors as }%
\begin{equation}
D_{f_{b}}=\eta_{0}I_{\mathcal{F}}+\tsum \limits_{1\leq n\leq r}\tsum
\limits_{1\leq j_{1}<\cdots<j_{n}\leq r}\eta_{j_{1}\cdots
j_{n}}n_{bj_{1}}\cdots n_{bj_{n}},
\end{equation}
which is consistent with the representation of vacuum states given by 
\begin{align}
D_{f_{b}} & =\tprod \limits_{1\leq n\leq r}\left( 2h_{bn}\right) =\tprod
\limits_{1\leq n\leq r}2\left( I_{\mathcal{F}}-\frac{1}{2}I_{\mathcal{F}%
}+ix_{n}x_{n+r}\right) =\tprod \limits_{1\leq n\leq r}\left( I_{\mathcal{F}%
}+2ix_{j}x_{j+r}\right) =I_{\mathcal{F}}+\tsum \limits_{1\leq n\leq r}\left(
2i\right) ^{n}\tsum \limits_{1\leq j_{1}<\cdots<j_{n}\leq
r}x_{j_{1}}x_{j_{1}+r}\cdots x_{j_{n}}x_{j_{n}+r} \\
& =\tprod \limits_{1\leq n\leq r}2\left( I_{\mathcal{F}}-n_{bj}\right)
=2^{n}I_{\mathcal{F}}+\tsum \limits_{1\leq n\leq r}2^{n}\left( -1\right)
^{n}\tsum \limits_{1\leq j_{1}<\cdots<j_{n}\leq r}n_{bj_{1}}\cdots n_{bj_{n}}
\end{align}
obtained elsewhere, which shows 
\begin{equation}
\limfunc{tr}\left\{ D_{f_{b}}\right\} =\limfunc{tr}\left\{ \tprod
\limits_{1\leq n\leq r}\left( 2h_{bn}\right) \right\} =2^{n}\limfunc{tr}%
\left\{ \tprod \limits_{1\leq n\leq r}\left( h_{bn}\right) \right\}
=1,\quad1\leq r\leq\infty,
\end{equation}
as 
\begin{equation}
\limfunc{tr}\left\{ I_{\mathcal{F}}\right\} =1
\end{equation}
and 
\begin{equation}
\limfunc{tr}\left\{ x_{j_{1}}x_{j_{1}+r}\cdots x_{j_{n}}x_{j_{n}+r}\right\}
=0;1\leq j_{1}<\cdots<j_{n}\leq r,1\leq n\leq r.
\end{equation}
The projectors $\left\{ \left. n_{bj},h_{bj}\right\vert 1\leq j\leq
r\right\} $ are not normalized, while the density operators $\left\{ \left.
2n_{bj},2h_{bj}\right\vert 1\leq j\leq r\right\} $ are normalized but not
projectors.

As $r\rightarrow\infty$ one must consider infinite products of projectors
i.e. the sequences 
\begin{equation}
\left\{ 2h_{b1},\cdots,\tprod \limits_{1\leq n\leq r}\left( 2h_{bn}\right)
,\cdots\right\}
\end{equation}
and their limits 
\begin{equation}
\lim\limits_{r\rightarrow\infty}\tprod \limits_{1\leq n\leq r}\left(
2h_{bn}\right) .
\end{equation}
As 
\begin{equation}
\left\langle \lim\limits_{r\rightarrow\infty}\tprod \limits_{1\leq n\leq
r}\left( 2h_{bn}\right) \right\rangle _{0}=I_{\mathcal{F}};1\leq n\leq\infty
\end{equation}
one trivially obtains%
\begin{equation}
\limfunc{tr}\left\{ \lim\limits_{r\rightarrow\infty}\tprod \limits_{1\leq
n\leq r}\left( 2h_{bn}\right) \right\} =\left\langle \left\langle \lim
\limits_{r\rightarrow\infty}\tprod \limits_{1\leq n\leq r}\left(
2h_{bn}\right) \right\rangle \right\rangle _{0}=1.
\end{equation}

The identity 
\begin{equation}
\left\vert \phi_{b}\right\rangle \left\langle \phi_{b}\right\vert =\tprod
\limits_{1\leq n\leq r}h_{bn}
\end{equation}
\emph{only exists if} $\mathcal{A}$ acts on an Hilbert space where $%
\left\vert \phi_{b}\right\rangle $ exists. If no action on a Hilbert is
defined the notation is meaningless. \emph{The self adjoint idempotents }$%
\left\{ n_{b1},\cdots,n_{br}\right\} \equiv\left\{
h_{b1},\cdots,h_{br}\right\} $\emph{\ always exist in }$\mathcal{A}$\emph{\
and have the property }%
\begin{equation}
n_{bj_{1}}\cdots n_{bj_{p}}\tprod \limits_{1\leq n\leq r}\left(
2h_{bn}\right) =0;1\leq p\leq r
\end{equation}
\emph{and define vacuum states, }$f_{b},$\emph{\ that have density operators 
}$\tprod \limits_{1\leq n\leq r}\left( 2h_{bn}\right) $\emph{\ in }$\mathcal{%
A}.$ \emph{Vacuum vectors, }$\left\vert \phi_{b}\right\rangle
=\Phi_{f_{b}}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}}\right) ,$\emph{\ only exist in the GNS\
Hilbert space }$H_{f_{b}}.$

The density operators $\tprod \limits_{1\leq n\leq r}\left( 2h_{bn}\right) $
can be decomposed into the product of normalized amplitudes 
\begin{equation}
Q_{b}Q_{b}^{\dagger},
\end{equation}
where 
\begin{equation}
Q_{b}=\tprod \limits_{1\leq j\leq r}\left( \sqrt{2}b_{j}\right) ,
\end{equation}
\emph{that also have the vacuum property} 
\begin{equation}
n_{bj_{1}}\cdots n_{bj_{p}}Q_{b}=0;1\leq p\leq r.
\end{equation}
One should observe that the amplitude $Q_{b}$ is not grade zero, unlike the
GNS\ vacuum vector $\left\vert \phi_{b}\right\rangle .$

$\lceil$One might think that if the expansions of $D_{f_{b}}$ contained
multiples of $I_{\mathcal{F}}$ then it could not be annihilated by all $%
\left\{ n_{bj_{1}}\cdots n_{bj_{n}}\right\} ,$ that this is not true is
shown by 
\begin{align}
h_{b1}h_{b2} & =\left( I_{\mathcal{F}}-n_{b1}\right) \left( I_{\mathcal{F}%
}-n_{b2}\right) =I_{\mathcal{F}}-n_{b1}-n_{b2}+n_{b1}n_{b2} \\
n_{b1}\left( I_{\mathcal{F}}-n_{b1}-n_{b2}+n_{b1}n_{b2}\right) &
=n_{b1}-n_{b1}-n_{b1}n_{b2}+n_{b1}n_{b2}=0.
\end{align}%
\begin{gather}
n_{bj}=\frac{1}{2}I_{\mathcal{F}}+ix_{j}x_{j+r} \\
n_{b1}n_{b2}=\left( \frac{1}{2}I_{\mathcal{F}}+ix_{1}x_{3}\right) \left( 
\frac{1}{2}I_{\mathcal{F}}+ix_{2}x_{4}\right) =\frac{1}{4}I_{\mathcal{F}%
}+ix_{1}x_{3}+ix_{2}x_{2+r}-x_{1}x_{1+r}x_{2}x_{4} \\
\left\langle n_{b1}n_{b2}\right\rangle _{0}=\frac{1}{4}I_{\mathcal{F}} \\
\left\langle \left\langle n_{b1}n_{b2}\right\rangle \right\rangle _{0}=%
\limfunc{tr}\left\{ \left( 2n_{b1}\right) \left( 2n_{b2}\right) \right\} =1
\end{gather}
$\rfloor$

\emph{Thus it seems that }$U\left( r\right) $\emph{-invariance of the state }%
$f_{b}$\emph{\ is equivalent to all of the vacuum conditions in fact it is a
necessary and sufficient condition.}

\item 
\begin{gather}
\limfunc{tr}\left\{ b_{p_{1}}^{\dagger}\cdots b_{p_{n}}^{\dagger
}b_{q_{m}}\cdots b_{q_{1}}D_{f_{b}}\right\} =\limfunc{tr}\left\{
b_{p_{1}}^{\dagger}\cdots b_{p_{n}}^{\dagger}D_{f_{b}}\right\} =\limfunc{tr}%
\left\{ b_{q_{m}}\cdots b_{q_{1}}D_{f_{b}}\right\} =0 \\
\limfunc{tr}\left\{ b_{q_{m}}\cdots b_{q_{1}}b_{p_{1}}^{\dagger}\cdots
b_{p_{n}}^{\dagger}D_{f_{b}}\right\} =\delta_{mn}\delta_{\left( q_{1}\cdots
q_{n}\right) \left( p_{1}\cdots p_{n}\right) }, \\
1\leq p_{1}<\cdots<p_{n}\leq r,1\leq q_{1}<\cdots<q_{m}\leq r,1\leq m,n\leq
r, \\
\delta_{\left( q_{1}\cdots q_{n}\right) \left( p_{1}\cdots p_{n}\right)
}=\left\{ 
\begin{array}{c}
\left( -1\right) ^{\pi\left( \sigma\right) }\text{ if }\sigma\left\{
q_{1},\cdots,q_{n}\right\} =\left\{ p_{1},\cdots,p_{n}\right\} \\ 
0\text{ otherwise.}%
\end{array}
\right. .
\end{gather}

If $f_{b}$ is a normal vacuum state then in particular%
\begin{gather}
\limfunc{tr}\left\{ b_{j}^{\dagger}b_{j}D_{f_{b}}\right\} =0=\limfunc{tr}%
\left\{ b_{j}^{\dagger}b_{j}Q_{f_{b}}Q_{f_{b}}^{\dagger }\right\} =\limfunc{%
tr}\left\{ b_{j}Q_{f_{b}}Q_{f_{b}}^{\dagger}b_{j}^{\dagger}\right\} =%
\limfunc{tr}\left\{ b_{j}Q_{f_{b}}\left( b_{j}Q_{f_{b}}\right)
^{\dagger}\right\} \Leftrightarrow b_{j}Q_{f_{b}}=0 \\
\text{i.e. }\limfunc{tr}\left\{ b_{j}^{\dagger}b_{j}D_{f_{b}}\right\}
=0\Leftrightarrow b_{j}Q_{f_{b}}=0.
\end{gather}
Using 
\begin{equation}
b_{j}b_{j}^{\dagger}=I_{\mathcal{F}}-b_{j}^{\dagger}b_{j}
\end{equation}
one obtains%
\begin{gather}
\limfunc{tr}\left\{ b_{j}b_{j}^{\dagger}D_{f_{b}}\right\} =1=\limfunc{tr}%
\left\{ b_{j}b_{j}^{\dagger}Q_{f_{b}}Q_{f_{b}}^{\dagger }\right\} =\limfunc{%
tr}\left\{ b_{j}^{\dagger}Q_{f_{b}}Q_{f_{b}}^{\dagger}b_{j}\right\} \\
=\limfunc{tr}\left\{ b_{j}^{\dagger}Q_{f_{b}}\left( b_{j}^{\dagger
}Q_{f_{b}}\right) ^{\dagger}\right\} =\left\Vert
b_{j}^{\dagger}Q_{f_{b}}\right\Vert _{HS}=1\Rightarrow
b_{j}^{\dagger}Q_{f_{b}}\neq0\text{ and is in fact HS-normalized},
\end{gather}
while%
\begin{equation}
\left\Vert b_{j}^{\dagger}\right\Vert _{HS}^{2}=\left\Vert b_{j}\right\Vert
_{HS}^{2}=\limfunc{tr}\left\{ b_{j}^{\dagger}b_{j}\right\} =\limfunc{tr}%
\left\{ b_{j}b_{j}^{\dagger}\right\} =\frac{1}{2}.
\end{equation}
$\lceil$Even though $\left\{ b_{j}^{\dagger}\right\} $ are not normalized
wrt $\left\Vert {}\right\Vert _{HS}$ they form $\left\Vert {}\right\Vert
_{HS}$-normalized amplitudes $b_{j}^{\dagger}Q_{f_{b}}$ when acting on the $%
\left\Vert {}\right\Vert _{HS}$-normalized vacuum amplitudes$\rfloor$

Another way to obtain $\limfunc{tr}\left\{ b_{j}^{\dagger}D_{f_{b}}\right\}
=0$ follows from 
\begin{align}
\limfunc{tr}\left\{ b_{j}^{\dagger}D_{f_{b}}\right\} & =\limfunc{tr}\left\{ %
\left[ \mathcal{N}_{b},b_{j}^{\dagger}\right] D_{f_{b}}\right\} =\limfunc{tr}%
\left\{ \left( \mathcal{N}_{b}b_{j}^{\dagger}-b_{j}^{\dagger}\mathcal{N}%
_{b}\right) D_{f_{b}}\right\} =\limfunc{tr}\left\{ \left( \mathcal{N}%
_{b}b_{j}^{\dagger}D_{f_{b}}-b_{j}^{\dagger}\mathcal{N}_{b}D_{f_{b}}\right)
\right\} \\
& =\limfunc{tr}\left\{ \left( D_{f_{b}}\mathcal{N}_{b}b_{j}^{\dagger }-%
\mathcal{N}_{b}D_{f_{b}}b_{j}^{\dagger}\right) \right\} =\limfunc{tr}\left\{ %
\left[ D_{f_{b}},\mathcal{N}_{b}\right] b_{j}^{\dagger}\right\} =0\text{ as }%
\left[ \mathcal{N}_{b},D_{f_{b}}\right] =0 \\
\limfunc{tr}\left\{ b_{j}^{\dagger}D_{f_{b}}\right\} & =0\Leftrightarrow%
\limfunc{tr}\left\{ b_{j}D_{f_{b}}\right\} =0.
\end{align}

\item Diffferential generating relationships: 
\begin{equation}
\left\{ \exp\left( \tsum \limits_{1\leq j\leq
r}\beta_{j}b_{j}^{\dagger}b_{j}\right) \right\} =\left\{ \tprod
\limits_{1\leq j\leq r}e^{\beta_{j}b_{j}^{\dagger}b_{j}}\right\}
\end{equation}%
\begin{align}
& \left. \frac{d}{d\beta_{j}}\left\{ \tprod \limits_{1\leq j\leq
r}e^{\beta_{j}b_{j}^{\dagger}b_{j}}\right\} \right\vert _{\mathbf{\beta }=%
\mathbf{0}}=\limfunc{tr}\left\{ b_{j}^{\dagger}b_{j}\right\} \\
& \left. \frac{d^{2}}{d\beta_{j}d\beta_{k}}\left\{ \tprod \limits_{1\leq
j\leq r}e^{\beta_{j}b_{j}^{\dagger}b_{j}}\right\} \right\vert _{\mathbf{%
\beta }=\mathbf{0}}=\limfunc{tr}\left\{ b_{j}^{\dagger}b_{j}b_{k}^{\dagger
}b_{k}\right\}
\end{align}%
\begin{equation}
\widehat{\exp\left( i\tsum \limits_{1\leq j\leq
r}\beta_{j}b_{j}^{\dagger}b_{j}\right) }\left( D_{f_{b}}\right) =\tprod
\limits_{1\leq j\leq r}e^{i\beta_{j}b_{j}^{\dagger}b_{j}}D_{f_{b}}\tprod
\limits_{1\leq k\leq r}e^{-i\beta_{k}b_{k}^{\dagger}b_{k}}
\end{equation}%
\begin{equation}
\left. \frac{d}{d\beta_{j}}\left\{ \tprod \limits_{1\leq j\leq
r}e^{i\beta_{j}b_{j}^{\dagger}b_{j}}D_{f_{b}}\tprod \limits_{1\leq k\leq
r}e^{-i\beta_{k}b_{k}^{\dagger}b_{k}}\right\} \right\vert _{\mathbf{\beta }=%
\mathbf{0}}=ib_{j}^{\dagger}b_{j}D_{f_{b}}-iD_{f_{b}}b_{j}^{\dagger}b_{j}=i%
\left[ b_{j}^{\dagger}b_{j},D_{f_{b}}\right]
\end{equation}%
\begin{equation}
\exp\left( i\tsum \limits_{1\leq j\leq
r}\beta_{j}b_{j}^{\dagger}b_{j}\right) D_{f_{b}}=\tprod \limits_{1\leq j\leq
r}e^{i\beta_{j}b_{j}^{\dagger}b_{j}}D_{f_{b}}
\end{equation}%
\begin{equation}
\left. \frac{d}{d\beta_{j}}\left\{ \tprod \limits_{1\leq j\leq
r}e^{i\beta_{j}b_{j}^{\dagger}b_{j}}D_{f_{b}}\right\} \right\vert _{\mathbf{%
\beta}=\mathbf{0}}=ib_{j}^{\dagger}b_{j}D_{f_{b}}=ib_{j}^{\dagger
}b_{j}D_{f_{b}}
\end{equation}%
\begin{equation}
\left[ \mathcal{N}_{b},b_{j}^{\dagger}\right] =b_{j}^{\dagger}\Leftrightarrow%
\left[ \mathcal{N}_{b},b_{j}\right] =-b_{j}
\end{equation}%
\begin{equation}
\left( \mathcal{N}_{b}A-A\mathcal{N}_{b}\right) D_{f_{b}}=\mathcal{N}%
_{b}AD_{f_{b}}-A\mathcal{N}_{b}D_{f_{b}}=\mathcal{N}_{b}AD_{f_{b}}+AD_{f_{b}}%
\mathcal{N}_{b}
\end{equation}%
\begin{equation}
\limfunc{tr}\left\{ \left[ \mathcal{N}_{b},A\right] D_{f_{b}}\right\} =-%
\limfunc{tr}\left\{ \left[ \mathcal{N}_{b},D_{f_{b}}\right] A\right\}
\end{equation}

The same substitution gives 
\begin{equation}
\limfunc{tr}\left\{ b_{j_{1}}\cdots b_{j_{n}}D_{f_{b}}\right\} =0.
\end{equation}%
\begin{align}
\widehat{\exp\left( i\tsum \limits_{1\leq j\leq
r}\beta_{j}b_{j}^{\dagger}b_{j}\right) }\left( D_{f_{b}}\right) & =\tprod
\limits_{1\leq j\leq r}e^{i\beta_{j}b_{j}^{\dagger}b_{j}}D_{f_{b}}\tprod
\limits_{1\leq k\leq r}e^{-i\beta_{k}b_{k}^{\dagger}b_{k}} \\
& \Updownarrow \\
\tprod \limits_{1\leq j\leq r}e^{i\beta_{j}b_{j}^{\dagger}b_{j}}D_{f_{b}} &
=D_{f_{b}}\tprod \limits_{1\leq j\leq
r}e^{i\beta_{j}b_{j}^{\dagger}b_{j}}\equiv\left[ \tprod \limits_{1\leq j\leq
r}e^{i\beta_{j}b_{j}^{\dagger}b_{j}},D_{f_{b}}\right] =0.
\end{align}

\item Pure states in $\mathcal{A}$ are defined as extreme points of the
convex set $\mathcal{S}\left( \mathcal{A}\right) ,$ normal states are
defined by topological and/or algebraic conditions. Extremity does not
translte to idempotency wrt the normalized trace $\limfunc{tr}.$
\end{enumerate}

\subsubsection{Bases for $\mathcal{F}$}

Let $r=1$ the dimension of $\mathcal{F}_{2}=2=2r$. The underlying Fock space
has dimension 
\begin{equation}
2^{1}=2
\end{equation}
and the number of operator basis vectors is 
\begin{equation}
2^{2}=4,
\end{equation}
however the number of linearly independent normal form operators 
\begin{equation}
a_{1}^{\dagger},a_{1},a_{1}^{\dagger}a_{1}
\end{equation}
\emph{is only three}. Thus we must include one non normal form operator, $%
a_{1}a_{1}^{\dagger},$ in the basis or, more conveinently, the identity
operator $I_{\mathcal{A}}.$

$\lceil I_{\mathcal{A}}$ \emph{cannot be expanded} in terms of normally
ordered basis elements as 
\begin{equation}
I_{\mathcal{A}}\left\vert \phi\right\rangle =\left\vert \phi\right\rangle
\neq\left( \alpha_{1}a_{1}^{\dagger}+\overline{\alpha}_{1}a_{1}+\alpha
_{12}a_{1}^{\dagger}a_{1}\right) \left\vert \phi\right\rangle =\alpha
_{1}\left\vert \varphi_{1}\right\rangle .
\end{equation}
$\rfloor$

Let $r=2$ the the dimension of $\mathcal{F}_{2}=4=2r$. The underlying Fock
space has dimension 
\begin{equation}
2^{2}=4
\end{equation}
and the number of operator basis vectors is 
\begin{equation}
2^{4}=16,
\end{equation}
however the number of linearly independent normal form operators 
\begin{align}
& a_{1}^{\dagger},a_{2}^{\dagger},a_{1},a_{2}, \\
&
a_{1}^{\dagger}a_{1},a_{1}^{\dagger}a_{2},a_{2}^{\dagger}a_{1},a_{2}^{%
\dagger}a_{2}, \\
& a_{1}^{\dagger}a_{2}^{\dagger},a_{1}a_{2}, \\
&
a_{1}^{\dagger}a_{2}^{\dagger}a_{1},a_{1}^{\dagger}a_{2}^{%
\dagger}a_{2},a_{1}^{\dagger}a_{1}a_{2},a_{2}^{\dagger}a_{1}a_{2}, \\
& a_{1}^{\dagger}a_{2}^{\dagger}a_{2}a_{1},
\end{align}
\emph{is fifteen} and again the identity operator 
\begin{equation}
I_{\mathcal{A}}
\end{equation}
must be adjoined to obtain a full basis, which has cardenality 
\begin{equation}
2^{2r}=2^{2\times2}=2^{4}=16.
\end{equation}
Any operator can be expanded terms of the linearly independent normally
ordered basis as 
\begin{align}
A & =I_{\mathcal{A}}+a_{1}a_{1}^{\dagger}+a_{2}a_{2}^{\dagger}+\beta
_{1}a_{1}+\beta_{2}a_{2}+a_{11}a_{1}^{\dagger}a_{1}+a_{12}a_{1}^{%
\dagger}a_{2}+a_{21}a_{2}^{\dagger}a_{1}+a_{22}a_{2}^{%
\dagger}a_{2}+a_{12}a_{1}^{\dagger}a_{2}^{\dagger} \\
&
+\beta_{12}a_{1}a_{2}+a_{121}a_{1}^{\dagger}a_{2}^{%
\dagger}a_{1}+a_{122}a_{1}^{\dagger}a_{2}^{\dagger}a_{2}+a_{112}a_{1}^{%
\dagger}a_{1}a_{2}+a_{212}a_{2}^{\dagger}a_{1}a_{2}+a_{1221}a_{1}^{%
\dagger}a_{2}^{\dagger }a_{2}a_{1}.
\end{align}

\subsubsection{Self Adjoint Bases}

Let 
\begin{equation}
\left\{ I_{\mathcal{A}},\left\{ \left. x_{j_{1}}\cdots x_{j_{n}}\right\vert
1\leq n\leq2r,1\leq j_{1}<\cdots<j_{n}\leq2r\right\} \right\}
\end{equation}
be a self adjoint basis for $\mathcal{A}$ then the asociated metric matrix
for $\Phi_{f}\left( \mathcal{A}\right) $ is given by 
\begin{gather}
\left\{ \left. \left\langle \left. \Phi_{f}\left( x_{j_{1}}\cdots
x_{j_{n}}\right) \right\vert \Phi_{f}\left( x_{k_{1}}\cdots x_{k_{m}}\right)
\right\rangle =f\left( \left( x_{j_{1}}\cdots x_{j_{n}}\right)
^{t}x_{k_{1}}\cdots x_{k_{m}}\right) \right\vert \right. \\
\left. 1\leq j_{1}<\cdots<j_{n}\leq2r,1\leq k_{1}<\cdots<k_{m}\leq2r,1\leq
m,n\leq2r\right\} \\
\left\{ \left. \left\langle \left. \Phi_{f}\left( x_{j_{1}}\cdots
x_{j_{n}}\right) \right\vert \Phi_{f}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}}\right)
\right\rangle =f\left( x_{j_{1}}\cdots x_{j_{n}}\right) \right\vert 1\leq
j_{1}<\cdots<j_{n}\leq2r,1\leq n\leq2r\right\} \\
\left\langle \left. \Phi_{f}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}}\right) \right\vert
\Phi_{f}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}}\right) \right\rangle =f\left( I_{\mathcal{A}%
}\right) .
\end{gather}
If $\Phi_{f}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}}\right) $ is orthogonal to all $\left\{
\Phi_{f}\left( x_{j_{1}}\cdots x_{j_{n}}\right) \right\} $ then 
\begin{equation}
f\left( x_{j_{1}}\cdots x_{j_{n}}\right) =0\quad\forall x_{j_{1}}\cdots
x_{j_{n}},1\leq n\leq2r
\end{equation}
i.e. $f$ is zero accept on $\mathcal{F}_{0}.$

\subsubsection{Non Self Adjoint Bases}

Let 
\begin{equation}
\begin{array}{c}
\left\{ \left\{ \left. a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger}\cdots a_{j_{n}}^{\dagger
}a_{k_{m}}\cdots a_{k_{1}}\right\vert 1\leq j_{1}<\cdots<j_{n}\leq r,1\leq
k_{1}<\cdots<k_{m}\leq r,1\leq m,n\leq r\right\} \right. \\ 
\left. \left\{ \left. a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger}\cdots a_{j_{n}}^{\dagger
}\right\vert 1\leq j_{1}<\cdots<j_{n}\leq r\right\} ,\left\{ \left.
a_{k_{m}}^{\left. {}\right. }\cdots a_{k_{1}}\right\vert 1\leq
k_{1}<\cdots<k_{m}\leq r\right\} ,I_{\mathcal{A}}\right\}%
\end{array}%
\end{equation}
be a non self adjoint basis for $\mathcal{A}$ then the associated metric
matrix for $\Phi_{f}\left( \mathcal{A}\right) $ is given by 
\begin{gather}
\left\{ \left. \left\langle \left. \Phi_{f}\left( a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger }\cdots
a_{j_{n}}^{\dagger}a_{k_{m}}\cdots a_{k_{1}}\right) \right\vert
\Phi_{f}\left( a_{p_{1}}^{\dagger}\cdots a_{p_{n^{\prime}}}^{\dagger
}a_{q_{m^{\prime}}}\cdots a_{q_{1}}\right) \right\rangle =f\left( \left(
a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger}\cdots a_{j_{n}}^{\dagger}a_{k_{m}}\cdots
a_{k_{1}}\right) ^{\dagger}a_{p_{1}}^{\dagger}\cdots
a_{p_{n^{\prime}}}^{\dagger}a_{q_{m^{\prime}}}\cdots a_{q_{1}}\right)
\right\vert \right. \\
\left. 1\leq j_{1}<\cdots<j_{n}\leq r,1\leq k_{1}<\cdots<k_{m}\leq r,1\leq
m,n\leq r,1\leq p_{1}<\cdots<p_{n^{\prime}}\leq r,1\leq q_{1}<\cdots
<q_{m^{\prime}}\leq r,1\leq m^{\prime},n^{\prime}\leq r\right\}
\end{gather}%
\begin{align}
& \left\{ \left. \left\langle \left. \Phi_{f}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}}\right)
\right\vert \Phi_{f}\left( a_{p_{1}}^{\dagger}\cdots
a_{p_{n^{\prime}}}^{\dagger}a_{q_{m^{\prime}}}\cdots a_{q_{1}}\right)
\right\rangle =f\left( a_{p_{1}}^{\dagger}\cdots a_{p_{n^{\prime}}}^{\dagger
}a_{q_{m^{\prime}}}\cdots a_{q_{1}}\right) \right\vert \right. \\
& \left. 1\leq p_{1}<\cdots<p_{n^{\prime}}\leq r,1\leq q_{1}<\cdots
<q_{m^{\prime}}\leq r,1\leq m^{\prime},n^{\prime}\leq r\right\}
\end{align}%
\begin{equation}
\left\langle \left. \Phi_{f}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}}\right) \right\vert
\Phi_{f}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}}\right) \right\rangle =f\left( I_{\mathcal{A}%
}\right)
\end{equation}

\emph{The identity }$I_{\mathcal{A}}$\emph{\ is linearly independent of the
normally ordered non self adjoint basis as} \emph{it cannot be expressed as
the stong limit of normally ordered bases elements i.e.}

\begin{equation}
I_{\mathcal{A}}\neq s-\lim\left\{ \sum_{1\leq n,m\leq r}\tsum _{\substack{ %
1\leq j_{1},\cdots,j_{n}\leq r \\ 1\leq k_{1},\cdots,k_{m}\leq r}}%
\alpha_{j_{1}\cdots j_{n}k_{1}\cdots k_{m}}a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger}\cdots
a_{j_{n}}^{\dagger}a_{k_{1}}\cdots a_{k_{m}}\right\} .
\end{equation}
This is shown by the $r=1$ and $r=2$ examples in the preceding and by
observing that linear combinations of operators in normal order must
annihilate the corresponding vacuum vector.

\paragraph{Quasi Free Vacuum States}

If $\Phi_{f}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}}\right) $ is orthogonal \emph{to all of
the normally ordered basis elements } $\left\{ \Phi_{f}\left(
a_{p_{1}}^{\dagger}\cdots a_{p_{n}}^{\dagger}a_{q_{m}}\cdots
a_{q_{1}}\right) ,\Phi_{f}\left( a_{p_{1}}^{\dagger}\cdots
a_{p_{n}}^{\dagger}\right) ,\Phi_{f}\left( a_{q_{m}}\cdots a_{q_{1}}\right)
\right\} $ then 
\begin{equation}
f\left( a_{p_{1}}^{\dagger}\cdots a_{p_{n}}^{\dagger}a_{q_{m}}\cdots
a_{q_{1}}\right) =f\left( a_{p_{1}}^{\dagger}\cdots a_{p_{n}}^{\dagger
}\right) =f\left( a_{q_{m}}\cdots a_{q_{1}}\right) =0
\end{equation}
and if the state is a normal state 
\begin{equation}
\limfunc{tr}\left( a_{p_{1}}^{\dagger}\cdots a_{p_{n}}^{\dagger
}a_{q_{m}}\cdots a_{q_{1}}D_{f}\right) =\limfunc{tr}\left(
a_{p_{1}}^{\dagger}\cdots a_{p_{n}}^{\dagger}D_{f}\right) =\limfunc{tr}%
\left( a_{q_{m}}\cdots a_{q_{1}}D_{f}\right) =0
\end{equation}
for the range of subscripts%
\begin{equation}
1\leq p_{1}<\cdots<p_{n}\leq r,1\leq q_{1}<\cdots<q_{m}\leq r,1\leq m,n\leq
r.
\end{equation}

\emph{Quasi free vacuum states, }$f_{b},$\emph{\ are gauge invariant for a
specific gauge, }$\widehat{\emph{\mathcal{N}_{b}}}$\emph{\ i.e. }%
\begin{equation}
f_{b}\left( e^{i\widehat{\emph{\mathcal{N}}_{b}}}\left( a\right) \right)
=f_{b}\left( a\right) \,\forall a\in\mathcal{A},
\end{equation}
which for normal states gives \emph{and are charaterized by }%
\begin{gather}
f_{b}\left( b_{p_{1}}^{\dagger}\cdots b_{p_{n}}^{\dagger}b_{q_{m}}\cdots
b_{q_{1}}\right) =f_{b}\left( b_{p_{1}}\cdots b_{p_{n}}\right) =f_{b}\left(
b_{p_{1}}^{\dagger}\cdots b_{p_{n}}^{\dagger}\right) =0, \\
f_{b}\left( b_{p_{n}}\cdots b_{p_{1}}b_{p_{1}}^{\dagger}\cdots
b_{p_{n}}^{\dagger}\right) =1\text{ } \\
1\leq p_{1}<\cdots<p_{n}\leq r,1\leq n\leq r,1\leq q_{1}<\cdots<q_{m}\leq
r,1\leq m\leq r,
\end{gather}
where $\left\{ b_{1}^{\dagger},\cdots,b_{r}^{\dagger}\right\} $ are
canonical transforms of $\left\{ a_{1}^{\dagger},\cdots,a_{r}^{\dagger
}\right\} $ and $f_{b}$ is the corresponding vacuum state. \emph{Thus} $%
\Phi_{f_{b}}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}}\right) $\emph{\ is orthogonal to} $%
\left\{ \Phi_{f_{b}}\left( b_{p_{1}}^{\dagger}\cdots b_{p_{n}}^{\dagger
}b_{q_{m}}\cdots b_{q_{1}}\right) ,\Phi_{f_{b}}\left( b_{p_{1}}^{\dagger
}\cdots b_{p_{n}}^{\dagger}\right) ,\Phi_{f_{b}}\left( b_{q_{m}}\cdots
b_{q_{1}}\right) ;1\leq p_{1}<\cdots<p_{n}\leq r,1\leq
q_{1}<\cdots<q_{m}\leq r,1\leq m,n\leq r\right\} $.

Vacuum states are left invariant by $U\left( r\right) $ through the spinor
(adjoint) representation i.e.%
\begin{equation}
f_{b}\left( \widehat{u}^{\dagger}\left( A\right) \right) =f_{b}\left(
A\right) \,\forall A\in\mathcal{A},\forall\widehat{u}\in U\left( r\right) ,
\end{equation}
where 
\begin{equation}
\widehat{u}^{\dagger}\left( A\right) =u^{\dagger}au
\end{equation}
and 
\begin{equation}
u=\exp\left\{ i\tsum \limits_{1\leq j,k\leq
r}\lambda_{bjk}b_{j}^{\dagger}b_{k}\right\} ,\lambda_{bjk}=\overline{\lambda 
}_{bkj}.
\end{equation}
In particular this implies gauge invariance as $U\left( 1\right) \subset
U\left( r\right) $. For normal states invariance to $\mathcal{R}_{b}\left(
U\left( r\right) \right) $ gives 
\begin{equation}
\left[ D_{f_{b}},b_{j}^{\dagger}b_{k}\right] =0
\end{equation}%
\begin{equation}
\limfunc{tr}\left( u^{\dagger}auD_{f_{b}}\right) =\limfunc{tr}\left(
aD_{f_{b}}\right) \,\forall a\in\mathcal{A},\forall\widehat{u}\in U\left(
r\right) ,
\end{equation}%
\begin{equation}
uD_{f_{b}}u^{\dagger}=D_{f_{b}},\forall\widehat{u}\in U\left( r\right)
\end{equation}
If $u\in SU\left( r\right) $ then $\func{Tr}\left\{ \mathbf{\lambda }%
_{b}\right\} =0.$

\begin{theorem}
$f_{b}$ is a vacuum state Iff%
\begin{equation}
f_{b}\left( \widehat{u}\left( A\right) \right) =f_{b}\left( uA\right)
\,\,\forall\widehat{u}\in\mathcal{R}_{b}\left( U\left( r\right) \right)
,\forall A\in\mathcal{A}
\end{equation}
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}
\begin{enumerate}
\item 
\begin{equation}
\widehat{\emph{\mathcal{N}}}_{b}\left( \left\vert \phi_{b}\right\rangle
\left\langle \phi_{b}\right\vert \right) =\left[ \emph{\mathcal{N}}%
_{b},\left\vert \phi_{b}\right\rangle \left\langle \phi_{b}\right\vert %
\right] =0,
\end{equation}
where 
\begin{equation}
\emph{\mathcal{N}}_{b}=\sum_{1\leq j\leq r}b_{j}^{\dagger}b_{j}
\end{equation}

\item 
\begin{gather}
\limfunc{tr}\left\{ b_{p_{1}}^{\dagger}\cdots b_{p_{n}}^{\dagger
}b_{q_{m}}\cdots b_{q_{1}}\left\vert \phi_{b}\right\rangle \left\langle
\phi_{b}\right\vert \right\} =\limfunc{tr}\left\{ b_{p_{1}}^{\dagger }\cdots
b_{p_{n}}^{\dagger}\left\vert \phi_{b}\right\rangle \left\langle
\phi_{b}\right\vert \right\} =\limfunc{tr}\left\{ b_{q_{m}}\cdots
b_{q_{1}}\left\vert \phi_{b}\right\rangle \left\langle \phi_{b}\right\vert
\right\} =0, \\
\limfunc{tr}\left\{ b_{p_{n}}\cdots b_{p_{1}}b_{p_{1}}^{\dagger}\cdots
b_{p_{n}}^{\dagger}\left\vert \phi_{b}\right\rangle \left\langle \phi
_{b}\right\vert \right\} =1\text{ } \\
1\leq p_{1}<\cdots<p_{n}\leq r,1\leq n\leq r;\,1\leq q_{1}<\cdots<q_{m}\leq
r,1\leq m\leq r
\end{gather}
\end{enumerate}
\end{theorem}

\begin{corollary}
\begin{align}
1 & \Rightarrow2 \\
3 & \Rightarrow2
\end{align}
\end{corollary}

$\lceil$Two different representations of $U\left( r\right) $ do not have the
same vacuum states, even though they are unitarily equivalent i.e. 
\begin{equation}
\exp\left\{ i\tsum \limits_{1\leq j,k\leq
r}\lambda_{jk}b_{j}^{\dagger}b_{k}\right\} \left\vert \phi_{b}\right\rangle
\left\langle \phi_{b}\right\vert \left( \exp\left\{ i\tsum \limits_{1\leq
j,k\leq r}\lambda_{bk}b_{j}^{\dagger}b_{k}\right\} \right)
^{\dagger}=\left\vert \phi_{b}\right\rangle \left\langle \phi_{b}\right\vert
\end{equation}%
\begin{equation}
\exp\left\{ i\tsum \limits_{1\leq j,k\leq
r}\lambda_{jk}a_{j}^{\dagger}a_{k}\right\} \left\vert \phi_{b}\right\rangle
\left\langle \phi_{b}\right\vert \left( \exp\left\{ i\tsum \limits_{1\leq
j,k\leq r}\lambda_{jk}a_{j}^{\dagger}a_{k}\right\} \right)
^{\dagger}\neq\left\vert \phi_{b}\right\rangle \left\langle
\phi_{b}\right\vert
\end{equation}
$\rfloor$

\subsubsection{$\Pi_{f}\left( \mathcal{A}\right) $ is a $c^{\ast}$-algebra
and $\Pi_{f}\left( \mathcal{A}\right) ^{\prime\prime}$ is the (enveloping)
vNA generated by $\Pi_{f}\left( \mathcal{A}\right) .$}

As $\Pi_{f}\left( \mathcal{A}\right) ^{\prime\prime}$ is a vNA it is a dual
Banach space i.e. the dual of $\Pi_{f}\left( \mathcal{A}\right) _{\ast
}^{\prime\prime},$which is the predual of $\Pi_{f}\left( \mathcal{A}\right)
^{\prime\prime}.$ \emph{Note that} preduals are not in general algebras. In
the case of a vNA $\Pi_{f}\left( \mathcal{A}\right) ^{\prime\prime}$ that
does not have any normal states we can have the following situation for $%
l\in\Pi_{f}\left( \mathcal{A}\right) ^{\prime\prime}$%
\begin{equation}
l\left( a\right) =\func{Tr}\left\{ aD_{l}\right\} ;D_{l}\in\Pi _{f}\left( 
\mathcal{A}\right) _{\ast}^{\prime\prime}\subset\mathcal{B}\left( \mathcal{H}%
_{f}\right) \text{ and}\notin\Pi_{f}\left( \mathcal{A}\right)
^{\prime\prime},
\end{equation}
it is even possible 
\begin{equation}
\Pi_{f}\left( \mathcal{A}\right) ^{\prime\prime}\cap\Pi_{f}\left( \mathcal{A}%
\right) _{\ast}^{\prime\prime}=\phi.
\end{equation}

We now concentrate on the representation generated by the state $\limfunc{tr}
$ with density operator $I_{\mathcal{A}}$ 
\begin{equation}
\limfunc{tr}\left( A\right) =\limfunc{tr}\left\{ AI_{\mathcal{A}}\right\}
\end{equation}
produced by the Hilbert Space $\mathcal{A}_{HS}\equiv\Phi_{\limfunc{tr}%
}\left( \mathcal{A}\right) $ i.e. 
\begin{align}
\Phi_{\limfunc{tr}} & :\mathcal{A\longrightarrow H}_{\limfunc{tr}}\equiv%
\mathcal{A}_{HS} \\
\ker\left\{ \Phi_{\limfunc{tr}}\right\} & =\left\{ \left. \xi\right\vert 
\limfunc{tr}\left( \xi^{\dagger}\xi\right) =0\right\} \Rightarrow\xi=0 \\
\left\Vert \Phi_{\limfunc{tr}}\left( A\right) \right\Vert _{\mathcal{H}_{%
\limfunc{tr}}} & =\left( \limfunc{tr}\left\{ A^{\dagger}A\right\} \right) ^{%
\frac{1}{2}} \\
\left\langle \left. \Phi_{\limfunc{tr}}\left( A\right) \right\vert \Phi_{%
\limfunc{tr}}\left( B\right) \right\rangle _{\mathcal{H}_{\limfunc{tr}}} & =%
\limfunc{tr}\left\{ A^{\dagger}B\right\} .
\end{align}
The representation 
\begin{equation}
\Pi_{\limfunc{tr}}\left( \mathcal{A}\right) \subseteq\mathcal{B}\left( 
\mathcal{A}_{HS}\right)
\end{equation}
and is a faithful representation. A representation is faithful if 
\begin{equation}
\Pi_{f}\left( A\right) =0\Rightarrow A=0,
\end{equation}
i.e. 
\begin{equation}
\ker\left\{ \Pi_{f}\right\} =0,
\end{equation}
thus a $c^{\ast}$-algebra isomorphism. If $f$ is faithful so is $\Pi_{f}$
(i.e. this is a sufficient condition), however $\Pi_{f}$ can be faithful
even if $f$ is not (so it is not a neccesary condition).

The metric matrix associated with a state $f\in\mathcal{A}^{d}$ is 
\begin{align}
f\left( \left( x_{j_{1}}\cdots x_{j_{p}}\right) ^{\dagger}\left(
x_{k_{1}}\cdots x_{k_{q}}\right) \right) & =\left\langle \left.
\Phi_{f}\left( x_{j_{1}}\cdots x_{j_{p}}\right) \right\vert \Phi_{f}\left(
x_{k_{1}}\cdots x_{k_{q}}\right) \right\rangle _{\mathcal{H}_{f}} \\
0 & \leq j_{1}<\cdots<j_{p}\leq2r,0\leq k_{1}<\cdots<k_{p}\leq2r;0\leq
p,q\leq2r.
\end{align}
In particular the basis $\left\{ \Phi_{\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}}\left( I_{%
\mathcal{A}}\right) ,\left\{ \left. \Phi_{\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}}\left(
x_{j_{1}}\cdots x_{j_{p}}\right) \right\vert 1\leq
j_{1}<\cdots<j_{p}\leq2r\right\} ;1\leq p\leq2r\right\} $ is orthogonal i.e. 
\begin{equation}
\left\langle \left. \Phi_{\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}}\left( x_{j_{1}}\cdots
x_{j_{p}}\right) \right\vert \Phi_{\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}}\left(
x_{k_{1}}\cdots x_{k_{q}}\right) \right\rangle _{\mathcal{H}\mathit{\limfunc{%
tr}}}=\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}\left\{ \left( x_{j_{1}}\cdots x_{j_{p}}\right)
^{\dagger}\left( x_{k_{1}}\cdots x_{k_{q}}\right) \right\} =g_{j_{1}\cdots
j_{p}}\delta_{j_{1}\cdots j_{p};k_{1}\cdots k_{q};}
\end{equation}
as the metric matrix is diagonal.

Any polynomial $\ast$-algebra (that has a non infinite trace) contains
positve elements that have unit trace i.e. density operators, so that if a
state $f\in\mathcal{A}^{d}$ is normal then 
\begin{equation}
f\left( A\right) =\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}\left\{ AD_{f}\right\} =\mathit{%
\limfunc{tr}}\left\{ D_{f}A\right\}
\end{equation}
and%
\begin{align}
f\left( x_{j_{1}}\cdots x_{j_{p}}\right) & =\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}\left\{
x_{j_{1}}\cdots x_{j_{p}}D_{f}\right\} \\
f\left( A\right) & \geq0\text{ if }A\geq0 \\
f\left( I_{\mathcal{A}}\right) & =\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}\left\{ I_{\mathcal{A}%
}D_{f}\right\} =\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}\left\{ D_{f}\right\} =1.
\end{align}
Note that 
\begin{equation}
\left\langle \left. \Phi_{\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}}\left( A\right) \right\vert
\Phi_{\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}}\left( A\right) \right\rangle _{\mathcal{H}%
\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}}=\left\Vert A\right\Vert _{\mathcal{H}\mathit{\limfunc{%
tr}}}^{2}=\left\Vert A\right\Vert _{HS}^{2}=\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}\left\{
A^{\dagger}A\right\}
\end{equation}
and%
\begin{equation}
\left\Vert I_{\mathcal{A}}\right\Vert _{HS}^{2}=1.
\end{equation}

\subsubsection{Connection Between Traces}

$\lceil f_{D_{f}}$ and $f_{I_{\mathcal{A}}}$ are normal states wrt $\mathit{%
\limfunc{tr}}$ Iff 
\begin{align}
f_{D_{f}}\left( A\right) & =\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}\left\{ AD_{f}\right\} \\
f_{I_{\mathcal{A}}}\left( A\right) & =\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}\left\{ A\right\}
\end{align}
and%
\begin{align}
f_{D_{f}}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}}\right) & =\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}\left\{ I_{%
\mathcal{A}}D_{f}\right\} =\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}\left\{ D_{f}\right\} =1 \\
f_{I_{\mathcal{A}}}\left( D_{f}\right) & =\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}\left\{ I_{%
\mathcal{A}}D_{f}\right\} =\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}\left\{ D_{f}\right\} =1.
\end{align}
Normality can be also be expressed in terms of ultra $\equiv\sigma$ weak
continuity of a linear functional $f$ and supremums of operator filters.

If $f_{D_{f}}$ and $f_{I_{\mathcal{A}}}$ are normal states one can consider $%
\Pi_{\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}}\left( D_{f}\right) \equiv\Pi _{I_{\mathcal{A}%
}}\left( D_{f}\right) ,\Pi_{D_{f}}\left( D_{f}\right) $, $\Pi_{D_{f}}\left(
I_{\mathcal{A}}\right) ,\left\vert \Phi_{D_{f}}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}}\right)
\right\rangle \left\langle \Phi_{D_{f}}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}}\right)
\right\vert $ and $\left\vert \Phi_{I_{\mathcal{A}}}\left( D_{f}\right)
\right\rangle \left\langle \Phi_{I_{\mathcal{A}}}\left( D_{f}\right)
\right\vert $ these last two operators $\in\Pi_{D_{f}}\left( \mathcal{A}%
\right) $ or $\Pi_{I_{\mathcal{A}}}\left( \mathcal{A}\right) $ if $%
\Pi_{D_{f}}\left( \mathcal{A}\right) $ or $\Pi _{I_{\mathcal{A}}}\left( 
\mathcal{A}\right) $ are a type $I$ representations. (Can $\Pi_{D_{f}}\left(
D_{f}\right) =0?).$ In particular 
\begin{align}
\Pi_{I_{\mathcal{A}}}\left( D_{f}\right) \Phi_{I_{\mathcal{A}}}\left( I_{%
\mathcal{A}}\right) & =\Phi_{I_{\mathcal{A}}}\left( D_{f}\right) \\
\Pi_{D_{f}}\left( D_{f}\right) \Phi_{D_{f}}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}}\right) &
=\Phi_{D_{f}}\left( D_{f}\right) .
\end{align}
The values of the states $f_{D_{f}}$ and $f_{I_{\mathcal{A}}}$ can be
expressed in terms of cyclic vector expectation values as 
\begin{align}
f_{D_{f}}\left( A\right) & =\left\langle \left. \Phi_{D_{f}}\left( I_{%
\mathcal{A}}\right) \right\vert \Pi_{D_{f}}\left( A\right)
\Phi_{D_{f}}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}}\right) \right\rangle _{\mathcal{H}%
_{D_{f}}}=\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}\left\{ AD_{f}\right\} \\
f_{I_{\mathcal{A}}}\left( A\right) & =\left\langle \left. \Phi _{I_{\mathcal{%
A}}}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}}\right) \right\vert \Pi_{D_{f}}\left( A\right)
\Phi_{I_{\mathcal{A}}}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}}\right) \right\rangle _{\mathcal{%
H}_{I_{\mathcal{A}}}}=\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}\left\{ A\right\} .
\end{align}
The GNS inner products are be expressed as 
\begin{align}
f_{D_{f}}\left( A^{\dagger}B\right) & =\left\langle \left.
\Phi_{D_{f}}\left( A\right) \right\vert \Phi_{D_{f}}\left( B\right)
\right\rangle _{\mathcal{H}_{D_{f}}}=\func{Tr}\left\{ \Pi_{D_{f}}\left(
A\right) ^{\dagger}\Pi_{D_{f}}\left( B\right) \left\vert \Phi_{D_{f}}\left(
I_{\mathcal{A}}\right) \right\rangle \left\langle \Phi_{D_{f}}\left( I_{%
\mathcal{A}}\right) \right\vert \right\} =\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}\left\{
A^{\dagger}BD_{f}\right\} \\
f_{I_{\mathcal{A}}}\left( A^{\dagger}B\right) & =\left\langle \left.
\Phi_{I_{\mathcal{A}}}\left( A\right) \right\vert \Phi_{I_{\mathcal{A}%
}}\left( B\right) \right\rangle _{\mathcal{H}_{I_{\mathcal{A}}}}=\func{Tr}%
\left\{ \Pi_{I_{\mathcal{A}}}\left( A\right) ^{\dagger }\Pi_{I_{\mathcal{A}%
}}\left( B\right) \left\vert \Phi_{I_{\mathcal{A}}}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}%
}\right) \right\rangle \left\langle \Phi _{I_{\mathcal{A}}}\left( I_{%
\mathcal{A}}\right) \right\vert \right\} =\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}\left\{
A^{\dagger}B\right\}
\end{align}
The connection between the two GNS\ inner products is given by 
\begin{align}
f_{I_{\mathcal{A}}}\left( A^{\dagger}D_{f}B\right) & =\fbox{$\left\langle
\left. \Phi_I_\mathcal{A}\left( A\right) \right\vert \Pi_I_\mathcal{A}\left(
D_f\right) \Phi_I_\mathcal{A}\left( B\right) \right\rangle _\mathcal{H}_I_%
\mathcal{A}$=$\left\langle \left. \Phi_D_f\left( B^\dagger\right)
\right\vert \Phi_D_f\left( A^\dagger\right) \right\rangle _\mathcal{H}_D_f$}=%
\overline{\left\langle \left. \Phi_{D_{f}}\left( A^{\dagger}\right)
\right\vert \Phi_{D_{f}}\left( B^{\dagger}\right) \right\rangle }_{\mathcal{H%
}_{D_{f}}} \\
& =\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}\left\{ A^{\dagger}D_{f}B\right\} =\mathit{\limfunc{%
tr}}\left\{ BA^{\dagger}D_{f}\right\} =\overline {\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}%
\left\{ D_{f}AB^{\dagger}\right\} }=\overline{\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}\left\{
AB^{\dagger}D_{f}\right\} }.
\end{align}%
\begin{align}
f_{D_{f}}\left( A\right) & =\left\langle \left. \Phi_{D_{f}}\left( I_{%
\mathcal{A}}\right) \right\vert \Pi_{\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}}\left( A\right)
\Phi_{D_{f}}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}}\right) \right\rangle _{\mathcal{H}%
_{D_{f}}}=\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}\left\{ AD_{f}\right\} \\
f_{D_{f}}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}}\right) & =\left\langle \left. \Phi
_{D_{f}}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}}\right) \right\vert \Pi _{\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}%
}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}}\right) \Phi_{D_{f}}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}}\right)
\right\rangle _{\mathcal{H}_{D_{f}}}=\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}\left\{
D_{f}\right\} =1 \\
f_{D_{f}}\left( D_{f}\right) & =\left\langle \left. \Phi_{D_{f}}\left( I_{%
\mathcal{A}}\right) \right\vert \Pi_{\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}}\left(
D_{f}\right) \Phi_{D_{f}}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}}\right) \right\rangle _{%
\mathcal{H}_{D_{f}}}=\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}\left\{ D_{f}^{2}\right\} .
\end{align}
The representation spaces $\mathcal{H}_{D_{f}}$ and $\mathcal{H}_{I_{%
\mathcal{A}}}$always have the $\func{Tr}$ functional defined giving

\begin{enumerate}
\item 
\begin{equation}
f_{I_{\mathcal{A}}}\left( D_{f}\right) =\func{Tr}\left\{ \Pi_{\mathit{%
\limfunc{tr}}}\left( D_{f}\right) \left\vert \Phi_{\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}%
}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}}\right) \right\rangle \left\langle \Phi_{\mathit{%
\limfunc{tr}}}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}}\right) \right\vert \right\}
=\left\langle \left. \Phi_{\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}%
}\right) \right\vert \Phi_{\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}}\left( D_{f}\right)
\right\rangle _{\mathcal{H}_{I_{\mathcal{A}}}}=\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}\left\{
D_{f}\right\} =\left\langle \left. \Phi_{\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}}\left( I_{%
\mathcal{A}}\right) \right\vert \Pi_{\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}}\left(
D_{f}\right) \Phi_{\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}}\right)
\right\rangle _{\mathcal{H}_{I_{\mathcal{A}}}}=1
\end{equation}

\item 
\begin{equation}
\func{Tr}\left\{ \Pi_{D_{f}}\left( D_{f}\right) \right\} =\left\langle
\left. \Phi_{D_{f}}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}}\right) \right\vert
\Pi_{D_{f}}\left( D_{f}\right) \Phi_{D_{f}}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}}\right)
\right\rangle _{\mathcal{H}_{D_{f}}}+\cdots=2^{2r}\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}%
\left\{ D_{f}^{2}\right\}
\end{equation}

\item 
\begin{align}
& \func{Tr}\left\{ \Pi_{D_{f}}\left( A\right) ^{\dagger}\Pi _{D_{f}}\left(
D_{f}\right) \right\} \\
& =\frac{\left\langle \left. \Phi_{D_{f}}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}}\right)
\right\vert \Pi_{D_{f}}\left( A\right) ^{\dagger}\Pi_{D_{f}}\left(
D_{f}\right) \Phi_{D_{f}}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}}\right) \right\rangle _{%
\mathcal{H}_{D_{f}}}}{\left\langle \left. \Phi_{D_{f}}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}%
}\right) \right\vert \Phi_{D_{f}}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}}\right) \right\rangle
_{\mathcal{H}_{D_{f}}}}+\sum_{1\leq p\leq2r}\sum_{1\leq
j_{1}<\cdots<j_{p}\leq2r}\frac{\left\langle \left. \Phi_{D_{f}}\left(
x_{j_{1}}\cdots x_{j_{p}}\right) \right\vert \Pi_{D_{f}}\left( A\right)
^{\dagger}\Pi_{D_{f}}\left( D_{f}\right) \Phi_{D_{f}}\left( x_{j_{1}}\cdots
x_{j_{p}}\right) \right\rangle _{\mathcal{H}_{D_{f}}}}{\left\langle \left.
\Phi_{D_{f}}\left( x_{j_{1}}\cdots x_{j_{p}}\right) \right\vert
\Phi_{D_{f}}\left( x_{j_{1}}\cdots x_{j_{p}}\right) \right\rangle _{\mathcal{%
H}_{D_{f}}}} \\
& =\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}\left\{ A^{\dagger}D_{f}^{2}\right\} +\sum_{1\leq
p\leq2r}\sum_{1\leq j_{1}<\cdots<j_{p}\leq2r}\frac {\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}%
\left\{ \left( x_{j_{1}}\cdots x_{j_{p}}\right)
^{\dagger}A^{\dagger}D_{f}^{2}\left( x_{j_{1}}\cdots x_{j_{p}}\right)
\right\} }{\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}\left\{ \left( x_{j_{1}}\cdots
x_{j_{p}}\right) ^{\dagger}\left( x_{j_{1}}\cdots x_{j_{p}}\right) \right\} }%
=2^{2r}\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}\left\{ A^{\dagger}D_{f}^{2}\right\}
\end{align}

\item 
\begin{align}
& \func{Tr}\left\{ \Pi_{I_{\mathcal{A}}}\left( A\right) ^{\dagger }\Pi_{%
\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}}\left( D_{f}\right) \right\} \\
& =\frac{\left\langle \left. \Phi_{D_{f}}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}}\right)
\right\vert \Pi_{I_{\mathcal{A}}}\left( A\right) ^{\dagger}\Pi _{I_{\mathcal{%
A}}}\left( D_{f}\right) \Phi_{D_{f}}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}}\right)
\right\rangle _{\mathcal{H}\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}}}{\left\langle \left.
\Phi_{D_{f}}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}}\right) \right\vert \Phi_{D_{f}}\left( I_{%
\mathcal{A}}\right) \right\rangle _{\mathcal{H}\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}}}%
+\sum_{1\leq p\leq2r}\sum_{1\leq j_{1}<\cdots<j_{p}\leq2r}\frac{\left\langle
\left. \Phi_{D_{f}}\left( x_{j_{1}}\cdots x_{j_{p}}\right) \right\vert
\Pi_{I_{\mathcal{A}}}\left( A\right) ^{\dagger}\Pi_{I_{\mathcal{A}}}\left(
D_{f}\right) \Phi_{D_{f}}\left( x_{j_{1}}\cdots x_{j_{p}}\right)
\right\rangle _{\mathcal{H}\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}}}{\left\langle \left.
\Phi_{D_{f}}\left( x_{j_{1}}\cdots x_{j_{p}}\right) \right\vert
\Phi_{D_{f}}\left( x_{j_{1}}\cdots x_{j_{p}}\right) \right\rangle _{\mathcal{%
H}\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}}} \\
& =\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}\left\{ A^{\dagger}D_{f}\right\} +\sum_{1\leq
p\leq2r}\sum_{1\leq j_{1}<\cdots<j_{p}\leq2r}\frac{\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}%
\left\{ \left( x_{j_{1}}\cdots x_{j_{p}}\right)
^{\dagger}A^{\dagger}D_{f}\left( x_{j_{1}}\cdots x_{j_{p}}\right) \right\} }{%
\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}\left\{ \left( x_{j_{1}}\cdots x_{j_{p}}\right)
^{\dagger}\left( x_{j_{1}}\cdots x_{j_{p}}\right) \right\} }=2^{2r}\mathit{%
\limfunc{tr}}\left\{ A^{\dagger}D_{f}\right\}
\end{align}

\item 
\begin{equation}
\func{Tr}\left\{ \Pi_{\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}}\left( D_{f}\right) \right\}
=2^{2r}\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}\left\{ D_{f}\right\} =2^{2r}.
\end{equation}
$\rfloor$
\end{enumerate}

The connection between $\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}$ on $\mathcal{A}^{c}$ and $%
\func{Tr}$ on $\mathcal{B}\left( \mathcal{H}_{\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}}\right)
\equiv\mathcal{B}\left( \Phi _{\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}}\left( \mathcal{A}%
^{c}\right) \right) $ is given by 
\begin{align}
\func{Tr}\left\{ \Pi_{\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}}\left( A\right) \right\} & =%
\frac{\left\langle \left. \Phi_{\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}%
}\right) \right\vert \Pi_{\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}}\left( A\right) \Phi_{%
\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}}\right) \right\rangle _{\mathcal{%
H}\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}}}{\left\langle \left. \Phi_{\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}%
}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}}\right) \right\vert \Phi_{\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}%
}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}}\right) \right\rangle _{\mathcal{H}\mathit{\limfunc{tr%
}}}}+\sum_{1\leq p\leq2r}\sum_{1\leq j_{1}<\cdots<j_{p}\leq2r}\frac {%
\left\langle \left. \Phi_{\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}}\left( x_{j_{1}}\cdots
x_{j_{p}}\right) \right\vert \Pi_{\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}}\left( A\right)
\Phi_{\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}}\left( x_{j_{1}}\cdots x_{j_{p}}\right)
\right\rangle _{\mathcal{H}\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}}}{\left\langle \left. \Phi_{%
\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}}\left( x_{j_{1}}\cdots x_{j_{p}}\right) \right\vert
\Phi_{\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}}\left( x_{j_{1}}\cdots x_{j_{p}}\right)
\right\rangle _{\mathcal{H}\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}}} \\
& =\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}\left\{ A\right\} +\sum_{1\leq p\leq2r}\sum_{1\leq
j_{1}<\cdots<j_{p}\leq2r}\frac{\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}\left\{ \left(
x_{j_{1}}\cdots x_{j_{p}}\right) ^{\dagger}A\left( x_{j_{1}}\cdots
x_{j_{p}}\right) \right\} }{\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}\left\{ \left(
x_{j_{1}}\cdots x_{j_{p}}\right) ^{\dagger}\left( x_{j_{1}}\cdots
x_{j_{p}}\right) \right\} } \\
& =\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}\left\{ A\right\} +\sum_{1\leq p\leq2r}\sum_{1\leq
j_{1}<\cdots<j_{p}\leq2r}\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}\left\{ A\right\} \\
& =2^{2r}\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}\left\{ A\right\} ,
\end{align}
where 
\begin{equation}
\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}\left\{ A\right\} =\frac{\left\langle \left. \Phi_{%
\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}}\right) \right\vert \Pi_{\mathit{%
\limfunc{tr}}}\left( A\right) \Phi _{\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}}\left( I_{%
\mathcal{A}}\right) \right\rangle _{\mathcal{H}_{\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}}}}{%
\left\langle \left. \Phi_{\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}%
}\right) \right\vert \Phi_{\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}%
}\right) \right\rangle _{\mathcal{H}_{\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}}}}=\func{Tr}%
\left\{ \Pi_{\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}}\left( A\right) \left\vert \Phi_{\mathit{%
\limfunc{tr}}}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}}\right) \right\rangle \left\langle \Phi_{%
\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}}\right) \right\vert \right\}
=\left\langle \left. \Phi_{\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}%
}\right) \right\vert \Pi_{\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}}\left( A\right) \Phi _{%
\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}}\right) \right\rangle _{\mathcal{%
H}_{\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}}}.
\end{equation}
$\lceil$%
\begin{equation}
\left\langle \left. \Phi_{\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}%
}\right) \right\vert \Phi_{\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}%
}\right) \right\rangle _{\mathcal{H}\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}}=1
\end{equation}
$\rfloor$

This shows in particular that 
\begin{equation}
\func{Tr}\left\{ \Pi_{\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}}\right)
\right\} =2^{2r}\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}\left\{ I_{\mathcal{A}}\right\} =2^{2r}
\end{equation}
and if $r=\infty$ 
\begin{equation}
\func{Tr}\left\{ \Pi_{\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}}\right)
\right\} =\func{Tr}\left\{ \Pi _{\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}%
}\right) \right\} =\infty.
\end{equation}

One can introduce a normalized trace in $\mathcal{B}\left( \mathcal{H}_{%
\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}}\right) $ by 
\begin{equation}
\left. \mathit{\limfunc{tr}}\right\vert _{\mathcal{H}_{\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}%
}}\left\{ \Pi_{\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}}\left( A\right) \right\} =\frac{1}{%
2^{2r}}\func{Tr}\left\{ I_{\mathcal{H}_{\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}}}\right\} =%
\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}\left\{ A\right\} .
\end{equation}
Thus 
\begin{align}
\func{Tr}\left\{ \Pi_{\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}}\right)
\right\} & =2^{2r}=2^{2r}\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}\left\{ I_{\mathcal{A}}\right\}
\\
\func{Tr}\left\{ \Pi_{\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}}\left( D_{f}\right) \right\} &
=2^{2r}=2^{2r}\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}\left\{ D_{f}\right\}
\end{align}
$\lceil$%
\begin{equation}
\Pi_{\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}}\right) =I_{\mathcal{H}_{%
\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}}}
\end{equation}
as can be seen from 
\begin{equation}
\Pi_{\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}}\right) \Phi_{\mathit{%
\limfunc{tr}}}\left( A\right) =\Phi _{\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}}\left( A\right)
\,\forall A\in\mathcal{A}
\end{equation}

$\rfloor$

and%
\begin{equation}
\func{Tr}\left\{ \Pi_{\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}}\left( A\right) ^{\dagger}\Pi_{%
\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}}\left( D_{f}\right) \right\} =\func{Tr}\left\{ \Pi_{%
\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}}\left( A^{\dagger}D_{f}\right) \right\} =2^{2r}\mathit{%
\limfunc{tr}}\left( A^{\dagger}D_{f}\right) =\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}\left(
A^{\dagger}2^{2r}D_{f}\right) .
\end{equation}
Thus a density operator, $\mathrm{D},$ belonging to $\mathcal{B}\left( \Phi_{%
\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}}\left( \mathcal{F}\right) \right) $ and having the
property 
\begin{equation}
\func{Tr}\left\{ \mathrm{D}\right\} =1
\end{equation}
is given by 
\begin{equation}
\mathrm{D}=\frac{1}{2^{2r}}\Pi_{\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}}\left( D_{f}\right) .
\end{equation}
$\lceil$%
\begin{equation}
\mathrm{D}^{2}=\frac{1}{2^{4r}}\Pi_{\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}}\left(
D_{f}^{2}\right)
\end{equation}%
\begin{align}
\mathrm{D}^{2} & =\mathrm{D\Rightarrow}\frac{1}{2^{4r}}\Pi _{\mathit{%
\limfunc{tr}}}\left( D_{f}^{2}\right) =\frac{1}{2^{2r}}\Pi_{\mathit{\limfunc{%
tr}}}\left( D_{f}\right) \\
2^{2r}\Pi_{\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}}\left( D_{f}^{2}\right) & =\Pi_{\mathit{%
\limfunc{tr}}}\left( D_{f}\right) \\
\Pi_{\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}}\left( D_{f}^{2}\right) & =\frac {1}{2^{2r}}\Pi_{%
\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}}\left( D_{f}\right)
\end{align}
$\rfloor$

$\lceil$If $\left\vert \Phi_{\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}%
}\right) \right\rangle \left\langle \Phi _{\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}}\left( I_{%
\mathcal{A}}\right) \right\vert $ $\in\Pi_{\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}}\left( 
\mathcal{A}\right) $ then 
\begin{equation*}
\mathrm{D}=\left\vert \Phi_{\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}%
}\right) \right\rangle \left\langle \Phi_{\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}}\left( I_{%
\mathcal{A}}\right) \right\vert =\Pi_{\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}}\left( \frac{1}{%
2^{2r}}I_{\mathcal{A}}\right) 
\end{equation*}
and 
\begin{align}
& \func{Tr}\left\{ \left\vert \Phi_{\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}}\left( I_{\mathcal{%
A}}\right) \right\rangle \left\langle \Phi _{\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}}\left( I_{%
\mathcal{A}}\right) \right\vert \right\} =\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}\left\{ I_{%
\mathcal{A}}\right\} =1 \\
& \mathit{\limfunc{tr}}\left\{ AD_{f}\right\} =\func{Tr}\left\{ A\left\vert
\Phi_{\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}}\right) \right\rangle
\left\langle \Phi_{\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}}\right)
\right\vert \right\} =\left\langle \left. \Phi_{\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}}\left(
I_{\mathcal{A}}\right) \right\vert \Pi_{\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}}\left(
A\right) \Phi _{\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}}\right)
\right\rangle _{\mathcal{H}\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}}=\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}%
\left\{ A\right\} .
\end{align}
$\rfloor$

$\lceil$ 
\begin{equation}
\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}\left( D_{f}\right) =1
\end{equation}
no matter the dimension of $\mathcal{A}$ if we define 
\begin{equation}
D_{f}=I_{\mathcal{A}}+\sum_{1\leq j\leq2r}D_{fj}
\end{equation}
so that for any finite dimensional subspace 
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{A}_{\mathcal{V}_{ry}}=\tbigwedge \left( \mathcal{V}_{ry}\right) ,
\end{equation}
where 
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{V}_{ry}=\limfunc{linspan}\left\{ y_{1},\cdots,y_{2r}\right\} ,
\end{equation}
one has 
\begin{align}
\func{Tr}\left\{ \Pi_{\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}}\left( D_{f}\right) \right\} & =%
\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}\left\{ D_{f}\right\} +\sum_{1\leq p\leq2r}\sum_{1\leq
j_{1}<\cdots<j_{p}\leq2r}\frac{\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}\left\{ \left(
y_{j_{1}}\cdots y_{j_{p}}\right) ^{\dagger}D_{f}\left( y_{j_{1}}\cdots
y_{j_{p}}\right) \right\} }{\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}\left\{ \left(
y_{j_{1}}\cdots y_{j_{p}}\right) ^{\dagger}\left( y_{j_{1}}\cdots
y_{j_{p}}\right) \right\} }=\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}\left\{ D_{f}\right\}
+\sum_{1\leq p\leq2r}\sum_{1\leq j_{1}<\cdots<j_{p}\leq2r}\frac{\mathit{%
\limfunc{tr}}\left\{ D_{f}y_{j_{1}}\cdots y_{j_{p}}y_{j_{p}}^{\dagger}\cdots
y_{j_{1}}^{\dagger }\right\} }{\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}\left\{ y_{j_{1}}\cdots
y_{j_{p}}y_{j_{p}}^{\dagger}\cdots y_{j_{1}}^{\dagger}\right\} } \\
& =\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}\left\{ D_{f}\right\} +\sum_{1\leq p\leq
2r}\sum_{1\leq j_{1}<\cdots<j_{p}\leq2r}\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}\left\{
D_{f}\right\} =2^{2r}.
\end{align}
Giving that 
\begin{equation}
\lim\limits_{r\rightarrow\infty}\left\{ 2^{-2r}\func{Tr}\left\{ \Pi_{\mathit{%
\limfunc{tr}}}\left( D_{f}\right) \right\} \right\} =1
\end{equation}
for any sequence of subspaces 
\begin{equation}
\left\{ \mathcal{V}_{ry}\right\} _{r=0,\cdots,\infty}.
\end{equation}
If $r=0$ then 
\begin{equation}
D_{f}=I_{\mathcal{A}}
\end{equation}
and 
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{V}_{0y}\cong\mathbb{C}\cong\left\{ \left. zI_{\mathcal{A}%
}\right\vert z\in\mathbb{C}\right\} .
\end{equation}

$\rfloor$

In particular the \emph{central faithful linear} functional $\widetilde{\tau}
$%
\begin{equation}
\widetilde{\tau}:\Pi_{\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}}\left( \mathcal{A}\right)
\rightarrow\mathbb{C}
\end{equation}
given by 
\begin{align}
\widetilde{\tau}\left( \Pi_{\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}}\left( A\right) \right) & =%
\func{Tr}\left\{ \Pi_{\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}}\left( A\right) \left\vert \Phi_{%
\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}}\right) \right\rangle
\left\langle \Phi _{\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}}\right)
\right\vert \right\} =\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}\left\{ A\right\} \\
\widetilde{\tau}\left( \Pi_{\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}}\left( A\right) \Pi_{%
\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}}\left( B\right) \right) & =\widetilde{\tau}\left( \Pi_{%
\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}}\left( B\right) \Pi_{\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}}\left(
A\right) \right) =\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}\left\{ AB\right\} =\mathit{\limfunc{%
tr}}\left\{ BA\right\} \\
& =\left\langle \left. \Phi_{\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}%
}\right) \right\vert \Pi_{\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}}\left( A\right) \Pi_{\mathit{%
\limfunc{tr}}}\left( B\right) \Phi _{\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}}\left( I_{%
\mathcal{A}}\right) \right\rangle _{\mathcal{H}\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}%
}=\left\langle \left. \Phi _{\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}%
}\right) \right\vert \Pi_{\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}}\left( B\right) \Pi _{%
\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}}\left( A\right) \Phi _{\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}}\left( I_{%
\mathcal{A}}\right) \right\rangle _{\mathcal{H}\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}} \\
\widetilde{\tau}\left( \Pi_{\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}%
}\right) \right) & =\func{Tr}\left\{ \Pi _{\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}}\left( I_{%
\mathcal{A}}\right) \left\vert \Phi_{\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}}\left( I_{%
\mathcal{A}}\right) \right\rangle \left\langle \Phi_{\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}%
}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}}\right) \right\vert \right\} =\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}%
\left\{ I_{\mathcal{A}}\right\}
\end{align}
is a normalized trace on $\Pi_{\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}}\left( \mathcal{A}%
\right) .$ If $r=\infty$ then $\Pi_{\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}}\left( \mathcal{A}%
^{c}\right) $ is $c^{\ast}$-isomorphic to $\mathcal{A}^{c}$ and $\left( \Pi_{%
\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}}\left( \mathcal{A}^{c}\right) \right) ^{\prime\prime}$
is a type II$_{1}$ Von Neumann algebra. Of course $\mathcal{B}\left( 
\mathcal{A}_{HS}\right) $ is a $\,$type I$_{r}$ Von Neumann algebra \emph{as
is} $\left( \Pi_{\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}}\left( \mathcal{A}^{c}\right) \right)
^{\prime\prime}$ if $r<\infty$. $\widetilde{\tau}$ can be extended to $%
\mathcal{B}\left( \mathcal{A}_{HS}\right) $, where it cannot be assumed that
it is normalized, finite nor central. See section \cite{CAR Types}

\subsubsection{Convex Set of States}

States $f\in$ $\mathcal{A}^{\ast}$ 
\begin{align}
& f:\mathcal{A\longrightarrow}\mathbb{R} \\
& f\left( I_{\mathcal{A}}\right) =1
\end{align}
form a convex set $\mathcal{S}_{\mathcal{A}}\left( \mathcal{A}\right) \subset%
\mathcal{A}^{\ast}$ under convex linear combination 
\begin{equation}
f=\tsum \limits_{1\leq p\leq q}\alpha_{p}f_{p},
\end{equation}
where 
\begin{align}
& \tsum \limits_{1\leq p\leq q}\alpha_{p}=1 \\
& 0\leq\alpha_{p}\leq1;1\leq p\leq q \\
f & _{p}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}}\right) =1;1\leq p\leq q.
\end{align}
A state $f$ is an \emph{extreme point} of $\mathcal{S}_{c}\left( \mathcal{A}%
\right) $ if 
\begin{equation}
f=\tsum \limits_{1\leq p\leq
q}\alpha_{p}f_{p}\Rightarrow\alpha_{j}=1,\alpha_{k}=1;\alpha_{j}=0,1\leq
j\neq k\leq q.
\end{equation}
If $f$ is a normal state 
\begin{equation}
f\left( A\right) =\tau\left( AD_{f}\right)
\end{equation}
and normal states form a convex set $\mathcal{S}\left( \mathcal{A}\right)
\subset\mathcal{A}$ so that 
\begin{align}
& \tau\left( D_{f}\right) =1 \\
& D_{f}\geq0\Leftrightarrow D_{f}=Q_{f}Q_{f}^{\dagger} \\
& D_{f}=\tsum \limits_{1\leq p\leq q}\alpha_{p}D_{f_{p}};D_{f_{p}}\in%
\mathcal{S}\left( \mathcal{A}\right) ,1\leq p\leq q.
\end{align}
As density operators are postive operators we can write 
\begin{equation}
Q_{f}Q_{f}^{\dagger}=\tsum \limits_{1\leq p\leq
q}\alpha_{p}Q_{f_{p}}Q_{f_{p}}^{\dagger},
\end{equation}
where $\left\{ Q_{f},Q_{f_{p}}\right\} $ are normalized elements of $%
\mathcal{A}.$ A state $f$ is a \emph{normal} \emph{extreme point} of $%
\mathcal{S}\left( \mathcal{A}\right) $ if 
\begin{equation}
D_{f}=\tsum \limits_{1\leq p\leq
q}\alpha_{p}D_{f_{p}}\Rightarrow\alpha_{j}=1,\alpha_{k}=1;\alpha_{j}=0,1\leq
j\neq k\leq q,
\end{equation}
i.e. 
\begin{equation}
Q_{f}Q_{f}^{\dagger}=\tsum \limits_{1\leq p\leq
q}\alpha_{p}Q_{f_{p}}Q_{f_{p}}^{\dagger}\Rightarrow\alpha_{j}=1,\alpha
_{k}=1;\alpha_{j}=0,1\leq j\neq k\leq q.
\end{equation}
If $Q_{f_{p}}Q_{f_{p}}^{\dagger}$ is an orthogonal projector i.e. 
\begin{equation}
Q_{f_{p}}Q_{f_{p}}^{\dagger}Q_{f_{p}}Q_{f_{p}}^{%
\dagger}=Q_{f_{p}}Q_{f_{p}}^{\dagger}
\end{equation}
then $Q_{f_{p}}$ is a partial isometry.

\subsubsection{Identifications}

Do 
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{A}^{w}\,\mathcal{\cong\,\,}\left( \Pi_{\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}}\left( 
\mathcal{A}^{c}\right) \right) ^{\ast\ast}=\left( \Pi _{\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}%
}\left( \mathcal{A}^{c}\right) \right) ^{\prime\prime}
\end{equation}
as $w^{\ast}$-algebras? Yes, see \emph{Sherman--Takeda theorem} in the
preceeding.

\subsection{Symmetries and the GNS\ Construction}

\subsubsection{Unitary Symmetries}

Unitary symmetries are implemented in $\mathcal{A}$ by inner $\ast $%
-automorphisms \emph{that are unitary} wrt the $\limfunc{tr}$ inner product
in $\mathcal{A}_{HS}$ i.e. 
\begin{align}
\widehat{u}\left( A\right) & =uAu^{\dagger} \\
\left\langle \left. \widehat{u}\left( A\right) \right\vert \widehat{u}\left(
B\right) \right\rangle _{\mathcal{A}} & =\limfunc{tr}\left\{
uA^{\dagger}u^{\dagger}uBu^{\dagger}\right\} =\limfunc{tr}\left\{
uA^{\dagger}Bu^{\dagger}\right\} =\limfunc{tr}\left\{ A^{\dagger
}Bu^{\dagger}u\right\} =\limfunc{tr}\left\{ A^{\dagger}B\right\}
=\left\langle \left. A\right\vert B\right\rangle _{\mathcal{A}},
\end{align}
where 
\begin{equation}
uu^{\dagger}=u^{\dagger}u=I_{\mathcal{F}};u\in\mathcal{F}.
\end{equation}
$\lceil$One could also consider non unitary symmetries therefore not $\ast $%
-automorphisms 
\begin{equation}
\widehat{g}\left( A\right) =gAg^{-1}=A.
\end{equation}
$\rfloor$

If $A$ is invariant to $\widehat{u}$ i.e. 
\begin{equation}
\widehat{u}\left( A\right) =A
\end{equation}
then $A$ has the symmetry implemented by $\widehat{u}.$ The adjoint $%
\widehat{u}^{\dagger}$ of $\widehat{u}$ is given by 
\begin{equation}
\left\langle \left. \widehat{u}^{\dagger}\left( A\right) \right\vert
B\right\rangle _{\mathcal{A}}=\left\langle \left. A\right\vert \widehat{u}%
\left( B\right) \right\rangle _{\mathcal{A}}\forall B
\end{equation}
i.e. 
\begin{equation}
\limfunc{tr}\left\{ \widehat{u}^{\dagger}\left( A\right) B\right\} =\limfunc{%
tr}\left\{ AuBu^{\dagger}\right\} =\limfunc{tr}\left\{ u^{\dagger}AuB\right\}
\end{equation}
so that 
\begin{equation}
\widehat{u}^{\dagger}\left( A\right) =u^{\dagger}Au.
\end{equation}
As 
\begin{equation}
\widehat{u}^{-1}=\widehat{u}^{\dagger}
\end{equation}
$\lceil$%
\begin{equation}
\widehat{u}^{\dagger}\left( uAu^{\dagger}\right) =u^{\dagger}uAu^{\dagger
}u=A
\end{equation}
$\rfloor$

then 
\begin{equation}
\widehat{u}^{\dagger}\left( \widehat{u}\left( A\right) \right) =\widehat{u}%
\left( \widehat{u}^{\dagger}\left( A\right) \right) =A
\end{equation}
so if 
\begin{equation}
\widehat{u}\left( A\right) =A
\end{equation}
then 
\begin{equation}
\widehat{u}^{\dagger}\left( \widehat{u}\left( A\right) \right) =\widehat{u}%
^{\dagger}\left( A\right) =A,
\end{equation}
so if $\widehat{u}$ is a symmetry so is $\widehat{u}^{\dagger}.$

As $\widehat{u}$ is a unitary transformation it is generated by a self
adjoint operator $\widehat{h}$ i.e. 
\begin{align}
\widehat{u} & =\exp\left\{ i\func{adj}\left( h\right) \right\} \\
\func{adj}\left( h\right) \left( \cdot\right) & =\widehat{h}\left(
\cdot\right) =\left[ h,\cdot\right] \\
\widehat{h} & =\widehat{h}^{\dagger};h=h^{\dagger},
\end{align}
note that $h$ can belong to $\mathcal{F}_{\left( n\right) }$ for any $%
n\in\left\{ 0,\cdots,2r\right\} .$ The adjoint $\widehat{h}^{\dagger}$of $%
\widehat{h}$ is given by 
\begin{equation}
\left\langle \left. \widehat{h}^{\dagger}\left( A\right) \right\vert
B\right\rangle _{\mathcal{A}}=\left\langle \left. \overset{}{A}\right\vert 
\widehat{h}\left( B\right) \right\rangle _{\mathcal{A}}
\end{equation}
i.e. 
\begin{equation}
\limfunc{tr}\left\{ \left( \widehat{h}^{\dagger}\left( A\right) \right)
^{\dagger}B\right\} =\limfunc{tr}\left\{ A^{\dagger }\widehat{h}\left(
B\right) \right\} =\limfunc{tr}\left\{ A^{\dagger}\left[ h,B\right] \right\}
=\limfunc{tr}\left\{ A^{\dagger}hB-A^{\dagger}Bh\right\} =\limfunc{tr}%
\left\{ A^{\dagger }hB-hA^{\dagger}B\right\} =\limfunc{tr}\left\{ \left[
A^{\dagger },h\right] B\right\} ,
\end{equation}
so that the self adjoint condition 
\begin{align}
& \left( \widehat{h}^{\dagger}\left( A\right) \right) ^{\dagger}=\left[
A^{\dagger},h\right] \\
& \Rightarrow\widehat{h}^{\dagger}\left( A\right) =\left( \left[
A^{\dagger},h\right] \right) ^{\dagger}=h^{\dagger}A-Ah^{\dagger}=\left[ h,A%
\right] =\widehat{h}\left( A\right) .
\end{align}
One should note that as 
\begin{align}
\widehat{u}\left( A\right) & =uAu^{\dagger} \\
\widehat{u}^{\dagger}\left( A\right) & =u^{\dagger}Au \\
\left( \widehat{u}\left( A\right) \right) ^{\dagger} & =uA^{\dagger
}u^{\dagger}=\widehat{u}\left( A^{\dagger}\right) \\
\left( \widehat{u}\left( A^{\dagger}\right) \right) ^{\dagger} &
=uAu^{\dagger}=\widehat{u}\left( A\right)
\end{align}
one has 
\begin{equation}
\widehat{u}\left( A\right) ^{\dagger}=uA^{\dagger}u^{\dagger}=\widehat{u}%
\left( A^{\dagger}\right) \neq\widehat{u}^{\dagger}\left( A\right) \text{ in
general}
\end{equation}
but 
\begin{equation}
\widehat{h}\left( A\right) ^{\dagger}=-\widehat{h}\left( A^{\dagger }\right)
.
\end{equation}

\subsubsection{Projective Symmetries}

$A$ is \emph{projectively invariant} to $\widehat{u}$ if 
\begin{equation}
\widehat{u}\left( A\right) =\exp\left\{ i\func{adj}\left( h\right)
\lambda\right\} \left( A\right) =e^{i\lambda}A;\lambda \in\mathbb{R}.
\end{equation}
If $A$ is projectively invariant to $\widehat{u}$ then 
\begin{align}
\widehat{u}\left( A\right) \left( \widehat{u}\left( A\right) \right)
^{\dagger} & =AA^{\dagger} \\
\left( \widehat{u}\left( A\right) \right) ^{\dagger}\widehat{u}\left(
A\right) & =A^{\dagger}A
\end{align}
and $AA^{\dagger}$ and $A^{\dagger}A$ are invariant to $\widehat{u}$ i.e. 
\begin{align}
\widehat{u}\left( AA^{\dagger}\right) & =uAu^{\dagger}uA^{\dagger
}u^{\dagger}=\widehat{u}\left( A\right) \widehat{u}\left( A^{\dagger
}\right) =e^{i\lambda}Ae^{-i\lambda}A^{\dagger}=AA^{\dagger} \\
\widehat{u}\left( A^{\dagger}A\right) & =uA^{\dagger}u^{\dagger
}uAu^{\dagger}=\widehat{u}\left( A^{\dagger}\right) \widehat{u}\left(
A\right) =e^{-i\lambda}A^{\dagger}e^{i\lambda}A=A^{\dagger}A.
\end{align}
If 
\begin{equation}
Au=uA,\quad\text{i.e. }\left[ A,u\right] =0
\end{equation}
then 
\begin{equation}
\widehat{u}\left( A\right) =uAu^{\dagger}=A
\end{equation}
and 
\begin{equation}
\widehat{h}\left( A\right) =0,
\end{equation}
which is a special case of 
\begin{equation}
\widehat{h}\left( A\right) =\lambda A.
\end{equation}
Other special cases are given by 
\begin{equation}
\widehat{h}\left( A\right) =2n\pi iA;0\leq n\leq\infty,
\end{equation}
which give 
\begin{equation}
\widehat{u}\left( A\right) =uAu^{\dagger}=A.
\end{equation}

\subsubsection{Symmetry Adaptation}

A symmetry group, $G,$ is represented in $\mathcal{F}$ by 
\begin{equation}
\Pi:G\rightarrow\mathcal{B}\left( \mathcal{F}\right) ,
\end{equation}
where $\Pi$ can be outer or inner automorphisms, the inner automorphisms can
be classified into

\begin{itemize}
\item adjoint 
\begin{equation}
\Pi\left( g\right) \left( A\right) =\mathcal{R}\left( g\right) A\mathcal{R}%
\left( g\right) ^{-1}.
\end{equation}

\item adjoint and $\ast$-automorphisms therefore unitary%
\begin{equation}
\Pi\left( g\right) \left( A\right) =\mathcal{R}\left( g\right) A\mathcal{R}%
\left( g\right) ^{\dagger};\mathcal{R}\left( g\right) \mathcal{R}\left(
g\right) ^{\dagger}=I_{F}.
\end{equation}

\item twisted adjoint 
\begin{equation}
\Pi\left( g\right) \left( A\right) =\mathcal{R}\left( g\right) A\mathcal{R}%
\left( g\right) ^{\#};\mathcal{R}\left( g\right) ^{\#}=\left( \tsum
\limits_{0\leq j\leq2r}\mathcal{R}\left( g\right) \right) ^{\#}=\tsum
\limits_{0\leq j\leq2r}\left( \mathcal{R}\left( g\right) \right)
_{j}^{\#}=\tsum \limits_{0\leq j\leq2r}\left( -1\right) ^{j}\left( \mathcal{R%
}\left( g\right) \right) _{j}.
\end{equation}
\end{itemize}

$\mathcal{F}$ can be decomposed into a direct sum of subspaces invariant to
the action of $O\left( 2r,\mathbb{C}\right) ,O\left( 2r,\mathbb{R}\right)
,\ldots,etc.$ In particular to subgroups of these groups. E.g. $SO\left( 3,%
\mathbb{R}\right) $ and $SU\left( 2\right) .$ These groups act in $\mathcal{F%
}_{1}$ thus their action on $\mathcal{F}$ is induced by 
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{F}=\tbigwedge \left( \mathcal{F}_{1}\right) =\tbigwedge \left(
\tbigoplus \limits_{1\leq j\leq\nu}\mathcal{F}_{1j}\right) =\tbigwedge
\left( \mathcal{F}_{11}\right) \otimes\cdots\otimes\tbigwedge \left( 
\mathcal{F}_{1\nu}\right) ,
\end{equation}
where $\left\{ \left. \mathcal{F}_{1j}\right\vert 1\leq j\leq\nu\right\} $
are invariant subspaces, which are sums of irreducible subspaces, of the
relevant groups.

[Could invariant subspaces define particles/regions/fields that lead to
multiparticle/multifield/multiparameter description?.]

$\lceil O\left( 2r,\mathbb{C}\right) $ and $O\left( 2r,\mathbb{R}\right) $
act irreducibly on $\left\{ \left. \mathcal{F}_{n}\right\vert 0\leq
n\leq2r\right\} $ in a unitary fashion i.e. 
\begin{equation}
\Pi\left( O\left( 2r,\mathbb{C}\right) \right) \left( \mathcal{F}_{n}\right)
=\mathcal{F}_{n},
\end{equation}
while $\Pi\left( U\left( r,\mathbb{R}\right) \right) $ is reducible 
\begin{align}
& \Pi\left( U\left( r,\mathbb{R}\right) \right) \left( \mathcal{F}_{n}\oplus
i\mathcal{F}_{n}\right) =\left( \mathcal{F}_{n}\oplus i\mathcal{F}%
_{n}\right) =\Pi\left( U\left( r,\mathbb{R}\right) \right) \left( \mathcal{F}%
_{n0}\right) =\mathcal{F}_{n0} \\
& \Pi\left( U\left( r,\mathbb{R}\right) \right) \left( \mathcal{F}%
_{n}\ominus i\mathcal{F}_{n}\right) =\left( \mathcal{F}_{n}\ominus i\mathcal{%
F}_{n}\right) =\Pi\left( U\left( r,\mathbb{R}\right) \right) \left( \mathcal{%
F}_{0n}\right) =\mathcal{F}_{0n} \\
& \mathcal{F}_{n}=\left( \mathcal{F}_{n}\oplus i\mathcal{F}_{n}\right)
\oplus\left( \mathcal{F}_{n}\ominus i\mathcal{F}_{n}\right) =\mathcal{F}%
_{n0}\oplus\mathcal{F}_{0n}\equiv\mathcal{F}_{n0}\oplus\overline {\mathcal{F}%
_{n0}}\equiv\mathcal{F}_{n0}\oplus\mathcal{F}_{n0}^{\dagger}
\end{align}%
\begin{align}
\mathcal{F}_{10} & =\left\{ \left. x_{j}+ix_{j}\right\vert 1\leq j\leq
r\right\} \\
\mathcal{F}_{01} & =\left\{ \left. x_{j}-ix_{j}\right\vert 1\leq j\leq
r\right\}
\end{align}%
\begin{align}
\Pi\left( Gl\left( r,\mathbb{C}\right) \right) \left( \mathcal{F}_{n}\oplus i%
\mathcal{F}_{n}\right) & =\left( \mathcal{F}_{n}\oplus i\mathcal{F}%
_{n}\right) =\Pi\left( Gl\left( r,\mathbb{C}\right) \right) \left( \mathcal{F%
}_{n0}\right) =\mathcal{F}_{n0} \\
\Pi\left( Gl\left( r,\mathbb{C}\right) \right) \left( \mathcal{F}_{n}\ominus
i\mathcal{F}_{n}\right) & =\left( \mathcal{F}_{n}\ominus i\mathcal{F}%
_{n}\right) =\Pi\left( Gl\left( r,\mathbb{C}\right) \right) \left( \mathcal{F%
}_{0n}\right) =\mathcal{F}_{0n}
\end{align}%
\begin{equation}
S^{2},S_{0}
\end{equation}
$\mathcal{F}_{n}$ is real space under real linear combinations, and complex
under complex linear combinations%
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{F}_{n}=\mathcal{F}_{n}\otimes\mathbb{C}=\mathcal{F}_{n}\oplus i%
\mathcal{F}_{n}
\end{equation}%
\begin{equation}
V=\left( V\oplus\overline{V}\right) \oplus i\left( V\ominus\overline {V}%
\right)
\end{equation}
The set 
\begin{equation}
\left\{ v-\sigma_{J}\left( v\right) \right\} \neq V\oplus\sigma_{J}\left(
V\right)
\end{equation}

We can define a conjugation $\sigma_{b}$ by 
\begin{align}
\sigma_{b} & :\mathcal{F}_{1}\rightarrow\mathcal{F}_{1} \\
\sigma_{b}\left( b_{j}\right) & =b_{j}^{\dagger};\sigma_{b}\left(
b_{j}^{\dagger}\right) =b_{j};1\leq j\leq r
\end{align}

$\rfloor$

\subsubsection{Well Defined GNS Representations}

A transformation $T\in\mathcal{B}\left( \mathcal{A}\right) $ induces a
transformation belonging to $\mathcal{B}\left( \Phi_{f}\left( \mathcal{A}%
\right) \right) $ if 
\begin{equation}
T:\ker\Phi_{f}\rightarrow\ker\Phi_{f}
\end{equation}
i.e. it produces a \emph{well defined operator} $\Pi_{f}\left( T\right) .$

The GNS representation of $\widehat{u}$, $\Pi_{f}\left( \widehat{u}\right) ,$
is well defined if 
\begin{equation}
\widehat{u}:\ker\Phi_{f}\rightarrow\ker\Phi_{f}
\end{equation}
i.e. 
\begin{equation}
f\left( B^{\dagger}B\right) =f\left( uA^{\dagger}u^{\dagger}uAu^{\dagger
}\right) =f\left( uA^{\dagger}Au^{\dagger}\right) =0\Leftrightarrow f\left(
A^{\dagger}A\right) =0;A,B\in\ker\Phi_{f},
\end{equation}
where 
\begin{equation}
B=\widehat{u}\left( A\right) .
\end{equation}
Thus 
\begin{equation}
\limfunc{tr}\left\{ uA^{\dagger}Au^{\dagger}D_{f}\right\} =\limfunc{tr}%
\left\{ A^{\dagger}Au^{\dagger}D_{f}u\right\} =\limfunc{tr}\left\{
A^{\dagger}AD_{f}\right\} =0.
\end{equation}
Hence one can see that a \emph{sufficient condition }for $\Pi_{f}\left( 
\widehat{u}\right) $ and its infinitessimal generator $\Pi_{f}\left( 
\widehat{h}\right) $ to be well defined is that $\widehat{u}$ and $\widehat{u%
}^{\dagger}$ are symmetries of $D_{f}$ i.e. 
\begin{equation}
\widehat{u}\left( D_{f}\right)
^{\dagger}=u^{\dagger}D_{f}u=uD_{f}u^{\dagger}=D_{f},
\end{equation}
which is equivalent to 
\begin{equation}
\widehat{h}\left( D_{f}\right) =\left[ h,D_{f}\right] =0.
\end{equation}
\emph{Neccesary conditions }for well definedness of $\Pi_{f}\left( \widehat{u%
}\right) $ and $\Pi_{f}\left( \widehat{h}\right) $ are given by 
\begin{equation}
f\left( \left[ h,X\right] ^{\dagger}\left[ h,X\right] \right) =0\text{ if }%
f\left( X^{\dagger}X\right) =0
\end{equation}
and thus 
\begin{equation}
f\left( \left[ h,X\right] ^{\dagger}\right) =f\left( \left[ h,X\right]
\right) =0.
\end{equation}
The condition 
\begin{equation}
f\left( \left[ h,X\right] ^{\dagger}\left[ h,X\right] \right) =0\text{ }
\end{equation}
can be further developed to give%
\begin{equation}
f\left( \left[ h,X\right] ^{\dagger}\left[ h,X\right] \right) =\limfunc{tr}%
\left\{ \left[ h,X\right] ^{\dagger}\left[ h,X\right] Q_{f}Q_{f}^{\dagger}%
\right\} =\limfunc{tr}\left\{ Q_{f}^{\dagger }\left[ h,X\right] ^{\dagger}%
\left[ h,X\right] Q_{f}\right\} =\limfunc{tr}\left\{ \left( \left[ h,X\right]
Q_{f}\right) ^{\dagger}\left( \left[ h,X\right] Q_{f}\right) \right\} =0,
\end{equation}
which gives 
\begin{equation}
\left[ h,X\right] Q_{f}=0=hXQ_{f}-XhQ_{f}\Rightarrow hXQ_{f}=XhQ_{f}
\end{equation}
and thus 
\begin{equation}
\left[ h,X\right] Q_{f}Q_{f}^{\dagger}=\left[ h,X\right] D_{f}=0\quad\forall
X\in\ker\Phi_{f}.
\end{equation}
The condition 
\begin{equation}
f\left( \left[ h,X\right] \right) =0
\end{equation}
can be further developed to give

\begin{align}
f\left( \left[ h,X\right] \right) & =\limfunc{tr}\left\{ \left[ h,X\right]
D_{f}\right\} =\limfunc{tr}\left\{ \left( hX-Xh\right) D_{f}\right\} =%
\limfunc{tr}\left\{ \left( hXD_{f}-XhD_{f}\right) \right\} \\
& =\limfunc{tr}\left\{ \left( XD_{f}h-XhD_{f}\right) \right\} =-\limfunc{tr}%
\left\{ X\left[ h,D_{f}\right] \right\} =0\quad\forall X\in\ker\Phi_{f}.
\end{align}

\subsubsection{Time Evolution}

The time evolution can be described and characterized by 
\begin{align}
D_{Q}\left( t\right) & =\widehat{u}\left( t\right) \left( D\right) =\widehat{%
u}\left( t\right) \left( QQ^{\dagger}\right) =Q\left( t\right) Q\left(
t\right) ^{\dagger} \\
-i\frac{d}{dt}\left\{ Q\left( t\right) Q\left( t\right) ^{\dagger }\right\}
& =\left( -i\frac{d}{dt}Q\left( t\right) \right) Q\left( t\right)
^{\dagger}+Q\left( t\right) \left( -i\frac{d}{dt}Q\left( t\right)
^{\dagger}\right) \\
& =\left[ H,Q\left( t\right) \right] Q\left( t\right) ^{\dagger }+Q\left(
t\right) \left( \left[ H,Q\left( t\right) ^{\dagger}\right] \right) \\
& =\left[ H,Q\left( t\right) Q\left( t\right) ^{\dagger}\right] .
\end{align}

$D_{Q}\left( t\right) $ is a stationary state if%
\begin{equation}
-i\frac{d}{dt}\left\{ D_{Q}\left( t\right) \right\} =\left[ H,D_{Q}\left(
t\right) \right] =0\text{ i.e. }D_{Q}\left( t\right) =D_{Q}\quad\forall t.
\end{equation}
The operators $Q\left( t\right) $ and $Q\left( t\right) ^{\dagger}$ are
called \emph{amplitudes} of $D_{Q}\left( t\right) $ and if they are eigen
operators of $\widehat{H}$ i.e. 
\begin{gather}
\widehat{H}\left( Q\left( t\right) \right) =\left[ H,Q\left( t\right) \right]
=EQ\left( t\right) \\
\Updownarrow \\
\widehat{H}\left( Q^{\dagger}\left( t\right) \right) =\left[ H,Q\left(
t\right) ^{\dagger}\right] =-EQ\left( t\right) ^{\dagger}
\end{gather}
they are \emph{stationary operator amplitudes} of $\widehat{H}$ i.e. 
\begin{align}
\widehat{u}\left( t\right) \left( Q\right) & =e^{-iEt}Q \\
\widehat{u}\left( t\right) \left( Q^{\dagger}\right) & =e^{iEt}Q^{\dagger},
\end{align}
which implies that $D_{Q}$ and $D_{Q^{\dagger}}$ are stationary states of $%
\widehat{H}.$ As $Q$ and $Q^{\dagger}$ are eigen operators of the self
adjoint (super) operator $\widehat{H}$ associated with different eigen
values one has the orthogonality relationships 
\begin{align}
\limfunc{tr}\left\{ QQ\right\} & =\limfunc{tr}\left\{
Q^{\dagger}Q^{\dagger}\right\} =0 \\
\limfunc{tr}\left\{ Q\left[ H,Q\right] \right\} & =\limfunc{tr}\left\{
Q^{\dagger}\left[ H,Q^{\dagger}\right] \right\} =0 \\
& \Updownarrow \\
\limfunc{tr}\left\{ QHQ-QQH\right\} & =0\Longleftrightarrow \limfunc{tr}%
\left\{ QHQ\right\} =\limfunc{tr}\left\{ QQH\right\} \text{ (which is
trivial and always true)}.
\end{align}
A representation $\Pi_{f}$ is faithful if 
\begin{equation}
\Pi_{f}\left( A\right) =0\Longleftrightarrow A=0.
\end{equation}
If a time evolution is defined by 
\begin{equation}
\widehat{u}\left( t\right) =\exp\left\{ -i\widehat{H}t\right\}
\end{equation}
then $\Pi_{f}\left( \widehat{u}\right) $ is well defined if 
\begin{equation}
\left[ H,D_{f}\right] =0,
\end{equation}
i.e. $D_{f}$ is a stationary state of the Hamiltonian $\widehat{H},$ and its
action on $\Phi_{f}\left( \mathcal{A}\right) $ is given by 
\begin{align}
\Pi_{f}\left( \widehat{H}\right) \Phi_{f}\left( A\right) & =\Phi _{f}\left( %
\left[ H,A\right] \right) \\
\Pi_{f}\left( \widehat{u}\left( t\right) \right) \Phi_{f}\left( A\right) &
=\Phi_{f}\left( u\left( t\right) Au\left( t\right) ^{\dagger}\right)
\,\forall A\in\mathcal{A}.
\end{align}
In particular 
\begin{equation}
\Phi_{f}\left( \left[ H,D_{f}\right] \right) =0.
\end{equation}
The inner product in $\Phi_{f}\left( \mathcal{A}\right) $ is given by 
\begin{equation}
\left\langle \Phi_{f}\left( A\right) \left\vert \Phi_{f}\left( B\right)
\right. \right\rangle _{\mathcal{H}_{f}}=\limfunc{tr}\left\{
A^{\dagger}BD\right\} =\limfunc{tr}\left\{ A^{\dagger}BQQ^{\dagger }\right\}
,
\end{equation}
where 
\begin{equation}
D_{Q}=QQ^{\dagger}.
\end{equation}
Then 
\begin{align}
\Pi_{f}\left( \widehat{H}\right) \Phi_{f}\left( Q\right) & =\Phi _{f}\left( %
\left[ H,Q\right] \right) =E\Phi_{f}\left( Q\right) \\
\Pi_{f}\left( \widehat{u}\left( t\right) \right) \Phi_{f}\left( Q\right) &
=\Phi_{f}\left( u\left( t\right) Qu\left( t\right) ^{\dagger}\right)
=e^{-iEt}\Phi_{f}\left( Q\right) .
\end{align}
From the eigenvalue equations and the orthogonality of $Q^{\dagger}$ and $Q$
one has 
\begin{align}
\left\langle \Phi_{f}\left( Q\right) \left\vert \Pi_{f}\left( \widehat{H}%
\right) \Phi_{f}\left( Q\right) \right. \right\rangle _{Q} & =\limfunc{tr}%
\left\{ Q^{\dagger}\left[ H,Q\right] QQ^{\dagger }\right\} =E\limfunc{tr}%
\left\{ Q^{\dagger}QQQ^{\dagger}\right\} \\
\left\langle \Phi_{f}\left( Q^{\dagger}\right) \left\vert \Pi_{f}\left( 
\widehat{H}\right) \Phi_{f}\left( Q^{\dagger}\right) \right. \right\rangle
_{Q} & =\limfunc{tr}\left\{ Q\left[ H,Q^{\dagger}\right] QQ^{\dagger}\right%
\} =-E\limfunc{tr}\left\{ QQ^{\dagger}QQ^{\dagger }\right\}
\end{align}%
\begin{align}
\left\langle \Phi_{f}\left( Q\right) \left\vert \Pi_{f}\left( \widehat{H}%
\right) \Phi_{f}\left( Q\right) \right. \right\rangle _{Q^{\dagger}} & =%
\limfunc{tr}\left\{ Q^{\dagger}\left[ H,Q\right] Q^{\dagger}Q\right\} =E%
\limfunc{tr}\left\{ Q^{\dagger}QQ^{\dagger }Q\right\} \\
\left\langle \Phi_{f}\left( Q^{\dagger}\right) \left\vert \Pi_{f}\left( 
\widehat{H}\right) \Phi_{f}\left( Q^{\dagger}\right) \right. \right\rangle
_{Q^{\dagger}} & =\limfunc{tr}\left\{ Q\left[ H,Q^{\dagger}\right]
Q^{\dagger}Q\right\} =-E\limfunc{tr}\left\{ QQ^{\dagger}Q^{\dagger }Q\right\}
\end{align}
and%
\begin{align}
\left\langle \Phi_{f}\left( Q^{\dagger}\right) \left\vert \Pi_{f}\left( 
\widehat{H}\right) \Phi_{f}\left( Q\right) \right. \right\rangle _{Q} & =%
\limfunc{tr}\left\{ Q\left[ H,Q\right] QQ^{\dagger}\right\} =\limfunc{tr}%
\left\{ Q\left( HQ-QH\right) QQ^{\dagger}\right\} =E\limfunc{tr}\left\{
QQQQ^{\dagger}\right\} =0 \\
& \Rightarrow\limfunc{tr}\left\{ HQQQ^{\dagger}Q\right\} =\limfunc{tr}%
\left\{ HQQ^{\dagger}QQ\right\} \\
\left\langle \Phi_{f}\left( Q\right) \left\vert \Pi_{f}\left( \widehat{H}%
\right) \Phi_{f}\left( Q^{\dagger}\right) \right. \right\rangle _{Q} & =%
\limfunc{tr}\left\{ Q^{\dagger}\left[ H,Q^{\dagger}\right]
QQ^{\dagger}\right\} =\limfunc{tr}\left\{ Q^{\dagger}\left(
HQ^{\dagger}-Q^{\dagger}H\right) QQ^{\dagger}\right\} =-E\limfunc{tr}\left\{
Q^{\dagger}Q^{\dagger}QQ^{\dagger}\right\} =0 \\
& \Rightarrow\limfunc{tr}\left\{ HQ^{\dagger}QQ^{\dagger}Q^{\dagger
}\right\} =\limfunc{tr}\left\{ HQQ^{\dagger}Q^{\dagger}Q^{\dagger }\right\}
\end{align}%
\begin{align}
\left\langle \Phi_{f}\left( Q^{\dagger}\right) \left\vert \Pi_{f}\left( 
\widehat{H}\right) \Phi_{f}\left( Q\right) \right. \right\rangle
_{Q^{\dagger}} & =\limfunc{tr}\left\{ Q\left[ H,Q\right] Q^{\dagger}Q\right%
\} =\limfunc{tr}\left\{ Q\left( HQ-QH\right) Q^{\dagger}Q\right\} =E\limfunc{%
tr}\left\{ QQQ^{\dagger}Q\right\} =0 \\
& \Rightarrow\limfunc{tr}\left\{ HQQ^{\dagger}QQ\right\} =\limfunc{tr}%
\left\{ HQ^{\dagger}QQQ\right\} \\
\left\langle \Phi_{f}\left( Q\right) \left\vert \Pi_{f}\left( \widehat{H}%
\right) \Phi_{f}\left( Q^{\dagger}\right) \right. \right\rangle
_{Q^{\dagger}} & =\limfunc{tr}\left\{ Q^{\dagger}\left[ H,Q^{\dagger}\right]
Q^{\dagger}Q\right\} =\limfunc{tr}\left\{ Q^{\dagger}\left(
HQ^{\dagger}-Q^{\dagger}H\right) Q^{\dagger}Q\right\} =-E\limfunc{tr}\left\{
Q^{\dagger}Q^{\dagger}Q^{\dagger}Q\right\} =0 \\
& \Rightarrow\limfunc{tr}\left\{ HQ^{\dagger}Q^{\dagger}QQ^{\dagger
}\right\} =\limfunc{tr}\left\{ HQ^{\dagger}QQ^{\dagger}Q^{\dagger }\right\} .
\end{align}

$\lceil$%
\begin{align}
\left[ H,Q\left( t\right) \right] Q\left( t\right) ^{\dagger}+Q\left(
t\right) \left[ H,Q\left( t\right) ^{\dagger}\right] & =\left( HQ\left(
t\right) -Q\left( t\right) H\right) Q\left( t\right) ^{\dagger}+Q\left(
t\right) \left( HQ\left( t\right) ^{\dagger}-Q\left( t\right)
^{\dagger}H\right) \\
& =HQ\left( t\right) Q\left( t\right) ^{\dagger}-Q\left( t\right) HQ\left(
t\right) ^{\dagger}+Q\left( t\right) HQ\left( t\right) ^{\dagger}-Q\left(
t\right) Q\left( t\right) ^{\dagger}H \\
& =HQ\left( t\right) Q\left( t\right) ^{\dagger}-Q\left( t\right) Q\left(
t\right) ^{\dagger}H.
\end{align}
$\rfloor$

$\lceil$%
\begin{align}
\frac{d}{dt}\left( e^{At}Xe^{-At}e^{At}Ye^{-At}\right) & =\frac{d\left(
e^{At}Xe^{-At}\right) }{dt}e^{At}Ye^{-At}+e^{At}Xe^{-At}\frac{d\left(
e^{At}Ye^{-At}\right) }{dt} \\
& =\left[ A,X\left( t\right) \right] e^{At}Ye^{-At}+e^{At}Xe^{-At}\left[
A,Y\left( t\right) \right] \\
& =\left[ A,X\left( t\right) \right] Y\left( t\right) +X\left( t\right) %
\left[ A,Y\left( t\right) \right] \\
& =\left( AX\left( t\right) -X\left( t\right) A\right) Y\left( t\right)
+X\left( t\right) \left( AY\left( t\right) -Y\left( t\right) A\right) \\
& =AX\left( t\right) Y\left( t\right) -X\left( t\right) AY\left( t\right)
+X\left( t\right) AY\left( t\right) -X\left( t\right) Y\left( t\right) A \\
& =AX\left( t\right) Y\left( t\right) -X\left( t\right) Y\left( t\right) A=%
\frac{d}{dt}\left( e^{At}XYe^{-At}\right) .
\end{align}

$\rfloor$

Note that $D_{Q}$ and $D_{Q^{\dagger}}$ are eigen vectors of $\widehat{H}$
with eigen values zero.

\subsection{Gauge Group}

The number operator is given by 
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{N}=\sum_{1\leq j\leq r}n_{j}=\mathcal{N}_{0}+\mathcal{N}_{2}=%
\mathcal{N}=\frac{r}{2}I_{\mathcal{F}}+i\sum_{1\leq j\leq r}x_{j}x_{j+r}.
\end{equation}
leading to 
\begin{align}
\func{Tr}\left\{ \Pi_{\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}}\left( \mathcal{N}\right)
^{\dagger}\frac{1}{2^{2r}}\Pi_{\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}}\left( D_{f}\right)
\right\} & =\frac{1}{2^{2r}}\func{Tr}\left\{ \Pi_{\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}%
}\left( \mathcal{N}D_{f}\right) \right\} =\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}\left( 
\mathcal{N}D_{f}\right) =\left\langle \left\langle \mathcal{N}%
D_{f}\right\rangle \right\rangle _{0} \\
& =\frac{1}{2^{2r}}\func{Tr}\left\{ \Pi_{\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}}\left( \left( 
\frac{r}{2}I_{\mathcal{F}}+i\sum_{1\leq j\leq r}x_{j}x_{j+r}\right)
D_{f}\right) \right\} \\
& =\func{Tr}\left\{ \Pi_{\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}}\left( \left( \frac{r}{2^{2r}}%
I_{\mathcal{F}}+\frac{i}{2^{2r}}\sum_{1\leq j\leq r}x_{j}x_{j+r}\right)
D_{f}\right) \right\} .
\end{align}

The \emph{Gauge} group is defined as 
\begin{equation}
u\left( \theta\right) =e^{i\mathcal{N}\theta}=\exp\left\{ \left( \frac {r}{2}%
I_{\mathcal{F}}+i\sum_{1\leq j\leq r}x_{j}x_{j+r}\right) \theta \right\}
,\theta\in\mathbb{R}
\end{equation}
and its action on $\mathcal{F}$ is given by 
\begin{equation}
Adj\left( u\left( \theta\right) \right) \left( \cdot\right) =\widehat{%
u\left( \theta\right) }\left( \cdot\right) =\exp\left\{ adj\left( \mathcal{N}%
\right) \theta\right\} \left( \cdot\right) =\exp\left\{ \left[ N,\cdot\right]
\theta\right\} =\exp\left\{ \widehat{\mathcal{N}}\theta\right\} \left(
\cdot\right) ,
\end{equation}
i.e. 
\begin{equation}
\widehat{u\left( \theta\right) }\left( A\right) =e^{i\mathcal{N}\theta
}Ae^{-i\mathcal{N}\theta}=\exp\left\{ i\sum_{1\leq j\leq
r}x_{j}x_{j+r}\right\} A\exp\left\{ -i\sum_{1\leq j\leq
r}x_{j}x_{j+r}\right\} =\exp\left\{ i\left[ \mathcal{N},A\right]
\theta\right\} =\exp\left\{ i\left[ \mathcal{N}_{maj},A\right]
\theta\right\} ,
\end{equation}
so the generator of $\widehat{u\left( \theta\right) }$ is not unique and can
be in particular either $\widehat{\mathcal{N}}$ or $\widehat{\mathcal{N}}%
_{maj}.$

As 
\begin{equation}
\left[ x_{j}x_{k},x_{l}x_{m}\right] =0,\left\{ jk\right\} \neq\left\{
lm\right\} ,
\end{equation}
but 
\begin{equation}
\left[ x_{j}x_{k},x_{l}x_{m}\right] \neq0,\left\{ jk\right\} \cap\left\{
lm\right\} =\left\{ j\right\} \text{ or }\left\{ k\right\} ,
\end{equation}
thus in general 
\begin{equation}
\left[ \mathcal{N},A\right] =\left[ \mathcal{N}_{maj},A\right] \neq0
\end{equation}
and 
\begin{equation}
\widehat{u\left( \theta\right) }\left( A\right) \neq A.
\end{equation}
$\mathcal{F}_{2}$ decomposes into irreducible subspaces of $\widehat{%
\mathcal{N}}$, $\widehat{\mathcal{N}_{maj}}$ and $\widehat{u\left(
\theta\right) }$.

\subsubsection{Properties of the Number Operator}

1. One should observe%
\begin{equation}
\exp\left\{ 2\pi i\mathcal{N}\right\} =I_{\mathcal{F}},
\end{equation}
as 
\begin{align}
\exp\left\{ 2\pi i\mathcal{N}\right\} \left\vert \Psi\right\rangle &
=\exp\left\{ 2\pi i\mathcal{N}\right\} \sum_{0\leq N\leq\infty}\left\vert
\Psi_{N}\right\rangle =\sum_{0\leq n\leq\infty}e^{2\pi iN}\left\vert \Psi
_{n}\right\rangle =\sum_{0\leq N\leq\infty}\left\{ \cos\left( 2\pi N\right)
+i\sin\left( 2\pi N\right) \right\} \left\vert \Psi_{N}\right\rangle \\
& =\sum_{0\leq N\leq\infty}\left\{ \cos\left( 2\pi N\right) \right\}
\left\vert \Psi_{N}\right\rangle =\sum_{0\leq N\leq\infty}\left\vert \Psi
_{N}\right\rangle =\left\vert \Psi\right\rangle ,
\end{align}
while in general 
\begin{equation}
\exp\left\{ \mathcal{N}\theta\right\} \left\vert \Psi\right\rangle
\neq\left\vert \Psi\right\rangle .
\end{equation}

2. The projector onto a $N-$particle subspace is given by 
\begin{equation}
P_{N}=\tint \nolimits_{0}^{1}e^{2\pi it\left( \mathcal{N}-NI_{\mathcal{F}%
}\right) }dt.
\end{equation}

A complexification $\widehat{J_{a}}=\widehat{\mathcal{N}}_{a}$ is given by
the adjoint action on 
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{F}_{1}=\mathbf{a}^{\dagger}\oplus\mathbf{a=x}_{1}\oplus\mathbf{x}%
_{2}
\end{equation}
given by 
\begin{align}
\widehat{J_{a}}\left( \mathbf{a}^{\dagger}\right) & =\left[ J_{a},\mathbf{a}%
^{\dagger}\right] =\mathbf{a}^{\dagger} \\
\widehat{J_{a}}\left( \mathbf{a}\right) & =\left[ J_{a},\mathbf{a}\right] =-%
\mathbf{a}
\end{align}
and 
\begin{equation}
\exp\left\{ \theta\widehat{J_{a}}\right\} \left( \mathbf{a}^{\dagger
}\right) =e^{\theta}\mathbf{a}^{\dagger}.
\end{equation}

\section{Representations of CAR algebras \label{CAR Types}}

Let $\mathcal{H}$ be a seperable complex Hilbert space, with conb $\left\{
\left. \varphi_{j}\right\vert 1\leq j\leq\infty\right\} $ and $a^{\dagger}$a
linear map 
\begin{align}
& a^{\dagger}:\mathcal{H}\rightarrow\mathcal{A}\left( \mathcal{H}\right) \\
& a^{\dagger}\left( \mathcal{H}\right) \subset\mathcal{A}\left( \mathcal{H}%
\right) \\
& a^{\dagger}\left( f\right) =a\left( f\right) ^{\dagger}=\Gamma\left(
\left\vert f\right\rangle \left\langle \phi\right\vert \right) =\sum_{0\leq
N\leq\infty}\sum_{1\leq j_{1}<\cdots<j_{N}\leq\infty}\left\vert \varphi
_{j}\varphi_{j_{1}}\cdots\varphi_{j_{N}}\right\rangle \left\langle
\varphi_{j_{1}}\cdots\varphi_{j_{N}}\right\vert
\end{align}
satisfying 
\begin{align}
a\left( f\right) a\left( g\right) +a\left( g\right) a\left( f\right) &
=\left\{ a\left( f\right) ,a\left( g\right) \right\} =0 \\
a\left( f\right) a\left( g\right) ^{\dagger}+a\left( g\right) ^{\dagger
}a\left( f\right) & =\left\{ a\left( f\right) ,a\left( g\right)
^{\dagger}\right\} =\left\langle f\left\vert g\right. \right\rangle _{%
\mathcal{H}}I_{\mathcal{A}},
\end{align}
then $a^{\dagger}$ is called a representation of the CAR. If $\Pi\left( 
\mathcal{A}\left( \mathcal{H}\right) \right) =\mathcal{B}\left( \tbigwedge
\left( \mathcal{H}\right) \right) $ then it is called a Fock representation,
(\emph{states generating Fock representations satisfy the vacuum condition}%
). There are many unitary inequivalent Fock representations.

A state $\omega$ on $\mathcal{A}\left( \mathcal{H}\right) $ is determined by
the set of functions $\left\{ W_{n,m}\left( f_{1}\cdots f_{n};g_{1}\cdots
g_{m}\right) \right\} $ (\emph{Wightman functions}) 
\begin{equation}
\omega\left( a\left( g_{1}\right) ^{\dagger}\cdots a\left( g_{m}\right)
^{\dagger}a\left( f_{1}\right) \cdots a\left( f_{n}\right) \right)
=W_{n,m}^{\omega}\left( f_{1}\cdots f_{n};g_{1}\cdots g_{m}\right) .
\end{equation}
The state $\omega$ produces a representation, $\pi_{\omega}$ via the GNS
procedure.

Let $T\in\mathcal{B}_{0}\left( \mathcal{H}\right) $ $\left( \text{compact
operators}\right) $ with $0\leq T\leq I_{\mathcal{H}},$ so that 
\begin{equation}
T=\sum_{1\leq j\leq\infty}\lambda_{j}P_{j},0\leq\lambda_{j}\leq1,
\end{equation}
where $\left\{ P_{j}\right\} $ is a family of orthogonal projectors. Each
one of these projectors defines a subspace 
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{M}_{j}=P_{j}\mathcal{H},
\end{equation}
which can be used in the orthogonal decomposition 
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{H}=\dbigoplus \limits_{1\leq j\leq\infty}\mathcal{M}_{j}.
\end{equation}
The subspaces $\left\{ \mathcal{M}_{j}\right\} $ generate $\left\{
\tbigwedge \left( \mathcal{M}_{j}\right) \right\} $ and $\left\{ \mathcal{A}%
\left( \mathcal{M}_{j}\right) \right\} $ and it can be shown that 
\begin{equation}
\dbigwedge \left( \overset{}{\mathcal{H}}\right) =\dbigwedge \left(
\tbigoplus _{1\leq j\leq\infty}\mathcal{M}_{j}\right) =\dbigotimes
\limits_{1\leq j\leq\infty}\tbigwedge \left( \mathcal{M}_{j}\right) .
\end{equation}
Recall 
\begin{align}
\mathcal{A}_{even} & =\dbigoplus \limits_{0\leq j\leq\infty}\mathcal{A}_{2j}
\\
\mathcal{A}_{odd} & =\dbigoplus \limits_{0\leq j\leq\infty}\mathcal{A}_{2j+1}
\\
\mathcal{A} & =\mathcal{A}_{even}\oplus\mathcal{A}_{odd}.
\end{align}
One can show that if $\mathcal{M}_{j}\bot\mathcal{M}_{k}$ 
\begin{align}
\left[ x,y\right] & =0\text{ if }x\in\mathcal{A}\left( \mathcal{M}%
_{j}\right) \text{ and }y\in\mathcal{A}\left( \mathcal{M}_{k}\right) \\
x & \in\mathcal{A}_{even}\text{ \emph{or} }y\in\mathcal{A}_{even}
\end{align}
and 
\begin{align}
\left\{ x,y\right\} & =0\text{ if }x\in\mathcal{A}\left( \mathcal{M}%
_{j}\right) \text{ and }y\in\mathcal{A}\left( \mathcal{M}_{k}\right) \\
x & \in\mathcal{A}_{odd}\text{ \emph{and} }y\in\mathcal{A}_{odd}.
\end{align}
A state $\omega$ is a \emph{product state} relative to $\left\{ \left. 
\mathcal{M}_{j}\right\vert 1\leq j\leq\infty\right\} $ if 
\begin{equation}
\omega\left( xy\right) =\omega\left( x\right) \omega\left( y\right) \forall
x\in\mathcal{A}\left( \mathcal{M}_{j}\right) ,\forall y\in \mathcal{A}\left( 
\mathcal{M}_{k}\right) ;1\leq j\neq k\leq\infty.
\end{equation}
Define 
\begin{equation}
W_{n,m}^{T}\left( f_{1}\cdots f_{n};g_{1}\cdots g_{m}\right) =\det\left\{
\left\langle \left. Tf_{j}\right\vert g_{k}\right\rangle \right\} \delta_{nm}
\end{equation}
then $\left\{ W_{n,m}^{T}\right\} $ determines an even state, $\omega_{T},$
on $\mathcal{A}$ i.e.%
\begin{equation}
\omega_{T}\left( \mathcal{A}_{odd}\right) =0.
\end{equation}

If $T=0$ then 
\begin{equation}
\omega_{0}\left( a\left( g_{1}\right) ^{\dagger}\cdots a\left( g_{m}\right)
^{\dagger}a\left( f_{1}\right) \cdots a\left( f_{n}\right) \right)
=W_{n,m}^{0}\left( f_{1}\cdots f_{n};g_{1}\cdots g_{m}\right) =0,
\end{equation}
and the state is identical to the one given by the Fock vacuum vector in the
Fock representation, and gives rise to the Fock representation $\pi
_{\omega_{0}}.$ Different Fock representations come from different $\mathcal{%
H}^{\prime}s.$ These are generated by different real form decompositions of $%
\mathcal{H},$ some of which are unitarily inequivalent.

If $T=\frac{1}{2}I_{\mathcal{H}}$ then 
\begin{equation}
\omega_{\frac{1}{2}I_{\mathcal{H}}}\left( a\left( g_{1}\right) ^{\dagger
}\cdots a\left( g_{m}\right) ^{\dagger}a\left( f_{1}\right) \cdots a\left(
f_{n}\right) \right) =W_{n,m}^{\frac{1}{2}I_{\mathcal{H}}}\left( f_{1}\cdots
f_{n};g_{1}\cdots g_{m}\right) =\frac{1}{2^{n}}\det\left\{ \left\langle
\left. f_{j}\right\vert g_{k}\right\rangle \right\} \delta_{nm}
\end{equation}
$\tau=\omega_{\frac{1}{2}I_{\mathcal{H}}}$ is a tracial state on $\mathcal{A}
$ and the enveloping von Neumann algebra generated by $\pi_{\omega_{\frac{1}{%
2}I_{\mathcal{H}}}}$ is isomorphic to $\dbigotimes \left( M\left( 2;\mathbb{C%
}\right) \right) $ relative to the normalized trace $\tau$ and is a factor
of type II$_{1}$ (Takesaki).%
\begin{align}
\left\langle \left. \Phi_{\omega_{\frac{1}{2}I_{\mathcal{H}}}}\left( a\left(
f\right) \right) \right\vert \Phi_{\omega_{\frac{1}{2}I_{\mathcal{H}%
}}}\left( a\left( f\right) \right) \right\rangle _{\omega_{\frac{1}{2}I_{%
\mathcal{H}}}} & =\omega_{\frac{1}{2}I_{\mathcal{H}}}\left( a\left( f\right)
^{\dagger}a\left( f\right) \right) =\frac {1}{2}\left\langle \left.
f\right\vert f\right\rangle \\
& =\left\langle \left. \Phi_{\omega_{\frac{1}{2}I_{\mathcal{H}}}}\left(
a\left( f\right) \right) \right\vert \Pi_{\omega_{\frac{1}{2}I_{\mathcal{H}%
}}}\left( a\left( f\right) \right) \Phi_{\omega_{\frac{1}{2}I_{\mathcal{H}%
}}}\left( I\right) \right\rangle _{\omega_{\frac{1}{2}I_{\mathcal{H}}}} \\
& =\left\langle \left. \Pi_{\omega_{\frac{1}{2}I_{\mathcal{H}}}}\left(
a\left( f\right) \right) \Phi_{\omega_{\frac{1}{2}I_{\mathcal{H}}}}\left(
I\right) \right\vert \Pi_{\omega_{\frac{1}{2}I_{\mathcal{H}}}}\left( a\left(
f\right) \right) \Phi_{\omega_{\frac{1}{2}I_{\mathcal{H}}}}\left( I\right)
\right\rangle _{\omega_{\frac{1}{2}I_{\mathcal{H}}}} \\
& =\left\langle \left. \Phi_{\omega_{\frac{1}{2}I_{\mathcal{H}}}}\left(
I\right) \right\vert \Pi_{\omega_{\frac{1}{2}I_{\mathcal{H}}}}\left( a\left(
f\right) ^{\dagger}a\left( f\right) \right) \Phi_{\omega _{\frac{1}{2}I_{%
\mathcal{H}}}}\left( I\right) \right\rangle _{\omega _{\frac{1}{2}I_{%
\mathcal{H}}}}.
\end{align}

If $T=\mu I_{\mathcal{H}},$ $0<\mu<\frac{1}{2},$the state $\omega_{\mu I_{%
\mathcal{H}}}$ gives rise to a factor of Type III$_{\lambda}$, where 
\begin{equation}
\lambda=\frac{\mu}{\left( 1-\mu\right) }.
\end{equation}

The von Neumann algebra generated by the cyclic representations $\pi
_{\omega_{T}}$ are AFD (Almost Finite Dimension) factor for any $T.$

\section{Clifford Norms}

By construction and definition of the CAR/Clifford algebra 
\begin{align}
\limfunc{Trace}\left\{ A\right\} & =\left\langle A\right\rangle _{0}=\mathit{%
\limfunc{tr}}\left( A\right) I_{\mathcal{F}} \\
\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}\left( A\right) & \equiv\left\langle \left\langle
A\right\rangle \right\rangle _{0}\in\mathbb{C} \\
\limfunc{Trace}\left\{ I_{\mathcal{F}}\right\} & =\left\langle I_{\mathcal{F}%
}\right\rangle _{0}=I_{\mathcal{F}} \\
\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}\left( I_{\mathcal{F}}\right) & =1
\end{align}
and one can define 
\begin{align}
\left\langle \left. A\right\vert B\right\rangle _{\mathcal{F}} &
=\left\langle \left\langle A^{\dagger}B\right\rangle \right\rangle _{0} \\
\left( \left. A\right\vert B\right) _{\mathcal{F}} & =\left\langle
\left\langle A^{t}B\right\rangle \right\rangle _{0} \\
\left\Vert A\right\Vert _{\mathcal{HS}} & =\left( \left\langle \left.
A\right\vert A\right\rangle _{\mathcal{F}}\right) ^{\frac{1}{2}} \\
\left\Vert A\right\Vert _{\mathcal{TC}} & =\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}\left\{
\left( A^{\dagger}A\right) ^{\frac{1}{2}}\right\} .
\end{align}

On the Von Neumann closure one has 
\begin{align}
\limfunc{Trace} & :\mathcal{F\longrightarrow F} \\
\mathit{\limfunc{tr}} & :\mathcal{F\longrightarrow}\left[ -\infty,\infty%
\right] .
\end{align}

\emph{The trace is zero on} 
\begin{equation}
\dbigoplus \limits_{1\leq n\leq2r}\mathcal{F}_{n}
\end{equation}
as 
\begin{equation}
\left\langle \sum_{1\leq n\leq2r}A_{n}\right\rangle _{0}=0.
\end{equation}
The subspaces $\left\{ \mathcal{F}_{n}\right\} $ are orthogonal wrt $%
\left\langle \left. A\right\vert B\right\rangle _{\mathcal{F}}$ i.e. 
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{F}_{n}\bot\mathcal{F}_{m};0\leq n\neq m\leq2r,
\end{equation}
as the geometric product between two general elements $A^{\dagger}$ and $B$
is defined as 
\begin{equation}
A^{\dagger}B=\sum_{0\leq m,n\leq2r}A_{m}^{\dagger}B_{n},
\end{equation}
where 
\begin{equation}
A_{m}^{\dagger}B_{n}=\sum_{j=0}^{\min\left( m,n\right) }\left\langle
A_{m}^{\dagger}B_{n}\right\rangle _{\left\vert m-n\right\vert +2j},
\end{equation}
which can never have a zeroth component if $m\neq n$ giving that 
\begin{equation}
\left\langle A_{m}^{\dagger}B_{n}\right\rangle _{0}=0,\,m\neq n,
\end{equation}
leading to 
\begin{equation}
\left\langle A^{\dagger}B\right\rangle _{0}=\sum_{0\leq m\leq2r}\left\langle
A_{m}^{\dagger}B_{m}\right\rangle _{0},
\end{equation}
in particular (\emph{for normalized basis})%
\begin{equation}
\left\langle \left\langle A_{1}B_{1}\right\rangle \right\rangle
_{0}=\left\langle \left\langle \sum_{1\leq
j,k\leq2r}A_{1j}B_{1k}x_{j}x_{k}\right\rangle \right\rangle
_{0}=\left\langle \left\langle \sum_{1\leq
j\leq2r}A_{1j}B_{1j}x_{j}^{2}\right\rangle \right\rangle _{0}=\sum_{1\leq
j\leq2r}A_{1j}B_{1j}.
\end{equation}
Let 
\begin{align}
A_{p} & =a_{1}\wedge\cdots\wedge a_{p} \\
B_{p} & =b_{1}\wedge\cdots\wedge b_{p}
\end{align}
then 
\begin{align}
\left\langle \left. A_{p}\right\vert B_{p}\right\rangle & =\left( -1\right)
^{\frac{p\left( p-1\right) }{2}}\left\langle \left. a_{p}\wedge\cdots\wedge
a_{1}\right\vert b_{1}\wedge\cdots\wedge b_{p}\right\rangle =\left(
-1\right) \left\langle ^{\frac{p\left( p-1\right) }{2}}\left\langle \left(
a_{p}\wedge\cdots\wedge a_{1}\right) \left( b_{1}\wedge\cdots\wedge
b_{p}\right) \right\rangle \right\rangle _{0} \\
& =\left( -1\right) ^{\frac{p\left( p-1\right) }{2}}\sum_{\substack{ 1\leq
j_{1}<\cdots<j_{p}\leq2r \\ 1\leq k_{1}<\cdots<k_{p}\leq2r}}\left\langle
\left\langle a_{pj_{1}}\cdots a_{1j_{1}}x_{j_{p}}\wedge\cdots\wedge
x_{j_{1}}b_{pk_{p}}\cdots b_{1k_{1}}x_{k_{1}}\wedge\cdots\wedge
x_{k_{p}}\right\rangle \right\rangle _{0} \\
& =\left( -1\right) ^{\frac{p\left( p-1\right) }{2}}\sum_{\substack{ 1\leq
j_{1}<\cdots<j_{p}\leq2r \\ 1\leq k_{1}<\cdots<k_{p}\leq2r}}\left\vert 
\begin{array}{ccc}
\left( \left. a_{j_{1}}\right\vert b_{k_{1}}\right) & \cdots & \left( \left.
a_{j_{1}}\right\vert b_{k_{p}}\right) \\ 
\vdots & \vdots & \vdots \\ 
\left( \left. a_{j_{p}}\right\vert b_{k_{1}}\right) & \cdots & \left( \left.
a_{j_{p}}\right\vert b_{k_{p}}\right)%
\end{array}
\right\vert .
\end{align}
Note that 
\begin{align}
A_{m}^{\dagger} & =\sum_{1\leq j_{1}<\cdots<j_{m}\leq2r}\overline{A}%
_{mj_{1}\cdots j_{m}}x_{j_{m}}\wedge\cdots\wedge x_{j_{1}} \\
B_{m} & =\sum_{1\leq j_{1}<\cdots<j_{m}\leq2r}B_{mj_{1}\cdots
j_{m}}x_{j_{1}}\wedge\cdots\wedge x_{j_{m}}
\end{align}%
\begin{align}
\left\langle \left. A_{m}\right\vert B_{m}\right\rangle & =\sum _{\substack{ %
1\leq j_{1}<\cdots<j_{m}\leq2r \\ 1\leq k_{1}<\cdots<k_{m}\leq 2r}}\overline{%
A}_{mj_{1}\cdots j_{m}}B_{mk_{1}\cdots k_{m}}\left\langle \left\langle
\left( x_{j_{m}}\wedge\cdots\wedge x_{j_{1}}\right) \left(
x_{k_{1}}\wedge\cdots\wedge x_{k_{m}}\right) \right\rangle \right\rangle _{0}
\\
& =\sum_{1\leq j_{1}<\cdots<j_{m}\leq2r}\overline{A}_{mj_{1}\cdots
j_{m}}B_{mj_{1}\cdots j_{m}}
\end{align}%
\begin{equation}
\left\langle \left\langle A^{\dagger}B\right\rangle \right\rangle
_{0}=\sum_{0\leq m\leq2r}\left\langle \left\langle
A_{m}^{\dagger}B_{m}\right\rangle \right\rangle _{0}=\sum_{0\leq
m\leq2r}\sum_{1\leq j_{1}<\cdots<j_{m}\leq2r}\overline{A}_{mj_{1}\cdots
j_{m}}B_{mj_{1}\cdots j_{m}}.
\end{equation}

\subsubsection{Inner and Scalar Products}

The Clifford inner and scalar products can be expressed in terms of the
trace as 
\begin{align}
\left\langle \left. A\right\vert B\right\rangle _{\mathcal{F}} &
=\left\langle A^{\dagger}B\right\rangle _{0}=\limfunc{Trace}\left\{
A^{\dagger}B\right\} =\limfunc{tr}\left\{ A^{\dagger}B\right\} I_{\mathcal{F}%
}\in\mathcal{F}_{0} \\
\left( \left. A\right\vert B\right) _{\mathcal{F}} & =\left\langle
A^{t}B\right\rangle _{0}=\limfunc{Trace}\left\{ A^{t}B\right\} =\limfunc{tr}%
\left\{ A^{t}B\right\} I_{\mathcal{F}}\in\mathcal{F}_{0} \\
\left\langle \left. A\right\vert B\right\rangle & =\overline{\left\langle
\left. B\right\vert A\right\rangle }=\left\langle \left\langle A^{\dagger
}B\right\rangle \right\rangle _{0}=\limfunc{tr}\left\{ A^{\dagger }B\right\}
\in\mathbb{C} \\
\left( \left. A\right\vert B\right) & =\left( \left. B\right\vert A\right)
=\left\langle \left\langle A^{t}B\right\rangle \right\rangle _{0}=\limfunc{tr%
}\left\{ A^{t}B\right\} \in\mathbb{C}.
\end{align}
Other internal products in $\mathcal{F}$ can be defined in terms of these
preceeding products.

\subsection{States $f_{D}$}

States are positive normalized linear functionals defined on $\mathcal{F}$
and if the state is a \emph{normal }state it can be associated with a
density operator $D\in\mathcal{F}$ i.e. 
\begin{equation}
f_{D}\left( I_{\mathcal{F}}\right) =1=\mathit{\limfunc{tr}}\left( D\right)
=\left\langle \left\langle \left. I\right\vert D\right\rangle \right\rangle
_{0}.
\end{equation}
We can always define $D$ by 
\begin{equation}
D=\frac{QQ^{\dagger}}{\limfunc{tr}\left\{ QQ^{\dagger}\right\} }.
\end{equation}

\subsection{Differentials and Directional Derrivatives}

A function $f$ between Banach spaces $X$ and $Y$ 
\begin{equation}
f:U\subset X\longrightarrow Y
\end{equation}
is differentiable at $x\in U$ if $\exists$ a linear transformation $df\left(
x\right) \in\mathcal{B}\left( X,Y\right) ,$ the differential $df\left(
x\right) $ of $f$ at $x,$ such that 
\begin{align}
f\left( x+h\right) -f\left( x\right) -df\left( x\right) & =o\left( h\right)
\\
o\left( h\right) & =\left\Vert h\right\Vert \psi\left( h\right) \\
\psi & :X\longrightarrow Y \\
\lim\limits_{h\rightarrow0}\psi\left( h\right) & =0\text{ (Banach space
norms).}
\end{align}
The directional derrivative $\frac{\partial}{\partial h}f\left( x\right) $
of $f$ at $x$ in the direction $h$ is given by 
\begin{align}
\lim\limits_{t\rightarrow0}\left\{ \frac{f\left( x+th\right) -f\left(
x\right) }{t}\right\} & =\left. \frac{d}{dt}f\left( x+th\right) \right\vert
_{t=0}=df\left( x\right) \left( h\right) =\frac{\partial }{\partial h}%
f\left( x\right) =\sum_{1\leq j\leq2r}\frac{\partial f\left( x\right) }{%
\partial x_{j}}\mathit{h}_{j}=\sum_{1\leq j\leq2r}\mathit{h}_{j}\frac{%
\partial}{\partial x_{j}}f\left( x\right) \\
& \left( \text{If }\emph{df}\left( x\right) \text{ exists!}\right) \\
h & =\sum_{1\leq j\leq2r}\mathit{h}_{j}x_{j};\left\{ \left. \mathit{h}%
_{j}\right\vert 1\leq j\leq2r\right\} \text{ are scalars.}
\end{align}
The existance of $\left\{ \frac{\partial f\left( x\right) }{\partial h}%
\right\} $ for all $h$ does not imply that $df\left( x\right) $ exists.

The Dirac Operator, $\mathfrak{D,}$is defined as 
\begin{equation}
\mathfrak{D}=\sum_{1\leq j\leq2r}x_{j}\frac{\partial}{\partial x_{j}}%
,x_{j}\in\mathcal{F}_{1}
\end{equation}
where $\mathfrak{D}$ acts on functions $f$ 
\begin{equation}
f:\mathcal{F\longrightarrow F}.
\end{equation}

\subsection{Expectation Values}

The density and quartic Hamiltonian operators can be expanded in terms of
the generators as 
\begin{equation}
D=I_{\mathcal{F}}+\sum_{1\leq j\leq2^{2r}}D_{j}=I_{\mathcal{F}}+i\sum_{1\leq
j_{1}<j_{2}\leq2r}d_{2j_{1}j_{2}}x_{j_{1}}x_{j_{2}}+\sum_{1\leq
j_{1}<j_{2}<j_{3}<j_{4}%
\leq2r}d_{4j_{1}j_{2}j_{3}j_{4}}x_{j_{1}}x_{j_{2}}x_{j_{3}}x_{j_{4}}+\cdots
\end{equation}
and 
\begin{equation}
H=\eta_{0}I_{\mathcal{F}}+i\sum_{1\leq
j_{1}<j_{2}\leq2r}\eta_{2j_{1}j_{2}}x_{j_{1}}x_{j_{2}}+\sum_{1\leq
j_{1}<j_{2}<j_{3}<j_{4}\leq2r}\eta
_{4j_{1}j_{2}j_{3}j_{4}}x_{j_{1}}x_{j_{2}}x_{j_{3}}x_{j_{4}}.
\end{equation}
The energy in a state $f_{D}$ is then given by 
\begin{align}
f_{D}\left( H\right) & =\limfunc{tr}\left\{ \left\langle
H_{0}D_{0}\right\rangle _{0}\right\} +\limfunc{tr}\left\{ \left\langle
H_{2}D_{2}\right\rangle _{0}\right\} +\limfunc{tr}\left\{ \left\langle
H_{4}D_{4}\right\rangle _{0}\right\}  \notag \\
& =\eta_{0}+\sum_{1\leq
j_{1}<j_{2}\leq2r}\eta_{2j_{1}j_{2}}d_{2j_{2}j_{1}}+\sum_{1\leq
j_{1}<j_{2}<j_{3}<j_{4}\leq2r}%
\eta_{4j_{1}j_{2}j_{3}j_{4}}d_{4j_{1}j_{2}j_{3}j_{4}},
\end{align}
where%
\begin{align}
& D=QQ^{\dagger}=\sum_{0\leq m,n\leq2r}Q_{m}Q_{n}^{\dagger}=\sum_{0\leq
m,n\leq2r}\sum_{j=0}^{\min\left( m,n\right) }\left\langle
Q_{m}Q_{n}^{\dagger}\right\rangle _{\left\vert m-n\right\vert +2j} \\
& \left\langle QQ^{\dagger}\right\rangle _{0}=I_{\mathcal{F}}\equiv
\left\langle \left\langle QQ^{\dagger}\right\rangle \right\rangle
_{0}=1\equiv\left\Vert Q_{0}\right\Vert _{HS}=1.
\end{align}
As $D$ is even, i.e. gauge invariant, then 
\begin{equation}
Q=\sum_{0\leq p\leq r}Q_{2p},
\end{equation}
otherwise $D$ would contain terms like $Q_{0}Q_{2p+1}^{\dagger}$ etc.

$\lceil2r=2$ 
\begin{align}
& D=QQ^{\dagger}=\left( Q_{0}+Q_{2}\right) \left( Q_{0}+Q_{2}\right)
^{\dagger} \\
& =Q_{0}Q_{0}^{\dagger}+\left\langle Q_{2}Q_{2}^{\dagger}\right\rangle
_{0}+Q_{0}Q_{2}^{\dagger}+Q_{2}Q_{0}^{\dagger}+\left\langle
Q_{2}Q_{2}^{\dagger}\right\rangle _{2}
\end{align}
$2r=4$ 
\begin{align}
& D=QQ^{\dagger}=\left( Q_{0}+Q_{2}+Q_{4}\right) \left(
Q_{0}+Q_{2}+Q_{4}\right) ^{\dagger} \\
& \left. =\right. Q_{0}Q_{0}^{\dagger}+\left\langle Q_{2}Q_{2}^{\dagger
}\right\rangle _{0}+\left\langle Q_{4}Q_{4}^{\dagger}\right\rangle _{0} \\
& +Q_{0}Q_{2}^{\dagger}+Q_{2}Q_{0}^{\dagger}+\left\langle
Q_{2}Q_{4}^{\dagger}\right\rangle _{2}+\left\langle
Q_{4}Q_{2}^{\dagger}\right\rangle _{2}+\left\langle
Q_{2}Q_{2}^{\dagger}\right\rangle _{2}+\left\langle
Q_{4}Q_{4}^{\dagger}\right\rangle _{2} \\
& +Q_{0}Q_{4}^{\dagger}+Q_{4}Q_{0}^{\dagger}+\left\langle
Q_{2}Q_{4}^{\dagger}\right\rangle _{4}+\left\langle
Q_{4}Q_{2}^{\dagger}\right\rangle _{4}+\left\langle
Q_{2}Q_{2}^{\dagger}\right\rangle _{4}+\left\langle
Q_{4}Q_{4}^{\dagger}\right\rangle _{4}
\end{align}
$2r=6$ 
\begin{align}
& D=QQ^{\dagger}=\left( Q_{0}+Q_{2}+Q_{4}+Q_{6}\right) \left(
Q_{0}+Q_{2}+Q_{4}+Q_{6}\right) ^{\dagger} \\
& \left. =\right. \left\langle Q_{0}Q_{0}^{\dagger}\right\rangle
_{0}+\left\langle Q_{2}Q_{2}^{\dagger}\right\rangle _{0}+\left\langle
Q_{4}Q_{4}^{\dagger}\right\rangle _{0}+\left\langle Q_{6}Q_{6}^{\dagger
}\right\rangle _{0} \\
& +\left\langle Q_{0}Q_{2}^{\dagger}\right\rangle _{2}+\left\langle
Q_{2}Q_{0}^{\dagger}\right\rangle _{2}+\left\langle Q_{2}Q_{2}^{\dagger
}\right\rangle _{2}+\left\langle Q_{2}Q_{4}^{\dagger}\right\rangle
_{2}+\left\langle Q_{4}Q_{2}^{\dagger}\right\rangle _{2}+\left\langle
Q_{6}Q_{2}^{\dagger}\right\rangle _{2}+\left\langle Q_{2}Q_{6}^{\dagger
}\right\rangle _{2}+\left\langle Q_{4}Q_{4}^{\dagger}\right\rangle
_{2}+\left\langle Q_{6}Q_{4}^{\dagger}\right\rangle _{2}+\left\langle
Q_{4}Q_{6}^{\dagger}\right\rangle _{2}+\left\langle Q_{6}Q_{6}^{\dagger
}\right\rangle _{2} \\
& +\left\langle Q_{0}Q_{4}^{\dagger}\right\rangle _{4}+\left\langle
Q_{4}Q_{0}^{\dagger}\right\rangle _{4}+\left\langle Q_{2}Q_{4}^{\dagger
}\right\rangle _{4}+\left\langle Q_{4}Q_{2}^{\dagger}\right\rangle
_{4}+\left\langle Q_{6}Q_{2}^{\dagger}\right\rangle _{4}+\left\langle
Q_{2}Q_{6}^{\dagger}\right\rangle _{4}+\left\langle Q_{6}Q_{4}^{\dagger
}\right\rangle _{4}+\left\langle Q_{4}Q_{6}^{\dagger}\right\rangle
_{4}+\left\langle Q_{2}Q_{2}^{\dagger}\right\rangle _{4}+\left\langle
Q_{4}Q_{4}^{\dagger}\right\rangle _{4}+\left\langle Q_{6}Q_{6}^{\dagger
}\right\rangle _{4} \\
& +\left\langle Q_{0}Q_{6}^{\dagger}\right\rangle _{6}+\left\langle
Q_{6}Q_{0}^{\dagger}\right\rangle _{6}+\left\langle Q_{2}Q_{4}^{\dagger
}\right\rangle _{6}+\left\langle Q_{4}Q_{2}^{\dagger}\right\rangle
_{6}+\left\langle Q_{6}Q_{2}^{\dagger}\right\rangle _{6}+\left\langle
Q_{2}Q_{6}^{\dagger}\right\rangle _{6}+\left\langle Q_{6}Q_{4}^{\dagger
}\right\rangle _{6}+\left\langle Q_{4}Q_{6}^{\dagger}\right\rangle
_{6}+\left\langle Q_{4}Q_{4}^{\dagger}\right\rangle _{6}+\left\langle
Q_{6}Q_{6}^{\dagger}\right\rangle _{6}
\end{align}
Hence 
\begin{equation}
QQ^{\dagger}=\sum_{0\leq p,q\leq3}\left\langle Q_{2p}Q_{2q}^{\dagger
}\right\rangle _{0}+\sum_{0\leq p,q\leq3}\left\langle Q_{2p}Q_{2q}^{\dagger
}\right\rangle _{2}+\sum_{0\leq p,q\leq3}\left\langle Q_{2p}Q_{2q}^{\dagger
}\right\rangle _{4}+\sum_{0\leq p,q\leq3}\left\langle Q_{2p}Q_{2q}^{\dagger
}\right\rangle _{6}=\sum_{0\leq p,q\leq3}\sum_{j=0}^{\min\left( p,q\right)
}\left\langle Q_{2p}Q_{2q}^{\dagger}\right\rangle _{2\left\vert
p-q\right\vert +2j}.
\end{equation}
$\rfloor$

Thus in general 
\begin{equation}
QQ^{\dagger}=\sum_{0\leq p,q\leq r}\sum_{j=0}^{\min\left( p,q\right)
}\left\langle Q_{2p}Q_{2q}^{\dagger}\right\rangle _{2\left\vert
p-q\right\vert +2j}
\end{equation}
and 
\begin{equation}
D=QQ^{\dagger}=\sum_{0\leq j\leq r}D_{2j}=\sum_{0\leq p,q\leq r}\sum
_{j=0}^{\min\left( p,q\right) }\left\langle Q_{2p}Q_{2q}^{\dagger
}\right\rangle _{2\left\vert p-q\right\vert +2j},
\end{equation}
which gives 
\begin{equation}
D_{2j}=\sum_{\substack{ 0\leq p,q\leq r \\ \left\vert p-q\right\vert \leq j}}%
\left\langle Q_{2p}Q_{2q}^{\dagger}\right\rangle _{2j}.
\end{equation}
In particular 
\begin{align}
D_{0} & =\sum_{\substack{ 0\leq p,q\leq r \\ \left\vert p-q\right\vert \leq 0
}}\left\langle Q_{2p}Q_{2q}^{\dagger}\right\rangle _{0}=\left\Vert
Q_{0}\right\Vert _{HS} \\
D_{2} & =\sum_{\substack{ 0\leq p,q\leq r \\ \left\vert p-q\right\vert \leq 1
}}\left\langle Q_{2p}Q_{2q}^{\dagger}\right\rangle _{2} \\
D_{4} & =\sum_{\substack{ 0\leq p,q\leq r \\ \left\vert p-q\right\vert \leq 2
}}\left\langle Q_{2p}Q_{2q}^{\dagger}\right\rangle _{4}.
\end{align}
As $Q$ is normalized one has 
\begin{align}
\left\langle H\right\rangle _{D} & =\limfunc{tr}\left\{ HD\right\} =\limfunc{%
tr}\left\{ Q^{\dagger}HQ\right\} =\limfunc{tr}\left\{ HQQ^{\dagger}\right\}
=\left\langle \left\langle HQQ^{\dagger}\right\rangle \right\rangle
_{0}=\left\langle \left\langle Q^{\dagger}HQ\right\rangle \right\rangle
_{0}=\left\langle Q\left\vert HQ\right. \right\rangle _{\mathcal{F}} \\
& \neq\left\langle \widehat{H}\right\rangle _{D}=\limfunc{tr}\left\{ 
\widehat{H}\left( Q\right) Q^{\dagger}\right\} =\limfunc{tr}\left\{ \left(
HQ-QH\right) Q^{\dagger}\right\} =\limfunc{tr}\left\{ \left(
HQQ^{\dagger}-QHQ^{\dagger}\right) \right\} \\
& =\limfunc{tr}\left\{ \left( HQQ^{\dagger}-HQ^{\dagger}Q\right) \right\} =E%
\limfunc{tr}\left\{ QQ^{\dagger}\right\} =\left\langle Q\left\vert \widehat{H%
}\left( Q\right) \right. \right\rangle _{\mathcal{F}}=E.
\end{align}

In particular if 
\begin{equation}
D=\frac{aa^{\dagger}}{\left\langle \left\langle aa^{\dagger}\right\rangle
\right\rangle _{0}},
\end{equation}
where 
\begin{align}
& \limfunc{tr}\left\{ aa^{\dagger}+a^{\dagger}a\right\} =\limfunc{tr}\left\{
I_{\mathcal{F}}\right\} =1, \\
& \text{i.e. }\limfunc{tr}\left\{ aa^{\dagger}\right\} =\limfunc{tr}\left\{
a^{\dagger}a\right\} =\frac{1}{2}=\left\Vert a^{\dagger}\right\Vert
_{HS}^{2}=\left\Vert a\right\Vert _{HS}^{2}
\end{align}
then 
\begin{align}
\left\langle \widehat{H}\right\rangle _{D} & =E=\frac{\limfunc{tr}\left\{
\left( aHa^{\dagger}-aa^{\dagger}H\right) \right\} }{\limfunc{tr}\left\{
a^{\dagger}a\right\} } \\
& =\frac{\limfunc{tr}\left\{ \left( Ha^{\dagger}a-H\left(
1-a^{\dagger}a\right) \right) \right\} }{\limfunc{tr}\left\{
a^{\dagger}a\right\} }=4\limfunc{tr}\left\{ Ha^{\dagger}a\right\} -2\limfunc{%
tr}\left\{ H\right\} =E,
\end{align}
and 
\begin{equation}
\left\langle H\right\rangle _{D}=\frac{\limfunc{tr}\left\{ Ha^{\dagger
}a\right\} }{\limfunc{tr}\left\{ a^{\dagger}a\right\} }=2\limfunc{tr}\left\{
Ha^{\dagger}a\right\} ,
\end{equation}
while 
\begin{align}
\func{Tr}\left\{ \tprod \nolimits_{\left\vert \phi\right\rangle \left\langle
\phi\right\vert }\left( \widehat{H}\right) \left\vert \overset{}{%
\Phi_{\left\vert \phi\right\rangle \left\langle \phi\right\vert }\left(
a^{\dagger}\right) }\right\rangle \left\langle \overset{}{\Phi_{\left\vert
\phi\right\rangle \left\langle \phi\right\vert }\left( a^{\dagger}\right) }%
\right\vert \right\} & =\left\langle \Phi_{\left\vert \phi\right\rangle
\left\langle \phi\right\vert }\left( a^{\dagger}\right) \left\vert \tprod
\nolimits_{\left\vert \phi\right\rangle \left\langle \phi\right\vert }\left( 
\widehat{H}\right) \right. \Phi_{\left\vert \phi\right\rangle \left\langle
\phi\right\vert }\left( a^{\dagger}\right) \right\rangle \\
& =\limfunc{tr}\left\{ \left( aHa^{\dagger}-aa^{\dagger}H\right) \left\vert
\phi\right\rangle \left\langle \phi\right\vert \right\} =E\limfunc{tr}%
\left\{ aa^{\dagger}\left\vert \phi\right\rangle \left\langle
\phi\right\vert \right\} =E,
\end{align}
as 
\begin{equation}
\func{Tr}\left\{ \left\vert \Phi_{\left\vert \phi\right\rangle \left\langle
\phi\right\vert }\left( a^{\dagger}\right) \right\rangle \left\langle
\Phi_{\left\vert \phi\right\rangle \left\langle \phi\right\vert }\left(
a^{\dagger}\right) \right\vert \right\} =\limfunc{tr}\left\{
aa^{\dagger}\left\vert \phi\right\rangle \left\langle \phi\right\vert
\right\} =1.
\end{equation}

The amplitudes $a,a^{\dagger}$ are eigen amplitudes of the Hamiltonian 
\begin{equation}
\widehat{H}\left( X\right) =\left[ aa^{\dagger},X\right]
\end{equation}
giving 
\begin{equation}
\widehat{H}\left( a\right) =\left[ aa^{\dagger},a\right] =aa^{\dagger
}a-aaa^{\dagger}=aa^{\dagger}a=\left( 1-a^{\dagger}a\right) a=a
\end{equation}
and 
\begin{equation}
\widehat{H}\left( a^{\dagger}\right) =-a^{\dagger}.
\end{equation}
The Number operator (Gauge operator) has the effect 
\begin{align}
\widehat{\mathcal{N}_{a}}\left( a^{\dagger}\right) & =\left[ a^{\dagger
}a,a^{\dagger}\right] =a^{\dagger}aa^{\dagger}-a^{\dagger}a^{\dagger
}a=a^{\dagger}\left( 1-a^{\dagger}a\right) =a^{\dagger} \\
\widehat{\mathcal{N}_{a}}\left( a\right) & =\left( -\widehat{\mathcal{N}_{a}}%
\left( a^{\dagger}\right) \right) ^{\dagger}=-a. \\
a^{\dagger}a & =\frac{1}{2}\left( x_{1}-ix_{2}\right) \left(
x_{1}+ix_{2}\right) =\frac{1}{2}\left(
x_{1}^{2}+ix_{1}x_{2}-ix_{2}x_{1}+x_{2}^{2}\right) =I_{\mathcal{F}%
}+ix_{1}x_{2} \\
\widehat{\mathcal{N}_{a}}\left( x_{1}\right) & =\left[ I_{\mathcal{F}%
}+ix_{1}x_{2},x_{1}\right] =\left[ ix_{1}x_{2},x_{1}\right]
=ix_{1}x_{2}x_{1}-ix_{1}x_{1}x_{2}=-\frac{i}{2}x_{2}-\frac{i}{2}x_{2}=-ix_{2}
\\
\widehat{\mathcal{N}_{a}}\left( x_{2}\right) & =\left[ I_{\mathcal{F}%
}+ix_{1}x_{2},x_{2}\right] =\left[ ix_{1}x_{2},x_{2}\right]
=ix_{1}x_{2}x_{2}-ix_{2}x_{1}x_{2}=\frac{i}{2}x_{1}+\frac{i}{2}x_{1}=ix_{1}
\end{align}
and the complexifiction 
\begin{align}
\widehat{J_{x}}\left( x_{1}\right) & =-x_{2} \\
\widehat{J_{x}}\left( x_{2}\right) & =x_{1}.
\end{align}

\subsubsection{Gauge Invariant Solutions}

If one envokes gauge invariance then 
\begin{equation}
\widehat{\mathcal{N}}_{a}\left( Q\right) =N_{a}Q,
\end{equation}
where%
\begin{equation}
Q\in\mathcal{F}_{\left[ N_{a}\right] }
\end{equation}
and 
\begin{equation}
Q_{\left[ N_{a}\right] }=\sum_{\substack{ 0\leq p,q\leq r \\ p-q=N_{a}}}\sum
_{\substack{ 1\leq j_{1}<\cdots<j_{p}\leq r \\ 1\leq k_{1}<\cdots<k_{q}\leq r
}}q_{pqj_{1}\cdots j_{p}k_{1}\cdots k_{q}}a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger}\cdots
a_{j_{p}}^{\dagger}a_{k_{1}}\cdots a_{k_{q}},
\end{equation}
which can be expressed in a Majorana basis as 
\begin{equation}
Q_{\left[ N_{a}\right] }=\sum_{\substack{ 0\leq p,q\leq r \\ p-q=N_{a}}}\sum
_{\substack{ 1\leq j_{1}<\cdots<j_{p}\leq r \\ 1\leq k_{1}<\cdots<k_{q}\leq r
}}q_{xpqj_{1}\cdots j_{p}k_{1}\cdots k_{q}}x_{j_{1}}\cdots
x_{j_{p}}x_{k_{1}}\cdots x_{k_{q}},
\end{equation}
where%
\begin{equation}
\frac{1}{\sqrt{2}}\left( 
\begin{array}{cc}
x_{j} & x_{j+r}%
\end{array}
\right) \left( 
\begin{array}{cc}
1 & 1 \\ 
-i & i%
\end{array}
\right) =\left( 
\begin{array}{cc}
a_{j}^{\dagger} & a_{j}%
\end{array}
\right)
\end{equation}
and thus 
\begin{equation}
Q_{\left[ N_{a}\right] }=\sum_{\substack{ 0\leq p,q\leq r \\ p-q=N_{a}}}\sum
_{\substack{ 1\leq j_{1}<\cdots<j_{p}\leq r \\ 1\leq k_{1}<\cdots<k_{q}\leq r
}}q_{pqj_{1}\cdots j_{p}k_{1}\cdots k_{q}}\left(
x_{j_{1}}-ix_{j_{1}+r}\right) \cdots\left( x_{j_{p}}-ix_{j_{p}+r}\right)
\left( x_{k_{1}}+ix_{k_{1}+r}\right) \cdots\left(
x_{k_{q}}+ix_{k_{q}+r}\right) .
\end{equation}
$\lceil$%
\begin{equation}
\left( 
\begin{array}{cc}
x_{j} & x_{j+r}%
\end{array}
\right) =\frac{1}{\sqrt{2}}\left( 
\begin{array}{cc}
a_{j}^{\dagger} & a_{j}%
\end{array}
\right) \left( 
\begin{array}{cc}
1 & i \\ 
1 & -i%
\end{array}
\right)
\end{equation}
$\rfloor$

The corresponding density operators are given by 
\begin{align}
D & =QQ^{\dagger}= \\
& \sum_{\substack{ p_{1}-q_{1}=N_{a} \\ 0\leq p_{1},q_{1}\leq r}}\sum 
_{\substack{ 1\leq j_{1}<\cdots<j_{p_{1}}\leq r \\ 1\leq
k_{1}<\cdots<k_{q_{1}}\leq r}}\sum_{\substack{ p_{2}-q_{2}=N_{a} \\ 0\leq
p_{2},q_{2}\leq r}}\sum_{\substack{ 1\leq l_{1}<\cdots<l_{p_{2}}\leq r \\ %
1\leq m_{1}<\cdots<m_{q_{2}}\leq r}}q_{p_{1}q_{1}j_{1}\cdots
j_{p_{1}}k_{1}\cdots k_{q_{1}}}\overline{q}_{p_{2}q_{2}l_{1}\cdots
l_{p_{2}}m_{1}\cdots m_{q_{2}}}a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger}\cdots
a_{j_{p_{2}}}^{\dagger}a_{k_{1}}\cdots a_{k_{q_{2}}}\left( a_{m_{1}}\cdots
a_{m_{q_{2}}}\right) ^{\dagger}\left( a_{l_{1}}^{\dagger}\cdots
a_{l_{p_{2}}}^{\dagger}\right) ^{\dagger} \\
& =\sum_{\substack{ p_{1}-q_{1}=N_{a} \\ 0\leq p_{1},q_{1}\leq r}}\sum 
_{\substack{ 1\leq j_{1}<\cdots<j_{p_{1}}\leq r \\ 1\leq
k_{1}<\cdots<k_{q_{1}}\leq r}}\sum_{\substack{ p_{2}-q_{2}=N_{a} \\ 0\leq
p_{2},q_{2}\leq r}}\sum_{\substack{ 1\leq l_{1}<\cdots<l_{p_{2}}\leq r \\ %
1\leq m_{1}<\cdots<m_{q_{2}}\leq r}}q_{p_{1}q_{1}j_{1}\cdots
j_{p_{1}}k_{1}\cdots k_{q_{1}}}\overline{q}_{p_{2}q_{2}l_{1}\cdots
l_{p_{2}}m_{1}\cdots m_{q_{2}}}a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger}\cdots
a_{j_{p_{2}}}^{\dagger}a_{k_{1}}\cdots
a_{k_{q_{2}}}a_{m_{q_{2}}}^{\dagger}\cdots
a_{m_{1}}^{\dagger}a_{l_{p_{2}}}\cdots a_{l_{1}}
\end{align}
In general $Q$ can be expanded as 
\begin{align}
Q & =\sum_{-r\leq N_{a}\leq r}\sum_{\substack{ 1\leq j_{1}<\cdots
<j_{\left\vert N_{a}\right\vert }\leq r}}\sum_{0\leq p\leq r}\sum 
_{\substack{ 1\leq m_{1}<\cdots<m_{p}\leq r \\ 1\leq l_{1}<\cdots<l_{p}\leq r
}}q_{j_{1}\cdots j_{N_{a}}m_{1}\cdots m_{p}l_{1}\cdots l_{p}}\left[ a\right]
_{j_{1}}\cdots\left[ a\right] _{j_{\left\vert N_{a}\right\vert
}}a_{m_{1}}^{\dagger}\cdots a_{m_{p}}^{\dagger}a_{l_{1}}\cdots a_{l_{p}} \\
& =\sum_{-r\leq N_{a}\leq r}Q_{\left[ N_{a}\right] },
\end{align}
where 
\begin{align}
\left[ a\right] _{j} & =a_{j};N_{a}<0 \\
\left[ a\right] _{j} & =a_{j}^{\dagger};N_{a}>0 \\
\left[ a\right] _{j} & =I_{\mathcal{F}};N_{a}=0.
\end{align}
The number eigen amplitudes $Q_{\left[ N_{a}\right] }$ can be expressed as 
\begin{align}
Q_{\left[ N_{a}\right] } & =\sum_{\substack{ 1\leq j_{1}<\cdots
<j_{\left\vert N_{a}\right\vert }\leq r}}\sum_{0\leq p\leq r}q_{j_{1}\cdots
j_{\left\vert N_{a}\right\vert }m_{1}\cdots m_{p}l_{1}\cdots l_{p}}\left[ a%
\right] _{j_{1}}\cdots\left[ a\right] _{j_{\left\vert N_{a}\right\vert }}\sum
_{\substack{ 1\leq m_{1}<\cdots<m_{p}\leq r \\ 1\leq l_{1}<\cdots<l_{p}\leq r
}}a_{m_{1}}^{\dagger}\cdots a_{m_{p}}^{\dagger}a_{l_{1}}\cdots a_{l_{p}} \\
& =\sum_{0\leq p\leq r}Q_{\left\{ N_{a},p\right\} }=\sum_{0\leq p\leq r}
\left[ a\right] _{j_{1}}\cdots\left[ a\right] _{j_{\left\vert
N_{a}\right\vert }}\widetilde{Q}_{\left\{ N_{a},p\right\} },
\end{align}
where $Q_{\left\{ N_{a},p\right\} }$ and $\widetilde{Q}_{\left\{
N_{a},p\right\} }$ are defined as 
\begin{align}
Q_{\left\{ N_{a},p\right\} } & =\left[ a\right] _{j_{1}}\cdots\left[ a\right]
_{j_{\left\vert N_{a}\right\vert }}\sum_{\substack{ 1\leq
m_{1}<\cdots<m_{p}\leq r \\ 1\leq l_{1}<\cdots<l_{p}\leq r}}q_{j_{1}\cdots
j_{\left\vert N_{a}\right\vert }m_{1}\cdots m_{p}l_{1}\cdots
l_{p}}a_{m_{1}}^{\dagger}\cdots a_{m_{p}}^{\dagger}a_{l_{1}}\cdots a_{l_{p}}
\\
\widetilde{Q}_{\left\{ N_{a},p\right\} } & =\sum_{\substack{ 1\leq
m_{1}<\cdots<m_{p}\leq r \\ 1\leq l_{1}<\cdots<l_{p}\leq r}}q_{j_{1}\cdots
j_{\left\vert N_{a}\right\vert }m_{1}\cdots m_{p}l_{1}\cdots
l_{p}}a_{m_{1}}^{\dagger}\cdots a_{m_{p}}^{\dagger}a_{l_{1}}\cdots a_{l_{p}}.
\end{align}
In particular 
\begin{align}
& Q_{\left\{ N_{a},0\right\} }=\sum_{1\leq j_{1}<\cdots<j_{\left\vert
N_{a}\right\vert }\leq r}q_{j_{1}\cdots j_{N_{a}}}a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger}\cdots
a_{j_{N_{a}}}^{\dagger}=\sum_{1\leq j_{1}<\cdots<j_{N_{a}}\leq
r}q_{j_{1}\cdots j_{N_{a}}}\left( x_{j_{1}}-ix_{j_{1}+r}\right) \cdots\left(
x_{j_{N_{a}}}-ix_{j_{N_{a}}+r}\right) \\
& =\sum_{1\leq j_{1}<\cdots<j_{N_{a}}\leq r}q_{j_{1}\cdots j_{N_{a}}}\left\{
x_{j_{1}}\cdots x_{j_{N_{a}}}+\left( -i\right) x_{j_{1}}\cdots
x_{j_{N_{a}-1}}x_{j_{1}+r}+\left( -i\right) ^{2}x_{j_{1}}\cdots
x_{j_{N_{a}-2}}x_{j_{1}+r}x_{j_{2}+r}+\left( -i\right) ^{r-1}x_{j_{1}}\cdots
x_{j_{N_{a}}+r-1}+\left( -i\right) ^{N_{a}}x_{j_{1}+r}x_{j_{2}+r}\cdots
x_{j_{N_{a}}+r}\right\} .
\end{align}

$\lceil$Most general form of factored expression is 
\begin{align}
Q_{\left\{ N_{a},0\right\} } & =\left\langle \left( \sum_{1\leq j_{1}\leq
r}q_{1j_{1}}a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger}+\sum_{1\leq j_{1}<j_{2}\leq
r}q_{1j_{1}j_{2}}a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger}a_{j_{2}}^{\dagger}+\cdots+\sum_{1\leq
j_{1}<\cdots<j_{\left\vert N_{a}\right\vert }\leq r}q_{1j_{1}\cdots
j_{N_{a}}}a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger}\cdots a_{j_{N_{a}}}^{\dagger}\right)
\times\cdots\right. \\
& \left. \cdots\times\left( \sum_{1\leq j_{1}\leq
r}q_{N_{a}-1j_{1}}a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger}+\sum_{1\leq j_{1}<j_{2}\leq
r}q_{N_{a}-11j_{1}j_{2}}a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger}a_{j_{2}}^{\dagger}+\cdots+%
\sum_{1\leq j_{1}<\cdots<j_{\left\vert N_{a}\right\vert }\leq
r}q_{N_{a}j_{1}\cdots j_{N_{a}-1}}a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger}\cdots
a_{j_{N_{a}-1}}^{\dagger}\right) \right\rangle _{N_{a}} \\
& =\sum_{1\leq j_{1}<\cdots<j_{\left\vert N_{a}\right\vert }\leq
r}q_{j_{1}\cdots j_{N_{a}}}a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger}\cdots a_{j_{N_{a}}}^{\dagger}.
\end{align}
Hence 
\begin{equation}
q_{j_{1}\cdots j_{N_{a}}}=q_{j_{1}\cdots j_{N_{a}}}\left( \mathbf{q}_{j_{1}},%
\mathbf{q}_{j_{1}j_{2}},\cdots,\mathbf{q}_{j_{1}j_{2}\cdots
j_{N_{a}-1}}\right) ,
\end{equation}
where 
\begin{equation}
\mathbf{q}_{j_{1}\cdots j_{p}}=\left( q_{1j_{1}\cdots
j_{p}},\cdots,q_{\kappa j_{1}\cdots j_{p}}\right) .
\end{equation}
$\rfloor$

$\lceil$%
\begin{align}
\sum_{1\leq j_{1}<j_{2}\leq
r}q_{j_{1}j_{2}}a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger}a_{j_{2}}^{\dagger} & =\sum_{1\leq
j_{1}<j_{2}\leq r}q_{j_{1}j_{2}}\left( x_{j_{1}}-ix_{j_{1}+r}\right) \left(
x_{j_{2}}-ix_{j_{2}+r}\right) =\sum_{1\leq j_{1}<j_{2}\leq
r}q_{j_{1}j_{2}}\left\{
x_{j_{1}}x_{j_{2}}-ix_{j_{1}+r}x_{j_{2}}-ix_{j_{1}}x_{j_{2}+r}-x_{j_{1}+r}x_{j_{2}+r}\right\}
\\
& =\sum_{1\leq j_{1}<j_{2}\leq r}q_{j_{1}j_{2}}\left\{
x_{j_{1}}x_{j_{2}}-x_{j_{1}+r}x_{j_{2}+r}-i\left(
x_{j_{1}}x_{j_{2}+r}-x_{j_{2}}x_{j_{1}+r}\right) \right\}
\end{align}%
\begin{equation}
\sum_{1\leq j_{1}\leq
s}\alpha_{j_{1}}a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger}a_{j_{1}+s}^{\dagger }=\sum_{1\leq
j_{1}\leq s}\alpha_{j_{1}}\left( x_{j_{1}}-ix_{j_{1}+r}\right) \left(
x_{j_{1}+s}-ix_{j_{1}+r+s}\right) =\sum_{1\leq j_{1}\leq
s}\alpha_{j_{1}}\left\{
x_{j_{1}}x_{j_{1}+s}-x_{j_{1}+r}x_{j_{1}+r+s}-i\left(
x_{j_{1}}x_{j_{1}+r+s}-x_{j_{1}+s}x_{j_{1}+r}\right) \right\}
\end{equation}
$\rfloor$

These operators produce density operators%
\begin{equation}
Q_{\left\{ N_{a},0\right\} }Q_{\left\{ N_{a},0\right\} }^{\dagger}=\sum
_{\substack{ 1\leq j_{1}<\cdots<j_{N_{a}}\leq r \\ 1\leq
k_{1}<\cdots<k_{N_{a}}\leq r}}q_{j_{1}\cdots j_{N_{a}}}\overline{q}%
_{k_{1}\cdots k_{N_{a}}}a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger}\cdots
a_{j_{N_{a}}}^{\dagger}a_{k_{N_{a}}}\cdots a_{k_{1}}=\sum\limits_{0\leq
n\leq2r}\left\langle Q_{\left\{ N_{a},0\right\} }Q_{\left\{ N_{a},0\right\}
}^{\dagger}\right\rangle _{n},
\end{equation}
where 
\begin{equation}
\left\langle Q_{\left\{ N_{a},0\right\} }Q_{\left\{ N_{a},0\right\}
}^{\dagger}\right\rangle _{n}=\sum_{\substack{ 1\leq j_{1}<\cdots<j_{n}\leq r
\\ 1\leq k_{1}<\cdots<k_{n}\leq r}}d_{j_{1}\cdots j_{n}k_{1}\cdots
k_{n}}a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger}\cdots a_{j_{n}}^{\dagger}a_{k_{n}}\cdots
a_{k_{1}};0\leq n\leq N_{a}.
\end{equation}
The only components we are interested in are $n=0,2,4$ i.e. 
\begin{align}
D_{0} & =I_{\mathcal{F}}=\left\langle Q_{\left\{ N_{a},0\right\} }Q_{\left\{
N_{a},0\right\} }^{\dagger}\right\rangle _{0}=\sum _{\substack{ 1\leq
j_{1}<\cdots<j_{\left\vert N_{a}\right\vert }\leq r \\ 1\leq
k_{1}<\cdots<k_{\left\vert N_{a}\right\vert }\leq r}}q_{j_{1}\cdots
j_{N_{a}}}\overline{q}_{k_{1}\cdots k_{N_{a}}}\left\langle
a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger}\cdots a_{j_{N_{a}}}^{\dagger}a_{k_{N_{a}}}\cdots
a_{k_{1}}\right\rangle _{0} \\
D_{2} & =\sum_{\substack{ 1\leq j_{1}\leq r \\ 1\leq k_{1}\leq r}}%
d_{j_{1}k_{1}}a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger}a_{k_{1}}=\left\langle Q_{\left\{
N_{a},0\right\} }Q_{\left\{ N_{a},0\right\} }^{\dagger}\right\rangle
_{2}=\sum _{\substack{ 1\leq j_{1}<\cdots<j_{\left\vert N_{a}\right\vert
}\leq r \\ 1\leq k_{1}<\cdots<k_{\left\vert N_{a}\right\vert }\leq r}}%
q_{j_{1}\cdots j_{N_{a}}}\overline{q}_{k_{1}\cdots k_{N_{a}}}\left\langle
a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger}\cdots a_{j_{N_{a}}}^{\dagger}a_{k_{N_{a}}}\cdots
a_{k_{1}}\right\rangle _{2} \\
D_{4} & =\sum_{\substack{ 1\leq j_{1}<j_{2}\leq r \\ 1\leq k_{1}<k_{2}\leq r
}}d_{j_{1}j_{2}k_{1}k_{2}}a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger}a_{j2}^{%
\dagger}a_{k_{2}}a_{k_{1}}=\left\langle Q_{\left\{ N_{a},0\right\}
}Q_{\left\{ N_{a},0\right\} }^{\dagger}\right\rangle _{4}=\sum_{\substack{ %
1\leq j_{1}<\cdots<j_{\left\vert N_{a}\right\vert }\leq r \\ 1\leq
k_{1}<\cdots<k_{\left\vert N_{a}\right\vert }\leq r}}q_{j_{1}\cdots
j_{N_{a}}}\overline{q}_{k_{1}\cdots k_{N_{a}}}\left\langle
a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger}\cdots a_{j_{N_{a}}}^{\dagger}a_{k_{N_{a}}}\cdots
a_{k_{1}}\right\rangle _{4}.
\end{align}

\subsubsection{Evolution Equations}

Time dependent operators generated by unitary evolution operators e.g. in
the case of one particle Hamiltonians 
\begin{equation}
\prod_{1\leq j\leq N}a_{j}^{\dagger}\left( t\right) =\widehat{u}_{H}\left(
t\right) \left( \prod_{1\leq j\leq N}a_{j}^{\dagger}\right) =\prod_{1\leq
j\leq N}\widehat{u}_{H}\left( t\right) \left( a_{j}^{\dagger}\right) ,
\end{equation}
satisfy the EOM 
\begin{gather}
A\left( t\right) =\widehat{u}_{H}\left( t\right) \left( A\right)
=\exp\left\{ iHt\right\} A\exp\left\{ -iHt\right\} =\exp\left\{ i\func{adj}%
\left( H\right) t\right\} \left( A\right) \\
\Downarrow \\
-i\frac{dA\left( t\right) }{dt}=-\left[ H,A\left( t\right) \right] .
\end{gather}
Hence in particular 
\begin{align}
-i\frac{d}{dt}\left\{ Q\left( t\right) Q\left( t\right) ^{\dagger }\right\}
& =\frac{dQ\left( t\right) }{dt}Q\left( t\right) ^{\dagger }+Q\left(
t\right) \frac{dQ^{\dagger}\left( t\right) }{dt}=\left[ H,Q\left( t\right) %
\right] Q\left( t\right) ^{\dagger}+Q\left( t\right) \left[
H,Q^{\dagger}\left( t\right) \right] \\
& =HQ\left( t\right) Q\left( t\right) ^{\dagger}-Q\left( t\right) HQ\left(
t\right) ^{\dagger}+Q\left( t\right) HQ^{\dagger}\left( t\right) -Q\left(
t\right) Q^{\dagger}\left( t\right) H=\left[ H,Q\left( t\right)
Q^{\dagger}\left( t\right) \right] ,
\end{align}
giving 
\begin{equation}
-i\frac{d}{dt}D\left( t\right) =\left[ H,D\left( t\right) \right] =\widehat{H%
}\left( D\left( t\right) \right) .
\end{equation}
The time evolution of basis elements generated by one particle Hamiltonians
is given by 
\begin{align}
\widehat{u}_{H}\left( t\right) \left( \prod_{1\leq j\leq N}a_{j}^{\dagger
}\right) & =\prod_{1\leq j\leq N}\widehat{u}_{H}\left( t\right) \left(
a_{j}^{\dagger}\right) =\prod_{1\leq j\leq N}a_{j}^{\dagger}\left( t\right)
\\
-i\frac{d}{dt}\left( \prod_{1\leq j\leq N}a_{j}^{\dagger}\left( t\right)
\right) & =\left[ H,\prod_{1\leq j\leq N}a_{j}^{\dagger}\left( t\right) %
\right] \\
-i\frac{d}{dt}\left( \prod_{1\leq j\leq N}a_{j}^{\dagger}\left( t\right)
\right) & =\left[ H,a_{1}^{\dagger}\left( t\right) \right] \left(
\prod_{2\leq j\leq N}a_{j}^{\dagger}\left( t\right) \right)
+a_{1}^{\dagger}\left( t\right) \left[ H,a_{2}^{\dagger}\left( t\right) %
\right] \left( \prod_{3\leq j\leq N}a_{j}^{\dagger}\left( t\right) \right) +
\\
& \cdots+\left( \prod_{1\leq j\leq p}a_{j}^{\dagger}\left( t\right) \right) 
\left[ H,a_{p+1}^{\dagger}\left( t\right) \right] \left( \prod_{p+2\leq
j\leq N}a_{j}^{\dagger}\left( t\right) \right) +\cdots+\left( \prod_{1\leq
j\leq N-1}a_{j}^{\dagger}\left( t\right) \right) \left[ H,a_{N}^{\dagger}%
\left( t\right) \right] \\
& =\left( -i\frac{d}{dt}a_{1}^{\dagger}\left( t\right) \right) \left(
\prod_{2\leq j\leq N}a_{j}^{\dagger}\left( t\right) \right) +\cdots+\left(
\prod_{1\leq j\leq N-1}a_{j}^{\dagger}\left( t\right) \right) \left( -i\frac{%
d}{dt}a_{N}^{\dagger}\left( t\right) \right) .
\end{align}

\subsubsection{Eigen Vector Equations{}}

$Q\in\mathcal{F}$ is an eigen operator of the operator $\widehat{H}:\mathcal{%
F}\rightarrow$ $\mathcal{F}$ if 
\begin{equation}
\widehat{H}\left( Q\right) =\left[ H,Q\right] =EI_{\mathcal{F}}.
\end{equation}
It is \emph{IMPORTANT to note that }%
\begin{align}
\widehat{H}\left( Q_{1}\right) Q_{2} & =\left[ H,Q_{1}\right]
Q_{2}=HQ_{1}Q_{2}-Q_{1}HQ_{2} \\
\widehat{H}\left( Q_{1}Q_{2}\right) & =\left[ H,Q_{1}\right] Q_{2}+Q_{1}%
\left[ H,Q_{2}\right] =HQ_{1}Q_{2}-Q_{1}HQ_{2}+Q_{1}HQ_{2}-Q_{1}Q_{2}H=\left[
H,Q_{1}Q_{2}\right]
\end{align}
thus in general 
\begin{equation}
\widehat{H}\left( Q_{1}\right) Q_{2}\neq\widehat{H}\left( Q_{1}Q_{2}\right) .
\end{equation}

Density Operators $D$ are \emph{Stationary }wrt a unitary time evolution
generated by $H$ if 
\begin{equation}
-i\frac{d}{dt}D\left( t\right) =\left[ H,D\left( t\right) \right] =\widehat{H%
}\left( D\left( t\right) \right) =0,
\end{equation}
i.e. 
\begin{equation}
D\left( t\right) =D,
\end{equation}
where 
\begin{equation}
D\left( t\right) =\widehat{u}\left( D\right) =u\left( t\right) Du\left(
t\right) ^{\dagger}\equiv\exp\left\{ -i\widehat{H}t\right\} \left( D\right) .
\end{equation}
Density operators are positive and normalized i.e. 
\begin{equation}
D=\frac{QQ^{\dagger}}{\left\langle \left\langle QQ^{\dagger}\right\rangle
\right\rangle _{0}}=\frac{QQ^{\dagger}}{\limfunc{tr}\left\{
QQ^{\dagger}\right\} }=\widetilde{Q}\widetilde{Q}^{\dagger}.
\end{equation}
$\widetilde{Q}$ are defined up to unitary transformation that change the
phase of $\widetilde{Q}$ i.e. 
\begin{equation}
\widehat{v}\left( \widetilde{Q}\right) =v\widetilde{Q}v^{\dagger}=e^{i\theta}%
\widetilde{Q},
\end{equation}
where 
\begin{equation}
v=\exp\left\{ i\eta\right\} ;\quad\eta=\eta^{\dagger}
\end{equation}
and that leaves the density operator invariant 
\begin{equation}
\widehat{v}\left( D\right) =v\widetilde{Q}v^{\dagger}v\widetilde{Q}%
^{\dagger}v^{\dagger}=vDv^{\dagger}=e^{i\theta}\widetilde{Q}\widetilde{Q}%
^{\dagger}e^{-i\theta}=D.
\end{equation}
Thus 
\begin{equation}
\left[ \eta,D\right] =0
\end{equation}
and%
\begin{equation}
\widehat{\eta}\left( \widetilde{Q}\right) =\left[ \eta,\widetilde{Q}\right]
=\theta\widetilde{Q}
\end{equation}
i.e. 
\begin{equation}
\widetilde{Q}\equiv\widetilde{Q}_{\theta}.
\end{equation}
One can also observe that 
\begin{align}
v\widetilde{Q} & =e^{i\theta}\widetilde{Q}v \\
\left( v\widetilde{Q}\right) ^{\dagger} & =\left( e^{i\theta }\widetilde{Q}%
v\right) ^{\dagger} \\
& \Updownarrow \\
\widetilde{Q}v^{\dagger} & =e^{-i\theta}v^{\dagger}\widetilde{Q}^{\dagger}.
\end{align}

\begin{theorem}
\begin{gather}
D=\widehat{v}\left( D\right) =v\widetilde{Q}v^{\dagger}v\widetilde{Q}%
^{\dagger}v^{\dagger}=vDv^{\dagger} \\
\Updownarrow \\
\widehat{v}\left( \widetilde{Q}\right) =v\widetilde{Q}v^{\dagger}=e^{i\theta}%
\widetilde{Q}.
\end{gather}
\end{theorem}

This gives the important corollary that shows in particular that time
stationarity is equivalent to an eigen value problem.

\begin{corollary}
\begin{gather}
\widehat{v}\left( \widetilde{Q}\right) =v\widetilde{Q}v^{\dagger}=e^{i\theta}%
\widetilde{Q} \\
\Updownarrow \\
\widehat{\eta}\left( \widetilde{Q}\right) =\theta\widetilde{Q}.
\end{gather}
\end{corollary}

\subsubsection{Optimization Formulation of Eigenvalue Problem}

The variational problem that is associated with time stationarity is 
\begin{equation}
\min\limits_{Q}\left\{ \frac{\left\langle Q\left\vert \widehat{H}\left(
Q\right) \right. \right\rangle _{\mathcal{F}}}{\left\langle Q\left\vert
Q\right. \right\rangle _{\mathcal{F}}}\right\} =\min\limits_{\left\langle
Q\left\vert Q\right. \right\rangle _{\mathcal{F}}=1}\left\{ \left\langle
Q\left\vert \widehat{H}\left( Q\right) \right. \right\rangle _{\mathcal{F}%
}\right\} =\max\limits_{\lambda}\min\limits_{Q}\left\{ \left\langle
Q\left\vert \widehat{H}\left( Q\right) \right. \right\rangle _{\mathcal{F}%
}-\lambda\left( \left\langle Q\left\vert Q\right. \right\rangle _{\mathcal{F}%
}-1\right) \right\} .
\end{equation}
The sufficient condition for $Q$ to be a energy stationary operator, in the
sense of optimization, with energy $E$ is thus 
\begin{equation}
\left( \widehat{H}-EI_{\mathcal{F}}\right) Q=0,
\end{equation}
i.e. 
\begin{equation}
\widehat{H}\left( Q\right) =EQ.
\end{equation}
This a minimum if 
\begin{equation}
\left( \widehat{H}-EI_{\mathcal{F}}\right) \geq0
\end{equation}
otherwise it is stationary.

One should observe%
\begin{align}
\widehat{H}\left( Q^{\dagger}\right) & =HQ^{\dagger}-Q^{\dagger}H \\
\left( \widehat{H}\left( Q\right) \right) ^{\dagger} & =\left( HQ-QH\right)
^{\dagger}=Q^{\dagger}H-HQ^{\dagger}=-\widehat{H}\left( Q^{\dagger}\right)
\end{align}
hence if $E_{k}$ is an eigenvalue associated to eigenoperator $Q_{k},$ $%
-E_{k}$ is an eigenvalue associated to the eigenoperator $Q_{k}^{\dagger}$
i.e. 
\begin{equation}
\widehat{H}\left( Q^{\dagger}\right) =-\left( \widehat{H}\left( Q\right)
\right) ^{\dagger}=-EQ^{\dagger}.
\end{equation}
One can observe 
\begin{equation}
\widehat{H}\left( Q\right) Q^{\dagger}=EQQ^{\dagger}\equiv ED
\end{equation}
$\lceil$of course 
\begin{equation}
\widehat{H}\left( QQ^{\dagger}\right) =\widehat{H}\left( D\right) =0.
\end{equation}
$\rfloor$

\subsubsection{Evaluation of $\protect\widehat{H}\left( Q\right) $}

The product $HQ$ can be expanded as 
\begin{align}
HQ & =\left( H_{0}+H_{2}+H_{4}\right) Q=H_{0}Q+H_{2}Q+H_{4}Q \\
& =\eta_{0}Q+\sum_{0\leq n\leq2r}\left\langle H_{2}Q\right\rangle
_{n}+\sum_{0\leq n\leq2r}\left\langle H_{4}Q\right\rangle _{n} \\
& =\sum_{0\leq n\leq2r}\left\{ \eta_{0}\left\langle Q\right\rangle
_{n}+\left\langle H_{2}Q\right\rangle _{n}+\left\langle H_{4}Q\right\rangle
_{n}\right\}
\end{align}
The term $H_{2}Q$ has the expansion 
\begin{equation}
H_{2}Q=\sum_{0\leq
n\leq2r}H_{2}Q_{n}=H_{2}Q_{0}+H_{2}Q_{1}+H_{2}Q_{2}+H_{2}Q_{3}+%
\cdots+H_{2}Q_{2r},
\end{equation}
where 
\begin{align}
H_{2}Q_{0} & =q_{0}H_{2}, \\
H_{2}Q_{1} & =\sum_{j=0}^{1}\left\langle H_{2}Q_{1}\right\rangle
_{1+2j}=\left\langle H_{2}Q_{1}\right\rangle _{1}+\left\langle
H_{2}Q_{1}\right\rangle _{3} \\
H_{2}Q_{2} & =\sum_{j=0}^{2}\left\langle H_{2}Q_{2}\right\rangle
_{2j}=\left\langle H_{2}Q_{2}\right\rangle _{0}+\left\langle
H_{2}Q_{2}\right\rangle _{2}+\left\langle H_{2}Q_{2}\right\rangle _{3} \\
H_{2}Q_{3} & =\sum_{j=0}^{2}\left\langle H_{2}Q_{3}\right\rangle
_{1+2j}=\left\langle H_{2}Q_{3}\right\rangle _{1}+\left\langle
H_{2}Q_{3}\right\rangle _{3}+\left\langle H_{2}Q_{3}\right\rangle _{5} \\
H_{2}Q_{4} & =\sum_{j=0}^{2}\left\langle H_{2}Q_{4}\right\rangle
_{2+2j}=\left\langle H_{2}Q_{4}\right\rangle _{2}+\left\langle
H_{2}Q_{4}\right\rangle _{4}+\left\langle H_{2}Q_{4}\right\rangle _{6} \\
& \vdots
\end{align}
The term $H_{4}Q$ has the expansion 
\begin{equation}
H_{4}Q=\sum_{0\leq
n\leq2r}H_{4}Q_{n}=H_{4}Q_{0}+H_{4}Q_{1}+H_{4}Q_{2}+H_{4}Q_{3}+%
\cdots+H_{4}Q_{2r},
\end{equation}
where each term has the expansion 
\begin{align}
H_{4}Q_{0} & =q_{0}H_{4}, \\
H_{4}Q_{1} & =\sum_{j=0}^{1}\left\langle H_{4}Q_{1}\right\rangle
_{3+2j}=\left\langle H_{4}Q_{1}\right\rangle _{3}+\left\langle
H_{4}Q_{1}\right\rangle _{5} \\
H_{4}Q_{2} & =\sum_{j=0}^{2}\left\langle H_{4}Q_{2}\right\rangle
_{2+2j}=\left\langle H_{4}Q_{2}\right\rangle _{2}+\left\langle
H_{4}Q_{2}\right\rangle _{4}+\left\langle H_{4}Q_{2}\right\rangle _{6} \\
H_{4}Q_{3} & =\sum_{j=0}^{3}\left\langle H_{4}Q_{3}\right\rangle
_{1+2j}=\left\langle H_{4}Q_{3}\right\rangle _{1}+\left\langle
H_{4}Q_{3}\right\rangle _{3}+\left\langle H_{4}Q_{3}\right\rangle
_{5}+\left\langle H_{4}Q_{3}\right\rangle _{7} \\
H_{4}Q_{4} & =\sum_{j=0}^{4}\left\langle H_{4}Q_{4}\right\rangle
_{2j}=\left\langle H_{4}Q_{4}\right\rangle _{0}+\left\langle
H_{4}Q_{4}\right\rangle _{2}+\left\langle H_{4}Q_{4}\right\rangle
_{4}+\left\langle H_{4}Q_{4}\right\rangle _{6}+\left\langle
H_{4}Q_{4}\right\rangle _{8} \\
H_{4}Q_{5} & =\sum_{j=0}^{4}\left\langle H_{4}Q_{5}\right\rangle
_{1+2j}=\left\langle H_{4}Q_{5}\right\rangle _{1}+\left\langle
H_{4}Q_{5}\right\rangle _{3}+\left\langle H_{4}Q_{5}\right\rangle
_{5}+\left\langle H_{4}Q_{5}\right\rangle _{7}+\left\langle
H_{4}Q_{5}\right\rangle _{9} \\
H_{4}Q_{6} & =\sum_{j=0}^{4}\left\langle H_{4}Q_{6}\right\rangle
_{2+2j}=\left\langle H_{4}Q_{6}\right\rangle _{2}+\left\langle
H_{4}Q_{6}\right\rangle _{4}+\left\langle H_{4}Q_{6}\right\rangle
_{6}+\left\langle H_{4}Q_{6}\right\rangle _{8}+\left\langle
H_{4}Q_{6}\right\rangle _{10} \\
& \vdots
\end{align}

\section{Gauge Invariance}

In order for the density operators $D$ to be gauge invariant i.e.%
\begin{align}
\widehat{u_{\mathcal{N}_{b}}}\left( \theta\right) \left( D\right) & =D \\
\widehat{\mathcal{N}}_{b}\left( D\right) & =0
\end{align}%
\begin{equation}
Q\left( \theta\right) =\widehat{u_{\mathcal{N}_{b}}}\left( \theta\right)
Q=e^{iN_{b}\theta}Q,
\end{equation}
they need to be products of operators, $Q,Q^{\dagger},$ that are stationary
solutions of the problem 
\begin{equation}
\min\left\{ \frac{\left\langle Q\left\vert \widehat{\mathcal{N}}_{b}\left(
Q\right) \right. \right\rangle _{\mathcal{F}}}{\left\langle Q\left\vert
Q\right. \right\rangle _{\mathcal{F}}}\right\} =\min\limits_{\left\langle
Q^{\dagger}\left\vert Q\right. \right\rangle =1}\left\{ \left\langle
Q\left\vert \widehat{\mathcal{N}}_{b}\left( Q\right) \right. \right\rangle _{%
\mathcal{F}}\right\} =\max\limits_{\lambda}\min\limits_{Q}\left\{
\left\langle Q\left\vert \widehat{\mathcal{N}}_{b}\left( Q\right) \right.
\right\rangle _{\mathcal{F}}-\lambda\left( \left\langle Q\left\vert Q\right.
\right\rangle _{\mathcal{F}}-1\right) \right\} ,
\end{equation}
where 
\begin{align}
\widehat{\mathcal{N}}_{b}\left( Q\right) & =\left[ \mathcal{N}_{b},Q\right]
\\
\mathcal{N}_{b} & =\sum_{1\leq j\leq r}b_{j}^{\dagger}b_{j}.
\end{align}
A sufficient condition for $\frac{\left\langle Q\left\vert \widehat{\mathcal{%
N}}_{b}\left( Q\right) \right. \right\rangle _{\mathcal{F}}}{\left\langle
Q\left\vert Q\right. \right\rangle _{\mathcal{F}}}$ to be stationary with
value $N_{b}$ at $Q$ is thus 
\begin{equation}
\left( \widehat{\mathcal{N}}_{b}-N_{b}I_{\mathcal{F}}\right) Q=0,
\end{equation}
i.e. 
\begin{equation}
\widehat{\mathcal{N}}_{b}\left( Q\right) =N_{b}Q.
\end{equation}
This a minimum if 
\begin{equation}
\left( \widehat{\mathcal{N}}_{b}-N_{b}I_{\mathcal{F}}\right) \geq0,
\end{equation}
otherwise it is stationary. If $Q$ is eigenoperator with eigenvalue $N_{b}$
then $Q^{\dagger}$ is eigenoperator with eigenvalue $-N_{b},$ so when $%
r=\infty$ the operator $\widehat{\mathcal{N}_{b}}$ is an unbounded self
adjoint operator with a discrete spectra that is unbounded below and above.

It follows trivially that gauge invariance implies that 
\begin{equation}
\left\langle \left\langle \widehat{\mathcal{N}}_{b}\left( Q\right)
Q^{\dagger}\right\rangle \right\rangle _{0}=N_{b},
\end{equation}
and%
\begin{equation}
\left\langle \left\langle \widehat{\mathcal{N}}_{b}\left( Q\left(
\theta\right) \right) Q^{\dagger}\left( \theta\right) \right\rangle
\right\rangle _{0}=N_{b}.
\end{equation}

$\lceil$%
\begin{align}
\left(
a_{1}^{\dagger}a_{1}+a_{2}^{\dagger}a_{2}+a_{3}^{\dagger}a_{3}+a_{4}^{%
\dagger}a_{4}\right) a_{1}^{\dagger} &
=a_{1}^{\dagger}+a_{2}^{\dagger}a_{2}a_{1}^{\dagger}+a_{3}^{%
\dagger}a_{3}a_{1}^{\dagger}+a_{4}^{\dagger}a_{4}a_{1}^{\dagger} \\
a_{1}^{\dagger}\left(
a_{1}^{\dagger}a_{1}+a_{2}^{\dagger}a_{2}+a_{3}^{\dagger}a_{3}+a_{4}^{%
\dagger}a_{4}\right) &
=a_{1}^{\dagger}a_{2}^{\dagger}a_{2}+a_{1}^{\dagger}a_{3}^{%
\dagger}a_{3}+a_{1}^{\dagger}a_{4}^{\dagger}a_{4} \\
\left[ \mathcal{N},a_{1}^{\dagger}\right] & =a_{1}^{\dagger}
\end{align}%
\begin{align}
\left(
a_{1}^{\dagger}a_{1}+a_{2}^{\dagger}a_{2}+a_{3}^{\dagger}a_{3}+a_{4}^{%
\dagger}a_{4}\right) a_{1} &
=a_{2}^{\dagger}a_{2}a_{1}+a_{3}^{\dagger}a_{3}a_{1}+a_{4}^{%
\dagger}a_{4}a_{1} \\
a_{1}\left(
a_{1}^{\dagger}a_{1}+a_{2}^{\dagger}a_{2}+a_{3}^{\dagger}a_{3}+a_{4}^{%
\dagger}a_{4}\right) &
=a_{1}+a_{1}a_{2}^{\dagger}a_{2}+a_{1}a_{3}^{\dagger}a_{3}+a_{1}a_{4}^{%
\dagger}a_{4} \\
\left[ \mathcal{N},a_{1}\right] & =-a_{1}
\end{align}
$\rfloor$

The action of $\widehat{\mathcal{N}}_{a}\equiv\func{adj}\left( \mathcal{N}%
_{a}\right) $ on $\mathcal{F}_{1}$ is given by 
\begin{align}
\func{adj}\left( \mathcal{N}_{a}\right) \left( a_{j}^{\dagger }\right) & =%
\widehat{\mathcal{N}}_{a}\left( a_{j}^{\dagger}\right) =\left[ \mathcal{N}%
_{a},a_{j}^{\dagger}\right] =a_{j}^{\dagger} \\
\func{adj}\left( \mathcal{N}_{a}\right) \left( x_{j}\right) & =\widehat{%
\mathcal{N}}_{a}\left( x_{j}\right) =\left[ \mathcal{N}_{a},x_{j}\right] =%
\left[ \mathcal{N}_{a},\frac{1}{\sqrt{2}}\left( a_{j}^{\dagger}+a_{j}\right) %
\right] =\frac{1}{\sqrt{2}}\left( a_{j}^{\dagger}-a_{j}\right) =x_{j+r} \\
\func{adj}\left( \mathcal{N}_{a}\right) \left( a_{j}\right) & =\widehat{%
\mathcal{N}}_{a}\left( a_{j}\right) =\left[ \mathcal{N}_{a},a_{j}\right]
=-a_{j} \\
\func{adj}\left( \mathcal{N}_{a}\right) \left( x_{j+r}\right) & =\widehat{%
\mathcal{N}}_{a}\left( x_{j+r}\right) =\left[ \mathcal{N}_{a},x_{j+r}\right]
=\left[ \mathcal{N}_{a},\frac{i}{\sqrt{2}}\left(
a_{j}^{\dagger}-a_{j}\right) \right] =\frac{i}{\sqrt{2}}\left( \left(
a_{j}^{\dagger}+a_{j}\right) \right) =x_{j},
\end{align}
which shows that $\left\{ \left. a_{j}^{\dagger},a_{j}\right\vert 1\leq
j\leq\infty\right\} $ are eigen operator amplitudes of $\widehat{\mathcal{N}%
_{a}}$ with eigenvalues $\left\{ 1,-1\right\} $ respectively$.$ This is a
particular example of $\mathcal{F}_{1}$ being an invariant subspace of $%
O\left( 2r,\mathbb{C}\right) $ as the Gauge groups are abelian subgroups of $%
O\left( 2r,\mathbb{C}\right) ,$ ($\mathcal{F}_{1}$ is the direct sum of two
isomorphic subspaces that are invariant to $Gl\left( r,\mathbb{C}\right) ,$%
which can realized as inner automorphisms of $\mathcal{F}$ generated by $%
\mathcal{F}_{2}.$ The subspace $\mathcal{F}_{1}$ is invariant to $Gl\left(
2r,\mathbb{C}\right) ,$ but this group cannot be represented as inner
automorphisms of $\mathcal{F}$ generated by $\mathcal{F}_{\left( 2\right) }).
$ The preceding also shows that 
\begin{align}
\left[ i\mathcal{N}_{a},a_{j}^{\dagger}\right] & =ia_{j}^{\dagger} \\
\left[ i\mathcal{N}_{a},x_{j}\right] & =\left[ i\mathcal{N}_{a},\frac {1}{%
\sqrt{2}}\left( a_{j}^{\dagger}+a_{j}\right) \right] =\frac{i}{\sqrt {2}}%
\left( a_{j}^{\dagger}-a_{j}\right) =x_{j+r} \\
\left[ i\mathcal{N}_{a},a_{j}\right] & =-ia_{j} \\
\left[ i\mathcal{N}_{a},x_{j+r}\right] & =\left[ i\mathcal{N}_{a},\frac{i}{%
\sqrt{2}}\left( a_{j}^{\dagger}-a_{j}\right) \right] =-\frac {1}{\sqrt{2}}%
\left( \left( a_{j}^{\dagger}+a_{j}\right) \right) =-x_{j}.
\end{align}
The action of the Gauge group and its generator, (the Number operator $%
\widehat{\mathcal{N}}_{a}$), on $\mathcal{F}$ is thus given by 
\begin{align}
\widehat{u}_{N_{a}}\left( \theta\right) \left( \prod_{1\leq j\leq
N}a_{j}^{\dagger}\right) & =\prod_{1\leq j\leq N}\widehat{u_{1}}\left(
\theta\right) \left( a_{j}^{\dagger}\right) \\
\left. -i\frac{d}{d\theta}\widehat{u}_{N_{a}}\left( \theta\right)
\right\vert _{\theta=0}\left( \prod_{1\leq j\leq N}a_{j}^{\dagger}\right) & =%
\left[ \mathcal{N}_{a},\prod_{1\leq j\leq N}a_{j}^{\dagger}\right] \\
\left. -i\frac{d}{d\theta}\prod_{1\leq j\leq N}\left( \widehat{u_{1}}\left(
\theta\right) \left( a_{j}^{\dagger}\right) \right) \right\vert _{t=0} & =%
\left[ \mathcal{N}_{a},a_{1}^{\dagger}\right] \left( \prod_{2\leq j\leq
N}a_{j}^{\dagger}\right) +a_{1}^{\dagger}\left[ \mathcal{N}%
_{a},a_{2}^{\dagger}\right] \left( \prod_{3\leq j\leq
N}a_{j}^{\dagger}\right) + \\
& \cdots+\left( \prod_{1\leq j\leq p}a_{j}^{\dagger}\right) \left[ \mathcal{N%
}_{a},a_{p+1}^{\dagger}\right] \left( \prod_{p+2\leq j\leq
N}a_{j}^{\dagger}\right) +\cdots+\left( \prod_{1\leq j\leq
N-1}a_{j}^{\dagger }\right) \left[ \mathcal{N}_{a},a_{N}^{\dagger}\right] \\
& =N\left( \prod_{1\leq j\leq N}a_{j}^{\dagger}\right) =N\left( 2^{-\frac{N}{%
2}}\prod_{1\leq j\leq N}\left( x_{j}+ix_{j+r}\right) \right) ,
\end{align}
as in general any element of $\mathcal{F}$ can be expressed as strong limits
of polynomials formed from the mononomials 
\begin{equation}
\prod_{0\leq j\leq N_{p}}\widehat{u_{1}}\left( \theta\right) \left(
a_{j}^{\dagger}\right) \prod_{0\leq j\leq N_{h}}\widehat{u_{1}}\left(
\theta\right) \left( a_{j}\right) ;0\leq N_{p},N_{h}\leq\infty,
\end{equation}
where 
\begin{equation}
a_{0}^{\dagger}=a_{0}=I_{\mathcal{F}}.
\end{equation}
The general action of any Number operator $\widehat{\mathcal{N}}$ is given
by considering the EOM produced by the Gauge group 
\begin{equation}
-i\frac{dA\left( \theta\right) }{d\theta}=\left[ \mathcal{N},A\left(
\theta\right) \right] =\widehat{\mathcal{N}}\left( A\left( \theta\right)
\right) ,
\end{equation}
that gives for basis elements of $\mathcal{F}$ 
\begin{align}
& \left. \frac{d}{d\theta}\left( \prod_{1\leq j\leq N_{p}}\widehat{u_{1}}%
\left( \theta\right) \left( a_{j}^{\dagger}\right) \prod_{1\leq j\leq N_{h}}%
\widehat{u_{1}}\left( \theta\right) \left( a_{j}\right) \right) \right\vert
_{\theta=0}=\left( N_{p}\prod_{1\leq j\leq N_{p}}a_{j}^{\dagger }\right)
\left( -N_{h}\prod_{1\leq j\leq N_{h}}a_{j}\right) \\
& =\left( N_{p}-N_{h}\right) \prod_{1\leq j\leq
N_{p}}a_{j}^{\dagger}\prod_{1\leq j\leq N_{h}}a_{j}=\left(
N_{p}-N_{h}\right) 2^{-\frac{N_{p}}{2}}\prod_{1\leq j\leq N_{p}}\left(
x_{j}+ix_{j+r}\right) 2^{-\frac{N_{h}}{2}}\prod_{1\leq j\leq N_{h}}\left(
x_{j}-ix_{j+r}\right) ,
\end{align}
the last term can be simplified by noting 
\begin{align}
2^{-\frac{N_{p}+N_{h}}{2}}\prod_{1\leq j_{p}\leq N_{p}}\left(
x_{j_{p}}+ix_{j_{p}+r}\right) \prod_{1\leq j_{h}\leq N_{h}}\left(
x_{j_{h}}-ix_{j_{h}+r}\right) & =2^{-\frac{N_{p}+N_{h}}{2}}\prod_{\substack{ %
1\leq j_{p}\leq N_{p} \\ 1\leq j_{h}\leq N_{h}}}\left(
x_{j_{p}}+ix_{j_{p}+r}\right) \left( x_{j_{h}}-ix_{j_{h}+r}\right) \\
& =2^{-\frac{N_{p}+N_{h}}{2}}\prod_{\substack{ 1\leq j_{p}\leq N_{p} \\ %
1\leq j_{h}\leq N_{h}}}\left(
x_{j_{p}}x_{j_{h}}+x_{j_{p}+r}x_{j_{h}+r}+i\left(
x_{j_{p}+r}x_{j_{h}}-x_{j_{p}}x_{j_{h}+r}\right) \right) ,
\end{align}
giving 
\begin{align}
& \widehat{\mathcal{N}}_{a}\left( \prod_{1\leq j\leq N_{p}}a_{j}^{\dagger
}\prod_{1\leq j\leq N_{h}}a_{j}\right) =\widehat{\mathcal{N}}_{a}\left( 2^{-%
\frac{N_{p}+N_{h}}{2}}\prod_{\substack{ 1\leq j_{p}\leq N_{p} \\ 1\leq
j_{h}\leq N_{h}}}\left( x_{j_{p}}x_{j_{h}}+x_{j_{p}+r}x_{j_{h}+r}+i\left(
x_{j_{p}+r}x_{j_{h}}-x_{j_{p}}x_{j_{h}+r}\right) \right) \right) \\
& =\left( N_{p}-N_{h}\right) \left( 2^{-\frac{N_{p}+N_{h}}{2}}\prod
_{\substack{ 1\leq j_{p}\leq N_{p} \\ 1\leq j_{h}\leq N_{h}}}\left(
x_{j_{p}}x_{j_{h}}+x_{j_{p}+r}x_{j_{h}+r}+i\left(
x_{j_{p}+r}x_{j_{h}}-x_{j_{p}}x_{j_{h}+r}\right) \right) \right) \\
& =\left( N_{p}-N_{h}\right) \left( \prod_{1\leq j\leq
N_{p}}a_{j}^{\dagger}\prod_{1\leq j\leq N_{h}}a_{j}\right) .
\end{align}
Thus 
\begin{equation}
\left( \prod_{1\leq j\leq N_{p}}a_{j}^{\dagger}\prod_{1\leq j\leq
N_{h}}a_{j}\right) =\left( 2^{-\frac{N_{p}+N_{h}}{2}}\prod_{\substack{ 1\leq
j_{p}\leq N_{p} \\ 1\leq j_{h}\leq N_{h}}}\left(
x_{j_{p}}x_{j_{h}}+x_{j_{p}+r}x_{j_{h}+r}+i\left(
x_{j_{p}+r}x_{j_{h}}-x_{j_{p}}x_{j_{h}+r}\right) \right) \right)
\end{equation}
are eigen operators of $\widehat{\mathcal{N}}_{a},$which allows one to
decompose $\mathcal{F}$ as the direct sum of the eigen vector spaces 
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{F}=\bigoplus\limits_{-\infty\leq N_{a}\leq\infty}\mathcal{F}_{\left[
N_{a}\right] },
\end{equation}
where 
\begin{equation}
\widehat{\mathcal{N}}_{a}\mathcal{F}_{\left[ N_{a}\right] }=N_{a}\mathcal{F}%
_{\left[ N_{a}\right] },
\end{equation}
i.e. $\mathcal{F}_{\left[ N_{a}\right] }$ are invariant, (eigen), subspaces
of $\widehat{\mathcal{N}}_{a}.$ The subspace $\mathcal{F}_{\left[ 0\right] }=%
\mathcal{F}_{e}$ is the subalgebra of Gauge invariant operators. The
subspaces $N_{a}\neq0$ are not in general invariant to $SO\left( 2r,\mathbb{R%
}\right) ,SO\left( 2r+1,\mathbb{R}\right) ,$ but are invariant to $U\left( r,%
\mathbb{R}\right) .$

In general 
\begin{equation}
\sum_{1\leq j\leq\infty}a_{j}^{\dagger}a_{j}\neq\sum_{1\leq
j\leq\infty}b_{j}^{\dagger}b_{j}
\end{equation}
even though both $\left\{ a_{j}^{\dagger},a_{j}\right\} $ and $\left\{
b_{j}^{\dagger},b_{j}\right\} $ satisfy the CAR. These correspond to
different complexifications of $\mathcal{F}_{1\mathbb{R}}$ and thus
representations of $\mathcal{F}$ (or in more precise notation $\mathcal{A}%
_{CAR}),$representations also depend on states. Note that $\mathcal{F}$
often denotes the standard Fock representation, which is based on a
particular Hilbert space $\mathcal{H}_{\mathcal{F}}=\wedge\left( \mathcal{H}%
^{1}\right) .$

\paragraph{Dispersion Theorem}

\begin{theorem}
$Av=\left( v^{\dagger}Av\right) v\Longleftrightarrow
v^{\dagger}A^{2}v=\left( v^{\dagger}Av\right) ^{2},$ where $v^{\dagger}v=1$
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
$Av=\alpha v\Rightarrow A^{2}v=\alpha^{2}v\Rightarrow
v^{\dagger}A^{2}v=\left( v^{\dagger}Av\right) ^{2}$
\end{proof}

\begin{proof}
$v^{\dagger}A^{2}v=\left( v^{\dagger}Av\right) ^{2}\Rightarrow$
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}
\begin{proof}
$v^{\dagger}\left( A-\left( v^{\dagger}Av\right) I\right) ^{2}v=0$ as this
equals $v^{\dagger}\left( A^{2}-2A\left( v^{\dagger}Av\right) +\left(
v^{\dagger}Av\right) ^{2}I\right) v=0=v^{\dagger}A^{2}v-\left( v^{\dagger
}Av\right) ^{2}$
\end{proof}

\begin{proof}
and $\left( \left( A-\left( v^{\dagger}Av\right) I\right) v\right)
^{\dagger}\left( \left( A-\left( v^{\dagger}Av\right) I\right) v\right) =0$
\end{proof}

\begin{proof}
$\Rightarrow\left( A-\left( v^{\dagger}Av\right) I\right) v=0\Rightarrow
Av=\left( v^{\dagger}Av\right) v$ QED.
\end{proof}
\end{theorem}

By using this theorem one obtains for gauge invariant $Q$ 
\begin{equation}
\widehat{\mathcal{N}}\left( Q\right) =NQ\Leftrightarrow\left\langle
\left\langle Q^{\dagger}\widehat{\mathcal{N}}^{2}\left( Q\right)
\right\rangle \right\rangle _{0}=\left\langle \left\langle Q^{\dagger }%
\widehat{\mathcal{N}}\left( Q\right) \right\rangle \right\rangle
_{0}^{2}=N^{2},
\end{equation}
which is equivalent to%
\begin{align}
\left\langle \left\langle \widehat{\mathcal{N}}\left( Q\right) Q^{\dagger
}\right\rangle \right\rangle _{0} & =N \\
\left\langle \left\langle \widehat{\mathcal{N}}\left( QQ^{\dagger}\right)
\right\rangle \right\rangle _{0} & =0.
\end{align}

Gauge properties of general operators can be deduced from 
\begin{align}
\frac{\left\langle \left( \prod_{1\leq j\leq
N_{p}}a_{j}^{\dagger}\prod_{1\leq j\leq N_{h}}a_{j}\right) ^{\dagger}%
\widehat{\mathcal{N}}\left( \prod_{1\leq j\leq
N_{p}}a_{j}^{\dagger}\prod_{1\leq j\leq N_{h}}a_{j}\right) \right\rangle _{0}%
}{\left\langle \left( \prod_{1\leq j\leq N_{p}}a_{j}^{\dagger}\prod_{1\leq
j\leq N_{h}}a_{j}\right) ^{\dagger}\left( \prod_{1\leq j\leq
N_{p}}a_{j}^{\dagger}\prod_{1\leq j\leq N_{h}}a_{j}\right) \right\rangle _{0}%
} & =\frac{\left\langle \widehat{\mathcal{N}}\left( \left( \prod_{1\leq
j\leq N_{p}}a_{j}^{\dagger}\prod_{1\leq j\leq N_{h}}a_{j}\right) \right)
\left( \prod_{1\leq j\leq N_{p}}a_{j}^{\dagger}\prod_{1\leq j\leq
N_{h}}a_{j}\right) ^{\dagger}\right\rangle _{0}}{\left\langle \left(
\prod_{1\leq j\leq N_{p}}a_{j}^{\dagger}\prod_{1\leq j\leq
N_{h}}a_{j}\right) \left( \prod_{1\leq j\leq N_{p}}a_{j}^{\dagger
}\prod_{1\leq j\leq N_{h}}a_{j}\right) ^{\dagger}\right\rangle _{0}} \\
& =\left( N_{p}-N_{h}\right) I_{\mathcal{F}}
\end{align}

\section{One Electron Example}

Consider the one particle Hilbert space $\mathcal{H}^{1},$ the Fock space
that it generates $\mathcal{H}_{\mathcal{F}}\equiv\bigwedge\left( \mathcal{H}%
^{1}\right) $ and the Fock algebra $\mathcal{F}\equiv\mathcal{B}\left(
\bigwedge\left( \mathcal{H}^{1}\right) \right) \equiv\mathcal{B}\left( 
\mathcal{H}_{\mathcal{F}}\right) .$ Notice $\mathcal{H}_{\mathcal{F}}$ is
itself an algebra. Then 
\begin{align}
\mathcal{H}^{1} & \cong\limfunc{linspan}\left\{ \left.
a_{j}^{\dagger}\right\vert 1\leq j\leq r\right\} \\
\overline{\mathcal{H}}^{1} & \cong\limfunc{linspan}\left\{ \left.
a_{j}\right\vert 1\leq j\leq r\right\} \\
\mathfrak{F}_{1} & \cong\mathcal{H}^{1}\oplus\overline{\mathcal{H}}^{1},
\end{align}
as 
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{H}^{1d}\cong\overline{\mathcal{H}}^{1},
\end{equation}
where we can introduce the notation 
\begin{equation}
\mathfrak{F}_{1}=\mathfrak{F}_{10}\oplus\mathfrak{F}_{01}
\end{equation}
and%
\begin{align}
\mathbf{a} & =\mathfrak{F}_{01}=\limfunc{linspan}\left\{ \left.
a_{j}\right\vert 1\leq j\leq r\right\} \\
\mathbf{a}^{\dagger} & =\mathfrak{F}_{10}=\limfunc{linspan}\left\{ \left.
a_{j}^{\dagger}\right\vert 1\leq j\leq r\right\}
\end{align}
This gives%
\begin{align}
\mathfrak{F}_{n} & =\dbigoplus \limits_{\substack{ p+q=n \\ 1\leq p,q\leq r}}%
\mathfrak{F}_{pq} \\
\mathcal{F} & =\bigoplus\limits_{1\leq n\leq2r}\mathfrak{F}_{n}.
\end{align}
The subspaces $\left\{ \left. \mathfrak{F}_{n}\right\vert 1\leq
n\leq2r\right\} $ and $\left\{ \left. \mathfrak{F}_{pq}\right\vert 1\leq
p,q\leq r\right\} $ are not traceless in contrast to the subspaces $\left\{
\left. \mathcal{F}_{n}\right\vert 1\leq n\leq2r\right\} .$

The subspaces $\left\{ \left. \mathfrak{F}_{n}\right\vert 1\leq
n\leq2r\right\} $ are invariant to $O\left( 2r,\mathbb{R}\right) ,$while the
subspaces $\left\{ \left. \mathfrak{F}_{\left[ N_{a}\right] }\right\vert
-2r\leq N_{a}\leq2r\right\} $ 
\begin{align}
\mathfrak{F}_{\left[ N_{a}\right] } & =\dbigoplus \limits_{\substack{ %
p-q=N_{a} \\ 0\leq p,q\leq r}}\mathfrak{F}_{pq} \\
\mathcal{F} & =\bigoplus\limits_{-2r\leq N_{a}\leq2r}\mathfrak{F}_{\left[
N_{a}\right] }
\end{align}
are not, though they are invariant to $U\left( r\right) $ and are in general
reducible eigen spaces of $\widehat{\mathcal{N}}_{a}.$ Irreducible eigen
spaces associated with the eigenvalue $N_{a}$ are $\left\{ \left. \mathfrak{F%
}_{pq}\right\vert p-q=N_{a},0\leq p,q\leq r\right\} .$

Let us consider a one particle Hamiltonian that has finite trace i.e $\in%
\mathcal{B}_{1}\left( \mathcal{H}^{1}\right) $ 
\begin{equation}
H=\sum_{1\leq j\leq r}E_{j}\left\vert \varphi_{j}\right\rangle \left\langle
\varphi_{j}\right\vert
\end{equation}
and its representation in the Fock algebra $\mathcal{F}$ 
\begin{equation}
\Gamma\left( H\right) =\sum_{1\leq j\leq r}E_{j}a_{j}^{\dagger}a_{j},
\end{equation}
where 
\begin{equation}
a_{j}^{\dagger}=\Gamma\left( \left\vert \varphi_{j}\right\rangle
\left\langle \phi\right\vert \right) =a^{\dagger}\left( \varphi_{j}\right) .
\end{equation}
$\lceil$

\begin{align}
\left( \sum_{1\leq j\leq r}E_{j}a_{j}^{\dagger}a_{j}\right) a_{k} &
=0\Leftrightarrow a_{k}^{\dagger}\left( \sum_{1\leq j\leq
r}E_{j}a_{j}^{\dagger}a_{j}\right) =0 \\
\left( \sum_{1\leq j\leq r}E_{j}a_{j}^{\dagger}a_{j}\right) a_{k}^{\dagger}
& =\sum_{1\leq j\leq r}E_{j}a_{j}^{\dagger}\left(
\delta_{jk}-a_{k}^{\dagger}a_{j}\right) =\sum_{1\leq j\leq
r}E_{j}a_{j}^{\dagger}\delta _{jk}=E_{k}a_{k}^{\dagger} \\
a_{k}\left( \sum_{1\leq j\leq r}E_{j}a_{j}^{\dagger}a_{j}\right) &
=E_{k}a_{k}
\end{align}%
\begin{gather}
\left[ \left( \sum_{1\leq j\leq r}E_{j}a_{j}^{\dagger}a_{j}\right)
,a_{k}^{\dagger}\right] =\left( \sum_{1\leq j\leq r}E_{j}a_{j}^{\dagger
}a_{j}\right) a_{k}^{\dagger}-a_{k}^{\dagger}\left( \sum_{1\leq j\leq
r}E_{j}a_{j}^{\dagger}a_{j}\right) =E_{k}a_{k}^{\dagger} \\
\Updownarrow \\
a_{k}\left( \sum_{1\leq j\leq r}E_{j}a_{j}^{\dagger}a_{j}\right) -\left(
\sum_{1\leq j\leq r}E_{j}a_{j}^{\dagger}a_{j}\right) a_{k}=\left[
a_{k},\left( \sum_{1\leq j\leq r}E_{j}a_{j}^{\dagger}a_{j}\right) \right]
=E_{k}a_{k} \\
\Updownarrow \\
\left( \sum_{1\leq j\leq r}E_{j}a_{j}^{\dagger}a_{j}\right)
a_{k}-a_{k}\left( \sum_{1\leq j\leq r}E_{j}a_{j}^{\dagger}a_{j}\right) =
\left[ \left( \sum_{1\leq j\leq r}E_{j}a_{j}^{\dagger}a_{j}\right) ,a_{k}%
\right] =-E_{k}a_{k}
\end{gather}
$\rfloor$

The tracial state $\limfunc{tr}$ on $\mathcal{F}$ is faithful and produces
the GNS\ representation $\Pi_{\limfunc{tr}}\left( \mathcal{F}\right)
\subseteq\mathcal{B}\left( \mathcal{B}_{2}\left( \mathcal{H}_{\mathcal{F}%
}\right) \right) $ on $\Phi_{\limfunc{tr}}\left( \mathcal{F}\right) \equiv%
\mathcal{F}_{HS}\equiv\mathcal{B}_{2}\left( \mathcal{H}_{\mathcal{F}}\right)
,$ in particular 
\begin{equation}
\Phi_{\limfunc{tr}}\left( \mathcal{F}_{1}\right) =\limfunc{linspan}\left\{
\left. \Phi_{\limfunc{tr}}\left( a_{j}^{\dagger}\right) ,\Phi_{\limfunc{tr}%
}\left( a_{j}\right) \right\vert 1\leq j\leq r\right\} .
\end{equation}
In this representation 
\begin{align}
\left\langle \left. \Phi_{\limfunc{tr}}\left( a_{j}^{\dagger}\right)
\right\vert \Pi_{\limfunc{tr}}\left( \widehat{H}\right) \Phi _{\limfunc{tr}%
}\left( a_{j}^{\dagger}\right) \right\rangle & =\limfunc{tr}\left\{ a_{j}%
\widehat{H}\left( a_{j}^{\dagger}\right) \right\} =E_{j}\limfunc{tr}\left\{
a_{j}a_{j}^{\dagger}\right\} =\frac{E_{j}}{2} \\
\left\langle \left. \Phi_{\limfunc{tr}}\left( a_{j}\right) \right\vert \Pi_{%
\limfunc{tr}}\left( \widehat{H}\right) \Phi _{\limfunc{tr}}\left(
a_{j}\right) \right\rangle & =\limfunc{tr}\left\{ a_{j}^{\dagger}\widehat{H}%
\left( a_{j}\right) \right\} =-E_{j}\limfunc{tr}\left\{
a_{j}^{\dagger}a_{j}\right\} =-\frac{E_{j}}{2}
\end{align}%
\begin{align}
\left\langle \left. \Phi_{\limfunc{tr}}\left( a_{j}^{\dagger}\right)
\right\vert \Pi_{\limfunc{tr}}\left( H\right) \Phi_{\limfunc{tr}}\left(
a_{j}^{\dagger}\right) \right\rangle & =\limfunc{tr}\left\{ a_{j}H\left(
a_{j}^{\dagger}\right) \right\} =E_{j}\limfunc{tr}\left\{
a_{j}a_{j}^{\dagger}\right\} =\frac{E_{j}}{2} \\
\left\langle \left. \Phi_{\limfunc{tr}}\left( a_{j}\right) \right\vert \Pi_{%
\limfunc{tr}}\left( H\right) \Phi_{\limfunc{tr}}\left( a_{j}\right)
\right\rangle & =\limfunc{tr}\left\{ a_{j}^{\dagger}H\left( a_{j}\right)
\right\} =0,
\end{align}
which shows that 
\begin{equation}
\Pi_{\limfunc{tr}}\left( \widehat{H}\right) \neq\Pi _{\limfunc{tr}}\left(
H\right) .
\end{equation}
$($Check against \ref{rep check}$)$ It will be shown that in a vacuum
representation this is not true and in fact 
\begin{equation}
\Pi_{vac}\left( \widehat{H}\right) =\Pi_{vac}\left( H\right) .
\end{equation}

\section{Vacuum States}

The vacuum state $f_{\left\vert \phi\right\rangle \left\langle
\phi\right\vert }\left( \cdot\right) =\limfunc{tr}\left\{ \cdot\left\vert
\phi\right\rangle \left\langle \phi\right\vert \right\} $ gives 
\begin{align}
& \mathcal{H}^{1}\cong\limfunc{linspan}\left\{ \left. \Phi _{\left\vert
\phi\right\rangle \left\langle \phi\right\vert }\left(
a_{j}^{\dagger}\right) \right\vert 1\leq j\leq r\right\} =\Phi_{\left\vert
\phi\right\rangle \left\langle \phi\right\vert }\left( \mathcal{F}%
_{10}\right) \\
& \Phi_{\left\vert \phi\right\rangle \left\langle \phi\right\vert }\left( 
\mathcal{F}_{01}\right) =0 \\
& \mathcal{H}_{\mathcal{F}}=\Phi_{\left\vert \phi\right\rangle \left\langle
\phi\right\vert }\left( \mathcal{F}\right) =\wedge\left( \mathcal{H}%
^{1}\right) .
\end{align}
A (normal) vacuum state is characterized by 
\begin{align}
\limfunc{tr}\left\{ D_{vac}\right\} & =1 \\
D_{vac} & \geq0 \\
\widehat{\mathcal{N}}_{a}\left( D_{vac}\right) & =0 \\
\limfunc{tr}\left\{ \mathcal{N}_{a}D_{vac}\right\} & =0,
\end{align}
where $\mathcal{N}_{a}$ is the number operator corresponding to the non self
adjoint basis $\left\{ \left. a_{j},a_{j}^{\dagger}\right\vert 1\leq j\leq
r\right\} $ and 
\begin{equation}
\Gamma_{a}\left( D_{vac}\right) =I_{\mathcal{F}}.
\end{equation}
$\lceil$A basic condition for $f_{\left\vert \phi\left( g\right)
\right\rangle \left\langle \phi\left( g\right) \right\vert }\left(
\cdot\right) $ being a vacuum state could be 
\begin{equation}
\left\vert \phi\left( g\right) \right\rangle \left\langle \phi\left(
g\right) \right\vert \wedge\left\vert \phi\left( g\right) \right\rangle
\left\langle \phi\left( g\right) \right\vert =\left\vert \phi\left( g\right)
\right\rangle \left\langle \phi\left( g\right) \right\vert
\end{equation}
and 
\begin{equation}
\left\vert \phi\left( g\right) \right\rangle \wedge\left\vert \phi\left(
g\right) \right\rangle =\left\vert \phi\left( g\right) \right\rangle ,
\end{equation}
\emph{but this does not seem to be. }The bare vacuum $\left\vert
\phi\right\rangle $ is obtained when $g=0$ i.e. 
\begin{equation}
\left\vert \phi\right\rangle =\exp\left\{ 0\right\} \left\vert
\phi\right\rangle .
\end{equation}

One can also observe that the preceding implies that it must be true that 
\begin{equation}
\Gamma\left( D_{vac}\left( g\right) \right) =I_{\mathcal{F}}
\end{equation}
\emph{for} \emph{dressed vacuums }$D_{vac}\left( g\right) .?\rfloor$

One can observe that, $\limfunc{tr},$ \emph{is not a vacuum state}.

States are pure if 
\begin{equation}
D=D^{2}=D^{\dagger}.
\end{equation}

The action of the representation of the "annihilators" on the image of the
identity (the generating state (but not necessarily a vacuum state) for any
representation) is 
\begin{equation}
\Pi_{f}\left( a_{j}\right) \Phi_{f}\left( I_{\mathcal{F}}\right) =\Phi
_{f}\left( a_{j}\right)
\end{equation}
giving 
\begin{equation}
\left\langle \left. \Phi_{f}\left( a_{j}\right) \right\vert \Phi_{f}\left(
a_{j}\right) \right\rangle =f\left( a_{j}^{\dagger}a_{j}\right) =\limfunc{tr}%
\left\{ a_{j}^{\dagger}a_{j}D_{f}\right\} ,
\end{equation}
showing that if 
\begin{equation}
f\left( a_{j}^{\dagger}a_{j}\right) =0
\end{equation}
then 
\begin{equation}
\Pi_{f}\left( a_{j}\right) \Phi_{f}\left( I_{\mathcal{F}}\right) =0.
\end{equation}
Thus a state $f$ is a vacuum state \emph{and produces a vacuum vector }$%
\Phi_{f}\left( I_{\mathcal{F}}\right) $\emph{\ via the GNS construction} if
there exists a splitting of $\mathcal{F}_{1}$ into 
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{F}_{1}=\mathbf{a}^{\dagger}\oplus\mathbf{a}
\end{equation}
such that 
\begin{align}
& \mathbf{a\subset}\ker\left\{ \Phi_{f}\right\} \\
& \mathbf{a}^{\dagger}\cap\ker\left\{ \Phi_{f}\right\} =\phi.
\end{align}
(Note that $\ker\left\{ \Phi_{f}\right\} $ is a left ideal containing the
two sided ideal $\tbigwedge \mathbf{a}$)

If $\Phi_{f}\left( I_{\mathcal{F}}\right) $ is a vacuum vector belonging to
the Fock space $\Phi_{f}\left( \mathcal{F}\right) $ then it is orthogonal to
all other vectors in $\mathcal{H}_{f}$ as 
\begin{equation}
f\left( a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger}\cdots a_{j_{n}}^{\dagger}\right) =\left\langle
\left. \Phi_{f}\left( I_{\mathcal{F}}\right) \right\vert \Phi_{f}\left(
a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger}\cdots a_{j_{n}}^{\dagger}\right) \right\rangle =0;1\leq
j_{1}<\cdots<j_{n}\leq r,1\leq n\leq r,
\end{equation}
this follows directly from 
\begin{equation}
a_{j_{1}}\cdots a_{j_{n}}\in\ker\left\{ \Phi_{f}\right\} ;1\leq
j_{1}<\cdots<j_{n}\leq r,1\leq n\leq r
\end{equation}
and that $\left\{ a_{1},\cdots,a_{r}\right\} $ generate a left ideal.

States that are vacuum states produce generalized quasi free Fock
representations (or Fock representations) that are not all unitarily
equivalent. Not all states are vacuum states and these produce non Fock
representations of the CAR.

$\limfunc{tr}$ $\equiv I_{\mathcal{F}}$ is not a vacuum state. This can be
seen by considering the values of $\limfunc{tr}$ \emph{on any} split non
self adjoint basis that satisfy the CAR which are given by 
\begin{align}
\limfunc{tr}\left\{ a_{j}^{\dagger}a_{k}\right\} & =\left\langle
\left\langle a_{j}^{\dagger}a_{k}\right\rangle \right\rangle _{0}=\frac{1}{2}%
\left\langle \left\langle \left( x_{j}-ix_{j+r}\right) \left(
x_{k}+ix_{k+r}\right) \right\rangle \right\rangle _{0}=\frac{1}{2}%
\left\langle \left\langle
x_{j}x_{k}-ix_{j+r}x_{k}+ix_{j}x_{k+r}+x_{j+r}x_{k+r}\right\rangle
\right\rangle _{0} \\
& =\frac{1}{2}\left\langle \left\langle
x_{j}x_{k}-ix_{j+r}x_{k}+ix_{j}x_{k+r}+x_{j+r}x_{k+r}\right\rangle
\right\rangle _{0}=\frac{1}{2}\delta_{jk}=\limfunc{tr}\left\{
a_{k}a_{j}^{\dagger}\right\} \\
\limfunc{tr}\left\{ a_{k}a_{j}^{\dagger}\right\} & =\left\langle
\left\langle a_{k}a_{j}^{\dagger}\right\rangle \right\rangle _{0}=\limfunc{tr%
}\left\{ \delta_{jk}-a_{j}^{\dagger}a_{k}\right\} =\delta_{jk}-\limfunc{tr}%
\left\{ a_{j}^{\dagger}a_{k}\right\} \\
& \Rightarrow\left\{ 
\begin{array}{c}
\limfunc{tr}\left\{ a_{k}a_{j}^{\dagger}\right\} =-\limfunc{tr}\left\{
a_{j}^{\dagger}a_{k}\right\} ;j\neq k \\ 
\limfunc{tr}\left\{ a_{j}a_{j}^{\dagger}\right\} =1-\limfunc{tr}\left\{
a_{j}^{\dagger}a_{j}\right\} ;j=k%
\end{array}
\right. \\
& \Rightarrow\limfunc{tr}\left\{ a_{j}a_{j}^{\dagger}\right\} =\limfunc{tr}%
\left\{ a_{j}^{\dagger}a_{j}\right\} =\frac{1}{2} \\
\limfunc{tr}\left\{ a_{j}a_{k}\right\} & =-\limfunc{tr}\left\{
a_{k}a_{j}\right\} =\limfunc{tr}\left\{ a_{k}a_{j}\right\} \Rightarrow%
\limfunc{tr}\left\{ a_{j}a_{k}\right\} =0
\end{align}
$\lceil$Thus 
\begin{equation}
\left\langle a_{j}a_{j}^{\dagger}\right\rangle _{0}=\left\langle
a_{j}^{\dagger}a_{j}\right\rangle _{0}=\frac{1}{2}\neq0
\end{equation}
and hence 
\begin{equation}
\left\{ a_{j}a_{j}^{\dagger},a_{j}^{\dagger}a_{j}\right\}
\notin\bigoplus\limits_{1\leq n\leq2r}\mathcal{F}_{n}
\end{equation}
as they contain components belonging to $\mathcal{F}_{0}\rfloor$.

The values on the associated (unnormalized) self adjoint bases are then 
\begin{equation}
\limfunc{tr}\left\{ x_{j}x_{k}\right\} =\delta_{jk}\left\langle \left\langle
x_{j}^{2}\right\rangle \right\rangle _{0}=\frac{1}{2}\delta _{jk};1\leq
j\leq2r.
\end{equation}
This shows that $\limfunc{tr}$ is \emph{NOT }a vacuum state. Hence the
representation $\Pi_{\limfunc{tr}}$ is \emph{NOT }a Fock representation and
in fact generates a type II$_{1}$ Von Neumann algebra. In contrast Fock
representations produce type I$_{r}$ Von Neumann algebras (maybe?). These
observations show that the left regular representation of the CAR/Clifford
algebra induced by the central state $\limfunc{tr}$ that defines its $%
c^{\ast}$-algebra norm, can thus be viewed as a type II$_{1}$ $w^{\ast}$%
-algebra/Von Neumann algebra.

\paragraph{Splitting of $\mathcal{F}_{1}$ into non adjoint subspaces.}

The number operators $\widehat{\mathcal{N}}_{a}$ based on $\left\{ \left.
a_{j}^{\dagger},a_{j}\right\vert 1\leq j\leq r\right\} $ split $\mathcal{F}%
_{1}$ into eigenspaces corresponding to the eigenvalues $+1$ and $-1$ i.e. 
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{F}_{1}=\mathbf{a}^{\dagger}\oplus\mathbf{a}
\end{equation}
and have the properties%
\begin{align}
\widehat{\mathcal{N}}_{a}\left( a_{j}^{\dagger}\right) & =\left[ \mathcal{N}%
_{a},a_{j}^{\dagger}\right] =\left[ \sum_{1\leq k\leq
r}a_{k}^{\dagger}a_{k},a_{j}^{\dagger}\right] =a_{j}^{\dagger} \\
\widehat{\mathcal{N}}_{a}\left( a_{j}\right) & =\left[ \mathcal{N}_{a},a_{j}%
\right] =\left[ \sum_{1\leq k\leq r}a_{k}^{\dagger}a_{k},a_{j}^{\dagger}%
\right] =-a_{j} \\
\left\langle \left. a_{k}\right\vert \widehat{\mathcal{N}}_{a}\left(
a_{j}^{\dagger}\right) \right\rangle & =\left\langle a_{k}^{\dagger }%
\widehat{\mathcal{N}}_{a}\left( a_{j}^{\dagger}\right) \right\rangle
_{0}=\left\langle \left. a_{k}\right\vert a_{j}^{\dagger}\right\rangle =%
\limfunc{tr}\left\{ a_{k}^{\dagger}a_{j}^{\dagger}\right\} =-\limfunc{tr}%
\left\{ a_{j}^{\dagger}a_{k}^{\dagger}\right\} \Rightarrow\limfunc{tr}%
\left\{ a_{k}^{\dagger}a_{j}^{\dagger}\right\} =0 \\
\left\langle \left. a_{k}^{\dagger}\right\vert \widehat{\mathcal{N}}%
_{a}\left( a_{j}^{\dagger}\right) \right\rangle & =\left\langle a_{k}^{{}}%
\widehat{\mathcal{N}}_{a}\left( a_{j}^{\dagger}\right) \right\rangle
_{0}=\left\langle \left. a_{k}^{\dagger}\right\vert a_{j}^{\dagger
}\right\rangle =\limfunc{tr}\left\{ a_{k}a_{j}^{\dagger}\right\} =\left\{ 
\begin{array}{c}
0\text{ if }k\neq j \\ 
\frac{1}{2}\text{ if }k=j%
\end{array}
\right. \\
\left\langle \left. a_{k}^{{}}\right\vert \widehat{\mathcal{N}}_{a}\left(
a_{j}^{{}}\right) \right\rangle & =\left\langle a_{k}^{\dagger }\widehat{%
\mathcal{N}}_{a}\left( a_{j}^{{}}\right) \right\rangle _{0}=\left\langle
\left. a_{k}^{{}}\right\vert -a_{j}^{{}}\right\rangle =-\limfunc{tr}\left\{
a_{k}^{\dagger}a_{j}^{{}}\right\} =\left\{ 
\begin{array}{c}
0\text{ if }k\neq j \\ 
-\frac{1}{2}\text{ if }k=j%
\end{array}
\right. \\
\limfunc{tr}\left\{ a_{k}^{\dagger}a_{k}^{{}}+a_{k}a_{k}^{\dagger }\right\}
& =\frac{1}{2}+\frac{1}{2}=1 \\
\dim\left\{ \mathbf{a}^{\dagger}\right\} & =\dim\left\{ \mathbf{a}\right\}
=r.
\end{align}

One also has the real linear conjugation maps%
\begin{align}
\left. \sigma_{a}\right\vert _{\mathcal{F}} & :\mathbf{a}^{\dagger
}\rightarrow\mathbf{a} \\
\left. \sigma_{a}\right\vert _{\mathcal{F}} & :\mathbf{a}\rightarrow \mathbf{%
a}^{\dagger}
\end{align}%
\begin{align}
\left. \dagger\right\vert _{\mathcal{F}} & :\mathbf{a}^{\dagger}\rightarrow%
\mathbf{a} \\
\left. \dagger\right\vert _{\mathcal{F}} & :\mathbf{a}\rightarrow \mathbf{a}%
^{\dagger}
\end{align}%
\begin{equation}
\left. \sigma_{a}\right\vert _{\mathcal{F}}=\sum_{1\leq j\leq r}\left\{
\left\vert a_{j}^{{}}\right) _{\mathcal{F}}\left( a_{j}^{\dagger}\right\vert
+\left\vert a_{j}^{\dagger}\right) _{\mathcal{F}}\left(
a_{j}^{{}}\right\vert \right\}
\end{equation}

$\left\{ a_{j}^{\dagger},a_{j}\right\} $ are partial isometries with
associated projectors $\left\{ n_{aj},h_{aj}\right\} $ as 
\begin{equation}
n_{aj}=a_{j}^{\dagger}a_{j};h_{aj}=a_{j}a_{j}^{%
\dagger};n_{aj}^{2}=n_{aj};h_{aj}^{2}=h_{aj}.
\end{equation}%
\begin{equation}
a_{j}^{\dagger}a_{j}+a_{j}a_{j}^{\dagger}=I_{\mathcal{F}}=n_{aj}+h_{aj}.
\end{equation}

\begin{theorem}
$\widehat{\mathcal{N}}_{a}\left( Q\right) =N_{a}Q\Longleftrightarrow
Q=\left\langle Q\right\rangle _{N_{a}}\in\mathcal{F}_{\left[ N_{a}\right]
};-\infty\leq N_{a}\leq\infty$
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Let $\widehat{\mathcal{N}}\left( Q\right) =mQ=m\sum\limits_{-\infty\leq
N_{a}\leq\infty}Q_{\left[ N_{a}\right] }=\sum\limits_{-\infty\leq
N_{a}\leq\infty}N_{a}Q_{\left[ N_{a}\right] }$ then $m=N_{a}$ and $\widehat{%
\mathcal{N}}\left( Q\right) =\widehat{\mathcal{N}}\left( Q_{\left[ N_{a}%
\right] }\right) =N_{a}Q_{\left[ N_{a}\right] }$

If $Q=Q_{\left[ N_{a}\right] }$ then $\widehat{\mathcal{N}}\left( Q_{\left[
N_{a}\right] }\right) =N_{a}Q_{\left[ N_{a}\right] }.$
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}
\begin{corollary}
$QQ^{\dagger}$ and $Q^{\dagger}Q\in\mathcal{F}_{\left[ 0\right] }=\mathcal{F}%
_{e}.$
\end{corollary}
\end{theorem}

\paragraph{Constructing Vacuum States}

If $f_{b}$ is a vacuum state then $\exists\left\{ \left. b_{j}^{\dagger
},b_{j}\right\vert 1\leq j\leq r\right\} $ such that%
\begin{align}
f_{b}\left( n_{bj}\right) & =f_{b}\left( b_{j}^{\dagger}b_{j}\right)
=f_{b}\left( b_{j}^{\dagger}\right) =f_{b}\left( b_{j}\right) =0 \\
f_{b}\left( h_{bj}\right) & =f_{b}\left( b_{j}b_{j}^{\dagger}\right)
=1=f_{b}\left( I_{\mathcal{F}}\right) ;1\leq j\leq r
\end{align}%
\begin{align}
n_{bj}D_{f_{b}} & =0;h_{bj}D_{f_{b}}=D_{f_{b}} \\
\limfunc{tr}\left\{ n_{bj}D_{f_{b}}\right\} & =0;\limfunc{tr}\left\{
h_{bj}D_{f_{b}}\right\} =1;1\leq j\leq r \\
\ker\left\{ \Phi_{f_{b}}\right\} & =\mathcal{F\times}\limfunc{ideal}\left\{
\left. b_{j}\right\vert 1\leq j\leq r\right\} .
\end{align}
Vacuum states are $\widehat{\mathcal{N}_{b}}$ guage invariant, correspond to
different complexifications of $\mathcal{F}_{1\mathbb{R}}$ and satisfy the
orthonormality relationships

\begin{equation}
f_{b}\left( \prod_{1\leq\alpha\leq N_{h}}b_{k_{\alpha}}\prod_{1\leq\beta\leq
N_{p}}b_{j_{\beta}}^{\dagger}\right) =\delta_{k_{1}\cdots
k_{N_{h}}j_{1}\cdots j_{N_{p}}}=\func{Tr}\left\{ \prod_{1\leq\alpha\leq
N_{h}}b_{k_{\alpha}}\prod_{1\leq\beta\leq
N_{p}}b_{j_{\beta}}^{\dagger}\left\vert \overset{}{\phi_{b}}\right\rangle
\left\langle \overset{}{\phi_{b}}\right\vert \right\} =\left\langle
\phi_{b}\left\vert \prod_{1\leq\alpha\leq
N_{h}}b_{k_{\alpha}}\prod_{1\leq\beta\leq
N_{p}}b_{j_{\beta}}^{\dagger}\right. \phi_{b}\right\rangle .
\end{equation}
The states 
\begin{equation}
\left\vert \overset{}{\phi_{b}}\right\rangle \left\langle \overset{}{\phi_{b}%
}\right\vert =\exp\left\{ \tsum \limits_{1\leq j<k\leq
r}g_{jk}a_{j}^{\dagger}a_{k}^{\dagger}\right\} \left\vert \overset{}{\phi_{a}%
}\right\rangle \left\langle \overset{}{\phi_{a}}\right\vert \exp\left\{
\tsum \limits_{1\leq j<k\leq r}g_{jk}a_{j}^{\dagger}a_{k}^{\dagger}\right\}
^{\dagger}
\end{equation}
are vacuum states where $\left\{ a_{j}^{\dagger},a_{j}\right\} $ refer to
standerd fermion particles. The dressed states 
\begin{equation}
\prod_{1\leq\beta\leq N_{p}}b_{j_{\beta}}^{\dagger}\left\vert \overset{}{%
\phi _{b}}\right\rangle \left\langle \overset{}{\phi_{b}}\right\vert \left(
\prod_{1\leq\beta\leq N_{p}}b_{j_{\beta}}\right) ^{\dagger}
\end{equation}%
\begin{equation}
=\prod_{1\leq\beta\leq N_{p}}b_{j_{\beta}}^{\dagger}\exp\left\{ \tsum
\limits_{1\leq j<k\leq r}g_{jk}a_{j}^{\dagger}a_{k}^{\dagger}\right\}
\left\vert \overset{}{\phi_{a}}\right\rangle \left\langle \overset{}{\phi_{a}%
}\right\vert \exp\left\{ \tsum \limits_{1\leq j<k\leq
r}g_{jk}a_{j}^{\dagger}a_{k}^{\dagger}\right\} ^{\dagger}\left( \prod
_{1\leq\beta\leq N_{p}}b_{j_{\beta}}^{\dagger}\right) ^{\dagger},
\end{equation}
are also vacuum states, where 
\begin{equation}
b_{l}\exp\left\{ \tsum \limits_{1\leq j<k\leq
r}g_{jk}a_{j}^{\dagger}a_{k}^{\dagger}\right\} \left\vert \overset{}{\phi_{a}%
}\right\rangle =0;1\leq j\leq r
\end{equation}
for some $g$. The states 
\begin{equation}
\prod_{1\leq\beta\leq N_{p}}c_{j_{\beta}}^{\dagger}\exp\left\{ \tsum
\limits_{1\leq j<k\leq r}g_{jk}b_{j}^{\dagger}b_{k}^{\dagger}\right\}
\left\vert \overset{}{\phi_{b}}\right\rangle \left\langle \overset{}{\phi_{b}%
}\right\vert \exp\left\{ \tsum \limits_{1\leq j<k\leq
r}g_{jk}b_{j}^{\dagger}b_{k}^{\dagger}\right\} ^{\dagger}\left( \prod
_{1\leq\beta\leq N_{p}}c_{j_{\beta}}^{\dagger}\right) ^{\dagger},
\end{equation}
are also vacuum states where 
\begin{equation}
c_{l}\exp\left\{ \tsum \limits_{1\leq j<k\leq
r}g_{jk}b_{j}^{\dagger}b_{k}^{\dagger}\right\} \left\vert \overset{}{\phi_{b}%
}\right\rangle =0;1\leq j\leq r,
\end{equation}
$\lceil$which can be further expanded to give%
\begin{equation}
c_{l}\exp\left\{ \tsum \limits_{1\leq j<k\leq
r}g_{jk}b_{j}^{\dagger}b_{k}^{\dagger}\right\} \exp\left\{ \tsum
\limits_{1\leq j<k\leq r}g_{jk}a_{j}^{\dagger}a_{k}^{\dagger}\right\}
\left\vert \overset{}{\phi_{a}}\right\rangle =0;1\leq j\leq r
\end{equation}
$\rfloor$

and $\left\{ \left. c_{j}\right\vert 1\leq j\leq r\right\} $ are general
Bogoliubon annihilators.

$\left\vert \overset{}{\phi_{b}}\right\rangle $ are $O\left( 2r,\mathbb{R}%
\right) $ coherent states i.e. 
\begin{equation}
\left\vert \overset{}{\phi_{b}}\right\rangle =\exp\left\{ \tsum
\limits_{1\leq j<k\leq r}z_{jk}a_{j}^{\dagger}a_{k}^{\dagger}\right\}
\exp\left\{ \tsum \limits_{1\leq j,k\leq
r}h_{jk}a_{j}^{\dagger}a_{k}\right\} \exp\left\{ \tsum \limits_{1\leq
j<k\leq r}\overline{z}_{jk}a_{j}a_{k}\right\} \left\vert \overset{}{\phi_{a}}%
\right\rangle =\exp\left\{ \tsum \limits_{1\leq j<k\leq
r}z_{jk}a_{j}^{\dagger}a_{k}^{\dagger}\right\} \left\vert \overset{}{\phi_{a}%
}\right\rangle .
\end{equation}
$\rfloor$

\paragraph{GNS representations produced by vacuum (quasi free) states}

\begin{equation}
\mathcal{H}_{f_{a}}=\Phi_{f_{a}}\left( \mathcal{F}\right)
=\Phi_{f_{a}}\left( \mathcal{F}_{a}\right) =\Phi_{f_{a}}\left( \tbigwedge 
\mathcal{F}_{1}\right) =\Phi_{f_{a}}\left( \tbigwedge \mathbf{a}%
^{\dagger}\right) =\tbigwedge \Phi_{f_{a}}\left( \mathbf{a}^{\dagger}\right)
\end{equation}%
\begin{equation}
\Pi_{f_{a}}\left( \mathcal{F}\right) \subseteq\mathcal{B}\left( \mathcal{H}%
_{f_{a}}\right)
\end{equation}%
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{B}\left( \mathcal{H}_{f_{a}}\right) =\limfunc{linspan}\left\{
\left. \left\vert \Phi_{f_{a}}\left( a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger}\cdots
a_{j_{p}}^{\dagger}\right) \right\rangle \left\langle \Phi_{f_{a}}\left(
a_{k_{1}}^{\dagger}\cdots a_{k_{q}}^{\dagger}\right) \right\vert \right\vert
1\leq j_{1}<\cdots<j_{p}\leq r,1\leq k_{1}<\cdots<k_{q}\leq r,0\leq p,q\leq
r.\right\}
\end{equation}
where 
\begin{equation}
\left\{ \left. \Phi_{f_{a}}\left( a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger}\cdots
a_{j_{p}}^{\dagger}\right) \right\vert 1\leq j_{1}<\cdots<j_{p}\leq r,0\leq
p\leq r\right\}
\end{equation}
is a conb for $\mathcal{H}_{f_{a}}$ i.e. 
\begin{align}
\left\langle \left. \Phi_{f_{a}}\left( a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger}\cdots
a_{j_{n}}^{\dagger}\right) \right\vert \Phi_{f_{a}}\left(
a_{k_{1}}^{\dagger}\cdots a_{k_{m}}^{\dagger}\right) \right\rangle &
=f_{a}\left( a_{j_{n}}\cdots a_{j_{1}}a_{k_{1}}^{\dagger}\cdots
a_{k_{m}}^{\dagger}\right) =\limfunc{tr}\left\{ a_{j_{n}}\cdots
a_{j_{1}}a_{k_{1}}^{\dagger}\cdots a_{k_{m}}^{\dagger}D_{f_{a}}\right\}
=\delta_{j_{1}\cdots j_{p}k_{1}\cdots k_{q}} \\
1 & \leq j_{1}<\cdots<j_{p}\leq r,1\leq k_{1}<\cdots<k_{q}\leq r,0\leq
p,q\leq r
\end{align}%
\begin{align}
\left\vert \overset{}{\phi_{a}}\right\rangle \left\langle \overset{}{\phi_{a}%
}\right\vert & =\mathcal{N}\left( \prod_{1\leq\beta\leq
N_{p}}b_{j_{\beta}}^{\dagger}\right) \exp\left\{ \tsum \limits_{1\leq
j<k\leq r}g_{jk}b_{j}^{\dagger}b_{k}^{\dagger}\right\} \left\vert \overset{}{%
\phi_{b}}\right\rangle \left\langle \overset{}{\phi_{b}}\right\vert
\exp\left\{ \tsum \limits_{1\leq j<k\leq
r}g_{jk}b_{j}^{\dagger}b_{k}^{\dagger}\right\} ^{\dagger}\left( \prod
_{1\leq\beta\leq N_{p}}b_{j_{\beta}}^{\dagger}\right) ^{\dagger} \\
\dprod \limits_{1\leq l\leq r}h_{ai} & =\left( \prod_{1\leq\beta\leq
N_{p}}b_{j_{\beta}}^{\dagger }\right) \exp\left\{ \tsum \limits_{1\leq
j<k\leq r}g_{jk}b_{j}^{\dagger}b_{k}^{\dagger}\right\} \dprod \limits_{1\leq
l\leq r}h_{bi}\exp\left\{ \tsum \limits_{1\leq j<k\leq
r}g_{jk}b_{j}^{\dagger}b_{k}^{\dagger}\right\} ^{\dagger}\left( \prod
_{1\leq\beta\leq N_{p}}b_{j_{\beta}}^{\dagger}\right) ^{\dagger} \\
& =Q_{\phi_{a}}Q_{\phi_{a}}^{\dagger}
\end{align}
where 
\begin{align}
h_{ai} & =a_{l}a_{l}^{\dagger};1\leq l\leq r \\
h_{bi} & =b_{l}b_{l}^{\dagger};1\leq l\leq r \\
Q_{\phi_{a}} & =\left( -1\right) ^{r}\left( \prod_{1\leq\beta\leq
N_{p}}b_{j_{\beta}}^{\dagger}\right) \exp\left\{ \tsum \limits_{1\leq
j<k\leq r}g_{jk}b_{j}^{\dagger}b_{k}^{\dagger}\right\} \dprod \limits_{1\leq
l\leq r}b_{i}
\end{align}%
\begin{equation*}
A_{j}^{\dagger}=\left\vert \Phi_{f_{a}}\left( a_{j}^{\dagger}\right)
\right\rangle \left\langle \Phi_{f_{a}}\left( I_{\mathcal{F}}\right)
\right\vert +\sum_{1\leq p\leq\infty}\sum_{1\leq
j_{1}<\cdots<j_{p}\leq\infty }\left\vert \Phi_{f_{a}}\left(
a_{j}^{\dagger}a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger}\cdots a_{j_{p}}^{\dagger}\right)
\right\rangle \left\langle \Phi_{f_{a}}\left( a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger}\cdots
a_{j_{p}}^{\dagger}\right) \right\vert 
\end{equation*}%
\begin{align}
A_{j}^{\dagger}\left\vert \Phi_{f_{a}}\left( a_{k_{1}}^{\dagger}\cdots
a_{k_{q}}^{\dagger}\right) \right\rangle & =\left\vert \Phi_{f_{a}}\left(
a_{j}^{\dagger}\right) \right\rangle \left\langle \Phi_{f_{a}}\left( I_{%
\mathcal{F}}\right) \right\vert \left\vert \Phi_{f_{a}}\left(
a_{k_{1}}^{\dagger}\cdots a_{k_{q}}^{\dagger}\right) \right\rangle \\
& +\sum_{1\leq p\leq\infty}\sum_{1\leq j_{1}<\cdots<j_{p}\leq\infty
}\left\vert \Phi_{f_{a}}\left( a_{j}^{\dagger}a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger}\cdots
a_{j_{p}}^{\dagger}\right) \right\rangle \left\langle \Phi_{f_{a}}\left(
a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger}\cdots a_{j_{p}}^{\dagger}\right) \right\vert \left\vert
\Phi_{f_{a}}\left( a_{k_{1}}^{\dagger}\cdots a_{k_{q}}^{\dagger}\right)
\right\rangle \\
& =0+\sum_{1\leq p\leq\infty}\sum_{1\leq j_{1}<\cdots<j_{p}\leq\infty
}\left\vert \Phi_{f_{a}}\left( a_{j}^{\dagger}a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger}\cdots
a_{j_{p}}^{\dagger}\right) \right\rangle \delta_{j_{1}\cdots
j_{p}k_{1}\cdots k_{q}} \\
& =\left\vert \Phi_{f_{a}}\left( a_{j}^{\dagger}a_{k_{1}}^{\dagger}\cdots
a_{k_{q}}^{\dagger}\right) \right\rangle
\end{align}
i.e. 
\begin{equation}
A_{j}^{\dagger}\left\vert \Phi_{f_{a}}\left( a_{k_{1}}^{\dagger}\cdots
a_{k_{q}}^{\dagger}\right) \right\rangle =\Pi_{f_{a}}\left( a_{j}^{\dagger
}\right) \left\vert \Phi_{f_{a}}\left( a_{k_{1}}^{\dagger}\cdots
a_{k_{q}}^{\dagger}\right) \right\rangle
\end{equation}
In order for the grade preserving property of the vacuum vector to exist the
following conditions must be fulfilled 
\begin{equation}
\Phi_{f_{a}}\left( I_{\mathcal{F}}\right) \wedge\Phi_{f_{a}}\left(
a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger}\cdots a_{j_{p}}^{\dagger}\right) =\Phi_{f_{a}}\left( I_{%
\mathcal{F}}\wedge a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger}\cdots a_{j_{p}}^{\dagger}\right)
=\Phi_{f_{a}}\left( a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger}\cdots a_{j_{p}}^{\dagger}\right)
;1\leq j_{1}<\cdots<j_{p}\leq r,0\leq p\leq r.
\end{equation}

\paragraph{Splitting of $\mathcal{F}_{1}$ into self adjoint subspaces}

\begin{equation}
\mathcal{F}_{1}=\mathbf{x}_{1}+_{\mathbb{C}}\mathbf{x}_{2}
\end{equation}
Is it is possible to have states $f$ that have the property 
\begin{equation}
\ker\left\{ \Phi_{f}\right\} \supset\mathbf{x}_{2}
\end{equation}
or 
\begin{equation}
\ker\left\{ \Phi_{f}\right\} \supset\mathbf{x}_{1}?
\end{equation}
If this was true then 
\begin{equation}
f\left( x_{1j}^{2}\right) =0
\end{equation}
or 
\begin{equation}
f\left( x_{2j}^{2}\right) =0
\end{equation}
but 
\begin{equation}
x_{1j}^{2}=x_{2j}^{2}=\frac{1}{2}I_{\mathcal{F}},
\end{equation}
thus $f$ cannot be a state.

\subsubsection{Exterior Algebra Generated by the GNS\ Construction}

The GNS\ Hilbert space generated by the state $f$ 
\begin{equation}
\Phi_{f}\left( \mathcal{F}\right) =\limfunc{linspan}\left\{ \left.
\Phi_{f}\left( A_{j}\right) \right\vert 1\leq j\leq2^{2r}\right\} ,
\end{equation}
where $\left\{ \left. A_{j}\right\vert 1\leq j\leq2^{2r}\right\} $ is a
basis for $\mathcal{F}$ and has metric matrix if $f$ is a normal state 
\begin{equation}
\left\langle \left. \Phi_{f}\left( A_{j}\right) \right\vert \Phi_{f}\left(
A_{k}\right) \right\rangle =f\left( A_{j}^{\dagger}A_{k}\right) =\limfunc{tr}%
\left\{ A_{j}^{\dagger}A_{k}D_{f}\right\} ;1\leq j,k\leq2^{2r}.
\end{equation}

$\mathcal{F}_{1}$ generates $\mathcal{F}$ via the exterior algebra
construction 
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{F}=\tbigwedge \mathcal{F}_{1}
\end{equation}
and thus $\Phi_{f}\left( \mathcal{F}\right) $ by 
\begin{equation}
\Phi_{f}\left( \mathcal{F}\right) =\Phi_{f}\left( \tbigwedge \mathcal{F}%
_{1}\right) .
\end{equation}
It is important to examine whether $\Phi_{f}$ is an exterior algebra map
(thus an exterior algebra homomorphism) i.e. 
\begin{align}
\Phi_{f}\left( \tbigwedge \mathcal{F}_{1}\right) & =\tbigwedge
\Phi_{f}\left( \mathcal{F}_{1}\right) \\
\Phi_{f}\left( A\wedge B\right) & =\Phi_{f}\left( A\right) \wedge
\Phi_{f}\left( B\right) \\
\Phi_{f}\left( \alpha A+\beta B\right) & =\alpha\Phi_{f}\left( A\right)
+\beta\Phi_{f}\left( B\right) \forall A,B\in\mathcal{F},\alpha,\beta \in%
\mathbb{C}.
\end{align}
This is straightforward and shown in Greub (chapter 5, second edition) for
any well defined exterior multiplication in $\mathcal{F}$ if $\Phi_{f}$ is
linear and is in fact a homomorphism i.e. 
\begin{equation}
\Phi_{f}\left( A\wedge B\right) =\Phi_{f}\left( A\right) \wedge\Phi
_{f}\left( B\right) \,\forall A,B\in\mathcal{F},
\end{equation}
\emph{however we will show that }$\Phi_{f}$ is in some cases \emph{not} a
homomorphism and not an exterior algebra map $\mathcal{F\rightarrow}\Phi
_{f}\left( \mathcal{F}\right) $.

If $\Phi_{f}$ was an exterior algebra map and 
\begin{equation}
B\in\ker\left\{ \Phi_{f}\right\}
\end{equation}
then 
\begin{align}
\Phi_{f}\left( A\wedge B\right) & =\Phi_{f}\left( A\right) \wedge
\Phi_{f}\left( B\right) =0,\forall A\in\mathcal{F},\forall\in\ker\left\{
\Phi_{f}\right\} \\
\Phi_{f}\left( B\wedge A\right) & =\Phi_{f}\left( B\right) \wedge
\Phi_{f}\left( A\right) =0,\forall A\in\mathcal{F},\forall\in\ker\left\{
\Phi_{f}\right\}
\end{align}
and $\ker\left\{ \Phi_{f}\right\} $ would be a $2$-sided ideal wrt "$\wedge".
$

Self adjoint, $\left\{ \mathbf{x_{1},x}_{2}\right\} ,$ and non self adjoint, 
$\left\{ \mathbf{a}^{\dagger},\mathbf{a}\right\} ,$ splitting bases of $%
\mathcal{F}_{1}$ generate $\mathcal{F}$ and $\Phi_{f}\left( \mathcal{F}%
\right) $ and have the following property 
\begin{equation}
\Phi_{f}\left( \mathcal{F}\right) =\bigwedge\Phi_{f}\left( \mathcal{F}%
_{1}\right) =\bigwedge\left( \Phi_{f}\left( \mathbf{x}_{1}\oplus _{\mathbb{C}%
}\mathbf{x}_{2}\cong\mathbf{x}_{1}+_{\mathbb{C}}\mathbf{x}_{2}\right)
\right) =\bigwedge\Phi_{f}\left( \mathbf{x}_{1}\right)
\otimes\bigwedge\Phi_{f}\left( \mathbf{x}_{2}\right) =\mathcal{H}_{f}
\end{equation}
and%
\begin{equation}
\Phi_{f}\left( \mathcal{F}\right) =\bigwedge\Phi_{f}\left( \mathcal{F}%
_{1}\right) =\bigwedge\left( \Phi_{f}\left( \mathbf{a}^{\dagger }+_{\mathbb{C%
}}\mathbf{a\cong a}^{\dagger}\oplus_{\mathbb{C}}\mathbf{a}\right) \right)
=\bigwedge\Phi_{f}\left( \mathbf{a}^{\dagger}\right)
\otimes\bigwedge\Phi_{f}\left( \mathbf{a}\right) =\mathcal{H}_{f}
\end{equation}
for all states $f$.

\emph{For non self adjoint splitting bases and vacuum states }$f_{a}$ \emph{%
one has the important identities} 
\begin{equation}
\bigwedge\left( \Phi_{f}\left( \mathbf{a}^{\dagger}+_{\mathbb{C}}\mathbf{%
a\cong a}^{\dagger}\oplus_{\mathbb{C}}\mathbf{a}\right) \right)
=\bigwedge\Phi_{f}\left( \mathbf{a}^{\dagger}\right) \otimes\bigwedge
\Phi_{f}\left( \mathbf{a}\right) =\bigwedge\left( \Phi_{f}\left( \mathbf{a}%
^{\dagger}\right) \right) =\Phi_{f}\left( \mathcal{F}\right) =\mathcal{H}%
_{f},
\end{equation}
Thus to emphasize this result if $f_{a}$ is a vacuum state corresponding to $%
\left\{ \mathbf{a}^{\dagger}\right\} $ 
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{H}_{f_{a}}=\bigwedge\left( \Phi_{f_{a}}\left( \mathbf{a}^{\dagger
}\right) \right) =\Phi_{f_{a}}\left( \bigwedge\left( \mathbf{a}^{\dagger
}\right) \right) .
\end{equation}
$\lceil$%
\begin{equation}
\bigwedge\Phi_{f}\left( \mathbf{a}^{\dagger}\right) \otimes\bigwedge\Phi
_{f}\left( \mathbf{a}\right) =\left\{ \left( \Phi_{f}\left( \mathbf{a}%
^{\dagger}\right) ,\Phi_{f}\left( \mathbf{a}\right) \right) \right\}
=\left\{ \left( \Phi_{f}\left( \mathbf{a}^{\dagger}\right) ,0\right)
\right\} \cong\Phi_{f}\left( \mathbf{a}^{\dagger}\right) .
\end{equation}
$\rfloor$

The metric matrix for the basis $\left\{ \left. \Phi_{f_{a}}\left( I_{%
\mathcal{F}}\right) ,\Phi_{f_{a}}\left( a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger}\cdots
a_{j_{n}}^{\dagger}\right) \right\vert 1\leq j_{1}<\cdots<j_{n}\leq r,1\leq
n\leq r\right\} $ of $\bigwedge\Phi_{f_{a}}\left( \mathbf{a}^{\dagger
}\right) $ for the vacuum state $f_{a}$ is given by 
\begin{equation}
\left\langle \left. \Phi_{f_{a}}\left( a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger}\cdots
a_{j_{n}}^{\dagger}\right) \right\vert \Phi_{f_{a}}\left(
a_{k_{1}}^{\dagger}\cdots a_{k_{m}}^{\dagger}\right) \right\rangle
=f_{a}\left( a_{j_{n}}\cdots a_{j_{1}}a_{k_{1}}^{\dagger}\cdots
a_{k_{m}}^{\dagger}\right) =\limfunc{tr}\left\{ a_{j_{n}}\cdots
a_{j_{1}}a_{k_{1}}^{\dagger}\cdots a_{k_{m}}^{\dagger}D_{f_{a}}\right\}
\end{equation}
and%
\begin{align}
\left\langle \left. \Phi_{f_{a}}\left( I_{\mathcal{F}}\right) \right\vert
\Phi_{f_{a}}\left( a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger}\cdots a_{j_{n}}^{\dagger}\right)
\right\rangle & =f_{a}\left( a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger}\cdots a_{j_{n}}^{\dagger
}\right) =\limfunc{tr}\left\{ a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger}\cdots
a_{j_{n}}^{\dagger}D_{f_{a}}\right\} =0 \\
\left\langle \left. \Phi_{f_{a}}\left( I_{\mathcal{F}}\right) \right\vert
\Phi_{f_{a}}\left( I_{\mathcal{F}}\right) \right\rangle & =1,
\end{align}
where 
\begin{align}
1 & \leq j_{1}<\cdots<j_{n}\leq r,1\leq n\leq r \\
1 & \leq k_{1}<\cdots<k_{m}\leq r,1\leq m\leq r.
\end{align}
$\lceil$%
\begin{align}
& \left\langle \left. \Phi_{f_{a}}\left( a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger}\cdots
a_{j_{n}}^{\dagger}a_{k_{1}}\cdots a_{k_{m}}\right) \right\vert
\Phi_{f_{a}}\left( a_{p_{1}}^{\dagger}\cdots
a_{p_{n}}^{\dagger}a_{q_{1}}\cdots a_{q_{m}}\right) \right\rangle
=f_{a}\left( a_{k_{m}}^{\dagger}\cdots a_{k_{1}}^{\dagger}a_{j_{n}}\cdots
a_{j_{1}}a_{p_{1}}^{\dagger}\cdots a_{p_{n}}^{\dagger}a_{q_{1}}\cdots
a_{q_{m}}\right) \\
& =\limfunc{tr}\left\{ a_{k_{m}}^{\dagger}\cdots a_{k_{1}}^{\dagger
}a_{j_{n}}\cdots a_{j_{1}}a_{p_{1}}^{\dagger}\cdots a_{p_{n}}^{\dagger
}a_{q_{1}}\cdots a_{q_{m}}D_{f}\right\} =\left\langle \left. \phi
_{a}\right\vert a_{k_{m}}^{\dagger}\cdots a_{k_{1}}^{\dagger}a_{j_{n}}\cdots
a_{j_{1}}a_{p_{1}}^{\dagger}\cdots a_{p_{n}}^{\dagger}a_{q_{1}}\cdots
a_{q_{m}}\phi_{a}\right\rangle =0
\end{align}%
\begin{align}
& \left\langle \left. \Phi_{f_{a}}\left( a_{k_{1}}\cdots
a_{k_{m}}a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger}\cdots a_{j_{n}}^{\dagger}\right) \right\vert
\Phi_{f_{a}}\left( a_{p_{1}}^{\dagger}\cdots
a_{p_{n}}^{\dagger}a_{q_{1}}\cdots a_{q_{m}}\right) \right\rangle =f\left(
a_{j_{n}}\cdots a_{j_{1}}a_{k_{m}}^{\dagger}\cdots
a_{k_{1}}^{\dagger}a_{p_{1}}^{\dagger}\cdots
a_{p_{n}}^{\dagger}a_{q_{1}}\cdots a_{q_{m}}\right) \\
& =\limfunc{tr}\left\{ a_{j_{n}}\cdots a_{j_{1}}a_{k_{m}}^{\dagger }\cdots
a_{k_{1}}^{\dagger}a_{p_{1}}^{\dagger}\cdots a_{p_{n}}^{\dagger
}a_{q_{1}}\cdots a_{q_{m}}D_{f}\right\} =\left\langle \left. \phi
_{a}\right\vert a_{j_{n}}\cdots a_{j_{1}}a_{k_{m}}^{\dagger}\cdots
a_{k_{1}}^{\dagger}a_{p_{1}}^{\dagger}\cdots
a_{p_{n}}^{\dagger}a_{q_{1}}\cdots a_{q_{m}}\phi_{a}\right\rangle =0
\end{align}%
\begin{align}
& \left\langle \left. \Phi_{f_{a}}\left( a_{k_{1}}\cdots
a_{k_{m}}a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger}\cdots a_{j_{n}}^{\dagger}\right) \right\vert
\Phi_{f_{a}}\left( a_{q_{1}}\cdots a_{q_{m}}a_{p_{1}}^{\dagger}\cdots
a_{p_{n}}^{\dagger}\right) \right\rangle =f_{a}\left( a_{k_{1}}\cdots
a_{k_{m}}a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger}\cdots a_{j_{n}}^{\dagger}a_{q_{1}}\cdots
a_{q_{m}}a_{p_{1}}^{\dagger}\cdots a_{p_{n}}^{\dagger}\right) \\
& =\limfunc{tr}\left\{ a_{j_{n}}\cdots a_{j_{1}}a_{k_{m}}^{\dagger }\cdots
a_{k_{1}}^{\dagger}a_{q_{1}}\cdots a_{q_{m}}a_{p_{1}}^{\dagger}\cdots
a_{p_{n}}^{\dagger}D_{f}\right\} =\left\langle \left. \phi_{a}\right\vert
a_{j_{n}}\cdots a_{j_{1}}a_{k_{m}}^{\dagger}\cdots
a_{k_{1}}^{\dagger}a_{q_{1}}\cdots a_{q_{m}}a_{p_{1}}^{\dagger}\cdots
a_{p_{n}}^{\dagger}\phi _{a}\right\rangle \overset{\text{in general}}{\neq}0
\end{align}
$\rfloor$

If $f$ is a \emph{vacuum state} then exists some complete orthonormal basis $%
\left\{ \left. \Phi_{f}\left( I_{\mathcal{F}}\right) ,\Phi_{f}\left(
b_{j_{1}}^{\dagger}\cdots b_{j_{n}}^{\dagger}\right) \right\vert 1\leq
j_{1}<\cdots<j_{n}\leq r,1\leq n\leq r\right\} ,$which for simplicity we
will denote by $\left\{ \left. \Phi_{f}\left( I_{\mathcal{F}}\right) ,\Phi
_{f}\left( a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger}\cdots a_{j_{n}}^{\dagger}\right) \right\vert
1\leq j_{1}<\cdots<j_{n}\leq r,1\leq n\leq r\right\} ,$ \emph{WHILE not
implying that it refers to the naked vacuum state }$\left\vert \phi
_{a}\right\rangle \left\langle \phi_{a}\right\vert $\emph{. }

\paragraph{Exterior Products}

The CAR gives%
\begin{align}
a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger}a_{j_{1}}+a_{j_{1}}a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger} & =\frac{1}{2}%
\left( x_{j_{1}}-ix_{j_{1}+r}\right) \left( x_{j_{1}}+ix_{j_{1}+r}\right) +%
\frac{1}{2}\left( x_{j_{1}}+ix_{j_{1}+r}\right) \left(
x_{j_{1}}-ix_{j_{1}+r}\right) \\
& =\frac{1}{2}\left(
x_{j_{1}}x_{j_{1}}+ix_{j_{1}}x_{j_{1}+r}-ix_{j_{1}+r}x_{j_{1}}+x_{j_{1}+r}x_{j_{1}+r}+x_{j_{1}}x_{j_{1}}-ix_{j_{1}}x_{j_{1}+r}+ix_{j_{1}+r}x_{j_{1}}+x_{j_{1}+r}x_{j_{1}+r}\right)
\\
& =x_{j_{1}}x_{j_{1}}+x_{j_{1}+r}x_{j_{1}+r}=I_{\mathcal{F}}
\end{align}
as it should.

The Clifford wedge product in $\bigwedge\left( \mathbf{a}^{\dagger}\right)
\equiv\bigwedge\left( \mathcal{F}_{\mathbf{a}^{\dagger}}\right) \mathcal{\ }$
is given by 
\begin{align}
& a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger}\wedge a_{j_{2}}^{\dagger}=\left\langle
a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger}a_{j_{2}}^{\dagger}\right\rangle _{2}=\frac{1}{2}%
\left\langle \left( x_{j_{1}}-ix_{j_{1}+r}\right) \left(
x_{j_{2}}-ix_{j_{2}+r}\right) \right\rangle _{2} \\
& =\left\{ 
\begin{array}{c}
\frac{1}{2}\left( \left\langle x_{j_{1}}x_{j_{1}}\right\rangle
_{2}-\left\langle x_{j_{1}+r}x_{j_{1}+r}\right\rangle _{2}-i\left(
\left\langle x_{j_{1}}x_{j_{1}+r}\right\rangle _{2}+\left\langle
x_{j_{1}+r}x_{j_{1}}\right\rangle _{2}\right) \right) =0\text{ if }%
j_{1}=j_{2} \\ 
\frac{1}{2}\left( \left( x_{j_{1}}x_{j_{2}}-x_{j_{1}+r}x_{j_{2}+r}\right)
-i\left( x_{j_{1}}x_{j_{2}+r}+x_{j_{1}+r}x_{j_{2}}\right) \right) \text{ }%
\neq0\text{ if }j_{1}\neq j_{2},%
\end{array}
\right. ,
\end{align}
which also gives via the adjoint operation the Clifford wedge product in $%
\bigwedge\left( \mathbf{a}\right) $ 
\begin{align}
& a_{j_{1}}\wedge a_{j_{2}}=\left\langle a_{j_{1}}a_{j_{2}}\right\rangle
_{2}=\frac{1}{2}\left\langle \left( x_{j_{1}}+ix_{j_{1}+r}\right) \left(
x_{j_{2}}+ix_{j_{2}+r}\right) \right\rangle _{2} \\
& =\left\{ 
\begin{array}{c}
\frac{1}{2}\left( \left\langle x_{j_{1}+r}x_{j_{1}+r}\right\rangle
_{2}-\left\langle x_{j_{1}}x_{j_{1}}\right\rangle _{2}++i\left( \left\langle
x_{j_{1}}x_{j_{1}+r}\right\rangle _{2}+\left\langle
x_{j_{1}+r}x_{j_{1}}\right\rangle _{2}\right) \right) =0\text{ if }%
j_{1}=j_{2} \\ 
\frac{1}{2}\left( \left( x_{j_{1}+r}x_{j_{2}+r}-x_{j_{1}}x_{j_{2}}\right)
+i\left( x_{j_{1}}x_{j_{2}+r}+x_{j_{1}+r}x_{j_{2}}\right) \right) \text{ }%
\neq0\text{ if }j_{1}\neq j_{2},%
\end{array}
\right. ,
\end{align}
where 
\begin{equation}
1\leq j_{1},j_{2}\leq r.
\end{equation}
This shows that 
\begin{align}
a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger}\wedge a_{j_{2}}^{\dagger} & =-a_{j_{2}}^{\dagger}\wedge
a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger}=a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger}a_{j_{2}}^{\dagger}\quad\text{ }%
j_{1}\neq j_{2} \\
a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger}\wedge a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger} & =0
\end{align}
and%
\begin{align}
a_{j_{1}}\wedge a_{j_{2}} & =-a_{j_{2}}\wedge
a_{j_{1}}=a_{j_{1}}a_{j_{2}}\quad\text{ }j_{1}\neq j_{2} \\
a_{j_{1}}\wedge a_{j_{1}} & =0.
\end{align}
The wedge product of $a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger}$ and $a_{j_{2}}$ is given by 
\begin{align}
a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger}\wedge a_{j_{2}} & =\left\langle a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger
}a_{j_{2}}\right\rangle _{2}=\frac{1}{2}\left\langle \left(
x_{j_{1}}-ix_{j_{1}+r}\right) \left( x_{j_{2}}+ix_{j_{2}+r}\right)
\right\rangle _{2} \\
& =\frac{1}{2}\left( \left( \left\langle x_{j_{1}}x_{j_{2}}\right\rangle
_{2}+\left\langle x_{j_{1}+r}x_{j_{2}+r}\right\rangle _{2}\right) +i\left(
\left\langle x_{j_{1}}x_{j_{2}+r}\right\rangle _{2}-\left\langle
x_{j_{1}+r}x_{j_{2}}\right\rangle _{2}\right) \right) \\
& =\frac{1}{2}\left( \left( x_{j_{1}}x_{j_{2}}+x_{j_{1}+r}x_{j_{2}+r}\right)
+i\left( x_{j_{1}}x_{j_{2}+r}-x_{j_{1}+r}x_{j_{2}}\right) \right) \text{ },
\end{align}
thus if $j_{1}\neq j_{2}$%
\begin{equation}
a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger}\wedge a_{j_{2}}=\frac{1}{2}\left( \left(
x_{j_{1}}x_{j_{2}}+x_{j_{1}+r}x_{j_{2}+r}\right) +i\left(
x_{j_{1}}x_{j_{2}+r}-x_{j_{1}+r}x_{j_{2}}\right) \right) =-a_{j_{2}}\wedge
a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger}
\end{equation}
as 
\begin{equation}
a_{j_{2}}a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger}=-a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger}a_{j_{2}}.
\end{equation}
If $j_{1}=j_{2}$ 
\begin{equation}
a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger}\wedge a_{j_{1}}=ix_{j_{1}}x_{j_{1}+r}
\end{equation}
and $a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger}\wedge a_{j_{1}}$ is self adjoint i.e. 
\begin{equation}
\left( a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger}\wedge a_{j_{1}}\right) ^{\dagger}=\left(
ix_{j_{1}}x_{j_{1}+r}\right)
^{\dagger}=-ix_{j_{1}+r}x_{j_{1}}=ix_{j_{1}}x_{j_{1}+r}=a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger}%
\wedge a_{j_{1}}.
\end{equation}

Again in a slightly different way if $j_{1}\neq j_{2}$ 
\begin{align}
a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger}\wedge a_{j_{2}} & =\left\langle a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger
}a_{j_{2}}\right\rangle _{2}=\frac{1}{2}\left\langle \left(
x_{j_{1}}-ix_{j_{1}+r}\right) \left( x_{j_{2}}+ix_{j_{2}+r}\right)
\right\rangle _{2} \\
& =\frac{1}{2}\left( \left( x_{j_{1}}x_{j_{2}}+x_{j_{1}+r}x_{j_{2}+r}\right)
+i\left( x_{j_{1}}x_{j_{2}+r}-x_{j_{1}+r}x_{j_{2}}\right) \right)
=a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger}a_{j_{2}},
\end{align}
If $j_{1}=j_{2}$ 
\begin{align}
a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger}\wedge a_{j_{1}} & =ix_{j_{1}}x_{j_{1}+r}=-\frac{1}{2}%
\left( a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger}+a_{j_{1}}\right) \left( a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger
}-a_{j_{1}}\right) \\
& =-\frac{1}{2}\left(
a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger}a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger}+a_{j_{1}}a_{j_{1}}^{%
\dagger}-a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger}a_{j_{1}}+a_{j_{1}}a_{j_{1}}\right) \\
& =-\frac{1}{2}\left( a_{j_{1}}a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger}-a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger
}a_{j_{1}}\right) =-\frac{1}{2}\left( I_{\mathcal{F}}-2a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger
}a_{j_{1}}\right) =a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger}a_{j_{1}}-\frac{1}{2}I_{\mathcal{F}}
\end{align}
and 
\begin{align}
a_{j_{1}} & \wedge a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger}=\left\langle
a_{j_{1}}a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger}\right\rangle _{2}=\frac{1}{2}\left\langle
\left( x_{j_{1}}+ix_{j_{1}+r}\right) \left( x_{j_{1}}-ix_{j_{1}+r}\right)
\right\rangle _{2} \\
& =\frac{1}{2}\left( \left\langle x_{j_{1}}x_{j_{1}}\right\rangle
_{2}+\left\langle x_{j_{1}+r}x_{j_{1}+r}\right\rangle _{2}+i\left(
\left\langle x_{j_{1}+r}x_{j_{1}}\right\rangle _{2}-\left\langle
x_{j_{1}}x_{j_{1}+r}\right\rangle _{2}\right) \right) =-ix_{j_{1}}x_{j_{1}+r}%
\text{ }.
\end{align}
Hence%
\begin{equation}
a_{j_{1}}\wedge
a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger}=-ix_{j_{1}}x_{j_{1}+r}=-a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger}\wedge
a_{j_{1}}.
\end{equation}
The Clifford/Geometric product of $a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger}$ and $a_{j_{2}}$ 
\begin{equation*}
a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger}a_{j_{2}}=\frac{1}{2}\left( x_{j_{1}}-ix_{j_{1}+r}\right)
\left( x_{j_{2}}+ix_{j_{2}+r}\right) =\frac{1}{2}\left(
x_{j_{1}}x_{j_{2}}+ix_{j_{1}}x_{j_{2}+r}-ix_{j_{1}+r}x_{j_{2}}+x_{j_{1}+r}x_{j_{2}+r}\right) , 
\end{equation*}
which gives in particular 
\begin{align}
n_{j_{1}} & =a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger}a_{j_{1}}=\frac{1}{2}\left(
x_{j_{1}}x_{j_{1}}+x_{j_{1}+r}x_{j_{1}+r}+i\left(
x_{j_{1}}x_{j_{2}+r}-x_{j_{1}+r}x_{j_{2}}\right) \right) \\
& =\frac{1}{2}\left( x_{j_{1}}x_{j_{2}}+x_{j_{1}+r}x_{j_{2}+r}+i\left(
x_{j_{1}}x_{j_{1}+r}-x_{j_{1}+r}x_{j_{1}}\right) \right) \\
& =\frac{1}{2}I_{\mathcal{F}}+ix_{j_{1}}x_{j_{1}+r}
\end{align}
which shows that 
\begin{equation}
h_{j_{1}}=a_{j_{1}}a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger}=\frac{1}{2}I_{\mathcal{F}%
}-ix_{j_{1}}x_{j_{1}+r}.
\end{equation}

\paragraph{GNS Maps}

The action of $\Phi_{f_{a}}$ on the self adjoint basis is 
\begin{align}
\Phi_{f_{a}}\left( x_{j_{1}}\right) & =\frac{1}{\sqrt{2}}\Phi_{f_{a}}\left(
a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger}+a_{j_{1}}\right) =\frac{1}{\sqrt{2}}\Phi_{f_{a}}\left(
a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger}\right) \\
\Phi_{f_{a}}\left( x_{j_{1}+r}\right) & =\frac{i}{\sqrt{2}}%
\Phi_{f_{a}}\left( a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger}-a_{j_{1}}\right) =\frac{i}{\sqrt{2}}%
\Phi_{f_{a}}\left( a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger}\right)
\end{align}
which shows that as a complex map it is not $1-1$ but as a real map it is.

For second order products where $j_{1}=j_{2}$ 
\begin{align}
\Phi_{f_{a}}\left( x_{j_{1}}\wedge x_{j_{1}}\right) & =\frac{1}{2}%
\Phi_{f_{a}}\left( \left( a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger}+a_{j_{1}}\right) \wedge\left(
a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger}+a_{j_{1}}\right) \right) =\frac{1}{2}\Phi_{f_{a}}\left(
a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger}\wedge a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger}+a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger}\wedge
a_{j_{1}}+a_{j_{1}}\wedge a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger}+a_{j_{1}}\wedge
a_{j_{1}}\right) \\
& =\frac{1}{2}\Phi_{f_{a}}\left( a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger}\wedge
a_{j_{1}}+a_{j_{1}}\wedge a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger}\right) =\frac{1}{2}%
\Phi_{f_{a}}\left( ix_{j_{1}}x_{j_{1}+r}-ix_{j_{1}}x_{j_{1}+r}\right) =0,
\end{align}
while for $j_{1}\neq j_{2}$ $\leq r$%
\begin{align}
\Phi_{f_{a}}\left( x_{j_{1}}\wedge x_{j_{2}}\right) & =\Phi_{f_{a}}\left(
x_{j_{1}}x_{j_{2}}\right) \text{ } \\
& =\frac{1}{2}\Phi_{f_{a}}\left( \left( a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger}+a_{j_{1}}\right)
\left( a_{j_{2}}^{\dagger}+a_{j_{2}}\right) \right) =\frac{1}{2}%
\Phi_{f_{a}}\left(
a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger}a_{j_{2}}^{\dagger}+a_{j_{1}}a_{j_{2}}^{%
\dagger}+a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger}a_{j_{2}}+a_{j_{1}}a_{j_{2}}\right) \\
& =\frac{1}{2}\Phi_{f_{a}}\left( a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger}a_{j_{2}}^{\dagger
}\right) =\frac{1}{\sqrt{2}}\Phi_{f_{a}}\left( a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger}\right)
\wedge\frac{1}{\sqrt{2}}\Phi_{f_{a}}\left( a_{j_{2}}^{\dagger}\right)
=\Phi_{f_{a}}\left( x_{j_{1}}\right) \wedge\Phi_{f_{a}}\left(
x_{j_{2}}\right) ,
\end{align}
for $j_{1}\neq j_{2}\leq r$ 
\begin{align}
\Phi_{f_{a}}\left( x_{j_{1}}\wedge x_{j_{2}+r}\right) & =\Phi_{f_{a}}\left(
x_{j_{1}}x_{j_{2}+r}\right) \\
& =\frac{i}{2}\Phi_{f_{a}}\left( \left( a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger}+a_{j_{1}}\right)
\left( a_{j_{2}}^{\dagger}-a_{j_{2}}\right) \right) =\frac{i}{2}%
\Phi_{f_{a}}\left(
a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger}a_{j_{2}}^{\dagger}-a_{j_{1}}a_{j_{2}}^{%
\dagger}+a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger}a_{j_{2}}-a_{j_{1}}a_{j_{2}}\right) \\
& =\frac{i}{2}\Phi_{f_{a}}\left( a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger}a_{j_{2}}^{\dagger
}\right) =\frac{1}{\sqrt{2}}\Phi_{f_{a}}\left( a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger}\right)
\wedge\frac{i}{\sqrt{2}}\Phi_{f_{a}}\left( a_{j_{2}}^{\dagger}\right)
=\Phi_{f_{a}}\left( x_{j_{1}}\right) \wedge\Phi_{f_{a}}\left(
x_{j_{2}+r}\right)
\end{align}
thus $\Phi_{f_{a}}$ is a homomorphism for all the preceeding cases, but for $%
j_{1}=j_{2}$ 
\begin{align}
\Phi_{f_{a}}\left( x_{j_{1}}\wedge x_{j_{1}+r}\right) & =\Phi_{f_{a}}\left(
x_{j_{1}}x_{j_{1}+r}\right) \\
& =\frac{i}{2}\Phi_{f_{a}}\left( \left( a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger}+a_{j_{1}}\right)
\left( a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger}-a_{j_{1}}\right) \right) =\frac{i}{2}%
\Phi_{f_{a}}\left(
a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger}a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger}-a_{j_{1}}a_{j_{1}}^{%
\dagger}+a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger}a_{j_{1}}-a_{j_{1}}a_{j_{2}}\right) \\
& =\frac{i}{2}\Phi_{f_{a}}\left(
a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger}a_{j_{1}}-a_{j_{1}}a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger}\right) =\frac{i}{2}%
\Phi_{f_{a}}\left( a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger}a_{j_{1}}-\left( I_{\mathcal{F}%
}-a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger}a_{j_{1}}\right) \right) =\frac{i}{2}\Phi_{f_{a}}\left(
2a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger }a_{j_{1}}-I_{\mathcal{F}}\right) \\
& =\frac{i}{2}\Phi_{f_{a}}\left( 2a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger}a_{j_{1}}-I_{\mathcal{F}%
}\right) =i\Phi_{f_{a}}\left( a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger}a_{j_{1}}\right) -\frac{i}{2%
}\Phi_{f_{a}}\left( I_{\mathcal{F}}\right) =-\frac {i}{2}\Phi_{f_{a}}\left(
I_{\mathcal{F}}\right) \neq\Phi_{f_{a}}\left( x_{j_{1}}\right)
\wedge\Phi_{f_{a}}\left( x_{j_{2}+r}\right) ,
\end{align}
as 
\begin{equation}
\Phi_{f_{a}}\left( x_{j_{1}}\right) \wedge\Phi_{f_{a}}\left(
x_{j_{2}+r}\right) =\frac{1}{\sqrt{2}}\Phi_{f_{a}}\left(
a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger}\right) \wedge\frac{i}{\sqrt{2}}\Phi_{f_{a}}\left(
a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger}\right) =0,
\end{equation}
hence it is \emph{NOT }homomorphic on these bases elements i.e. in the case $%
x_{j_{1}}x_{j_{1}+r}$ the GNS$\ $linear map, $\Phi_{f_{a}},$ fails to be an
exerior algebra map.

The action of $\Phi_{f_{a}}$ on the non self adjoint basis for $j_{1}\neq
j_{2}$ is 
\begin{align}
\Phi_{f_{a}}\left( a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger}\wedge a_{j_{2}}\right) &
=\Phi_{f_{a}}\left( a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger}a_{j_{2}}\right) =\frac{1}{2}\left(
\Phi_{f_{a}}\left( x_{j_{1}}x_{j_{2}}\right) +\Phi_{f_{a}}\left(
x_{j_{1}+r}x_{j_{2}+r}\right) +i\Phi_{f_{a}}\left(
x_{j_{1}}x_{j_{2}+r}\right) -i\Phi_{f_{a}}\left( x_{j_{1}+r}x_{j_{2}}\right)
\right) \\
& =\frac{1}{2}\left( \Phi_{f_{a}}\left( x_{j_{1}}x_{j_{2}}\right)
-\Phi_{f_{a}}\left( x_{j_{1}}x_{j_{2}}\right) +\Phi_{f_{a}}\left(
x_{j_{1}}x_{j_{2}}\right) -\Phi_{f_{a}}\left( x_{j_{1}}x_{j_{2}}\right)
\right) =0
\end{align}
hence 
\begin{equation}
\Phi_{f_{a}}\left( a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger}\wedge a_{j_{2}}\right)
=\Phi_{f_{a}}\left( a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger}\right) \wedge\Phi_{f_{a}}\left(
a_{j_{2}}\right) =0,
\end{equation}
which also shows that%
\begin{equation}
\Phi_{f_{a}}\left( a_{j_{1}}\wedge a_{j_{2}}^{\dagger}\right)
=\Phi_{f_{a}}\left( a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger}\right) \wedge\Phi_{f_{a}}\left(
a_{j_{2}}\right) =\Phi_{f_{a}}\left( a_{j_{1}}a_{j_{2}}^{\dagger}\right)
=-\Phi_{f_{a}}\left( a_{j_{2}}^{\dagger}a_{j_{1}}\right) =0,j_{1}\neq j_{2}.
\end{equation}
When $j_{1}=j_{2}$ 
\begin{equation}
\Phi_{f_{a}}\left( a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger}a_{j_{1}}\right) =\frac{1}{2}%
\Phi_{f_{a}}\left( I_{\mathcal{F}}\right) +i\Phi_{f_{a}}\left(
x_{j_{1}}x_{j_{1}+r}\right) =0\Rightarrow\Phi_{f_{a}}\left(
x_{j_{1}}x_{j_{1}+r}\right) =-\frac{1}{2i}\Phi_{f_{a}}\left( I_{\mathcal{F}%
}\right)
\end{equation}
showing that 
\begin{equation}
\Phi_{f_{a}}\left( a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger}\wedge a_{j_{1}}\right)
=i\Phi_{f_{a}}\left( x_{j_{1}}x_{j_{1}+r}\right) =-\frac{1}{2}%
\Phi_{f_{a}}\left( I_{\mathcal{F}}\right) \,\emph{\neq\,}\Phi_{f_{a}}\left(
a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger }\right) \wedge\Phi_{f_{a}}\left( a_{j_{1}}\right) =0
\end{equation}
and 
\begin{equation}
\Phi_{f_{a}}\left( a_{j_{1}}\wedge a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger}\right) =-i\Phi
_{f_{a}}\left( x_{j_{1}}x_{j_{1}+r}\right) =\frac{1}{2}\Phi_{f_{a}}\left( I_{%
\mathcal{F}}\right) \,\emph{\neq\,}\Phi_{f_{a}}\left( a_{j_{1}}\right)
\wedge\Phi_{f_{a}}\left( a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger}\right) =0.
\end{equation}
\emph{Thus again showing }$\Phi_{f_{a}}$ \emph{induced by a vacuum state} $%
f_{a}$ \emph{is NOT an exterior algebra map on }$\mathcal{F}$ \emph{only on }%
$\tbigwedge \left( \mathcal{F}_{a+}\right) .$ \emph{Note that this is not
true for states in general.}

$\lceil$The CAR gives 
\begin{gather}
a_{j}^{\dagger}a_{j}=I_{\mathcal{F}}-a_{j}a_{j}^{\dagger} \\
\Pi_{f}\left( I_{\mathcal{F}}-a_{j}a_{j}^{\dagger}\right) \Phi_{f}\left(
I\right) =\Phi_{f}\left( I\right) -\Phi_{f}\left( a_{j}a_{j}^{\dagger
}\right) =0 \\
\Longleftrightarrow\Phi_{f}\left( I\right) =\Phi_{f}\left(
a_{j}a_{j}^{\dagger}\right)
\end{gather}
which is consistent with%
\begin{equation}
\left\langle \left. \Phi_{f}\left( I_{\mathcal{F}}\right) \right\vert
\Phi_{f}\left( a_{j}a_{j}^{\dagger}\right) \right\rangle =\limfunc{tr}%
\left\{ a_{j}a_{j}^{\dagger}\left\vert \phi_{a}\right\rangle \left\langle
\phi_{a}\right\vert \right\} =1.
\end{equation}
One can double check that 
\begin{equation}
a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger}a_{j_{1}}\in\ker\left\{ \Phi_{f_{a}}\right\}
\end{equation}
by considering%
\begin{align}
& \left\langle \left. \Phi_{f}\left( a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger}a_{j_{1}}\right)
\right\vert \Phi_{f}\left( a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger}a_{j_{1}}\right) \right\rangle
\\
& =\left\langle \left. \frac{1}{2}\Phi_{f}\left( I_{\mathcal{F}}\right)
+i\Phi_{f}\left( x_{j_{1}}x_{j_{1}+r}\right) \right\vert \frac{1}{2}\Phi
_{f}\left( I_{\mathcal{F}}\right) +i\Phi_{f}\left(
x_{j_{1}}x_{j_{1}+r}\right) \right\rangle \\
& =\left\langle \left. \frac{1}{2}\Phi_{f}\left( I_{\mathcal{F}}\right)
\right\vert \frac{1}{2}\Phi_{f}\left( I_{\mathcal{F}}\right) \right\rangle
+\left\langle \left. \frac{1}{2}\Phi_{f}\left( I_{\mathcal{F}}\right)
\right\vert i\Phi_{f}\left( x_{j_{1}}x_{j_{1}+r}\right) \right\rangle \\
& +\left\langle i\Phi_{f}\left( x_{j_{1}}x_{j_{1}+r}\right) \left\vert \frac{%
1}{2}\Phi_{f}\left( I_{\mathcal{F}}\right) \right. \right\rangle
+\left\langle \left. i\Phi_{f}\left( x_{j_{1}}x_{j_{1}+r}\right) \right\vert
i\Phi_{f}\left( x_{j_{1}}x_{j_{1}+r}\right) \right\rangle \\
& =\frac{1}{4}+\frac{i}{2}\limfunc{tr}\left\{ x_{j_{1}}x_{j_{1}+r}\left\vert
\phi_{a}\right\rangle \left\langle \phi_{a}\right\vert \right\} +\frac{i}{2}%
\limfunc{tr}\left\{ x_{j_{1}+r}x_{j_{1}}\left\vert \phi _{a}\right\rangle
\left\langle \phi_{a}\right\vert \right\} -\limfunc{tr}\left\{
x_{j_{1}+r}x_{j_{1}}x_{j_{1}}x_{j_{1}+r}\left\vert \phi_{a}\right\rangle
\left\langle \phi_{a}\right\vert \right\} \\
& =\frac{1}{4}-\frac{1}{4}=0,
\end{align}
thus%
\begin{equation}
\Phi_{f}\left( a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger}a_{j_{1}}\right) =\frac{1}{2}\Phi
_{f}\left( I_{\mathcal{F}}\right) +i\Phi_{f}\left(
x_{j_{1}}x_{j_{1}+r}\right) =0.
\end{equation}
This again shows that 
\begin{align}
\Phi_{f}\left( a_{j_{1}}a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger}\right) & =\Phi_{f}\left( I_{%
\mathcal{F}}-a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger}a_{j_{1}}\right) =\Phi_{f}\left( I_{\mathcal{%
F}}\right) , \\
\Phi_{f_{a}}\left( x_{j_{1}}x_{j_{1}+r}\right) & =-\frac{1}{2i}\Phi
_{f_{a}}\left( I_{\mathcal{F}}\right) \\
\Phi_{f_{a}}\left( a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger}\wedge a_{j_{1}}\right) &
=i\Phi_{f_{a}}\left( x_{j_{1}}x_{j_{1}+r}\right) =-\frac{1}{2}%
\Phi_{f_{a}}\left( I_{\mathcal{F}}\right) \neq\Phi_{f_{a}}\left(
a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger }\right) \wedge\Phi_{f_{a}}\left( a_{j_{1}}\right) .
\end{align}
It is interesting to note that this also shows that 
\begin{equation}
\Phi_{f}\left( a_{j_{1}}a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger}-a_{j_{2}}a_{j_{2}}^{\dagger
}\right) =0.
\end{equation}
$\rfloor$

Note that according to the definition of wedge product in $\mathcal{F}$ 
\begin{equation*}
I_{\mathcal{F}}\wedge A=A\,\forall A\in\mathcal{F}
\end{equation*}
thus $I_{\mathcal{F}}$ is the identity wrt the exterior algebra structure of 
$\mathcal{F}.$

\emph{In summary} \emph{the map }$\Phi_{f}$\emph{\ is an exterior algebra
isomorphism from }$\bigwedge\left( \mathbf{a}^{\dagger}\right) $ to $%
\bigwedge\Phi_{f}\left( \mathbf{a}^{\dagger}\right) $ as it\emph{\ }is $1-1$
and onto as a vector space map 
\begin{equation}
\Phi_{f}:\bigwedge\left( \mathbf{a}^{\dagger}\right) \rightarrow
\bigwedge\Phi_{f}\left( \mathbf{a}^{\dagger}\right) \text{ i.e. }\Phi
_{f}\left( \bigwedge\left( \mathbf{a}^{\dagger}\right) \right)
=\bigwedge\Phi_{f}\left( \mathbf{a}^{\dagger}\right)
\end{equation}
and as an algebra map 
\begin{equation}
\Phi_{f}\left( \bigwedge\left( \mathbf{a}^{\dagger}\right) \right)
=\bigwedge\Phi_{f}\left( \mathbf{a}^{\dagger}\right) .
\end{equation}
\emph{The vector }$\Phi_{f}\left( I_{\mathcal{F}}\right) $ acts as the
identity in $\Phi_{f}\left( \bigwedge\left( \mathbf{a}^{\dagger}\right)
\right) $ i.e. \emph{\ } 
\begin{equation}
\Phi_{f_{a}}\left( I_{\mathcal{F}}\right) \wedge\Phi_{f_{a}}\left(
a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger}\cdots a_{j_{p}}^{\dagger}\right) =\Phi_{f_{a}}\left( I_{%
\mathcal{F}}\wedge a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger}\cdots a_{j_{p}}^{\dagger}\right)
=\Phi_{f_{a}}\left( a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger}\cdots a_{j_{p}}^{\dagger}\right)
,1\leq j_{1}<\cdots j_{p}\leq r,0\leq p\leq r.
\end{equation}

The representation of the c$^{\ast}$-algebra $\mathcal{F}$ given by the map $%
\Pi_{f_{a}}$ 
\begin{equation}
\Pi_{f_{a}}:\mathcal{F\rightarrow}\Pi_{f_{a}}\left( \mathcal{F}\right)
\subseteq\mathcal{B}\left( \Phi_{f_{a}}\left( \mathcal{F}\right) \right) =%
\mathcal{B}\left( \bigwedge\left( \Phi_{f_{a}}\left( \mathbf{a}^{\dagger
}\right) \right) \right) .
\end{equation}
produces a Fock representation as 
\begin{align}
\Pi_{f_{a}}\left( a_{j}^{\dagger}\right) \Phi_{f_{a}}\left( I_{\mathcal{F}%
}\right) & =\Phi_{f_{a}}\left( a_{j}^{\dagger}\right) \\
\Pi_{f_{a}}\left( a_{j}\right) \Phi_{f_{a}}\left( I_{\mathcal{F}}\right) &
=\Phi_{f_{a}}\left( a_{j}\right) =0
\end{align}
showing that $\Phi_{f_{a}}\left( I_{\mathcal{F}}\right) $ acts as a
traditional generating vacuum vector state, while also acting as a grade
zero vector. All normal form operators 
\begin{equation}
\Pi_{f_{a}}\left( \tsum _{\substack{ 1\leq j_{1},\cdots,j_{n}\leq r \\ 1\leq
k_{1},\cdots,k_{m}\leq r}}\alpha_{j_{1}\cdots j_{n}k_{1}\cdots
k_{m}}a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger}\cdots a_{j_{n}}^{\dagger}a_{k_{1}}\cdots
a_{k_{m}}\right)
\end{equation}
annihilate $\Phi_{f_{a}}\left( I_{\mathcal{F}}\right) $ (as they should if $%
\Phi_{f_{a}}\left( I_{\mathcal{F}}\right) $ is a vacuum vector for $%
\Pi_{f_{a}}\left( \mathcal{F}\right) $)$,$ but are in general non zero on
other vectors in $\mathcal{H}_{f_{a}}.$

We now compare the operator wedge product defined by $\bigwedge\left(
\Phi_{f_{a}}\left( \mathbf{a}^{\dagger}\right) \right) $ in $\mathcal{B}%
\left( \bigwedge\left( \Phi_{f_{a}}\left( \mathbf{a}^{\dagger}\right)
\right) \right) $ by a vacuum state $f_{a}$ to the wedge product in $%
\mathcal{F}$ and determine whether $\Pi_{f_{a}}$ is an exterior algebra map
i.e. 
\begin{equation}
\Pi_{f_{a}}\left( A\wedge B\right) \overset{?}{=}\Pi_{f_{a}}\left( A\right)
\wedge\Pi_{f_{a}}\left( B\right) ,
\end{equation}
where the exterior products are the ones defined in $\mathcal{F}$ and $%
\mathcal{B}\left( \Phi_{f_{a}}\left( \mathcal{F}\right) \right) =\mathcal{B}%
\left( \bigwedge\left( \Phi_{f_{a}}\left( \mathcal{F}_{1}\right) \right)
\right) =\mathcal{B}\left( \bigwedge\left( \Phi_{f_{a}}\left( \mathbf{a}%
^{\dagger}\right) \right) \right) .$

The definition of the operator exterior product in $\mathcal{B}\left(
\bigwedge\left( \Phi_{f_{a}}\left( \mathbf{a}^{\dagger}\right) \right)
\right) $ is 
\begin{align}
& \left( \Pi_{f_{a}}\left( A_{1}\right) \wedge\cdots\wedge\Pi_{f_{a}}\left(
A_{n}\right) \right) \Phi_{f_{a}}\left( a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger}\cdots
a_{j_{n}}^{\dagger}\right) =\left( \Pi_{f_{a}}\left( A_{1}\right)
\wedge\cdots\wedge\Pi_{f_{a}}\left( A_{n}\right) \right) \Phi_{f_{a}}\left(
a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger}\right) \wedge\cdots\wedge\Phi_{f_{a}}\left(
a_{j_{n}}^{\dagger}\right) \\
& =\frac{1}{n!}\sum_{\sigma\in S_{n}}\Pi_{f_{a}}\left( A_{\sigma\left(
1\right) }\right) \Phi_{f_{a}}\left( a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger}\right)
\wedge\cdots\wedge\Pi_{f_{a}}\left( A_{\sigma\left( n\right) }\right)
\Phi_{f_{a}}\left( a_{j_{n}}^{\dagger}\right) =\frac{1}{n!}\sum_{\sigma\in
S_{n}}\Phi_{f_{a}}\left( A_{\sigma\left( 1\right) }a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger
}\right) \wedge\cdots\wedge\Phi_{f_{a}}\left( A_{\sigma\left( n\right)
}a_{j_{n}}^{\dagger}\right)
\end{align}
$\lceil$%
\begin{align}
& \left( \Pi_{f_{a}}\left( A_{1}\right) \wedge\Pi_{f_{a}}\left( A_{2}\right)
\right) \left\vert \Phi_{f}\left( a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger}\right)
\wedge\Phi_{f}\left( a_{j_{2}}^{\dagger}\right) \right\rangle =\frac{1}{2!}%
\sum_{\sigma\in S_{2}}\Pi_{f_{a}}\left( A_{\sigma\left( 1\right) }\right)
\Phi_{f_{a}}\left( a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger}\right) \wedge\Pi_{f_{a}}\left(
A_{\sigma\left( 2\right) }\right) \Phi_{f_{a}}\left(
a_{j_{2}}^{\dagger}\right) \\
& =\frac{1}{2}\left( \Pi_{f_{a}}\left( A_{1}\right) \Phi_{f_{a}}\left(
a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger}\right) \wedge\Pi_{f_{a}}\left( A_{2}\right) \Phi
_{f_{a}}\left( a_{j_{2}}^{\dagger}\right) +\Pi_{f_{a}}\left( A_{1}\right)
\Phi_{f_{a}}\left( a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger}\right) \wedge\Pi_{f_{a}}\left(
A_{2}\right) \Phi_{f_{a}}\left( a_{j_{2}}^{\dagger}\right) \right) \\
& =\frac{1}{2}\left( \Phi_{f_{a}}\left( A_{1}a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger}\right)
\wedge\Phi_{f_{a}}\left( A_{2}a_{j_{2}}^{\dagger}\right) +\Phi_{f_{a}}\left(
A_{2}a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger}\right) \wedge\Phi_{f_{a}}\left(
A_{1}a_{j_{2}}^{\dagger}\right) \right)
\end{align}
$\rfloor$

We have shown that for vacuum states 
\begin{equation}
\Phi_{f_{a}}\left( a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger}a_{j_{2}}^{\dagger}\right)
=\Phi_{f_{a}}\left( a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger}\wedge a_{j_{2}}^{\dagger}\right)
=\Phi_{f_{a}}\left( a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger}\right) \wedge\Phi_{f_{a}}\left(
a_{j_{2}}^{\dagger}\right)
\end{equation}
but it is not true that%
\begin{equation}
\Phi_{f_{a}}\left( a_{j_{1}}a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger}\right) =\Phi_{f_{a}}\left(
a_{j_{1}}\wedge a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger}\right) =\Phi_{f_{a}}\left(
a_{j_{1}}\right) \wedge\Phi_{f_{a}}\left( a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger}\right)
\end{equation}
i.e 
\begin{equation}
\Phi_{f_{a}}\left( a_{j_{1}}\wedge a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger}\right) \neq
\Phi_{f_{a}}\left( a_{j_{1}}\right) \wedge\Phi_{f_{a}}\left(
a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger}\right) .
\end{equation}
Hence 
\begin{equation}
\Pi_{f_{a}}\left( a_{j_{1}}\wedge a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger}\right)
\neq\Pi_{f_{a}}\left( a_{j_{1}}\right) \wedge\Pi_{f_{a}}\left(
a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger }\right)
\end{equation}
and $\Pi_{f_{a}}$ is \emph{NOT }an exterior algebra isomorphism from $%
\mathcal{F}$ to $\Pi_{f_{a}}\left( \mathcal{F}\right) $ wrt the Clifford
exterior product in $\mathcal{F}$ and the operator exterior product in $%
\Pi_{f_{a}}\left( \mathcal{F}\right) .$

The following relationships can be easily observed%
\begin{equation}
\Pi_{f_{a}}\left( a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger}a_{j_{2}}^{\dagger}\right)
\Phi_{f_{a}}\left( I_{\mathcal{F}}\right) =\Pi_{f_{a}}\left(
a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger }\right) \Pi_{f}\left( a_{j_{2}}^{\dagger}\right)
\Phi_{f_{a}}\left( I_{\mathcal{F}}\right) =\Pi_{f_{a}}\left(
a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger}\wedge a_{j_{2}}^{\dagger}\right) \Phi_{f_{a}}\left( I_{%
\mathcal{F}}\right) =\Pi_{f_{a}}\left( a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger}\right)
\wedge\Pi_{f_{a}}\left( a_{j_{2}}^{\dagger}\right) \Phi_{f_{a}}\left( I_{%
\mathcal{F}}\right) =\Phi_{f_{a}}\left(
a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger}a_{j_{2}}^{\dagger}\right)
\end{equation}%
\begin{equation}
\Pi_{f_{a}}\left( a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger}\right) \Phi_{f_{a}}\left(
a_{j_{2}}^{\dagger}\right) =\Phi_{f_{a}}\left(
a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger}a_{j_{2}}^{\dagger}\right) =\Pi_{f_{a}}\left(
a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger}a_{j_{2}}^{\dagger }\right) \Phi_{f_{a}}\left( I_{%
\mathcal{F}}\right) =\Pi_{f_{a}}\left( a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger}\right)
\wedge\Pi_{f_{a}}\left( a_{j_{2}}^{\dagger }\right) \Phi_{f_{a}}\left( I_{%
\mathcal{F}}\right)
\end{equation}%
\begin{equation}
\Pi_{f_{a}}\left( a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger}\wedge a_{j_{2}}^{\dagger}\right)
\Phi_{f_{a}}\left( A\right) =\Phi_{f_{a}}\left( a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger}\wedge
a_{j_{2}}^{\dagger}\wedge A\right) =\Phi_{f_{a}}\left( a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger
}a_{j_{2}}^{\dagger}A\right) =\Pi_{f_{a}}\left( a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger}\right)
\wedge\Pi_{f_{a}}\left( a_{j_{2}}^{\dagger}\right) \Phi_{f_{a}}\left(
A\right) \quad\forall A\in\bigwedge\left( \mathbf{a}^{\dagger}\right) .
\end{equation}
The exterior product in $\Pi_{f_{a}}\left( \mathcal{F}\right) $ has to be
defined as 
\begin{equation}
\Phi_{f_{a}}\left( a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger}\right) \wedge\Phi_{f_{a}}\left(
a_{j_{2}}^{\dagger}\right) =\frac{1}{2}\left( \Phi_{f_{a}}\left(
a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger}\right) \otimes\Phi_{f_{a}}\left(
a_{j_{2}}^{\dagger}\right) -\Phi_{f_{a}}\left( a_{j_{2}}^{\dagger}\right)
\otimes\Phi_{f_{a}}\left( a_{j_{1}}^{\dagger}\right) \right) ,
\end{equation}
which can be accomplished by isomorphisms.

$\lceil$%
\begin{align}
\Phi_{f_{a}}\left( I_{\mathcal{F}}\right) \otimes\Phi_{f_{a}}\left(
a_{j}^{\dagger}\right) & =\left( \Phi_{f_{a}}\left( I_{\mathcal{F}}\right)
,\Phi_{f_{a}}\left( a_{j}^{\dagger}\right) \right) \\
\Phi_{f_{a}}\left( I_{\mathcal{F}}\right) \wedge\Phi_{f_{a}}\left(
a_{j}^{\dagger}\right) & =\Phi_{f_{a}}\left( a_{j}^{\dagger}\right) \sim%
\frac{1}{2}\left( \Phi_{f_{a}}\left( I_{\mathcal{F}}\right) \otimes
\Phi_{f_{a}}\left( a_{j}^{\dagger}\right) -\Phi_{f_{a}}\left(
a_{j}^{\dagger}\right) \otimes\Phi_{f_{a}}\left( I_{\mathcal{F}}\right)
\right) .
\end{align}
$\rfloor$

\paragraph{\emph{Partial }Isometries in c$^{\ast}$and w$^{\ast}$-Algebras}

If $QQ^{\dagger}$ is an orthogonal projector (self adjoint idempotent) then
both $Q$ and $Q^{\dagger}$ are a partial isometries and $Q^{\dagger}Q$ is
also an orthogonal projector, moreover this is equivalent to 
\begin{equation}
Q^{\dagger}QQ^{\dagger}=Q^{\dagger}\Leftrightarrow QQ^{\dagger}Q=Q,
\end{equation}
which can be thought of as a projections from the left or eigenvalue
equations 
\begin{align}
\left( QQ^{\dagger}\right) Q & =Q \\
\left( Q^{\dagger}Q\right) Q^{\dagger} & =Q^{\dagger},
\end{align}
thus $Q$ and $Q^{\dagger}$ are eigenvectors of eigenvalue 1 of $QQ^{\dagger}$
and $Q^{\dagger}Q$ respectively.

These operations could also be written as acting from the right i.e. 
\begin{align}
Q\left( Q^{\dagger}Q\right) & =Q \\
Q^{\dagger}\left( QQ^{\dagger}\right) & =Q^{\dagger}.
\end{align}
Observe that $\left\{ \left. \eta Q,\eta Q^{\dagger}\right\vert \eta \in%
\mathbb{C}\right\} $ are also eigenvectors with the same eigenvalues. These
equations can be generalized to 
\begin{align}
\left( QQ^{\dagger}\right) QA & =QA \\
\left( Q^{\dagger}Q\right) Q^{\dagger}A & =Q^{\dagger}A
\end{align}
for any $A\in\mathcal{F}.$

The projectors $QQ^{\dagger}$ and $Q^{\dagger}Q$ are said to be equivalent
and this defines an equivalence relationship on the set of orthogonal
projectors (self adjoint idempotents) contained in $\mathcal{F}$.

\paragraph{GNS and Ket Bra Operators}

$f_{QQ^{\dagger}}$ is a normal state \emph{generated} by $QQ^{\dagger}$ if 
\begin{equation}
f\left( A\right) =\left\langle \left. Q\right\vert AQ\right\rangle _{%
\mathcal{F}}=\limfunc{tr}\left\{ Q^{\dagger}AQ\right\} =\limfunc{tr}\left\{
AQQ^{\dagger}\right\} =\left\langle \left\langle AQQ^{\dagger}\right\rangle
\right\rangle _{0}\forall A\in\mathcal{F},
\end{equation}
where $Q\in\mathcal{F}.$ As $f$ is a state 
\begin{equation}
f\left( A\right) =1
\end{equation}
showing that $Q$ and $Q^{\dagger}$ are \emph{normalized} i.e. 
\begin{equation}
\limfunc{tr}\left\{ Q^{\dagger}Q\right\} =\left\langle \left. Q\right\vert
AQ\right\rangle _{\mathcal{F}}=\limfunc{tr}\left\{ QQ^{\dagger}\right\}
=\left\langle \left. Q^{\dagger}\right\vert AQ^{\dagger}\right\rangle _{%
\mathcal{F}}=1.
\end{equation}

A normal state $f_{QQ^{\dagger}}$ is a pure $\limfunc{Iff}$ $QQ^{\dagger}$
is an orthogonal projector (self adjoint idempotent) i.e. 
\begin{equation}
\left( QQ^{\dagger}\right) ^{2}=QQ^{\dagger}QQ^{\dagger}=QQ^{\dagger}.
\end{equation}

In order to give meaning to the term projector one can consider the special
case of the left regular representation of $\mathcal{F}$ on $\mathcal{F},$
which is given by the GNS\ representation $\Pi_{\limfunc{tr}},$where 
\begin{equation}
\Pi_{\limfunc{tr}}\left( \mathcal{F}\right) \subseteq\mathcal{B}\left( \Phi_{%
\limfunc{tr}}\left( \mathcal{F}\right) \right) .
\end{equation}

The eigenvalues of projectors are $\left\{ 0,1\right\} $, but observe that
such projectors do not have to be $1$- dimensional (the dimension
corresponds to the multiplicity of the eigenvalue $1$) e.g. $%
h_{j}=a_{j}a_{j}^{\dagger}$ and $n_{j}=a_{j}^{\dagger}a_{j}$ can be infinite
dimensional projectors.

$\lceil$One can observe that $\left\{ a_{j}^{\dagger},a_{j}\right\} $ are
partial isometries but they are not normalized in the CAR/Clifford algebra $%
\mathcal{F}$ as 
\begin{equation}
\limfunc{tr}\left\{ a_{j}^{\dagger}a_{j}\right\} =\limfunc{tr}\left\{
a_{j}a_{j}^{\dagger}\right\} =\frac{1}{2}
\end{equation}
The normalized operators $\left\{ \sqrt{2}a_{j}^{\dagger},\sqrt{2}%
a_{j}\right\} $ are not partial isometries as 
\begin{equation}
\left( \left( \sqrt{2}a_{j}^{\dagger}\right) \left( \sqrt{2}a_{j}\right)
\right) ^{2}=\left( 2a_{j}^{\dagger}a_{j}\right)
^{2}=4a_{j}^{\dagger}a_{j}\neq2a_{j}^{\dagger}a_{j}.
\end{equation}
We can characterize the normalized solutions of 
\begin{equation}
a_{j}a_{j}^{\dagger}A=\lambda A,
\end{equation}
we know there is at least one, namely $\frac{a_{j}}{\sqrt{\left( \limfunc{tr}%
\left\{ a_{j}a_{j}^{\dagger}\right\} \right) }}.$

The projectors $a_{j}a_{j}^{\dagger}$ thus project onto the normalized
amplitudes $\frac{a_{j}}{\sqrt{\left( \limfunc{tr}\left\{
a_{j}a_{j}^{\dagger}\right\} \right) }}$ i.e.%
\begin{equation}
a_{j}a_{j}^{\dagger}\left( \frac{a_{j}}{\sqrt{\left( \limfunc{tr}\left\{
a_{j}a_{j}^{\dagger}\right\} \right) }}\right) =\frac{a_{j}}{\sqrt{\left( 
\limfunc{tr}\left\{ a_{j}a_{j}^{\dagger}\right\} \right) }},
\end{equation}
the induced operators $\widehat{a_{j}a_{j}^{\dagger}}\in\mathcal{B}\left( 
\mathcal{F}\right) $ are also orthogonal projecters 
\begin{align}
\widehat{a_{j}a_{j}^{\dagger}}\left( \frac{a_{j}}{\sqrt{\left( \limfunc{tr}%
\left\{ a_{j}a_{j}^{\dagger}\right\} \right) }}\right) & =\left[
a_{j}a_{j}^{\dagger},\frac{a_{j}}{\sqrt{\left( \limfunc{tr}\left\{
a_{j}a_{j}^{\dagger}\right\} \right) }}\right] =a_{j}a_{j}^{\dagger}\frac{%
a_{j}}{\sqrt{\left( \limfunc{tr}\left\{ a_{j}a_{j}^{\dagger}\right\} \right) 
}}-\frac{a_{j}}{\sqrt{\left( \limfunc{tr}\left\{
a_{j}a_{j}^{\dagger}\right\} \right) }}a_{j}a_{j}^{\dagger} \\
& =\frac{a_{j}}{\sqrt{\left( \limfunc{tr}\left\{ a_{j}a_{j}^{\dagger
}\right\} \right) }}.
\end{align}
The vectors 
\begin{equation}
\left\{ \frac{a_{j}}{\sqrt{\left( \limfunc{tr}\left\{
a_{j}a_{j}^{\dagger}\right\} \right) }},\frac{a_{k}}{\sqrt{\left( \limfunc{tr%
}\left\{ a_{k}a_{k}^{\dagger}\right\} \right) }}\right\} ,\quad j\neq k
\end{equation}
are orthogonal as%
\begin{equation}
\limfunc{tr}\left\{ a_{j}^{\dagger}a_{k}\right\} =0,\quad j\neq k.
\end{equation}
This is consistent with $\widehat{a_{j}a_{j}^{\dagger}}$ being a self
adjoint operator.$\rfloor$

One can consider the following cases:

\begin{enumerate}
\item The properties of normal states i.e. normalized positive operators $%
QQ^{\dagger}\in\mathcal{F}$ (both pure and mixed) in GNS representations
generated by normal pure states $Q_{f}Q_{f}^{\dagger}$%
\begin{align}
\left\langle \left. Q\right\vert AQ\right\rangle _{\mathcal{F}} & =\limfunc{%
tr}\left\{ AQQ^{\dagger}\right\} ;\limfunc{tr}\left\{ QQ^{\dagger}\right\} =1
\\
f\left( A\right) & =\limfunc{tr}\left\{ AQ_{f}Q_{f}^{\dagger }\right\}
=\left\langle \left. \Phi_{f}\left( I_{\mathcal{F}}\right) \right\vert
\Pi_{f}\left( A\right) \Phi_{f}\left( I_{\mathcal{F}}\right) \right\rangle \\
f\left( QQ^{\dagger}\right) & =\limfunc{tr}\left\{ QQ^{\dagger
}Q_{f}Q_{f}^{\dagger}\right\} =\left\langle \left. \Phi_{f}\left( I_{%
\mathcal{F}}\right) \right\vert \Pi_{f}\left( Q_{f}Q_{f}^{\dagger }\right)
\Phi_{f}\left( I_{\mathcal{F}}\right) \right\rangle =\left\langle \left.
\Phi_{f}\left( Q^{\dagger}\right) \right\vert \Phi_{f}\left(
Q^{\dagger}\right) \right\rangle _{f}.
\end{align}
Hence for a general normal state 
\begin{equation}
f\left( Q_{f}Q_{f}^{\dagger}\right) =\limfunc{tr}\left\{
Q_{f}Q_{f}^{\dagger}Q_{f}Q_{f}^{\dagger}\right\} =\left\langle \left. \Phi
_{f}\left( I_{\mathcal{F}}\right) \right\vert \Pi_{f}\left(
Q_{f}Q_{f}^{\dagger}\right) \Phi_{f}\left( I_{\mathcal{F}}\right)
\right\rangle =\left\langle \left. \Phi_{f}\left( Q_{f}^{\dagger}\right)
\right\vert \Phi_{f}\left( Q_{f}^{\dagger}\right) \right\rangle _{f}
\end{equation}

\item The properties of normal pure states i.e. partial isometries of $%
\mathcal{F}$ in GNS representations generated by the same normal pure states%
\begin{align}
\left\langle \left. Q_{f}\right\vert AQ_{f}\right\rangle _{\mathcal{F}} & =%
\limfunc{tr}\left\{ AQ_{f}Q_{f}^{\dagger}\right\} ;\limfunc{tr}\left\{
Q_{f}Q_{f}^{\dagger}\right\} =1 \\
f\left( A\right) & =\limfunc{tr}\left\{ AQ_{f}Q_{f}^{\dagger }\right\}
=\left\langle \left. \Phi_{f}\left( I\right) \right\vert \Pi _{f}\left(
A\right) \Phi_{f}\left( I\right) \right\rangle _{f} \\
f\left( Q_{f}Q_{f}^{\dagger}\right) & =\limfunc{tr}\left\{
Q_{f}Q_{f}^{\dagger}Q_{f}Q_{f}^{\dagger}\right\} =\limfunc{tr}\left\{
Q_{f}Q_{f}^{\dagger}\right\} =\left\langle \left. \Phi_{f}\left(
Q_{f}^{\dagger}\right) \right\vert \Phi_{f}\left( Q_{f}^{\dagger}\right)
\right\rangle _{f}=1
\end{align}

\item The properties of normal pure states $QQ^{\dagger}$ i.e. partial
isometries $Q$ of $\mathcal{F}$ in GNS representations generated by the
trace state%
\begin{align}
\left\langle \left. Q\right\vert AQ\right\rangle _{\mathcal{F}} & =\limfunc{%
tr}\left\{ AQQ^{\dagger}\right\} ;\limfunc{tr}\left\{ QQ^{\dagger}\right\} =1
\\
\limfunc{tr}\left\{ A\right\} & =\left\langle \left. \Phi _{\limfunc{tr}%
}\left( I_{\mathcal{F}}\right) \right\vert \Phi_{\limfunc{tr}}\left(
A\right) \right\rangle _{\limfunc{tr}} \\
\limfunc{tr}\left( QQ^{\dagger}\right) & =\limfunc{tr}\left\{
QQ^{\dagger}QQ^{\dagger}\right\} =\limfunc{tr}\left\{ QQ^{\dagger }\right\}
=\left\langle \left. \Phi_{\limfunc{tr}}\left( Q^{\dagger }\right)
\right\vert \Phi_{\limfunc{tr}}\left( Q^{\dagger}\right) \right\rangle _{%
\limfunc{tr}}=1
\end{align}

\item 
\begin{align}
QQ^{\dagger} & =I_{\mathcal{F}}+\sum_{1\leq p\leq2r}\sum_{1\leq
j_{1}<\cdots<j_{p}\leq2r}\xi_{j_{1}\cdots j_{p}}x_{j_{1}}\cdots x_{j_{p}} \\
Q^{\dagger}Q & =I_{\mathcal{F}}+\sum_{1\leq p\leq2r}\sum_{1\leq
j_{1}<\cdots<j_{p}\leq2r}\eta_{j_{1}\cdots j_{p}}x_{j_{1}}\cdots x_{j_{p}}
\end{align}
In general 
\begin{equation}
QQ^{\dagger}+Q^{\dagger}Q\leq I_{\mathcal{F}}.
\end{equation}
If $Q$ was unitary 
\begin{equation}
QQ^{\dagger}=Q^{\dagger}Q=I_{\mathcal{F}}
\end{equation}
If $P$ is an orthogonal projector then 
\begin{equation}
Q=I_{\mathcal{F}}-P
\end{equation}
is also an orthogonal projector as 
\begin{align}
Q & =Q^{\dagger} \\
Q^{2} & =\left( I_{\mathcal{F}}-P\right) ^{2}=I_{\mathcal{F}}-2P+P=I_{%
\mathcal{F}}-P.
\end{align}
The projector $P$ can be expanded as 
\begin{equation}
P=I_{\mathcal{F}}+\sum_{1\leq p\leq2r}\sum_{1\leq j_{1}<\cdots<j_{p}\leq
2r}\zeta_{j_{1}\cdots j_{p}}x_{j_{1}}\cdots x_{j_{p}},
\end{equation}
thus 
\begin{equation}
Q=I_{\mathcal{F}}-P=\sum_{1\leq p\leq2r}\sum_{1\leq j_{1}<\cdots<j_{p}\leq
2r}\zeta_{j_{1}\cdots j_{p}}x_{j_{1}}\cdots x_{j_{p}},
\end{equation}
but 
\begin{equation}
\limfunc{tr}\left\{ \sum_{1\leq p\leq2r}\sum_{1\leq j_{1}<\cdots
<j_{p}\leq2r}\zeta_{j_{1}\cdots j_{p}}x_{j_{1}}\cdots x_{j_{p}}\right\} =%
\limfunc{tr}\left\{ Q\right\} =0.
\end{equation}
This shows that if $QQ^{\dagger}$is a pure state then $I_{\mathcal{F}%
}-QQ^{\dagger}$ \emph{is not a state. }
\end{enumerate}

The GNS\ representation gives 
\begin{equation}
\Pi_{f}\left( QQ^{\dagger}\right) =\Pi_{f}\left( Q\right) \Pi_{f}\left(
Q^{\dagger}\right) =\Pi_{f}\left( Q\right) \Pi_{f}\left( Q\right) ^{\dagger},
\end{equation}
as $\Pi_{f}$ is c$^{\text{*}}$-algebra mapping so $\Pi_{f}\left( Q^{\dagger
}\right) =\Pi_{f}\left( Q\right) ^{\dagger}$.

If $\left\{ Q,Q^{\dagger}\right\} $ are partial isometries the
representations of the projection eigenvalue equations for \emph{any state} $%
f$ are 
\begin{align}
\Pi_{f}\left( QQ^{\dagger}\right) \left\vert \Phi_{f}\left( Q\right)
\right\rangle & =\Pi_{f}\left( Q\right) \Pi_{f}\left( Q\right)
^{\dagger}\left\vert \Phi_{f}\left( Q\right) \right\rangle =\Phi_{f}\left(
Q\right) \\
\Pi_{f}\left( Q^{\dagger}Q\right) \left\vert \Phi_{f}\left( Q^{\dagger
}\right) \right\rangle & =\Pi_{f}\left( Q\right) ^{\dagger}\Pi_{f}\left(
Q\right) \left\vert \Phi_{f}\left( Q^{\dagger}\right) \right\rangle
=\Phi_{f}\left( Q^{\dagger}\right) .
\end{align}
The vector states $\left\{ \Phi_{f}\left( Q^{\dagger}\right) ,\Phi
_{f}\left( Q\right) \right\} $ also satisfy the eigenvalue equations 
\begin{align}
\left\vert \Phi_{f}\left( Q\right) \right\rangle \left\langle \Phi
_{f}\left( Q\right) \right\vert \left\vert \Phi_{f}\left( Q\right)
\right\rangle & =\Phi_{f}\left( Q\right) \limfunc{tr}\left\{
Q^{\dagger}QQ_{f}^{\dagger}Q_{f}\right\} =\left\vert \Phi_{f}\left( Q\right)
\right\rangle \\
\left\vert \Phi_{f}\left( Q^{\dagger}\right) \right\rangle \left\langle
\Phi_{f}\left( Q^{\dagger}\right) \right\vert \left\vert \Phi_{f}\left(
Q^{\dagger}\right) \right\rangle & =\Phi_{f}\left( Q^{\dagger}\right) 
\limfunc{tr}\left\{ QQ^{\dagger}Q_{f}Q_{f}^{\dagger}\right\} =\left\vert
\Phi_{f}\left( Q^{\dagger}\right) \right\rangle ,
\end{align}
where normalized vectors $\left\vert \Phi_{f}\left( Q\right) \right\rangle $
are defined by 
\begin{equation}
\left\vert \Phi_{f}\left( Q\right) \right\rangle =\frac{1}{\sqrt {\limfunc{tr%
}\left\{ QQ^{\dagger}Q_{f}Q_{f}^{\dagger}\right\} }}\Phi_{f}\left( Q\right) .
\end{equation}
If $\Pi_{f}$ is a faithful representation then one can consider 
\begin{equation}
\Pi_{f}^{-1}\left( \left\vert \Phi_{f}\left( Q^{\dagger}\right)
\right\rangle \left\langle \Phi_{f}\left( Q^{\dagger}\right) \right\vert
\right) ,\Pi_{f}^{-1}\left( \left\vert \Phi_{f}\left( Q\right) \right\rangle
\left\langle \Phi_{f}\left( Q\right) \right\vert \right) :\mathcal{%
F\rightarrow F}.
\end{equation}

The GNS\ construction associated to the trace state and partial isometries
give the eigenvalue equations

\begin{align}
\Pi_{\limfunc{tr}}\left( QQ^{\dagger}\right) \Phi_{\limfunc{tr}}\left(
Q\right) & =\Phi_{\limfunc{tr}}\left( QQ^{\dagger}Q\right) =\Phi_{\limfunc{tr%
}}\left( Q\right) \\
\Pi_{\limfunc{tr}}\left( Q^{\dagger}Q\right) \Phi_{\limfunc{tr}}\left(
Q^{\dagger}\right) & =\Phi_{\limfunc{tr}}\left( Q^{\dagger
}QQ^{\dagger}\right) =\Phi_{\limfunc{tr}}\left( Q^{\dagger}\right)
\end{align}
while the corresponding vector state eigenvalue equations are 
\begin{align}
\left\vert \Phi_{\limfunc{tr}}\left( Q\right) \right\rangle \left\langle
\Phi_{\limfunc{tr}}\left( Q\right) \right\vert \left\vert \Phi_{\limfunc{tr}%
}\left( Q\right) \right\rangle & =\Phi _{\limfunc{tr}}\left( Q\right) 
\limfunc{tr}\left\{ Q^{\dagger }Q\right\} =\left\vert \Phi_{\limfunc{tr}%
}\left( Q\right) \right\rangle \\
\left\vert \Phi_{\limfunc{tr}}\left( Q^{\dagger}\right) \right\rangle
\left\langle \Phi_{\limfunc{tr}}\left( Q^{\dagger}\right) \right\vert
\left\vert \Phi_{\limfunc{tr}}\left( Q^{\dagger}\right) \right\rangle &
=\Phi_{\limfunc{tr}}\left( Q^{\dagger}\right) \limfunc{tr}\left\{
QQ^{\dagger}\right\} =\left\vert \Phi_{\limfunc{tr}}\left(
Q^{\dagger}\right) \right\rangle .
\end{align}
The dimension of the idempotent $QQ^{\dagger}$ in the Trace representation
is given by 
\begin{equation}
\dim_{\limfunc{tr}}\left\{ QQ^{\dagger}\right\} =\func{Tr}\left\{ \Pi_{%
\limfunc{tr}}\left( QQ^{\dagger}\right) \right\} =\sum_{1\leq
j\leq\infty}\left\langle \Phi_{\limfunc{tr}}\left( A_{j}\right) \left. \Pi_{%
\limfunc{tr}}\left( QQ^{\dagger}\right) \right\vert \Phi_{\limfunc{tr}%
}\left( A_{j}\right) \right\rangle _{\limfunc{tr}}=\sum_{1\leq j\leq\infty}%
\limfunc{tr}\left\{ Q^{\dagger}QA_{j}A_{j}^{\dagger}\right\} \in\mathbb{N},
\end{equation}
where $\left\{ \left. \Phi_{\limfunc{tr}}\left( A_{j}\right) \right\vert
1\leq j\leq\infty\right\} $ is a conb for $\mathcal{H}_{\limfunc{tr}}.$ In
general for any normal state $f$ and the GNS\ representation $\Pi_{f}$ 
\begin{equation}
\dim_{f}\left\{ QQ^{\dagger}\right\} =\func{Tr}\left\{ \Pi _{f}\left(
QQ^{\dagger}\right) \right\} =\sum_{1\leq j\leq\infty }\left\langle
\Phi_{f}\left( A_{j}\right) \left. \Pi_{f}\left( QQ^{\dagger}\right)
\right\vert \Phi_{f}\left( A_{j}\right) \right\rangle _{f}=\sum_{1\leq
j\leq\infty}\limfunc{tr}\left\{
Q^{\dagger}QA_{j}A_{j}^{\dagger}Q_{f}Q_{f}^{\dagger}\right\} \in\mathbb{N},
\end{equation}
where $\left\{ \left. \Phi_{f}\left( A_{j}\right) \right\vert 1\leq
j\leq\infty\right\} $ is a conb for $\mathcal{H}_{f}.$

If 
\begin{equation}
\dim_{f}\left\{ QQ^{\dagger}\right\} =\func{Tr}\left\{ \Pi _{f}\left(
QQ^{\dagger}\right) \right\} =1
\end{equation}
and $Q$ is a partial isometry then 
\begin{equation*}
\Pi_{f}\left( QQ^{\dagger}\right) =\frac{\left\vert \Phi_{f}\left( Q\right)
\right\rangle \left\langle \Phi_{f}\left( Q\right) \right\vert }{%
\left\langle \left. \Phi_{f}\left( Q\right) \right\vert \Phi_{f}\left(
Q\right) \right\rangle _{f}}. 
\end{equation*}
$\lceil$ For any state $f$ 
\begin{equation}
\left\langle \left. \Phi_{f}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}}\right) \right\vert
\Phi_{f}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}}\right) \right\rangle _{f}=1
\end{equation}
\begin{align}
\left( \left\vert \Phi_{f}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}}\right) \right\rangle
\left\langle \Phi_{f}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}}\right) \right\vert \right) ^{2}
& =\left\vert \Phi_{f}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}}\right) \right\rangle
\left\langle \Phi_{f}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}}\right) \right\vert \\
\func{Tr}\left\{ \left\vert \Phi_{f}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}}\right)
\right\rangle \left\langle \Phi_{f}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}}\right) \right\vert
\right\} & =\left\vert \Phi_{f}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}}\right) \right\rangle
\left\langle \Phi_{f}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}}\right) \right\vert =1 \\
\func{Tr}\left\{ \mathrm{A}\left\vert \Phi_{f}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}}\right)
\right\rangle \left\langle \Phi_{f}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}}\right) \right\vert
\right\} & =\func{Tr}\left\{ \mathrm{AD}_{f}\right\} =\left\langle \left.
\Phi_{f}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}}\right) \right\vert \mathrm{A}\Phi_{f}\left(
I_{\mathcal{A}}\right) \right\rangle _{f};\mathrm{A}\in\mathcal{B}\left( 
\mathcal{H}_{f}\right) ,
\end{align}
where 
\begin{equation}
\mathrm{D}_{f}=\left\vert \Phi_{f}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}}\right)
\right\rangle \left\langle \Phi_{f}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}}\right) \right\vert
.
\end{equation}
If 
\begin{equation}
\mathrm{A}=\Pi_{f}\left( A\right) ,
\end{equation}
i.e. lies in $\Pi_{f}\left( \mathcal{A}\right) ,$ and $f$ is a normal state
then 
\begin{equation}
\func{Tr}\left\{ \mathrm{AD}_{f}\right\} =\left\langle \left. \Phi_{f}\left(
I_{\mathcal{A}}\right) \right\vert \mathrm{A}\Phi_{f}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}%
}\right) \right\rangle _{f}=\limfunc{tr}\left\{ AD_{f}\right\} =\limfunc{tr}%
\left\{ AQ_{f}Q_{f}^{\dagger}\right\} =\limfunc{tr}\left\{
Q_{f}^{\dagger}AQ_{f}\right\} =\left\langle \left. Q_{f}\right\vert
AQ_{f}\right\rangle _{\mathcal{A}}=\left\langle \left\langle
Q_{f}^{\dagger}AQ_{f}\right\rangle \right\rangle _{0}.
\end{equation}

One should observe that 
\begin{equation}
\mathrm{D}_{f}=\left\vert \Phi_{f}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}}\right)
\right\rangle \left\langle \Phi_{f}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}}\right) \right\vert
\end{equation}
might or might not belong to $\Pi_{f}\left( \mathcal{A}\right) ,$ if it does%
\begin{equation}
\Pi_{f}^{-1}\left( \left\vert \Phi_{f}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}}\right)
\right\rangle \left\langle \Phi_{f}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}}\right) \right\vert
\right) \equiv\Pi_{f}^{-1}\left( \mathrm{D}_{f}\right)
\end{equation}
is a self adjoint idempotent belonging to $\mathcal{A}$ and 
\begin{align}
& \Pi_{f}^{-1}\left( \left\vert \Phi_{f}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}}\right)
\right\rangle \left\langle \Phi_{f}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}}\right) \right\vert
\right) \Pi_{f}^{-1}\left( \left\vert \Phi_{f}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}}\right)
\right\rangle \left\langle \Phi_{f}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}}\right) \right\vert
\right) =\Pi_{f}^{-1}\left( \left\vert \Phi_{f}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}}\right)
\right\rangle \left\langle \Phi_{f}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}}\right) \right\vert
\left\vert \Phi_{f}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}}\right) \right\rangle \left\langle
\Phi_{f}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}}\right) \right\vert \right) \\
& =\Pi_{f}^{-1}\left( \left\vert \Phi_{f}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}}\right)
\right\rangle \left\langle \Phi_{f}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}}\right) \right\vert
\right)
\end{align}
i.e. 
\begin{equation}
\Pi_{f}^{-1}\left( \mathrm{D}_{f}\right) \Pi_{f}^{-1}\left( \mathrm{D}%
_{f}\right) =\Pi_{f}^{-1}\left( \mathrm{D}_{f}\mathrm{D}_{f}\right)
=\Pi_{f}^{-1}\left( \mathrm{D}_{f}\right) .
\end{equation}
Under these circumstances, i.e. $\Pi_{f}^{-1}\left( \mathrm{D}_{f}\right) $
and $\Pi_{f}^{-1}\left( \mathrm{A}\right) $ exist, then 
\begin{equation}
\limfunc{tr}\left\{ A\Pi_{f}^{-1}\left( \mathrm{D}_{f}\right) \right\} =%
\func{Tr}\left\{ \mathrm{AD}_{f}\right\}
\end{equation}
as%
\begin{equation}
\func{Tr}\left\{ \mathrm{AD}_{f}\right\} =\left\langle \left. \Phi_{f}\left(
I_{\mathcal{A}}\right) \right\vert \mathrm{A}\Phi_{f}\left( I_{\mathcal{A}%
}\right) \right\rangle _{f}=\limfunc{tr}\left\{ AD_{f}\right\} .
\end{equation}

Note that $\Pi_{f}^{-1}$ exists in general if $\Pi_{f}$ is a faithful
representation, i.e. $\ker\left\{ \Pi_{f}\right\} =0,$which corresponds to $%
\Pi_{f}$ being injective. If $\Pi_{f}$ is not surjective on $\mathcal{B}%
\left( \mathcal{H}_{f}\right) $ then $\Pi_{f}^{-1}$ does not exist on all of 
$\mathcal{B}\left( \mathcal{H}_{f}\right) $ only $\Pi_{f}\left( \mathcal{A}%
\right) .\rfloor$

\paragraph{Vacuum States}

One can consider vacuum states (Quasi-Free) $f_{b}$ then 
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{H}_{f_{b}}=\limfunc{linspan}\left\{ \left. \Phi_{f_{b}}\left(
b_{j_{1}}^{\dagger}\cdots b_{j_{n}}^{\dagger}\right) \right\vert 1\leq
j_{1}<\cdots<j_{n}\leq r;0\leq n\leq r\right\} .
\end{equation}
The Fock representation $\Pi_{f_{b}}$ is faithful if 
\begin{equation}
\Pi_{f_{b}}\left( A\right) \Phi_{f_{b}}\left( b_{j_{1}}^{\dagger}\cdots
b_{j_{n}}^{\dagger}\right) =0\Rightarrow A=0;1\leq j_{1}<\cdots<j_{n}\leq
r;0\leq n\leq r,
\end{equation}
i.e. 
\begin{equation}
\Phi_{f_{b}}\left( Ab_{j_{1}}^{\dagger}\cdots b_{j_{n}}^{\dagger}\right)
=0\Rightarrow A=0;1\leq j_{1}<\cdots<j_{n}\leq r;0\leq n\leq r.
\end{equation}
Let 
\begin{equation}
\Phi_{f_{b}}\left( Ab_{j_{1}}^{\dagger}\cdots b_{j_{n}}^{\dagger}\right)
=0;1\leq j_{1}<\cdots<j_{n}\leq r;0\leq n\leq r.
\end{equation}%
\begin{align}
& \left\langle \Phi_{f_{b}}\left( Ab_{j_{1}}^{\dagger}\cdots
b_{j_{n}}^{\dagger}\right) \left. \Phi_{f_{b}}\left(
Ab_{j_{1}}^{\dagger}\cdots b_{j_{n}}^{\dagger}\right) \right\vert
\right\rangle =\limfunc{tr}\left\{ b_{j_{n}}\cdots
b_{j_{1}}A^{\dagger}Ab_{j_{1}}^{\dagger}\cdots
b_{j_{n}}^{\dagger}Q_{f_{b}}Q_{f_{b}}^{\dagger}\right\} \\
& =\limfunc{tr}\left\{ Q_{f_{b}}^{\dagger}b_{j_{n}}\cdots
b_{j_{1}}A^{\dagger}Ab_{j_{1}}^{\dagger}\cdots
b_{j_{n}}^{\dagger}Q_{f_{b}}\right\} =\limfunc{tr}\left\{ \left(
Ab_{j_{1}}^{\dagger}\cdots b_{j_{n}}^{\dagger}Q_{f_{b}}\right)
^{\dagger}Ab_{j_{1}}^{\dagger}\cdots b_{j_{n}}^{\dagger}Q_{f_{b}}\right\}
\end{align}%
\begin{equation}
A\left( b_{j_{1}}^{\dagger}\cdots b_{j_{n}}^{\dagger}Q_{f_{b}}\right) =0
\end{equation}%
\begin{equation}
\sum_{0\leq m,n\leq r}\sum_{\substack{ 1\leq j_{1}<\cdots<j_{n}\leq r \\ %
1\leq k_{1}<\cdots<k_{m}\leq r}}\beta_{k_{1}\cdots k_{m}j_{1}\cdots
j_{n}}b_{k_{1}}\cdots b_{k_{m}}b_{j_{1}}^{\dagger}\cdots b_{j_{n}}^{\dagger}
\end{equation}

The 
\begin{align}
\func{Tr}\left\{ \Pi_{f_{b}}\left( A\right) \right\} & =\sum_{0\leq n\leq
r}\sum_{1\leq j_{1}<\cdots<j_{n}\leq r}f_{b}\left( Ab_{j_{n}}\cdots
b_{j_{1}}b_{j_{1}}^{\dagger}\cdots b_{j_{n}}^{\dagger}\right) \\
& =\sum_{0\leq n\leq r}\sum_{1\leq j_{1}<\cdots<j_{n}\leq r}f_{b}\left(
Ah_{j_{n}}\cdots h_{j_{1}}\right) =\sum_{0\leq n\leq r}\sum_{1\leq
j_{1}<\cdots<j_{n}\leq r}\limfunc{tr}\left\{ Ab_{j_{n}}\cdots
b_{j_{1}}b_{j_{1}}^{\dagger}\cdots
b_{j_{n}}^{\dagger}Q_{f_{b}}Q_{f_{b}}^{\dagger }\right\} .
\end{align}
If the representation $\Pi_{f_{b}}$ does not contain any finite dimensional
projectors then 
\begin{equation}
\sum_{0\leq n\leq r}\sum_{1\leq j_{1}<\cdots<j_{n}\leq r}f_{b}\left(
Q^{\dagger}Qb_{j_{n}}\cdots b_{j_{1}}b_{j_{1}}^{\dagger}\cdots
b_{j_{n}}^{\dagger}\right) =\infty
\end{equation}
for any partial isometry $Q.$This shows that vacuum representations (Quasi
Free) must be of certain types.

\bigskip\ Any normal state must have the form $QQ^{\dagger},$ if $Q$ is a
normalized \emph{operator vector }then $QQ^{\dagger}$ is a pure state\emph{.}
I conjecture that \emph{operator vectors }are of the form\emph{\ }%
\begin{equation}
Q=\alpha_{0}I_{\mathcal{F}}+\sum_{1\leq n\leq r}\sum_{1\leq j_{1}<\cdots
j_{n}\leq r}\alpha_{j_{1}\cdots j_{n}}b_{j_{1}}^{\dagger}\cdots
b_{j_{n}}^{\dagger}
\end{equation}
for some cannonical generating set $\left\{ b_{1}^{\dagger},\cdots
,b_{r}^{\dagger}\right\} $ of $\mathcal{F}.$

The kernel and the orthogonal compliment of the kernels of the partial
isometries are given by 
\begin{align}
\ker\left\{ Q\right\} & =\left\{ \left. A\right\vert QA=0;A\in \mathcal{F}%
\right\} \\
\ker\left\{ Q^{\dagger}\right\} & =\left\{ \left. A\right\vert
Q^{\dagger}A=0;A\in\mathcal{F}\right\} .
\end{align}
and 
\begin{align}
\ker\left\{ Q\right\} ^{\bot} & =\left\{ \left. Q\right\vert \limfunc{tr}%
\left\{ Q^{\dagger}B\right\} =0;Q\in\mathcal{F};\forall B\in\ker\left\{
Q\right\} \right\} \equiv\text{initial subspace} \\
\ker\left\{ Q^{\dagger}\right\} ^{\bot} & =\left\{ \left. Q\right\vert 
\limfunc{tr}\left\{ Q^{\dagger}B\right\} =0;Q\in\mathcal{F};\forall
B\in\ker\left\{ Q^{\dagger}\right\} \right\} \equiv\text{final subspace}.
\end{align}

The relationship between these subspaces is given by%
\begin{equation}
\forall B\in\ker\left\{ Q\right\} \Rightarrow\left\langle \left.
Q\right\vert QB\right\rangle _{\mathcal{F}}=\left\langle \left. Q^{\dagger
}Q\right\vert B\right\rangle _{\mathcal{F}}=0\,\forall Q\in \mathcal{%
F\Rightarrow}Q^{\dagger}Q\in\ker\left\{ Q\right\} ^{\bot }\Leftrightarrow%
\limfunc{Range}\left\{ Q^{\dagger}\right\} =\ker\left\{ Q\right\} ^{\bot}
\end{equation}%
\begin{equation}
\forall B\in\ker\left\{ Q^{\dagger}\right\} \Rightarrow\left\langle \left.
Q\right\vert Q^{\dagger}B\right\rangle _{\mathcal{F}}=\left\langle \left.
QQ\right\vert B\right\rangle _{\mathcal{F}}=0\,\forall Q\in \mathcal{%
F\Rightarrow}QQ\in\ker\left\{ Q^{\dagger}\right\} ^{\bot }\Leftrightarrow%
\limfunc{Range}\left\{ Q\right\} =\ker\left\{ Q^{\dagger}\right\} ^{\bot}.
\end{equation}

$Q$ is an isometry on $\ker\left\{ Q\right\} ^{\bot}$, while $Q^{\dagger}$
is an isometry on $\ker\left\{ Q^{\dagger}\right\} ^{\bot}$, as

\begin{align}
\left\langle \left. QA\right\vert QA\right\rangle _{\mathcal{F}} &
=\left\langle \left. Q^{\dagger}QA\right\vert A\right\rangle _{\mathcal{F}%
}=\left\langle \left. A\right\vert A\right\rangle _{\mathcal{F}} \\
Q^{\dagger}Q & =P_{\limfunc{Range}\left\{ Q\right\} }=P_{\ker \left\{
Q^{\dagger}\right\} }^{\bot},
\end{align}
where 
\begin{equation}
\limfunc{Range}\left\{ Q\right\} =\left\{ \left. QA\right\vert \forall A\in%
\mathcal{F}\right\}
\end{equation}
and%
\begin{align}
\left\langle \left. Q^{\dagger}A\right\vert Q^{\dagger}A\right\rangle _{%
\mathcal{F}} & =\left\langle \left. QQ^{\dagger}A\right\vert A\right\rangle
_{\mathcal{F}}=\left\langle \left. A\right\vert A\right\rangle _{\mathcal{F}}
\\
QQ^{\dagger} & =P_{\limfunc{Range}\left\{ Q^{\dagger}\right\}
}=P_{\ker\left\{ Q\right\} }^{\bot}.
\end{align}
